HT_DATA.load("detail", {"aircAruvnKk":{"wy":"HN treats it as the standard first stop for learning neural networks, praised for making the math intuitive.","q":{"x":"the visualisations and explanations of the math/concepts are so amazing","i":16040048,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":39902838,"author":"6mian","month":"2024-04","story":"But what is a GPT?  Visual intro to Transformers [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent introduction that made Karpathy's videos understandable","quote":"an excellent introduction that made Andrej's videos understandable","text":"For me 3brown1blue series: [VIDEO] was an excellent introduction that made Andrej's videos understandable. Then I did 3 first chapters of fastai book, but found it too high level, while I was interested in how things works under the hood. Going through Andrej's makemore tutorials required quite a lot of time but it's definitely worth it. I used free tier of Google Colab until the last one. Pausing the video a lot after he explains what he plans to do and trying to do it by myself was a very rewarding way to learn, with a lot of \"aha\" moments."},{"id":35431798,"author":"superkuh","month":"2023-04","story":"State-of-the-art open-source chatbot, Vicuna-13B, just released model weights","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ties together neural nets, gradient descent and dot products","quote":"I highly recommend checking out 3blue1brown series on how neural nets, gradient descent, and the dot product","text":"Essentially a computer neural network is just a lot of addition (and matrix multiplication) of floating point numbers. The parameters are the \"strength\" or \"weights\" of the connections between neurons on different layers and the \"bias\" of each neuron. If neuron Alice is connected to neuron Bob and Alice has a value of 0.7, and the weight of Alice's connection to bob is 0.5, then the value sent from Alice to Bob is 0.35. This value (and the values from all the other incoming connections) are summed at added to the neuron's negative bias. I highly recommend checking out 3blue1brown series on how neural nets, gradient descent, and the dot product (implemented as a matrix multiplication) all tie together: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19981623,"author":"spandexcajun","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"High quality math explanations, maybe a little basic","quote":"all his math videos are just ridiculously good quality","text":"3blue1brown videos are probably the best equivalent. Maybe a little basic? But really, all his math videos are just ridiculously good quality. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16040048,"author":"mandliya","month":"2017-12","story":"What was the best CS, SE or Math video you watched in 2017?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing visualisations give intuition for neural networks","quote":"the visualisations and explanations of the math/concepts are so amazing","text":"[VIDEO] 3Blue1Brown’s playlist on neural networks. Like other 3Blue1Brown videos, the visualisations and explanations of the math/concepts are so amazing, they give you the intuition behind the neural networks naturally."},{"id":15689936,"author":"hal9000xp","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: Best way to get started with AI?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Accessible math explanations that removed fear of ML","quote":"explains math in very accessible way","text":"I'm in the same boat. For long time, I was interested in AI but at the same time intimidated by math. I'm relatively comfortable with discrete mathematics and classical algorithms and at the same time calculus and linear algebra is completely foreign to me. Also, I do not accept way to learn ML without good understanding of core principles behind it. So math is a must. A few months ago, I stumbled upon very amazing YouTube Channel 3Blue1Brown which explains math in very accessible way and at the same time I got feeling that I finally started understanding core ideas behind linear algebra and calculus. Just recently he published 4 videos about deep neural networks: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] So my fear of ML was gone away and I'm very excited to explore whole new world for neural networks and other things like support vector machines etc"},{"id":45227436,"author":"Razengan","month":"2025-09","story":"Qwen3-Next","stance":"rec","why":"Catch up on neural network basics via a legendary playlist","quote":"thanks to the legendary 3blue1brown and his playlist on Neural Networks","text":"A while ago I had caught up with the basics thanks to the legendary 3blue1brown and his playlist on Neural Networks: [VIDEO] and other helpers like Artem Kirsanov: [LINK]"},{"id":43391604,"author":"rottc0dd","month":"2025-03","story":"Deep Learning Is Not So Mysterious or Different","stance":"rec","why":"Visual introduction to deep learning","quote":"3b1b is great for getting some visual introduction","text":"If anyone wants to delve into machine learning, one of the superb resources I have found is, Stanfords \"Probability for computer scientists\"( [LINK] ). It delves into theoretical underpinnings of probability theory and ML, IMO better than any other course I have seen. (Yeah, Andrew Ng is legendary, but his course demands some mathematical familarity with linear algebra topics) And of course, for deep learning, 3b1b is great for getting some visual introduction ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":40225532,"author":"phkahler","month":"2024-05","story":"Better and Faster Large Language Models via Multi-Token Prediction","stance":"rec","why":"Visual, approachable explanation of neural networks and AI from 3blue1brown","quote":"I would recommend the channel 3blue1brown.","text":">> YouTube has always been a mixed bag As an outsider but avid reader of this stuff linked from HN, I would recommend the channel 3blue1brown. He's got several NN and AI related videos, and the couple I've seen were pretty good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36248931,"author":"segmondy","month":"2023-06","story":"Getting into AI This Summer","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for studying AI","quote":"","text":"Studying C or Python is studying C or Python. Rather, study AI. Pick up python along the way, skip C. [VIDEO] [LINK] There's tons of book, but I recommend this since it's hands on \"Hands-On Machine Learning with Scikit-Learn, Keras, and TensorFlow\" [LINK] [LINK] download some local LLMs and play with them along the way. Good luck."},{"id":35757475,"author":"circuit10","month":"2023-04","story":"The future of generative AI is niche, not generalized","stance":"rec","why":"Technical look at how modern AI training works","quote":"This is a more technical look at how exactly it works in more modern AIs","text":"If a human brain doesn’t use maths, what does it use? It’s Magic? > \"training\", more accurately described as references Uh, no, that's not at all how the training process works. The training process very roughly mirrors evolution/natural selection except more more direct and faster. This is a good introduction to the concept of how a computer could learn: [LINK] This is a more technical look at how exactly it works in more modern AIs: [VIDEO] (it's a series) > If it encounters a problem it hasn't been programmed for, it cannot reason a new solution. But an LLM can? It's not as good at it as a human but it can Relevant: [LINK] (if you don’t want to click it it says “referring to AI models as \"just math\" or \"matrix multiplication\" is as uselessly reductive as referring to tigers as \"just biology\" or \"biochemical reactions\"”) > By your logic, video game characters must use reasoning, when no, they don't. It's maths. “By your logic, if tigers are dangerous, then bananas must be too, when no, they aren’t. It’s biology.”"},{"id":34730345,"author":"ianbutler","month":"2023-02","story":"GPT in 60 Lines of NumPy","stance":"rec","why":"Great mathematical series on gradient descent and loss","quote":"3blue1brown has a great series of videos","text":"I think FastAI lesson 3 in \"Practical Deep Learning for Coders\", has one of the most intuitive buildups of gradient descent and loss that I've seen. * Lecture [1] Book Chapter [2] It doesn't go into the math but I don't think that's a bad thing for beginners. If you want mathematical, 3blue1brown has a great series of videos [3] on the topic. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] * I've been messing around with this stuff since 2016 and have done a few different courses like the original Andrew Ng course and more."},{"id":24595807,"author":"aero-glide","month":"2020-09","story":"Bringing the Mona Lisa Effect to Life with Tensorflow.js","stance":"rec","why":"Four-part series explaining the big picture intuitively","quote":"Explains the big picture in an intuitive way","text":"Here's a 4-part video series by 3blue1brown : [VIDEO] Explains the big picture in an intuitive way"},{"id":23085192,"author":"miklosme","month":"2020-05","story":"Humans Not Invited","stance":"rec","why":"Extensive explanation of neural networks in a playlist","quote":"I recommend the neural network playlist on 3Blue1Brown","text":"If you want an extensive answer, I recommend the neural network playlist on 3Blue1Brown: [VIDEO] For a quick answer, watch this segment: [LINK]"},{"id":16921516,"author":"dahart","month":"2018-04","story":"Microsoft makes AI training courses available to the public","stance":"rec","why":"Quicker, less applied neural network intro","quote":"maybe the 3b1b series on neural networks","text":"The Stanford course is pretty good. [LINK] For something quicker & less applied, maybe the 3b1b series on neural networks: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15625863,"author":"nouveaux","month":"2017-11","story":"What is backpropagation and what is it doing? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"First videos of the 3Blue1Brown neural network series","quote":"here are the first few videos of the series","text":"This was very timely for me and for anyone else learning, here are the first few videos of the series: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] (Original video) [LINK]"},{"id":15506349,"author":"ocharles","month":"2017-10","story":"A Neural Network in 11 Lines of Python (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Terrific series on how neural networks work","quote":"3blue1brown is currently doing a terrific series on how neural networks work","text":"3blue1brown is currently doing a terrific series on how neural networks work, which nicely compliments this blog post. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45892406,"author":"dpoloncsak","month":"2025-11","story":"I hate screenshots of text","stance":"reference","why":"Digit recognition as the Hello World of ML","quote":"ML models to recognize handwriting have existed way before LLMs could call tools","text":"They can , sure...that's really just LLMs though. ML models to recognize handwriting have existed way before LLMs could call tools, though Identifying digits is like the \"Hello World!\" of ML [VIDEO]"},{"id":22284047,"author":"afarrell","month":"2020-02","story":"Dopamine and Temporal Difference Learning","stance":"reference","why":"Intro to neural nets used as background","quote":"","text":"I can partly understand some this based on an EE101-level control theory, a High School level model of how neurons work, and 3blue1brown's intro to neural nets[1]. However, I have a strong personal interest in developing a much deeper understanding of dopamine neurons and their role in Executive Function. Can anyone recommend a good curriculum which can take a random web developer from \"Knows what a myelinated axon, a sigmoid function, and a feedback loop are\" to having a solid enough background to dive into the research on this? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16558705,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2018-03","story":"HALP: High-Accuracy Low-Precision Training","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the sigmoid function isn't really that used anymore","text":"Yes, while I haven't done anything with ML myself, I heard via the 3Blue1Brown video on Deep Learning[0] that the sigmoid function isn't really that used anymore. But I figured allowing that being able to tweak the dynamic range could make it a good fit for the rescaling and recentering approach. You know what, I'll just go ahead and post a link to this article on the Unum google group, perhaps someone there can add some thoughts[1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["IHZwWFHWa-w",4],["Ilg3gGewQ5U",2],["tIeHLnjs5U8",2],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["9i1WlcCudpU",1],["JRuNA2eK7w0",1],["hEUO6pjwFOo",1],["3TYT1QfdfsM",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["WrxZ4AZPdOQ",1],["LUFp6sjKbkE",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["hQVTIJBZook",1],["1-dUkyn_fZA",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["l7rce6IQDWs",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["7J7X7aZvMXQ",1]]},"F_Riqjdh2oM":{"wy":"It is HN's most frequently recommended starting point for quantum computing because it avoids pop-science hand-waving and needs only basic linear algebra.","q":{"x":"This video is the simplest explanation that I have found for Quantum Computing","i":42198969,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42198969,"author":"DarmokJalad1701","month":"2024-11","story":"AlphaQubit: AI to identify errors in Quantum Computers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Simplest explanation of quantum computing without pop-science nonsense.","quote":"This video is the simplest explanation that I have found for Quantum Computing","text":"[VIDEO] This video is the simplest explanation that I have found for Quantum Computing which doesn't do the whole pop-sciency \"is both zero and one at the same time\" nonsense."},{"id":22991013,"author":"mbo","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What scientific phenomenon do you wish someone would explain better?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Slow, math-based explanation that made quantum computing click.","quote":"really hammered it home for me. It goes slowly","text":"The Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists video by Microsoft Research ( [VIDEO] ) really hammered it home for me. It goes slowly and the presenter, in his own words \"shuts up and does the math\"."},{"id":43293467,"author":"thrance","month":"2025-03","story":"Why Quantum Engineering Is Emerging as a Distinct Industrial Sector","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful talk needing only basic linear algebra.","quote":"This talk [1] helped me at the start. The only prerequisite is knowing basic linear algebra.","text":"This talk [1] helped me at the start. The only prerequisite is knowing basic linear algebra. Now, if you're interested in how quantum computers operate, well good luck. That's a whole other beast. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42368468,"author":"potsandpans","month":"2024-12","story":"Willow, Our Quantum Chip","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended starting point for learning quantum computing.","quote":"Start here","text":"Start here: [VIDEO] You don't need to know quantum theory necessarily, but you will need to know some maths. Specifically linear algebra. There are a few youtube courses on linear algebra For a casual set of video: - [LINK] For a more formal approach: - [LINK] And the corresponding open courseware - [LINK] Linear algebra done right comes highly recommended - [LINK]"},{"id":42382304,"author":"rkp8000","month":"2024-12","story":"The Google Willow Thing","stance":"rec","why":"Clear, accessible tutorial on quantum computing fundamentals.","quote":"MSR has a very clear and accessible tutorial on quantum computing for anyone interested in getting up to speed with the fundamentals","text":"MSR has a very clear and accessible tutorial on quantum computing for anyone interested in getting up to speed with the fundamentals: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":38548935,"author":"potsandpans","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: How are quantum computing companies making money?","stance":"rec","why":"Good hands-on intro to programming quantum gates.","quote":"This video is a good hands on intro","text":"> what is programming a quantum computer like? You compose a series of gates that manipulate the state qbits to perform calculation. My understanding is that these gates can be abstracted in a somewhat familiar programming language environment. I've got a very rudimentary understanding, my crude summary would be: it's basically applied linear algebra, leveraging the properties of superposition to perform some types of computations more efficiently. Namely, reducing some search spaces to sqrt n. This video is a good hands on intro: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37579535,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2023-09","story":"Quantum Resistance and the Signal Protocol","stance":"rec","why":"Longer talk aimed at computer scientists beyond the short explanation.","quote":"I gave a longer talk aimed at computer scientists if you're interested beyond this explanation","text":"The shortest possible answer is that qubit states are modeled as two-dimensional vectors on the complex unit sphere. We arbitrarily designate two orthonormal vectors on this sphere as corresponding to classical states 0 and 1. If the qubit vector isn't in the 0 or 1 state, it's in some linear combination of them. This is called superposition. Since most people don't know what linear combination means, superposition is explained as \"sort of both at the same time\". Upon measurement the qubits are collapsed to 0 or 1 with some probability proportional to how close they are to the 0 and 1 states. The precise probabilities are given by something called the Born rule. I gave a longer talk aimed at computer scientists if you're interested beyond this explanation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35490549,"author":"supriyo-biswas","month":"2023-04","story":"Quantum Computing 101","stance":"rec","why":"Among the most accessible yet accurate quantum computing explanations found.","quote":"perhaps the most accessible and yet accurate explanations of quantum computing I have found so far.","text":"The videos by Microsoft Research[1] and PBS[2] are perhaps the most accessible and yet accurate explanations of quantum computing I have found so far. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30559865,"author":"drewrv","month":"2022-03","story":"Basic Quantum Algorithms","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible overview for coders covering entanglement and some Q# code.","quote":"It goes deeper than the pop-sci articles about \"doing all combinations at once\" but remains accessible","text":"For those who are interested, this lecture was sent to me as an overview of quantum computation for people like me: people who know how to code and know some basic linear algebra. It goes deeper than the pop-sci articles about \"doing all combinations at once\" but remains accessible and by the end you understand entanglement and can read some Q# code. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30364259,"author":"quaintquant","month":"2022-02","story":"Hello Many Worlds in Seven Quantum Languages (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent foot-in-the-door intro with a solid foundation and jargon.","quote":"I think this talk is an excellent 'foot in the door' intro.","text":"I think this talk is an excellent 'foot in the door' intro. Should provide a solid foundation, and plenty of insider jargon to follow up on! [VIDEO]"},{"id":27099783,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2021-05","story":"What did Einstein mean by “spooky action at a distance”?","stance":"rec","why":"Video aimed at computer scientists teaching the math of quantum mechanics.","quote":"I made a video aimed at computer scientists which you might find useful","text":"There is absolutely no way to understand quantum mechanics without learning the math. All metaphors are faulty and will lead you to incorrect conclusions. Fortunately the advent of quantum computing has made the math quite easy - it's just linear algebra of the type one would pick up within the first week of an undergraduate course. I made a video aimed at computer scientists which you might find useful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26557270,"author":"DarmokJalad1701","month":"2021-03","story":"Factoring 2048 RSA integers in 177 days with 13436 qubits and a multimode memory","stance":"rec","why":"Explains quantum computers without pop-science hand-waving.","quote":"Here is a long-ish video that explains quantum computers without using pop-science hand-wavy terminology","text":"Here is a long-ish video that explains quantum computers without using pop-science hand-wavy terminology [VIDEO]"},{"id":24997722,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2020-11","story":"CERN Online introductory lectures on quantum computing from 6 November","stance":"rec","why":"Short lecture aimed at software engineers and computer scientists.","quote":"a short 1.5 hour lecture on quantum computing aimed specifically at software engineers & computer scientists, which has proven popular","text":"If anyone is interested I've also created a short 1.5 hour lecture on quantum computing aimed specifically at software engineers & computer scientists, which has proven popular: [VIDEO] I'd love to answer any questions people have. In quantum computation, state behaves like a vector and logic gates behave like matrices that multiply the vector to get a new state value. It doesn't get too much more complicated than that."},{"id":23922353,"author":"martinmartinez","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: Resources to start learning about quantum computing?","stance":"rec","why":"Quantum computing primer listed among beginner resources.","quote":"Quantum Computing Primer (1.5hr)","text":"Learning Quantum Computing based on skill level (math is the biggest friction point, suggested pdf should save you lots of time) (Recc is personal recommendations, Hi-Recc is look at ASAP) # QC Main Ideas - Rotate, Compute, Rotate - Think in Amplitude Interference # Beginner: -(Hi-Recc) Quantum Computing Primer (1.5hr) : [VIDEO] -(Hi-Recc) Math Primer for Quantum Computing (easiest intro/primer I found on the topic; Highly Recommend ) : [LINK] -- understand Bra Ket notation [<Bra|Ket>] (Ket as Column vector, Bra (Row vector) as Complex Conjugate of Ket (denoted as dagger) ) -- understand Kronecker product ( for multi-qubit systems) - Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists book - [LINK] - Quantum Math Primer (Faculty of Khan) (found a bit hard the first time around, pretty dense) : [LINK] # Intermediate -(Recc) Ryan O'Donnell CMU course [is the best if you want to really understand the capabilities of quantum computing, get practice with math, intuition] (algos connection to Fourier, Quantum Complexity Theory, Math best practices, learning multi-quibit systems) -- Quantum Computation and Information at CMU : [LINK] -- Lecture Notes (use as reference in case video is not clear, or camera shot lags/changes) [LINK] - Mermin's Textbook [LINK] - Nielsen & Chuang's Textbook [LINK] -- Nielsen's Lectures [LINK] # Advanced -(Recc) Scott Aaronson Graduate Course [LINK] -(Recc) Scott Aaronson Papers (really interesting) [LINK] - Complexity Zoo - List of Algorithms [LINK] -(Recc) Machi"},{"id":22991715,"author":"whytai","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What scientific phenomenon do you wish someone would explain better?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture on the basics of how a quantum computer works.","quote":"Here is a video of a researcher at Microsoft Quantum lecturing on this","text":"Here is a video of a researcher at Microsoft Quantum lecturing on this: [VIDEO] \"Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists.\". However, even with understanding how a Quantum Computer works at its most basic level I still have difficulty understanding the more useful Quantum Algorithms: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":23033284,"author":"stewdio","month":"2020-04","story":"Show HN: Quantum JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"One of two videos that helped explain how to compute with a quantum computer.","quote":"These two videos in particular helped me get started","text":"I’ve begun to put together useful resources for learning more here: [LINK] I’d like to really flesh out that page; I’ve now come across so many folks that are amazing at explaining this stuff... It’s just a time crunch to juggle all this as you might imagine ;) These two videos in particular helped me get started: 1. What is a physical quantum computer? [LINK] 2. How might one compute using it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":38255226,"author":"westurner","month":"2023-11","story":"Future is quantum: universities look to train engineers for an emerging industry","stance":"reference","why":"Explains quantum operators as matrices.","quote":"explains classical and quantum operators as just matrices.","text":"> Instead [of the Bohr model], Morello uses a real-world example in his teaching — a material called a quantum dot, which is used in some LEDs and in some television screens. “I can now teach quantum mechanics in a way that is far more engaging than the way I was taught quantum mechanics when I was an undergrad in the 1990s,” he says. > Morello also teaches the mathematics behind quantum mechanics in a more computer-friendly way. His students learn to solve problems using matrices that they can represent using code written for the Python programming language, rather than conventional differential equations on paper. From [LINK] : >> This \"Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists\" video [VIDEO] explains classical and quantum operators as just matrices. What are other good references? Unfortunately the QuantumQ game doesn't yet have the matrix forms of the quantum logical operators in the (open source) game docs. Would be a helpful resource, in addition to the Quantum logic wikipedia page and numpy and/or SymPy without cirq: A Manim presentation demonstrating that quantum logical operator matrices are Bloch sphere rotations, are reversible, and why we restrict operators to the category of unitary transformations > His colleagues at the UNSW are also developing laboratory courses to give students hands-on experience with the hardware in quantum technologies. For example, they designed a teaching lab to convey the fundamental concept of quantum spin, a property of electrons and "},{"id":30782678,"author":"westurner","month":"2022-03","story":"The quantum technology ecosystem explained","stance":"reference","why":"Explains classical and quantum operators as matrices.","quote":"explains classical and quantum operators as just matrices.","text":"> one can literally write down a matrix of numbers in a program to define a new mathematical function, and later use that function to manipulate the contents of the computer's RAM[1]. This \"Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists\" video [VIDEO] explains classical and quantum operators as just matrices. What are other good references? Quantum state: [LINK] Quantum logic; quantum logical operators: [LINK] > All this business about energy levels, evolution, etc. are distractions, just as the electrodynamics of a transistor are distractions from what it means to program a computer. But a classical simulator - like e.g. qiskit - for a quantum circuit/experiment/function must run the experiment very^very^very many times to even probabilistically approximate a sufficient quantum system; because of the combinatorial probabilistic explosion that results from adding just one more basis state. What are the fundamental limitations of quantum simulators? Maybe it's possible. Quantum simulator: [LINK] - [ ] Maybe Twistor theory has insight into a classical geometrical formulation that could be run on a non-QC? Amplituhedron: [LINK] [Photon] wave-particle constructive superpositions approximate which operators, which may form a neat topology like this: - [ ] > A research question for a new school year: > The classical logical operators form a neat topology. Should we expect there to be such symmetry and structure amongst the quantum operators as well? [LINK]"},{"id":23920340,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2020-07","story":"Ask HN: Resources to start learning about quantum computing?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I will answer this question in two ways: one, I will tell you how I learned, and two I will tell you how I would have liked to have learned with the benefit of hindsight. Different people will value one more than the other, but both are more valuable than a giant list of resources with zero guidance where to start. How I learned: I started out like you, in possession of an undergraduate education in computer science. I began reading Quantum Computer Science: An Introduction [0] by N. David Mermin. This is a very good textbook, but I absolutely could not skim it. I had to ensure I understood every single line before moving onto the next. I had the impression I wasn't learning very quickly, when in fact (due to the textbook's density) I was taking in a huge amount of information. After a few weeks with the Mermin textbook, I bought Quantum Computing for Computer Scientists [1] by Yanofsky & Mannucci. This is a much softer introduction than Mermin, almost too soft: I skipped the first few chapters on linear algebra and complex numbers. However, in combination with the Mermin textbook, I acquired a good understanding of quantum computing basics. It was at this point I reached my own personal threshold for feeling I \"understood\" quantum computing. People often recommend Quantum Computation and Quantum Information by Nielsen & Chuang (also called \"Mike & Ike\") for beginners. I believe this is not good advice. Had I tried to learn from that textbook, I would have failed. However, it"},{"id":17823369,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2018-08","story":"First Large-Scale Quantum Simulation of Topological State of Matter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'll try to give here a crash course on how simulating quantum systems on a quantum computer works - not for the actual computer type in the article (D-Wave's quantum annealer) but on a traditional gate-based quantum computer. So, we have this thing called the Schrodinger equation. For our purposes, all you need to know is that it's an equation which can perfectly describe the behavior of a quantum system as it evolves in time. Examples of quantum systems include a single electron sitting in a magnetic field, photons going through closely-placed slits, or entire biomolecules doing biomolecule things. Simulating things on computers is great because of how darn easy & cheap it is compared to running the same scenario in the real world. We want to simulate quantum systems on computers. We simulate quantum systems by trying to mimic the action of the Schrodinger equation on the quantum system. However, (as Feynman famously pointed out in Simulating Physics with Computers [0]), classical computers are very bad at this. They take exponential resources to simulate a quantum system. Now, if you have a computer that obeys the same physical laws as the quantum system it's simulating - a quantum computer - we might be able to simulate the system with only linear resources. Let's take a very simple quantum system. Consider a single electron, sitting in a magnetic field. Electrons have a property called spin which is affected by magnetic fields. Electrons can have their spin be up, down, "}],"r":[["OWJCfOvochA",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["TVwKjGbz60k",1],["IrbJYsep45E",1],["Z8ZhQPaw4rE",1],["vNaEBbFbvcY",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1]]},"cZYNADOHhVY":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it in longevity and aging threads as a concise, fun case for fighting aging.","q":{"x":"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject.","i":22002471,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":37288104,"author":"tjlingham","month":"2023-08","story":"Scientists find way to wipe a cell's memory to reprogram it as a stem cell","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent exploration challenging the belief that natural death is acceptable","quote":"I'd suggest the following excellent exploration of the idea of extinguishing natural death by CGP Grey.","text":"That's very easy to say until it's your partner, mother/father, or child on the other side of that assertion. If you're curious and have time, I'd suggest the following excellent exploration of the idea of extinguishing natural death by CGP Grey. He uses an interesting analogy to help challenge the ingrained belief that natural death is just [VIDEO]"},{"id":22002471,"author":"seer","month":"2020-01","story":"Scientists identify pathways that extend nematode worm lifespan fivefold","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Concise, fun explanation of the case for ending aging","quote":"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject.","text":"CGP gray's videos on YouTube are maybe the most concise and fun to watch materials I've seen on the subject. [LINK] [VIDEO] And you'll be surprised how many people _are_ indeed thinking about all of the outcomes. Have in mind though that as with any technology it is not good or bad, and if it is possible someone will do it sooner or later. It makes no sense to not do it as your adversaries will, and then they'll dictate how it is used, probably not to your liking. If you develop it first you can talk and deliberate at your leisure and find solutions that look much better to you and yours."},{"id":20854536,"author":"mLuby","month":"2019-09","story":"Alan Turing to be the face of new £50 note","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fable arguing for accelerating anti-aging research","quote":"Make \"The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant\" required reading/watching for all schoolchildren","text":"All attempts to cure aging have been magic/religion-based until very recently. Also it's not like we \"learned to fly\" as if cavemen could have done it had they only known the right wing shape and arm technique. It was only possible because of an accumulation of industrial and scientific knowledge. Significant life extension will likely be built off the same accumulation of knowledge, not some rainforest frog extract the ancients could have refined. The only way I can imagine we don't have this in 1000 years is if someone's nuke finger gets twitchy. Want to accelerate research? Make \"The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant\" required reading/watching for all schoolchildren: [VIDEO] (That this might be controversial is itself interesting.)"},{"id":17381766,"author":"snvzz","month":"2018-06","story":"Around the World, People Have Surprisingly Modest Notions of the ‘Ideal’ Life","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated fable arguing aging should be fought","quote":"Everybody should watch The Fable of The Dragon Tyrant","text":"Most people confuse age with aging, because aging is still unavoidable, and as it's always been the case, it looks like it will remain so forever. But that's wrong. The outlook is different now. Yet, unfortunately, most still see it as unavoidable. Everybody should watch The Fable of The Dragon Tyrant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45765681,"author":"willis936","month":"2025-10","story":"How ancient people saw themselves","stance":"rec","why":"CGP Grey's thoughts on fighting aging","quote":"CGPGrey's thoughts on this mirror a lot of my own.","text":"I don't think that death is a certainty. It is today, but we are working on it. We are not supernatural. CGPGrey's thoughts on this mirror a lot of my own. I grant that not all care given to aged is a kindness, but not fighting aging is not virtuous. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42314014,"author":"nayuki","month":"2024-12","story":"‘With brain preservation, nobody has to die’","stance":"rec","why":"Animated essay on societal acceptance of death","quote":"CGP Grey captures this sentiment nicely in this animated essay","text":"> our societal acceptance of death comes from an inability to imagine anything otherwise CGP Grey captures this sentiment nicely in this animated essay: [VIDEO] \"Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant\" [2018-04-24]"},{"id":31121949,"author":"montenegrohugo","month":"2022-04","story":"Your real biological clock is you’re going to die (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Related argument for fighting death","quote":"Related","text":"I have to say I like this somewhat poetic way of writing, especially the sentences with which the author ends paragraphs. Very rich. On a different note, no. Let's fight death. Fuck aging. We can fight it. We don't have to suffer like cattle under it. We can beat it. There are plenty of longevity focused startups and academic research groups, and yet we're not spending nearly enough effort and money on this problem. It affects every human on earth, and the immense benefits of adding even one year of healthy lifespan to 7 billion people make it a moral imperative to act upon. Fighting death should be a multi-national effort that we focus on with all our might, _right now_. You shouldn't have to die. Related: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29535134,"author":"Tmpod","month":"2021-12","story":"Japanese scientists develop vaccine to eliminate cells behind aging","stance":"rec","why":"Narrated version of the short story over illustrations","quote":"CGP Grey has a video with him reading the short over some neat drawings, which is more enjoyable I suppose","text":"CGP Grey has a video with him reading the short over some neat drawings, which is more enjoyable I suppose: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27064166,"author":"chr1","month":"2021-05","story":"LiveLeak shuts down after 15 years online","stance":"rec","why":"Argues death is close to being solved","quote":"because we are very close to solving the problem of death for good","text":"Try watching videos of old people with Alzheimer's, and try imagining what people will be doing when you are dead to gauge how much your life is worth, and if small risk of dying in a car accident not caused by yourself is acceptable. For me in similar situation answers were not important and acceptable. But if you find that your life is precious to you it still would be better to channel the natural fear of death to more productive venues like [LINK] or much more importantly to donations to [LINK] because we are very close to solving the problem of death for good [VIDEO]"},{"id":24643968,"author":"cosmojg","month":"2020-09","story":"On Old Age","stance":"rec","why":"Fable urging not accepting old age without a fight","quote":"See also: The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant by CGP Grey","text":"\"Those who live in the desert learn to worship thirst.\" — vharuck There's no reason we should accept the consequences of old age without a fight. Donate to SENS, or even better, dedicate your own work to extending human healthspan. See also: The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant by CGP Grey — [VIDEO]"},{"id":19429355,"author":"dtujmer","month":"2019-03","story":"Longevity and anti-aging research: ‘Prime time for an impact on the globe’","stance":"rec","why":"Video form of Bostrom's story on the fight against aging","quote":"And here it is in video form as well","text":"\"The last enemy that shall be destroyed is death.\" Well, maybe not the last one, but still an important one. Nick Bostrom has a great story describing the underlying philosophy in the fight against aging: [LINK] And here it is in video form as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17421953,"author":"jeffreylees","month":"2018-06","story":"Matter: How Long Can We Live? The Limit Hasn’t Been Reached, Study Finds","stance":"rec","why":"Argues aging is a condition that could be solved","quote":"I've always found this to be an interesting topic. See here for more","text":"I've always found this to be an interesting topic. See here for more: [VIDEO] . The argument made there is basically that death (human aging, at any rate) is a sort of condition, or disease, if you will, like any other, that could be solved if we plied it with enough resources. Maybe the real question is where on the scale the extension to life happens? If we keep reaching adulthood, and then elderly, more inhibited lifestyles at the same ages, just prolonging the elderly state via good medical practices, that's one thing. But if we could slow aging in general to extend younger and middle-aged sections of life, that'd be another thing altogether."},{"id":44856529,"author":"mjevans","month":"2025-08","story":"Long-term exposure to outdoor air pollution linked to increased risk of dementia","stance":"reference","why":"Dragon metaphor for failing to defeat death","quote":"Time ran out and we did not slay the dragon.","text":"I don't think I'd be happy in that context. Medical technology failed 'us' and at that point the body has failed too. Time ran out and we did not slay the dragon. [VIDEO] CGP Grey : Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant"},{"id":43021828,"author":"dsign","month":"2025-02","story":"The year I didn't survive","stance":"reference","why":"Fable linked in context of grief and fighting death","quote":"","text":"That terrible brutal grief is what death leaves us every time. I remember Jake's posts. He fought, and he all of us to fight. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36192562,"author":"mjevans","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Dragon metaphor for defeating death","quote":"I hope technology can save me, save us all, before the end comes","text":"I hope technology can save me, save us all, before the end comes... but until we defeat that 'dragon'. ( [VIDEO] CGP Gray - Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant ) There's a point where if there is any of me left, which with dementia and other brain destroying disease / injury might not even be anything, it's just prolonging the suffering. Current medical forms don't really give a clear indication about when the infection point on that curve is reached; they assume all in or all out, at least from what I saw when it last came up among my parent's experiences. I know I wouldn't want to be kept around as effectively a vegetable, surviving on a 'port' forcing fluids into my GI tract, barely remembering anything about life and being strung out on many years of suffering. That's the exploitative part you mention. The lack of dignity you mention. The turning a patient into a profit center rather than looking out for their best interests. Maybe someday that won't be the lack of real choice we all currently face. Until then, and maybe even after, give people the freedom to choose. Also give them real choices, like counseling and assistance fitting into society as a productive member who's valued."},{"id":30027721,"author":"godelski","month":"2022-01","story":"A $3B bet on finding the fountain of youth","stance":"reference","why":"Dragon Tyrant as metaphor for accepting death","quote":"Also I think part of the problem is we've all gotten used to the Dragon Tyrant[1] that we accept our fate.","text":"I think it's because people think they stop growing. I was listening to Levitt's People I (Mostly) Admire and on a recent show he was talking about progress. How everyone will say in the last 10 years they've changed but if you ask them what they'll be in the next 10 years they think they'll be the same. But there's a lot of insight we often ignore. I think similarly people ignore political and cultural progress. It's easy to blame the lack of progress on the old guard. But if we look at history we go much faster than that. One great example of this is the legalization of gay marriage. Pee [0] tracks support of it and it's clear that attitude is changing faster than the old people dying. Also I think part of the problem is we've all gotten used to the Dragon Tyrant[1] that we accept our fate. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24902090,"author":"godelski","month":"2020-10","story":"No Implants Needed for Precise Control Deep into the Brain","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"- Several species have longer lifetimes than us. Doesn't seem to be a large disadvantage. - We've proven that we change faster culturally than biologically. If this weren't true most Americans still wouldn't be okay with gay people, black people, or pot. Beliefs seem more related to local tribalism than to age. I've seen plenty of older and younger people radically shift (for good or bad) their positions based on who they consider arbiters of truth. - We've heard this finite resource in the past. It has been solved many times. Fertilizer was the most recent one if you want to go back to 19th century history. We have hydroponics, aeroponics, etc on the way. We can also go into space. - Every first world country has a population growth below 1 (and are decreasing). The average EU is 0.15% and the US is at 0.5%[0]. There's no sign of it turning around and I imagine immortality would just accelerate this. (People will still kill themselves btw. Accidents will still happen. Bad health. Etc) - IDK about you, but I'm fine waiting till I'm 100 to have kids if I can live forever. Maybe even 200. I'm unsure, all I know is that if we make such a change that we'll completely reconsider things like adulthood and what we do with our life (how long does it take to live off of passive income?). It will be a radical change no matter what, but also one that seems inevitable. If your argument is that it is going to be bumpy, well if history has taught us anything it is that we don't confront pr"},{"id":21183057,"author":"mLuby","month":"2019-10","story":"Ageing in human cells successfully reversed in the lab (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Related fable about conquering death","quote":"Related: Fable of the Dragon Tyrant","text":"Related: Fable of the Dragon Tyrant [VIDEO] Ways to improve life expectancy: A. keep children alive till adulthood (mostly done) B. keep adults disease-free/healthy (somewhat done) C. keep old people alive till natural death (little done) D. stop natural death (possible?) E. reverse aging (possible?) If natural death is just rolling constitution checks long enough to get a critical miss, then perhaps it's enough to complete C. If natural death is some sort of cellular countdown, it may suffice to stop the time, as in D. I think for most people, what they really want is not eternal life in a 90+ year-old body, but to be able to live in a young body, which necessitates E. It's interesting to consider scenarios where we accomplish some but not all of these, especially where C and/or D are possible but E is not."},{"id":17840179,"author":"shawn","month":"2018-08","story":"Ethical OS and Silicon Valley’s Guilty Conscience","stance":"reference","why":"CGP Grey's stance that immortality should be invented","quote":"","text":"This is an excellent time to ask for a counterargument to CGP Grey's stance that immortality should be invented: [VIDEO] Suppose there were a technology X which was going to be invented eventually. Suppose also that it's a highly unethical technology, for some definition of unethical. Is it therefore unethical to create X? Note: The constraint is that X is inevitable . The only question is who creates it first. And in that context, isn't it at least possible to argue from multiple axes that you should help to create it? The limit case of this argument would be \"It's your duty to the society you live in to ensure it has the competitive advantage, not some other society.\" A less-hostile way to phrase that would be \"The first company to invent a technology can then try to enforce ethics onto that technology.\" That is, if you invent something, it's easier to dictate how it's used than if you didn't. Hence, paradoxically as it may seem, the logical conclusion would seem to be that you should work as hard as you can to invent whatever unethical technology you're worried about -- in the hopes that you can minimize the damage later. If it seems like a technology can't really be controlled (e.g. nuclear weapons), I counter with this: Bitcoin was the implementation of a set of ideas. The exact implementation could have been very different. It could have been inflationary rather than deflationary, for example. The precise choices were very important, because Bitcoin has huge first-mover"},{"id":16946189,"author":"kbenson","month":"2018-04","story":"The Fable of the Dragon-Tyrant (2005)","stance":"reference","why":"Video adaptation of Bostrom's fable narrated by CGP Grey","quote":"CGP Grey, the narrator of the video, is a fairly famous youtube personality","text":"To the dead comment, since it was killed right before I could finish and I can't reply there anymore (since I think a question deserves an answer): I suspect the video you say was this one.[1] If so, you might have noticed in the description Nick Bostrom is the original author, or might have noticed the credits at the end. CGP Grey, the narrator of the video, is a fairly famous youtube personality, and have been on a sort of crusade against death and ageing lately with a few videos. I think it's more likely that it was posted here because of CGP Grey's video bring some new (or more recent) popularity to it. 1: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["C25qzDhGLx8",3],["1Jwo5qc78QU",1],["dnHdqPBrtH8",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["P7GKK3liv8M",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["43XKfuptnik",1],["9W74aeuqsiU",1],["kdz6FeQLuHQ",1],["d4EgbgTm0Bg",1],["dqQw05Mr63E",1],["Y99Wj-NStok",1],["-opBifFfsMY",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["f7MzFfuNOtY",1],["ObhK8lUfIlc",1],["Kas0tIxDvrg",1]]},"ZSRHeXYDLko":{"wy":"It is the standard link in HN threads about software decay, lost knowledge and fragile technological civilization.","q":{"x":"Anyone who finds this concept interesting to think about and hasn't seen this video may enjoy it","i":29568085,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43054578,"author":"cathalc","month":"2025-02","story":"We were wrong about GPUs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fantastic talk on fragility of technological civilization.","quote":"A fantastic talk.","text":"\"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\" by Jonathan Blow comes to mind - [VIDEO] A fantastic talk."},{"id":45529514,"author":"iamwil","month":"2025-10","story":"The great software quality collapse or, how we normalized catastrophe","stance":"rec","why":"Early warning about declining software quality and competence.","quote":"Jonathan Blow already warned us six years ago.","text":"Jonathan Blow already warned us six years ago. \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\" [VIDEO] There is the Handmade and Better Software contingent that is pushing against this trend. I don't know if they'll succeed, but they at least care about quality in a way that a lot of software engineers aren't incentivized or don't know how to do anymore. [LINK]"},{"id":44643819,"author":"dzonga","month":"2025-07","story":"If writing is thinking then what happens if AI is doing the writing and reading?","stance":"rec","why":"Warns about society losing the ability to think and create.","quote":"every once in a while everyone should watch Jonathan's Blow: Collapse of Civilization [0]","text":"every once in a while everyone should watch Jonathan's Blow: Collapse of Civilization [0] by not reading and going through docs - we cut out our critique ability as we just ingest fully what A.I gives us. by not writing we don't exercise thinking - question our assumptions etc. since what AI writes will mostly be positive not negative. [0]: [VIDEO] and just like that society stops thinking. stops creating. then civilization collapses."},{"id":43941953,"author":"bsrkf","month":"2025-05","story":"Rust’s dependencies are starting to worry me","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on software complexity and quality as a whole.","quote":"is worth watching in my opinion","text":"Excuse me for not having much to add to the discussion but two interesting references for people to check out, if so inclined of course: a) Ginger Bill (the Odin language creator, no affiliation) stated on a podcast that Odin will never have an official pkg manager, since what they're, in his opinion, mainly automating is dependency hell, and this being one of the main reasons for rising software complexity and lower software quality; see [LINK] (timestamped to the correct position) (they mention Rust explicitly as an example) b) another programmer rather seriously worried about software quality/complexity is Jonathan Blow, who's talk \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\" is worth watching in my opinion: [VIDEO] (it's not talking about package managers specifically, but is on topic regarding software complexity/quality as a whole) Addendum: And sorry, I feel like almost everyone knows this xkcd by now, but since no one so far seems to have posted it; \"obligatory xkcd reference\": [LINK]"},{"id":33322973,"author":"mrw","month":"2022-10","story":"Forgetting the Asbestos – how we lose knowledge and technologies","stance":"rec","why":"Same topic of losing knowledge, applied to software engineering.","quote":"talk on the same topic from 2019.","text":"I liked Jonathan Blow’s ‘Preventing The Collapse of Civilisation’ talk on the same topic from 2019. [1] Although, it’s more related to software engineering. Previously discussed on a couple occasions. [2][3] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":32703001,"author":"asah","month":"2022-09","story":"The optimal amount of fraud is non-zero","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about how technology and craft knowledge gets lost.","quote":"See Jonathan Blow's excellent talk about lost technology","text":"\"optimal amount of fraud in society is 0\" - are you sure? why? Bad Things(tm) are useful for testing and improving safety/security, and when I see people/institutions with no experience reacting to Bad Things(tm), I know they're in for a world of hurt when it does happen. Perhaps you mean, the optimal amount of fraud that isn't prosecuted... or not detected... ? Even then, I'd argue that there's a tiny percentage that's useful for keeping the safety/security industry on its toes and at the ready. As a proof point, if you believe that war (world peace) is not a solved problem, then it's only a matter of time before your city/region/civilization/race faces an existential threat, for which the only true preparation is to be ready to innovate and mobilize. Sorry if this comes across as dark. I mean it in the same vein as having a small percentage of farmers is desirable. By contrast, I visited a traditional silk factory in Stockholm (amazing btw) and the craft has been lost to the point where they're struggling to find craftspeople able to work their looms and other old equipment. See Jonathan Blow's excellent talk about lost technology: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32916596,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2022-09","story":"In 2019 RX470 were $70, RX580 $100","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the software industry decomposing over time.","quote":"I really like this talk by [the possibly notorious] Jonathan Blow on the software industry","text":"Software has definitely gotten worse, and tbh it will only keep getting worse. I really like this talk by [the possibly notorious] Jonathan Blow on the software industry and software in general decomposing [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30953456,"author":"sjaak","month":"2022-04","story":"1.5-Inch Hand-Carved Agate from 1500 B.C. Shows Sub-Millimeter Details (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Argues progress is not always increasing; technology can be lost.","quote":"Super interesting if you have the time for it","text":"After reading this I can't help but think of a talk \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\" given by Jonathan Blow (the game dev) about progress and how it's not always increasing like we tend to think. Super interesting if you have the time for it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31005189,"author":"theonemind","month":"2022-04","story":"Ask HN: When did tech stop being cool?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues software is in a decadent age losing fundamentals.","quote":"I think Jonathan Blow made a decent argument that we're in a decadent age of software.","text":"[VIDEO] I think Jonathan Blow made a decent argument that we're in a decadent age of software. In a decadent age, the civilization still does and can do the thing, but has started to lose a grip on the fundamentals, so it's on the way out. A decadent age is a kind of end, doesn't necessarily feel like the end, it kind of feels like a stagnant bumbling. How many programmers do you know that can write assembly code? How many layers of abstraction between a web app and the silicon? Processor, OS, probably a byte-code VM, probably a framework... leaking a little bit all of the way. He makes a good point--where's the evidence that we can write good software? It seems possible , but it doesn't appear, so really, the feeling that maybe we could is just a feeling, not evidence."},{"id":30574086,"author":"ilrwbwrkhv","month":"2022-03","story":"What's the problem with old but excellent Mac apps?","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent talk on losing capability and software decay.","quote":"I am reminded of the excellent","text":"I am reminded of the excellent \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\" talk by Jonathan Blow: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29883617,"author":"elcapitan","month":"2022-01","story":"Disillusioned by Tech in 2022","stance":"rec","why":"Blow on declining software quality and technological regression.","quote":"Beautiful talk on that topic by Jonathan Blow, called","text":"I'm not even expecting technology to get better anymore, I just wish the quality of software wasn't constantly decreasing. I've turned off automatic updates for most of them, e.g. MacOS, Android Apps, Firefox. Just can't stand the constant messing up of UIs that may have worked or were at least not as bad as the next version with more bloated features. Beautiful talk on that topic by Jonathan Blow, called \"Preventing the collapse of civilization\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":29568085,"author":"anderspitman","month":"2021-12","story":"Tell HN: AWS appears to be down again","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on how civilizations can lose capabilities and collapse.","quote":"Anyone who finds this concept interesting to think about and hasn't seen this video may enjoy it","text":"Anyone who finds this concept interesting to think about and hasn't seen this video may enjoy it: [VIDEO] See also Foundation by Asimov."},{"id":26340031,"author":"isolli","month":"2021-03","story":"The Air Force is having to reverse engineer parts of its own stealth bomber","stance":"rec","why":"Shows importance of inter-generational knowledge transfer in sustaining technology","quote":"The eye-opening moment, for me, was the importance (and neglect) of the inter-generational transfer of knowledge in maintaining technological complexity.","text":"Tangentially reminds me of this video by Jonathan Blow [0], \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\". The eye-opening moment, for me, was the importance (and neglect) of the inter-generational transfer of knowledge in maintaining technological complexity. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25135368,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-11","story":"Let's Make the Web Weird Again [audio]","stance":"rec","why":"Argues software quality has significantly deteriorated","quote":"makes a good argument that software quality has already suffered significant deterioration","text":"> quality will deteriorate over time Jonathan Blow's talk \"Preventing the Collapse of Civilization\"[1] makes a good argument that software quality has already suffered significant deterioration. We've just gotten used to software not working. Reboot the computer; refresh the webpage; spend the morning trying to work around the \"improvements\", regressions, and removed features in the software that forced an update. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34293733,"author":"015a","month":"2023-01","story":"There Is No Software Maintenance","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I have had a really frustrating relationship with software maintenance in all of the companies I've worked for. I've always been a strong, strong believer in an analogy like: How much time would you estimate that nuclear engineers spend operating and maintaining existing reactors, versus building new ones? How about public highway departments and building new roads, versus fixing or improving existing ones? No analogy is ever perfect, but the only logical conclusion I can reach is that software engineering is a really weird discipline relative to practically all of its \"we build things that last years\" peers. Its weird because, well, our organizations in my experience chronically under-invest in maintenance then act flustered when new developments don't meet deadlines or take forever; but its also weird because all of my experience screams at me \"I'm not even sure if we know how to maintain systems well\". I've seen this in one org (a public company you'd recognize); where years ago we had and met lofty goals like \"99.999% availability\"; today its a good month if we hit three-nines, API wide. In team meetings with really senior, smart engineers when we ask How Can We Improve This, you start observing this learned helplessness where they suggest small changes like, you know, adding a cache for some external API, or replacing the built-in HTTP client with some other one that has a more comprehensive knob for timeouts, or whatever. I talk with them privately and say: Is this real"},{"id":29255053,"author":"rglover","month":"2021-11","story":"Netlify raises $105M and acquires OneGraph","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"They should/can, but not at the expense of understanding how the underlying system works. Right now, the major players significantly abstract away from \"how the web works\" which means newer developers don't understand and are being led into a trap. Cute marketing terms like \"serverless\" confuse developers into thinking there is no server (or even worse, that there's such a thing as a \"serverless developer\"). I'm working on this problem right now: [LINK] . I know I sound like a curmudgeon when I say this stuff but it's a serious problem that will have dire consequences later (see: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":27192565,"author":"ajdegol","month":"2021-05","story":"Nearly 200K Medical Records of US Military Veterans Leaked","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It might be referenced in Jon Blow's talk","text":"> like Plan-crashes technically it's always human error That's a $1M fine for misspelling \"Plane\". ;) Also, it's definitely the software. There's a wonderful talk by Alan Kay regarding this, but I cannot find it. It might be referenced in Jon Blow's talk: - [VIDEO]"},{"id":26647247,"author":"cxr","month":"2021-03","story":"Turing Award goes to Aho and Ullman","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It was just a few days ago when I mentioned how often I see the dragon book held up as a punching bag. It seems that at some point the book, along with K&R, have become the target for unsubstantiated criticism. I've pressed for details, and I've seen others do the same, but it never ends in anything concrete—just an implicit appeal to the new conventional wisdom that they do more harm than good. Reminds me of how \"truth\" gets synthesized on Reddit. Here's a counterexample: Russ Cox has a series on regular expressions, beginning with Regular Expression Matching Can Be Simple And Fast < [LINK] >. In the last several years, it has become very well-received. In it, he lays out the case for Thompson NFAs, and also laments in some places about how the approach is not more widely known, having become a sort of lost knowledge. (See also Jonathan Blow's < [VIDEO] >.) For all the criticism of the dragon book, though, you'd think that would mean enough people have read it that someone would have pointed out that Thompson is cited in Chapter 3, where his approach is described in some detail. I think that what's actually going on is that, like The Art Of Computer Programming, the dragon book is widely referenced and rarely read. Now, enter the 21st century meme factory. Having said that, the most recent comment I saw that was being casually dismissive of the dragon book—ironically for its \"second-rate methods\" that have \"held back\" compiler development—did motivate me into digging the boo"},{"id":26283903,"author":"diimdeep","month":"2021-02","story":"Ytmdl-web – download music from YouTube with metadata","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This. So much code so many frameworks so much wow, to do so little. And we a little closer to collapse of civilization. [0] There is not enough Jonathan Blow's for all JavaScript bloatware unmaintained unusable in a few years tools. [1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] `I think we're now in a situation where everybody in the world is flooded by low-quality software, and everybody wishes that they had higher quality software, at least people who make software I think. We're making a game, games have a lot of things they want to do, a lot of those things are potentially stuff that a library could do of some kind, and so, let's go out, whether it's maybe to do some constructive solid geometry or text layout or something. There's a lot of different little sub-jobs. If I go on the internet, what is the state of the art of what people out there are doing for this task? Very often I will find a number of things that claim to do the job. Some of them actually do it to an okay level and some don't, and a lot of them don't really work for high-stress situations, and a lot of them don't even like frickin compile anywhere. Just the amount of swimming through a sewer that I have to do to even figure out, \"What is the thing that I can seriously consider to maybe do this job versus writing it myself?\" That is a huge investment, time and energy investment`"},{"id":25185397,"author":"wolfgke","month":"2020-11","story":"The Birth of Unix with Brian Kernighan","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There is a talk by Jonathan Blow about this topic","text":"There is a talk by Jonathan Blow about this topic: Preventing the Collapse of Civilization / Jonathan Blow (Thekla, Inc) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["spUNpyF58BY",2],["Td_PGkfIdIQ",1],["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1]]},"T647CGsuOVU":{"wy":"HN commenters rate the series as some of the best visual teaching on complex numbers and recommend it for building intuition.","q":{"x":"The greatest audiovisual content on the topic I have ever seen","i":14942934,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":33403096,"author":"tails4e","month":"2022-10","story":"The paper that keeps showing up","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Easily digestible videos on imaginary numbers plus a history lesson.","quote":"I really like this series on it by Welch Labs - loads of easily digestible videos, and a history lesson to boot","text":"Yes, the name imaginary does it no favours. I was confused for so long about i, as it was never explained on classes, and it wasn't until 3blue1brown and other math youtubers showed me the light. I really like this series on it by Welch Labs - loads of easily digestible videos, and a history lesson to boot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28378375,"author":"wodenokoto","month":"2021-09","story":"A layman’s guide to recreational mathematics videos","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great explanations and high production value on complex numbers.","quote":"Great explanations, engaging content and high production value.","text":"Welch labs has an amazing series on complex numbers called “imaginary numbers are real”. Great explanations, engaging content and high production value. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15961779,"author":"rrauenza","month":"2017-12","story":"What are the complex numbers, really?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Welch Labs' wonderful series on imaginary numbers.","quote":"Welch Labs has a wonderful series on youtube on imaginary numbers","text":"Welch Labs has a wonderful series on youtube on imaginary numbers, \"Imaginary Numbers Are Real\": [VIDEO] I like to tell my kids that imaginary numbers aren't really imaginary .. that in many ways they're more real than the Reals."},{"id":14942934,"author":"pedrodelfino","month":"2017-08","story":"Imaginary Numbers Are Real – YouTube Series","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A multi-part audiovisual series on imaginary numbers.","quote":"The greatest audiovisual content on the topic I have ever seen","text":"The greatest audiovisual content on the topic I have ever seen: Part 1 - [VIDEO] Part 2 - [LINK] Part 3 - [LINK] Part 4 - [LINK] Part 5 - [LINK] Part 6 - [LINK] Part 7 - [LINK] Part 8 - [LINK] Part 9 - [LINK] Part 10 - [LINK] Part 11 - [LINK] Part 12 - [LINK] Part 13 - [LINK]"},{"id":42882219,"author":"wanderingmoose","month":"2025-01","story":"Quaternions and spherical trigonometry","stance":"rec","why":"Explains solving equations with no real roots; a precursor to quaternions.","quote":"It is a great precursor to then thinking about quaternions","text":"I really like the welch labs series on imaginary numbers which covers the first part of what you talk about -- leveling up the notion of what a complex number is. Though his focus was more on solving simple equations with no real roots, but really detailing how/what is really going on. It is a great precursor to then thinking about quaternions [VIDEO]"},{"id":34606859,"author":"magnetomax","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful series for understanding basics of imaginary numbers.","quote":"I found this YouTube series really helpful to understand basics of imaginary numbers.","text":"Regarding imaginary numbers, I found this YouTube series really helpful to understand basics of imaginary numbers. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17733099,"author":"jatsign","month":"2018-08","story":"A Visual, Intuitive Guide to Imaginary Numbers (2007)","stance":"rec","why":"Video series covering both the history and the math of imaginary numbers.","quote":"I really enjoyed this video series on imaginary numbers. Went through the history as well as the math","text":"I really enjoyed this video series on imaginary numbers. Went through the history as well as the math: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20805344,"author":"vector_spaces","month":"2019-08","story":"Ask HN: Best resources to gain math intuition?","stance":"reference","why":"Example covering complex numbers in historical context.","quote":"Here's a nice example covering complex numbers","text":"For a small buffet of abstract math topics (with lots of exposition putting ideas into historical context and an emphasis on building geometric intuition) try Courant & Robbins \"What is Mathematics?\". Covers number theory, number systems, geometry, topology, optimization, algebra, and knots, among others. You could also play around with branches of math like Knot and Braid Theory, which are interesting in their own right, give insight into lots of different branches of math, and many interesting problems are still accessible to lay people. Number theory is also like this I think it can also be helpful to learn some things about the history of math and the historical context that different ideas came from. Here's a nice example covering complex numbers [VIDEO] Maybe tangential to your ends, but the Crest of the Peacock is a nice book on non-European mathematical traditions, which provides some insight into how the process of establishing and validating mathematical knowledge works in other cultures. You might try books written by physicists or that are about mathematical physics (an author to look out for depending on your level is VI Arnold), since arguments will be of a more geometric or physical nature and appeal more to intuition. Stillwell is another author (not a physicist) that tends to write books that give context and geometric intuition You might like playing around with Pinter & Humphreys for Algebra, or Jänich for Topology (fantastic book for building intuition aro"},{"id":18052440,"author":"cheeko1234","month":"2018-09","story":"Walmart deploys Oculus Go headsets to train its employees","stance":"reference","why":"Example of math concepts easier to grasp when visualized.","quote":"","text":"I agree that for most of advanced math, VR won't help, however there are a lot of mathematical concepts that are easier to grasp when visualized. [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["KbB0FjPg0mw",1],["8eT1jaHmlx8",1],["uxC1fPE1QEE",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["PZbqAMEwtOE",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["8VqXBA-sGHA",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1]]},"7EmboKQH8lM":{"wy":"HN commenters call it better than the Clean Code book, a must-watch for engineers that changes how they read and write code.","q":{"x":"Clean Code is a great book, but the presentation is so much better","i":34312430,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34607000,"author":"srinidhikbhat","month":"2023-02","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed how commenter reads and writes code.","quote":"Changed the way I read/write code and my perspective about programming in general.","text":"Clean Code series by Bob Martin (Uncle Bob) Changed the way I read/write code and my perspective about programming in general. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34312430,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2023-01","story":"I don't read web articles anymore, but I read books","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presentation is better than the book.","quote":"Clean Code is a great book, but the presentation is so much better","text":"That's probably great as well, but I'm talking about searching for a book author. Just for example Clean Code is a great book, but the presentation is so much better: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34373127,"author":"xiphias2","month":"2023-01","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what agile really is.","quote":"The whole presentation should be a must watch by all engineers","text":"Maybe it's true at some companies, but it has nothing to do with agile / scrum, but letting non-tech people who love confrontation take over engineering leadership from engineers who are afraid of confrontation (which sounds like the disaster it is). Robert Martin (one of the creators of the agile manifesto) explains it quite well in ,,Clean Code'' presentation: [VIDEO] The whole presentation should be a must watch by all engineers so that they understand what _is_ agile to the point when they know it better than product / project managers / non-tech people. It's hard to get a better description than the original source, and no non-tech person has authority to override the real agile at that point."},{"id":47503227,"author":"misswaterfairy","month":"2026-03","story":"Cyberattack on vehicle breathalyzer company leaves drivers stranded in the US","stance":"rec","why":"Argues software needs an ethics body to avoid deadly failures.","quote":"The whole point of 'Clean Code' is to establish a body of ethics software 'engineers' collectively agree to","text":"> We need a software building code. This wouldn't be allowed to happen with non-software. The fact that anyone can build any product with software, make it work terribly, and when it fails impacts the lives of thousands (if not millions), needs to be stopped. Uncle Bob had a bit to say about this a while ago[1], and is largely what his Clean Code philosophy is trying to achieve, despite the detractors. To the detractors and down-voters... If you're complaining about over-abstraction, arguing about the 'better language' to use, or that 'Clean Code' doesn't work - you're missing the point by a few hundred miles. The whole point of 'Clean Code' is to establish a body of ethics software 'engineers' collectively agree to, and rigorously follow to avoid \"killing 10,000 people with a little software failure\". If you think you can make a better 'Clean Code', do so! That's the whole point about ethics debates. But! You need to proffer an alternative and prosecute why your version is better than Clean Code. [1] (19:47-24:33) [VIDEO]"},{"id":41203384,"author":"wickedsight","month":"2024-08","story":"Ask HN: Should we bring software dev in-house?","stance":"rec","why":"Why developers slow down.","quote":"Why do developers slow down","text":"If you're going to do this I recommend you at least take these things at heart: Why do developers slow down > [VIDEO] 80% of developers are unhappy. The problem is not AI, nor is coding > [LINK] What you're looking at is starting greenfield development. This always starts in the same way: 'we need to deliver functionality quickly because management wants results'. This always leads to the same issue, where code quality is ignored for sake of delivering functionality, which then leads to development slowing down and both management and devs becoming unhappy. If you want to build something for the long term, I'd recommend starting slow. Get some experienced freelance devs where you do thorough reference checks. Get them to set up a stable core and help in hiring some medior level devs to train. The medior level devs can grow to become the coaches of junior devs and then you can start scaling down the freelance devs. Whatever you do, don't plan to release something within the first year. Don't ask the team to estimate a delivery date within the first six months. After six months you should have a baseline that development can be continued with and then you probably have a team that can start estimating work and has a grasp of what they need to do to deliver something useful. Also, get a good product person, someone who can talk to business, draw out the different parts of the application and explain them to the developers. All of these things are hard, so you really need to comm"},{"id":40696990,"author":"misswaterfairy","month":"2024-06","story":"Post Office scandal victims in Scotland have convictions quashed","stance":"rec","why":"Segment on software failures and developer responsibility.","quote":"Uncle Bob, as divisive as he is, hits this nail on the head","text":"> About 700 subpostmasters were prosecuted for fraud and theft and over a hundred sent to prison And at least four purportedly related to these prosecutions committed suicide[1]. Perhaps most alarmingly: prior to 1999, the burden of proof for proving that the Horizon system was working correctly would have fallen to the Post Office (as the prosecutor in these instances) and Fujitsu (as the developer). That rule was changed to presume that the computer system worked correctly unless there was explicit evidence to the contrary[2][3]. A very high barrier for a non-technical person to prove, especially in an extremely complex system. It worries me how many people put unchecked faith into the systems that, now more-or-less, rule our lives whether we like it or not. It worries me even more that a lot of computer programmers, systems engineers, et al, don't understand how their code affects others in bad and unintended ways, and why testing matters so much. Uncle Bob, as divisive as he is, hits this nail on the head with a depleted uranium sledgehammer[4] (19:47-24:33). [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28409780,"author":"andrekandre","month":"2021-09","story":"GitHub Copilot Generated Insecure Code in 40% of Circumstances During Experiment","stance":"rec","why":"Lectures clarifying nuanced clean architecture advice.","quote":"i was honestly surprised when i watched a series of lectures from mr uncle (bob) where he clarified a lot of things","text":"agree i think the other thing is, theres clean architecture and then theres Clean Architecture TM where the thing is taken literally (leading to slavishly applying all the layers with lots of boilerplate, useless mocks and ridiculously coupled unit tests, over architected dependency injectors (assemblies) etc) i was honestly surprised when i watched a series of lectures from mr uncle (bob) where he clarified a lot of things such as \"use dependency injection only where it matters\" and \"unit tests should be replaced with integration tests after a system is finished being implemented\" to \"slavish following of agile \"customs\" is unproductive\" etc etc i think a lot of issues could be resolved if people took the time to think and listen carefully about these things and not stop at the first couple of search hits for \"clean architecture\" edit: heres the link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24447475,"author":"kowsheek","month":"2020-09","story":"The technical interview is an ego trip","stance":"rec","why":"Caring makes developers write better code.","quote":"If we care as a developer, we write better code, learn things and listen to our team, customers.","text":"I think \"caring\" is probably the only one that separates the best from the rest. Reminds me of this: [VIDEO] If we care as a developer, we write better code, learn things and listen to our team, customers."}],"r":[["tD5NrevFtbU",1],["skos4B5x7qE",1],["lJ3NC-R3gSI",1],["LKtk3HCgTa8",1],["PNbkv_DJ0f0",1]]},"l0GA901oGe4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an in-depth tech talk for urban planning, full of small design touches that make cycling safer.","q":{"x":"It's a great in depth look for anyone interested in this stuff, basically a tech talk for urban planning.","i":7930222,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":17714060,"author":"Vinnl","month":"2018-08","story":"New York’s success provides road map for others taking aim at pedestrian deaths","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Small infrastructure measures that slow cars and prevent traffic deaths","quote":"For those interested in this, I can highly recommend","text":"For those interested in this, I can highly recommend \"What Seattle can learn from Dutch street design\" [1]. It's a presentation that gives an overview of all sorts of small infrastructural measures, similar to the Queens Boulervard redesign, taken in the Netherlands to prevent traffic deaths. What makes it especially interesting is that e.g. rather than reducing the speed limit, cars are nudged to slow down through using different material for the road, or by simply making the road less wide. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12334474,"author":"raarts","month":"2016-08","story":"Guerrilla Bike Lanes in San Francisco","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Overview of Dutch cycling design from a Seattle planner","quote":"Lengthy, but I was glued to the screen.","text":"This is an overview of how the Dutch do it as presented by a city planning guy from Seattle. Lengthy, but I was glued to the screen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29305521,"author":"Vinnl","month":"2021-11","story":"The Stroad","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long talk on Dutch street design","quote":"Similar video (but then an hour long) that I always post together with that channel","text":"Similar video (but then an hour long) that I always post together with that channel: What can Seattle learn from Dutch street design? [VIDEO]"},{"id":20603267,"author":"Vinnl","month":"2019-08","story":"What We Can and Can’t Learn from Copenhagen","stance":"rec","why":"Small Dutch measures making cycling safe and pleasant","quote":"It helped me find out that apparently I'm a public infrastructure geek.","text":"This article might just as well have been about the Netherlands, so for those interested in this, you might also like this lecture discussing the many small measures taken in Dutch infrastructure to make cycling a safe and pleasant experience: What can Seattle learn from Dutch street design? [1] It helped me find out that apparently I'm a public infrastructure geek. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17866422,"author":"Vinnl","month":"2018-08","story":"How the Dutch created a casual biking culture","stance":"rec","why":"Many small touches that make Dutch cycling safer","quote":"A presentation with a ton of examples of small infrastructural touches that make it safer to cycle in the Netherlands.","text":"Relevant: what Seattle can learn from Dutch street design: [VIDEO] A presentation with a ton of examples of small infrastructural touches that make it safer to cycle in the Netherlands."},{"id":14427806,"author":"raarts","month":"2017-05","story":"Do We Overvalue Bike Lanes?","stance":"rec","why":"Extensive look at Dutch road safety","quote":"Way ahead in road safety.","text":"OP makes some good points but should go on vacation in the Netherlands. Way ahead in road safety. For example: [LINK] Or more extensively: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12685286,"author":"raarts","month":"2016-10","story":"The Dutch Reach: Clever Workaround to Keep Cyclists from Getting “Doored”","stance":"rec","why":"Visual tour of Dutch cycling infrastructure","quote":"For a pretty thorough description of how The Netherlands achieves safe biking, see this video","text":"For a pretty thorough description of how The Netherlands achieves safe biking, see this video: [VIDEO] \"Fred Young, a Landscape Architect based in Seattle, led us through a visual tour of Dutch cycling infrastructure, share insights of the transportation experts he met and show how cycling is a part of daily life in the Netherlands.\""},{"id":7930222,"author":"narwally","month":"2014-06","story":"A New Bike Lane That Could Save Lives and Make Cycling More Popular","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch bike infrastructure and how to adapt it for Seattle","quote":"It's a great in depth look for anyone interested in this stuff, basically a tech talk for urban planning.","text":"Here's a great talk[1] about all the clever bike infrastructure in the Netherlands, and how it can be adapted to work in Seattle specifically. It's a great in depth look for anyone interested in this stuff, basically a tech talk for urban planning. Edit: Here's a similar talk[2] I just found talking about adopting Dutch traffic planning to work in Vancouver. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["fnUc_W3xE1w",1],["FH2WeWgcSMk",1]]},"fpbOEoRrHyU":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as the go-to funny, accessible explanation of net neutrality and credit it with driving public comments to the FCC.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did a much better job (and funnier, btw)","i":8257993,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":13050425,"author":"musha68k","month":"2016-11","story":"European Union dedicated questionnaire on the Encryption of Data","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Drove stronger net neutrality policy and drew 12 million views","quote":"He and his team were the most effective drivers for stronger Net Neutrality policies in the US.","text":"What can a \"hacker\" possibly have against John Oliver? He and his team were the most effective drivers for stronger Net Neutrality policies in the US. The man is a living legend. Edit: 12 million views on the abstract topic of Net Neutrality. That's what I would call a feat indeed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8964584,"author":"e0m","month":"2015-01","story":"The Net Neutrality Bait and Switch","stance":"strong_rec","why":"13 minutes explaining net neutrality, including the FCC chair as a dingo","quote":"this video is 13 minutes well spent","text":"In case you haven't seen John Oliver's take on Net Neutrality, this video is 13 minutes well spent: [VIDEO] \"Tom Wheeler is [like] a dingo\""},{"id":8257993,"author":"felipesabino","month":"2014-09","story":"There Are Only Two Weeks Left to Comment on Net Neutrality","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Better and funnier mainstream explanation of net neutrality","quote":"John Oliver did a much better job (and funnier, btw)","text":"What was that? I am so disappointed to see that they featured a video showing net neutrality being ONLY about facebook, youtube and more video streaming... If they wanted to go mainstream, John Oliver did a much better job (and funnier, btw) [VIDEO]"},{"id":15750193,"author":"shmerl","month":"2017-11","story":"FCC plan would give Internet providers power to choose the sites customers see","stance":"rec","why":"A good way to explain net neutrality","quote":"John Oliver found a good way to explain it","text":"John Oliver found a good way to explain it: [VIDEO] [LINK] The problem is that ISP shills that make these decisions don't care about public opposition. They aren't elected officials."},{"id":15757330,"author":"sunnykgupta","month":"2017-11","story":"Join the Battle for Net Neutrality","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how government is tied to those who benefit from no net neutrality","quote":"For those who want to understand how closely the government is related to those who benefit from NO net-neutrality.","text":"While we are on the topic, may I bring up John Oliver's June 1 2014 episode. [VIDEO] tl;dw - For those who want to understand how closely the government is related to those who benefit from NO net-neutrality."},{"id":8049279,"author":"spuiszis","month":"2014-07","story":"Verizon's accidental mea culpa","stance":"rec","why":"John Oliver explains the issue eloquently","quote":"John Oliver also talks quite eloquently about this as well","text":"I have Comcast as well and it's gotten because of the deal Netflix struck with them... [LINK] EDIT: John Oliver also talks quite eloquently about this as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7874664,"author":"danesparza","month":"2014-06","story":"Netflix responds to Verizon","stance":"rec","why":"Sums up the competition problem behind US bandwidth pricing","quote":"John Oliver's rant on Net Neutrality (ironically on HBO) sums this up pretty nicely","text":"I agree that our bandwidth to price ratio is shitty in this country. But until we have viable competition in THIS country, our rates won't come down. John Oliver's rant on Net Neutrality (ironically on HBO) sums this up pretty nicely: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15932364,"author":"cookiecaper","month":"2017-12","story":"F.C.C. Repeals Net Neutrality Rules","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for jokes about Obama and the FCC during net neutrality debate","quote":"","text":">Nothing but damn lies. Please review the civility guidelines. If nothing else, mischaracterizing a clearly-labeled presumptive statement as a \"damn lie\" reveals your malice and discredits your POV. As I alluded to in the grandparent, it was not at all obvious that the FCC or other elements of the Obama administration were working toward net neutrality when the jokes about Obama leaving the baby to the dingos were getting flung around. [0] > What burden of net neutrality repeal? There was no burden because there was no requirement to repeal NN. It was clear that ISPs did not fit the legal definition of Title II carriers which is why they weren't just classified as such at the beginning. It was clear that it was not likely that they would retain this classification, whether a Democrat won the next cycle and a successful lawsuit overturned the rulings or whether the FCC undid it as is the case now with Ajit Pai (whose primary contention, by the way, is not that net neutrality shouldn't exist, just that Title II is not an appropriate regulatory framework in which to cast it). Of course, in politics, all that really matters is brownie points, so as long as the public sees you as the good guy, then you win and it doesn't matter if a judge overturns everything you've done. Obama made liberal use of this principle, and in some cases his staff would openly discuss the expectation that some executive action would not survive judicial review. Obama was pretty bad about his respect for "},{"id":15041677,"author":"vmarquet","month":"2017-08","story":"72% of Consumers Don't Know What Net Neutrality Is","stance":"reference","why":"Example of media coverage of net neutrality","quote":"see for example the two John Oliver talks about it","text":"I thought this was quite high, since the net neutrality issue had a lot of coverage in the media, see for example the two John Oliver talks about it: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["PZRI1IfStY0",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["pxBQLFLei70",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["rFejpH_tAHM",1],["92vuuZt7wak",1],["XgFbqSYdNK4",1],["W6aKQ-eBFk0",1],["nYkdrAPrdcw",1],["OoJsPvmFixU",1],["iCI3B5eT9NA",1]]},"w3_0x6oaDmI":{"wy":"It is the go-to short explainer HN commenters link whenever e-voting, voting machines or online voting come up.","q":{"x":"I love [this video]( ) from Tom Scott in which he explains quite well why e-voting is inherently bad.","i":18111146,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18111146,"author":"marcyb5st","month":"2018-10","story":"US mid-terms: Hackers expose 'staggering' voter machine flaws","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tom Scott explains well why e-voting is inherently bad.","quote":"I love [this video]( ) from Tom Scott in which he explains quite well why e-voting is inherently bad.","text":"I love [this video]( [VIDEO] ) from Tom Scott in which he explains quite well why e-voting is inherently bad. I am very happy that here in Switzerland, even if we have to vote several times every year, we still do it the old fashion way :)."},{"id":17723134,"author":"ajurna","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: Could programmers convince society not to use software voting systems?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good list of reasons software voting is a terrible idea.","quote":"it has a good list of reasons why software voting is a terrible idea.","text":"please watch this: [VIDEO] it has a good list of reasons why software voting is a terrible idea. also the secret ballet is very important to maintaining the fairness of elections."},{"id":9338428,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-04","story":"Unit tests fail when run in Australia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tom Scott on problems of electronic voting","quote":"I love Tom Scott's computerphile videos.","text":"I love Tom Scott's computerphile videos. His frustration grows over a few videos; his videos about the difficulties of internationalization[1] and the problems of electronic voting[2] are even crazier. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45752186,"author":"DontBreakAlex","month":"2025-10","story":"Tell HN: Azure outage","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott explains why not to use electronic or mail voting.","quote":"Tom Scott has a very good video about why we don't want electronic/mail voting","text":"In europe, voting typically happens in one day, where everyone physically goes to their designated voting place and puts papers in a transparent box. You can stay there and wait for the count at the end of the day if you want to. Tom Scott has a very good video about why we don't want electronic/mail voting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20622683,"author":"ninjaPixel","month":"2019-08","story":"Darpa wants help cracking the election security problem","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why paper voting is more secure than digital.","quote":"Here is a really good video explaining it","text":"I thought it was nonsense when I first heard it, but the traditional paper system is actually more secure than a completely digital system. Here is a really good video explaining it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17718277,"author":"herogreen","month":"2018-08","story":"XKCD: Voting software","stance":"rec","why":"Related Computerphile episode on voting software problems.","quote":"Related Computerphile episode","text":"Perhaps the most important political message I have seen on XKCD. Related Computerphile episode: [VIDEO] I guess that some computer-powered elections are coming in the US, EU ?"},{"id":14099568,"author":"mcast","month":"2017-04","story":"Election Commission of India throws “open challenge” to hack voting machines","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott explains problems with electronic voting.","quote":"Here's a great video by Tom Scott","text":"Having open source voting systems is a great thing, but electronic voting is inherehently flawed and is a terrible idea. There's no way to prove the checksum of your software is real unless you have root access to the voting machine and are assuming the OS is not compromised. Decentralized electronic machines that print paper ballots for you (which you can physically verify), and submitting to a tabulator seem to be the safest option. But your paper ballot can be compromised in transit by a worker. I have also heard talks about using a blockchain-esque system for voting and verifying it online with a hash code. Here's a great video by Tom Scott: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37220573,"author":"teddyh","month":"2023-08","story":"Brazilian hacker claims Bolsonaro asked him to hack into the 2022 voting system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Please explain how a “digital election system” could ever be a sane idea? • Why Electronic Voting is a BAD Idea < [VIDEO] > • Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea < [LINK] >"},{"id":29946019,"author":"V__","month":"2022-01","story":"It’s not still the early days of blockchain","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Tom Scott videos","text":"Obligatory Tom Scott videos: Why Electronic Voting is a BAD Idea - Computerphile | [VIDEO] Why Electronic Voting Is Still A Bad Idea | [LINK]"},{"id":28345364,"author":"throwjd764glug","month":"2021-08","story":"Republicans leak voting machines source code","stance":"reference","why":"Computerphile has strong arguments against electronic voting.","quote":"Computerphile has strong arguments against it as well [2].","text":"It's hard to decide which part to highlight, as every sentence is more farcical than the last, starting with the premise that the code that counts American's votes is kept secret from them, and the unquestioning trust placed by the author in what election officials and voting machine vendors say - election security is not something that should be taken on faith. > Dominion has filed suits contesting unfounded claims about its systems. In May, it called giving Cyber Ninjas access to its code “reckless” and said it would cause “irreparable damage” to election security. The machines are so very secure, that mere access to the source code (something which many people had even before - do we trust all of them? Do we even know who they are?) is downright reckless ? Then we shouldn't be using machines at all, as they are clearly untrustworthy even by their own admission. This is not a fringe opinion - electronic voting was ruled unconstitutional in Germany [1], and Computerphile has strong arguments against it as well [2]. In summary, we should be outraged that a leak was needed, not that it happened. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21389611,"author":"kennydude","month":"2019-10","story":"The Market for Voting Machines Is Broken. This Company Has Thrived in It","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The market for voting machines is broken because it should not exist. Voting machines are a bad idea. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20623612,"author":"jakeogh","month":"2019-08","story":"Darpa wants help cracking the election security problem","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as argument that e-voting is a bad idea even if auditable.","quote":"","text":"Nope. You never can audit most of it. Especially the hardware and firmware at vote time. And that's a false arg anyway, even if you could \"audit\" it (ask the hardware nicely to not lie to you:), it's still a (really) bad idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20430364,"author":"Fej","month":"2019-07","story":"New election systems use vulnerable software, Windows 7","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why digital election systems are a terrible idea.","quote":"here's the video from Computerphile on why digital election systems are a terrible, terrible idea","text":"Per usual, here's the video from Computerphile on why digital election systems are a terrible, terrible idea: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18386684,"author":"zyxzevn","month":"2018-11","story":"Hackers targeting election networks across country prior to midterms","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for argument that paper voting cannot be hacked.","quote":"","text":"[Computerphile - Why electric voting is a bad idea]( [VIDEO] ). No hacker can hack paper. Don't be triggered, but I still haven't seen any real evidence of \"Russian Hacking\" of the Election. Until now it has only been a political game to discredit Wikileaks. You can find some reasonable neutral info on [LINK] But there were some actual hacking reports like plugging USB-sticks in voting machines. The most recent one I found is an [alleged failed hack in Georgia]( [LINK] ). These are all cases that I want to be looked at, but I do not see that in the news. There are plans by Homelands security to take over the election infrastructure. If you trust them it is ok. If they start to plant their own votes, we may never know. That makes some people afraid that it is the patriot act for elections. The simple and cheap solution is to have no computer voting without a clear paper backlog and registration. Let's make recounts standard (and put those pensioners to work). No computers, no hackers, no cheaters, no problem. A more direct problem is corruption due to too much power and money in politics. Organisations like [LINK] may stop that. Anyway be friendly to humans that think differently than you, and vote for what you think is best."},{"id":15961415,"author":"foepys","month":"2017-12","story":"Estonia is planning to launch Estcoin","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott on problems with electronic voting.","quote":"Tom Scott on electronic voting","text":"Nobody sane would want e-voting. It has exactly the same problems as voting machines and in addition to that is heavily vulnerable to DDoS attacks and ID theft. Tom Scott on electronic voting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14967654,"author":"danhardman","month":"2017-08","story":"To Protect Voting, Use Open-Source Software","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott argues paper ballots work perfectly without electronic voting.","quote":"There is no need for an electronic voting system, paper ballots work perfectly.","text":"I'd like to reference Tom Scott's video[0] here. There is no need for an electronic voting system, paper ballots work perfectly. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14095770,"author":"fredley","month":"2017-04","story":"Brexit vote site may have been hacked, MPs say in report","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote supporting that digital voting is undesirable.","quote":"","text":"Because people move, and you need to be registered to vote in the right constituency and polling station (and those need to be well positioned/balanced so that too many people aren't voting in once place - which could mean some are unable to vote due to queues). Being able to vote anywhere is not an option - it would required digital voting, which we do not want .[1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12479089,"author":"zAy0LfpBZLC8mAC","month":"2016-09","story":"The careless errors of credit reporting agencies","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as further reading on why electronic voting is a bad idea.","quote":"See also","text":"I can, and it isn't. It cannot be. First of all, I wrote above you in this thread, regarding smart cards for government ID: > That's not really a good solution either, though, because that requires trust in an essentially unverifiable system and the entity producing it. So, maybe you want to address that instead of just pointing out that people are in fact using a system that obviously has this problem that I already mentioned. As for why electronic voting or electronic counting of votes is a terrible idea (I would have thought everyone on here knows that by now): It's impossible to audit. Elections are the failsafe of a democracy that has to be able to remove a government from power that tries to prevent being removed, and they are about the control of huge amounts of recources. Therefore, you cannot have trust in a small minority as the basis for its reliability, you have to have a system that is very hard to corrupt even by the government. A government server counting votes is the exact opposite of that. See also: [VIDEO] The fact that, so far, noone has seen any problems is completely missing the point. First of all, the whole system is set up such that it's really difficult to find out if something went wrong/was manipulated (that's just the nature of electronic voting). Secondly, most elections aren't all that problematic. In times of peace and prosperity, there usually isn't much contention over the results of an election. What makes a voting system good is when it's a"},{"id":12031771,"author":"vladimirralev","month":"2016-07","story":"Bulgaria Passes a Law Requiring Open Source","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source that verifiable electronic voting is not understood well.","quote":"","text":"The more people insist such verification is possible, the less you should trust them, they don't understand the issues[1]. It might be possible to come up with some provably verifiable scheme but they are very sophisticated and revised often because people still come up with ways to skew them[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11898304,"author":"mclemme","month":"2016-06","story":"Why Online Voting Is a Danger to Democracy","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I can't see how that in any way can be anywhere near secure enough. Online voting for anything important, is a bad idea. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1]]},"ZTC_RxWN_xo":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a favorite introduction to Valley history and cite it whenever government and military funding of tech comes up.","q":{"x":"my favorite introduction to the valleys history was Steve Blanks The Secret History of Silicon Valley. It's well worth the hour","i":3801009,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":7157167,"author":"cpr","month":"2014-01","story":"PayPal Mafia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Post-war roots of Silicon Valley in the 40s and 50s","quote":"You've gotta watch Steve Blank's \"The Secret History of Silicon Valley\". Goes back to its post-war roots in 40's and 50's. A real gem and quite surprising.","text":"You've gotta watch Steve Blank's \"The Secret History of Silicon Valley\". Goes back to its post-war roots in 40's and 50's. A real gem and quite surprising. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3801009,"author":"lukejduncan","month":"2012-04","story":"The Birth Of Silicon Valley (timeline)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite introduction to the Valley's history by Steve Blank","quote":"my favorite introduction to the valleys history was Steve Blanks The Secret History of Silicon Valley. It's well worth the hour","text":"When I first moved here my favorite introduction to the valleys history was Steve Blanks The Secret History of Silicon Valley. It's well worth the hour [VIDEO]"},{"id":40171884,"author":"rapsey","month":"2024-04","story":"Big Tech and Silicon Valley are transforming the military-industrial complex","stance":"rec","why":"Presentation on the history of Silicon Valley and Stanford","quote":"this is a very good presentation on the topic (of Silicon Valley and Stanford).","text":"I'm sure there are lots of reading options but this is a very good presentation on the topic (of Silicon Valley and Stanford). [VIDEO]"},{"id":32139352,"author":"dundarious","month":"2022-07","story":"A Look Back at the USSR Computer Industry","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the long-secret history of Silicon Valley","quote":"I like this talk given at the Computer History Museum about the history of Silicon Valley","text":"Yes, Silicon Valley grew out of quite sophisticated WW2 military R&D, so quite similar to USSR actually. I like this talk given at the Computer History Museum about the history of Silicon Valley, which was secret for many years: [VIDEO] You can argue USSR lacked followup demand for computing from outside the military, and that this was caused by organizational issues. It’s probably pretty important to also state the relative wealth disparity at the start of the relevant period, from 1940 onwards, say. Russia was basically feudal in 1910, and it would be hard for advancement to catch up to US levels by 1940 no matter the organization of the economy, and then we have to account for the devastation of WW2 which followed (WW2 was expensive for US, but it didn’t lose industrial capacity except via opportunity cost). I don’t say this as any kind of apologia, but think it makes more sense to compare USSR to less developed countries than US — where it still doesn’t compare favorably in many ways, but it’s far more interesting and debatable."},{"id":22910195,"author":"DeBraid","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What are some great posts or articles about history of computing?","stance":"rec","why":"Great video on Silicon Valley history via Steve Blank","quote":"Not a book, but a great video via Steve Blank","text":"Related thread: `Ask HN: What's the best book on the early history of the Internet and/or Web?` [LINK] My previous reco: Not a book, but a great video via Steve Blank: [VIDEO] Also `Bret Victor The Future of Programming`, which is misleading as above is performance piece where title slide reads `1973` [LINK]"},{"id":3994035,"author":"jstrate","month":"2012-05","story":"'The Golden Age of Silicon Valley Is Over, and We're Dancing on its Grave'","stance":"rec","why":"Steve Blank's take gives context on the Valley's evolution","quote":"I suspect many of you haven't seen his take on the history of the valley","text":"I see a lot of comments addressed to \"this guy\" referring to Steve Blank. Let's not forget he's been involved in silicon valley startups since the 70s, including founding semiconductor companies. While they're no hello world rails startup, he has a proven track record and a first-hand experience of how the valley has evolved. I suspect many of you haven't seen his take on the history of the valley, which might give context to his views. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47270400,"author":"bfung","month":"2026-03","story":"Where things stand with the Department of War","stance":"reference","why":"Silicon Valley's roots in WW2 and government funding","quote":"The Silicon Valley tech jobs we have now has a history rooted in World War 2 and funding of it by the US gov.","text":"I don’t feel that old, but I guess being 45 is ancient in tech. The Silicon Valley tech jobs we have now has a history rooted in World War 2 and funding of it by the US gov. [VIDEO] I’m not saying war is good or anything, but also don't ride a high horse cause none of it would be here w/o WW2."},{"id":43919055,"author":"api","month":"2025-05","story":"Mississippi Can't Possibly Have Good Schools","stance":"reference","why":"History of government funding behind Silicon Valley","quote":"None of this would exist without probably trillions in DARPA, NASA, DoD, and government-funded university research on computers and networks.","text":"> The rural way of life is already insanely subsidized. So is ours: [VIDEO] None of this would exist without probably trillions in DARPA, NASA, DoD, and government-funded university research on computers and networks. We live in a complex internetworked and interdependent civilization. Everyone is subsidized by everyone else, and balancing the books is incredibly hard."},{"id":39415099,"author":"nradov","month":"2024-02","story":"Silicon Valley learned to love America, drones, and glory","stance":"reference","why":"Original Valley companies grew from WW2 military contracts","quote":"The original companies largely grew out of WW2 military contracts for radar and electronics.","text":"There were tech companies in Silicon Valley long before the 1980s-90s boom. The original companies largely grew out of WW2 military contracts for radar and electronics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30562832,"author":"nickff","month":"2022-03","story":"Is the Russian Air Force incapable of complex air operations?","stance":"reference","why":"Radar countermeasures were critical to Silicon Valley's origins","quote":"Radar countermeasures were actually a critical part of Silicon Valley's origins","text":"The Germans and the Japanese developed radar systems during the war, though they were 'behind' the Brits and Americans. Radar countermeasures were actually a critical part of Silicon Valley's origins: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28955406,"author":"dragonelite","month":"2021-10","story":"Half a million South Korean workers walk off jobs in general strike","stance":"reference","why":"US military spending built Silicon Valley semiconductors","quote":"Silicon valley and western semi conductor supremacy was build by US military spending in ww2, 1950s and 1960s.","text":"Silicon valley and western semi conductor supremacy was build by US military spending in ww2, 1950s and 1960s. [VIDEO] Its was only in the late 1970s that wallstreet got their money into silicon valley. Given the channel I did not do any fact checking and just assume the presentation is historically correct."},{"id":28996063,"author":"SquishyPanda23","month":"2021-10","story":"The FBI's internal guide for getting data from AT&T, T-Mobile, Verizon","stance":"reference","why":"Government funds startups with tactical value","quote":"The government and military fund the creation of startups that have tactical value.","text":"> Essentially the government has built a surveillance state by outsourcing it to private enterprise. Well, yes, that's essentially the whole point of silicon valley. The government and military fund the creation of startups that have tactical value. Those businesses become self-funding and improve the US economy, which also has military value since a robust economy is harder to attack. This has been explored in a few places, e.g. [0], [1]. But it's not like any of this was secret. The off-loading of government operations to private industry, combined with the lobbying for reduced regulations on private industry effectively gives the government carte blanche with the added bonus of plausible deniability. Whether or not these trends are good has been debated for half a century in the US. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25594549,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-12","story":"How Texas Can Become the Next Silicon Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Terman and Cold War money built Silicon Valley","quote":"See Secret History of Silicon Valley","text":"> The Valley's seeds were planted in the 1920s when the Stanford-graduated, radio-enthusiast, electrical engineer sons of John Osborne Varian[0] patented the vibrating magnetometer. SV was built by Fred Terman using post-WW2 Cold War money: * [LINK] MIT, Caltech, and Harvard/Columbia were the pinnacle radio research in WW2 and got most of the money: * [VIDEO] Stanford was (considered) mostly a backwater at that time. It was only starting in ~1950 that Stanford took off. See Secret History of Silicon Valley: * [LINK]"},{"id":23575140,"author":"nibepins","month":"2020-06","story":"\"Secret\" History of Silicon Valley (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Military-funded Stanford research and Terman's startup encouragement","quote":"military funded research from WWI all the way through the Cold War led to the birth of Silicon Valley","text":"One of the great things about the Corbett Report is that you don't have to believe it, you can just look at the sources because every source is referenced. The case in point over Google's Sergey Brin and the NSA comes from a Freedom of Information Act request fulfilled by the NSA: [LINK] . The second point about backdoor data collection through PRISM comes from the Snowden leaks and the reporting from the Guardian: [LINK] . In the video lecture of this post, Steve explains that military funded research from WWI all the way through the Cold War led to the birth of Silicon Valley as we know it today. After making Stanford the \"MIT of the West\" by bringing hundreds of million dollars of taxpayer money to Stanford for MIL research, in the mid 1950's Fred Terman \"encourages his students to leave and start companies\" and \"professors to leave and consult for companies\", and makes his position clear that \"I don't want to build production systems for the military, I want to do research. I want other people to start companies and have the military fund them.\" [VIDEO] [LINK] Some of Silicon Valley's biggest companies today are the product of tax payer money through the same mechanism which privatizes profits from taxpayer funded MIL research. A prime example is Google. Sergey Brinn and Larry Page had been doing research and building Google at Stanford with taxpayer MIL research money since at least 1995: \"In 1995, one of the first and most promising MDDS grants went to a computer-scienc"},{"id":16693404,"author":"hueving","month":"2018-03","story":"Arizona Government Suspends Uber's Self Driving Cars from Roads","stance":"reference","why":"Military radar decisions made California the tech center","quote":"California remains the center because the companies are already headquartered there due to decisions made 50 years ago by the military for radar technology","text":"California remains the center because the companies are already headquartered there due to decisions made 50 years ago by the military for radar technology: [VIDEO] That's despite it's often idiotic regulations driven by populist ignorance. A governance policy in favor of developments of new technology most certainly can have an impact over time. > Laissez-faire regulation and rolling out the red carpet for bad actors in Arizona didn't change that. Oh, red carpet for bad actors? Where is it that Uber is headquartered again? And doesn't Waymo also test in Arizona?"},{"id":12718371,"author":"Noseshine","month":"2016-10","story":"Peter Higgs: I wouldn't be productive enough for today's academic system (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Government WWII spending seeded Silicon Valley","quote":"Reminds me of the real history of Silicon Valley","text":"Reminds me of the real history of Silicon Valley: [VIDEO] TL;DW The seeds are based on unrestrained spending of huge amounts of non-existing money by the government during WWII."},{"id":9604042,"author":"mpoloton","month":"2015-05","story":"As Facebook Sweeps Across Europe, Regulators Gird for Battle","stance":"reference","why":"Military-funded innovation origins of Silicon Valley","quote":"","text":"This might be the case but I don't think that is the main reason. In fact, Europe has many excellent universities. I believe one of the reasons that Europe is lacking IT giants is because investors are risk averse and they would rather invest in tangible industries. For example, in Switzerland which is ranked first in global innovation index [1] the IT industry is almost non-existing even though it has vibrant medical, food, financial industries. Another reason in my opinion is the lack (or weakness) of military industrial complex. In the US many innovations are funded directly or indirectly with public money through military contractors and projects. (DARPA, Silicon Valley [2], Internet,...). Just by looking at patent portfolio of NSA you get a taste of technology transfer with public money to private sector. 1- [LINK] 2- [VIDEO]"},{"id":2501353,"author":"patrickk","month":"2011-04","story":"Workers Leaving the Googleplex","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Is the U.S. rich in happiness? contentment? My perception is no.\" Happiness is relative. Struggling to scrape by on a dollar a day, and wondering where your next meal is going to come from must be pretty awful. Perhaps more people should do volunteer work to realise just how lucky we have it in the west. I think that by being wealthier, you can \"move up\" Maslow's Hierarchy of Human Needs[1], and if you reach 'self-actualisation' i.e. you are intellectually fulfilled, have pride in your work and accomplishments etc., that that is it's own kind of happiness. I agree that you are never going to achieve any kind of happiness by simply pursuing some arbitrary dollar amount. That is a fool's game. Beyond a certain point increasing your bank account balance is a meaningless exercise. The second and third paragraph mention some things are are an unfortunate byproduct of an industrialsed nation. They are also arguably unnecessary - the means are there to combat obesity, products made by slave labour, destruction of the environment etc. I don't really have a good answer for these. It's important to note that some of these things can happen in a poor country too - I remember in the movie \"Black Hawk Down\" that the soldiers were admiring the lovely view of the ocean, but they were warned to not go in as it was extremely polluted. It probably wasn't the Somalians that polluted it though! \"Your point appears to be that financial innovation (money pursuits) can and sometimes do lead to ben"},{"id":2250158,"author":"tastybites","month":"2011-02","story":"A Visitor's Guide to Silicon Valley","stance":"reference","why":"Parallels between Silicon Valley and San Fernando Valley aerospace","quote":"If you've seen Steve Blank's presentation on the 'secret history' of Silicon Valley","text":"A California subtlety often missed by those who didn't grow up here (and many that did) is the fact that Silicon Valley started out a lot like the other 'the valley' that grew up with the aerospace / defense industry, the San Fernando Valley, which have extensive similarities, including small details like the Fry's electronics and bigger details like the main drags through town. Ventura Blvd. and 82 are basically the same El Camino Real. The 101 (which parallels Ventura Blvd for quite a distance) is marked as El Camino Real, as is 82 in Silicon Valley. Both have foundations in the Spanish missionary trail. They're basically two sections of one very long road. If you've seen Steve Blank's presentation on the 'secret history' of Silicon Valley you'll see where I draw the parallels. Lockheed Martin, Rocketdyne, General Dynamics, Litton, Rockwell, and many others were once based there. [VIDEO] But the divergence was inevitable I suppose - the valley up north has gotten comparatively nicer in the past 15 years, though, as San Fernando's fortunes have faded a bit. The SF valley now basically lacks a coherent high technology business base and all the aerospace and defense has moved out. It's been replaced by Hollywood entertainment, healthcare, porno, import/export, and a bunch of other smaller industries and some light manufacturing. It's not defunct in any way - it's still pretty nice, but it's just not as nice as up north. Most of the newer wealth is in other areas of LA. Both ar"},{"id":1275131,"author":"samratjp","month":"2010-04","story":"The Columbia Math Professor Every NYC Startup & VC Should Know","stance":"reference","why":"Terman and military's role in Silicon Valley's rise","quote":"Stanford is what it is today thanks to Terman's meddling and the army","text":"He has a good frame of reference: “I see this as an opportunity for academics in New York to contribute to start-up culture,” says Wiggins, referring to the pivotal role that Stanford’s dean of engineering, Frederick Terman, played in the rise of Silicon Valley. Stanford is what it is today thanks to Terman's meddling and the army ( [VIDEO] ). If Wiggins is NYC's Terman, what's NYC's army equivalent?"}],"r":[["AxnotgLql0k",1],["r9eQth4Q5jg",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["5AqbCQuK0gM",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["SK4ezQrTqFw",1],["JKHUaNAxsTg",1]]},"ue-1JoJQaEg":{"wy":"HN points people to it as a deep dive showing how complex logic was built without computers.","q":{"x":"I shared it with everyone I know, and now I'm sharing it with you","i":44984615,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44984615,"author":"bsimpson","month":"2025-08","story":"VHS-C: When a lazy idea stumbles towards perfection [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour-long explanation of how switches and stepper motors implement complex logic","quote":"I shared it with everyone I know, and now I'm sharing it with you","text":"YT recently recommended his explanation of how pre-computer pinball machines worked to me - a series of 3, hour-long videos. Gave me something to look forward to on my commute. I shared it with everyone I know, and now I'm sharing it with you: [VIDEO] Fascinating (and insanely impressive) to see how a bunch of switches and stepper motors implement complex logic."},{"id":46970933,"author":"roskelld","month":"2026-02","story":"Fun With Pinball","stance":"rec","why":"Companion series on old pinball machines","quote":"The site is also a nice compliment to the Technology Connections series on old pinball machines","text":"I would have loved seeing this as a kid. I was obsessed with wanting to build a pinball machine when I was young. At the time my skills only stretched as far as cardboard builds with elastic band bumpers and pencils for flippers, but I got a huge kick out of making different layouts for the balls to travel around. It would be great to see some home made efforts now with so many amazing off the shelf bits of tech and 3D printers. The site is also a nice compliment to the Technology Connections series on old pinball machines [1]. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42611598,"author":"exhilaration","month":"2025-01","story":"A man keeping hope, and 70-year-old pinball machines, alive","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how incredibly complex old pinball machines are","quote":"Technology Connections has several videos about old pinball machines that showcase how incredibly complex they are","text":"Related - Technology Connections has several videos about old pinball machines that showcase how incredibly complex they are, here's the first video: Old pinball machines are amazingly complex [VIDEO]"},{"id":41746022,"author":"chongli","month":"2024-10","story":"How were 70s versions of games like Pong built without a programmable computer?","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive into workings of a 1970s pinball machine.","quote":"an amazingly deep dive into the workings of a 1970's model, check out Alec's pinball series on Technology Connections","text":"You still use paper. Instead of writing the game in a general purpose programming language you write it using logic gates. You get abstraction and modularity by designing larger components (adders, flip-flops, timers, shift registers) on separate pieces of paper and then including them as named black boxes in a higher-level diagram. The good news for the Pong developers is that most of those larger components were already available off-the-shelf. Common families of these chips, such as the venerable 4000-series and 7400-series logic families, began to appear on the market in the mid-1960's. Edit just to add another bit of nuance . If it still seems like an extremely difficult task without much precedent, I think the lineage of these early arcade games can be traced back through their older arcade siblings: pinball machines. People had been building more and more sophisticated pinball machines over the decades since their inception in the early 1930s. For a look into pinball machines, some of their history, and an amazingly deep dive into the workings of a 1970's model, check out Alec's pinball series on Technology Connections [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38869558,"author":"brewdad","month":"2024-01","story":"Why a toaster from 1949 is still smarter than any sold today (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at a 1970s pinball machine.","quote":"It goes horribly in-depth (in a good way).","text":"I've been watching his videos on a 1970s pinball machine. It goes horribly in-depth (in a good way). Fascinating how much complexity could be managed with the simplest of interfaces. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38165486,"author":"iancmceachern","month":"2023-11","story":"Automata: The Extraordinary \"Robots\" Designed Hundreds of Years Ago [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how complexity is done mechanically in pinball machines.","quote":"does a great job of explaining how a lot of this stuff can be done mechanically.","text":"This video from Technology Connections on old mechanical pinball machines does a great job of explaining how a lot of this stuff can be done mechanically. Re: your for loop question, check out how they handle the scoring and I think it'll give you some ideas. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45950992,"author":"gregsadetsky","month":"2025-11","story":"A 1961 Relay Computer Running in the Browser","stance":"reference","why":"Pinball machines use rotating wheel mechanisms like the Minivac rotary switch","quote":"","text":"The Rotary Switch - which is both input and output in the real Minivac - is absolutely wild yeah. One of the crucial things I didn't get to yet is making that wheel controllable - some circuits in the manuals do require you to turn it. That's high in the todo list (with mobile support). I also really want to share all of the videos I've been collecting of similar \"wheels\" - in (modern-ish) elevators [0] and light shows [1]... And also pinball machines! [2] Re: RadioShack boards, I did have the \"300 in 1\" kit growing up, so yes... it's full circle for me. :-) [0] [LINK] and [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43914156,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2025-05","story":"VVVVVV Source Code","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the electromechanical machine cycle like a state machine","quote":"Also reminds me of old electromechanical pinball machines. You can literally see the machine cycle.","text":"I think it's pretty charming. Games have so much abstraction these days it feels like there's no way to truly understand what it is they're even doing. One can spend months agonizing over the true nature of things and how ideas and concepts relate to each other and eventually distill it all into some object oriented organization that implements not just your game but all possible games. One can also just cycle the game's state machine in a big function, haha switch statement go brrr. Reminds me of the old NES games which would statically allocate memory for game objects, very much in the \"structure of arrays\" style, they too had game logic just like that. Also reminds me of old electromechanical pinball machines. You can literally see the machine cycle. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":41749790,"author":"dwh452","month":"2024-10","story":"How were 70s versions of games like Pong built without a programmable computer?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains complexity of electromechanical pinball machines","quote":"","text":"How were pinball machines built without a programmable computer? [VIDEO] I think the arcade industry was already comfortable dealing with complexity to make the mechanical games. The Rube Goldberg nature of the early video games probably weren't that much of a jump in effort/engineering."},{"id":38112871,"author":"shizcakes","month":"2023-11","story":"Now You're Thinking with Relays","stance":"reference","why":"Walkthrough of relay-based logic in a pinball machine.","quote":"Technology Connections very recently put out a walkthrough of relay-based logic as it applied to a pinball machine","text":"Technology Connections very recently put out a walkthrough of relay-based logic as it applied to a pinball machine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37933131,"author":"atum47","month":"2023-10","story":"Ask HN: Why is there no modern successor to the 3D Pinball games of yesteryear?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I was watching a video [1] about pinball machines the other day","text":"I was watching a video [1] about pinball machines the other day and thought about creating a pinball using my \"physics engine\" [2]. I was able to create a simple billiards [3] game using it. I've been pretty busy with work and other personal projects, but I've been meaning to get back to program for fun. 1 - [VIDEO] 2 - [LINK] 3 - [LINK]"}],"r":[["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["lnnsDi7Sxq0",1],["ZhuHCtR3xq8",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["aWvHrih-Juk",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["0UjpmT5noto",1],["b5P3QDm61go",1],["MkJkyMuBm3g",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["1fZTOjd_bOQ",1],["E3p_Cv32tEo",1]]},"Agu6jipKfYw":{"wy":"HN people point to it as the clearest explanation of what FRP actually means and how its flavors differ.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend Evan's Strange Loop talk","i":8730127,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":47475785,"author":"modulovalue","month":"2026-03","story":"We've had front end reactivity since 2018 – via Async Iterables","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Definitive talk on complexity of evaluating computation graphs in FRP.","quote":"here's THE talk on that topic that shows how complicated working out all the kinks can be","text":"There's an important issue with async iterables: what's the initial value of derived \"signals\"? In the Dart/Flutter world, async iterables are known as streams. The biggest problem is that they are asynchronous so if you derive new streams from existing streams, you won't have a value immediately for those streams available, you'll have to wait a frame for values to propagate. That's not a problem if you're just processing data, but if you're using them in a UI framework and your UI framework is coupled to the event loop, then you have a problem. This doesn't happen with the ReactiveX approach because things are evaluated immediately, one doesn't have to wait for the event loop to tick. (A fun historical artifact is that ReactiveX has not caught on in the Dart/Flutter community around 2019-ish because it was eventually migrated to use Streams and the initial-value-problem was a real problem that made the benefits of ReactiveX not carry its weight nobody noticed that but I was heavily involved and I am still using a custom framework derived from my observations back then). If we zoom out a bit, the problem is actually that we are trying to define a computation graph and then need a strategy for evaluation it. I think Elm has actually made the biggest progress on that front (here's THE talk on that topic that shows how complicated working out all the kinks can be: [VIDEO] ) I still think that functional reactive programming solves most of the problems we have in UI-land, but th"},{"id":8730127,"author":"dcre","month":"2014-12","story":"Elm 0.14 – Simpler Core, Better Tools","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduction to Elm and FRP formulations; needs functional background.","quote":"I highly recommend Evan's Strange Loop talk","text":"If you're interested in Elm but don't really know what it's about, I highly recommend Evan's Strange Loop talk, \"Controlling Time and Space: understanding the many formulations of FRP.\" It will help to already have a bit of background in functional programming. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10838312,"author":"ghuntley","month":"2016-01","story":"New Adventures for Elm","stance":"rec","why":"Strange Loop talk useful for those starting in FRP.","quote":"For those just getting started in FRP or whom may have not seen the presentations at StangeLoop","text":"For those just getting started in FRP or whom may have not seen the presentations at StangeLoop: - [VIDEO] - Controlling Time and Space: understanding the many formulations of FRP by Evan Czaplicki (Elm language designer/Prezi) - [LINK] - FRP In Practice: Taking a look at Reactive[UI/Cocoa] by Paul Betts (Slack/GitHub) - [LINK] - ReactiveUI - It's pretty neat by Brendan Forster (GitHub)"},{"id":10607011,"author":"michaelsbradley","month":"2015-11","story":"React vs. Angular 2","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on many formulations of FRP by Elm's designer.","quote":"See also Controlling Time and Space: understanding the many formulations of FRP , by the same author","text":"It's been around in various forms, and implemented on top of various languages, for quite some time. The earliest full-fledged FRP implementation for JavaScript was Flapjax – work on it dates all the way back to 2006! [LINK] The paper Flapjax: A Programming Language for Ajax Applications is worth reading. [LINK] The principal designer and maintainer of the Elm Language (a functional reactive lang that compiles to JS) did his Master's Thesis on FRP, and that's a great resource if you want the big picture: [LINK] See also Controlling Time and Space: understanding the many formulations of FRP , by the same author: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8543848,"author":"platz","month":"2014-11","story":"Introduction to Reactive Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies what FRP actually means.","quote":"For clarification on FRP there was a great talk at strangeloop this year","text":"> \"FRP is programming with asynchronous data streams.\" Maybe, if you drop the F. For clarification on FRP there was a great talk at strangeloop this year: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12159378,"author":"baggers","month":"2016-07","story":"Purely Functional Retrogames (2008)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on Elm dropping FRP.","quote":"","text":"A few notes: - Elm dropped frp [0] - Even in it's older frp incarnation, elm didnt allow changing of the flow graph (sorry I cant remember the correct elm term for this) while the program ran [1], making it hard to say this would be an ideal way to go for true 'interactive coding' for games. To clarify my opinion I would like to add that I am assuming an ideal of 'interactive game coding' where one would start a GL context & repl session at the start of the day and make the game whilst never closing the session or killing the context. The OP limited himself to retrogames which is a wise move for exploring this topic, _one_ of the things that makes writing modern games hard is the extent to which you are pushing what the hardware can do. It is normal to be required to really be strict about what happens when in each frame and it's not possible to do this if, part-way through rendering you have to process events coming from some input thread that has no idea about the context your program is in. Lastly I would add that your program itself is state. Interactive programming requires you to create and remove functions from your running program and bugs particular to doing this can occur (some object has a reference to a version of a function you have removed etc etc). Unless we move to a point where our code (compiled & text) live in a some persistant data-structure that allows rollback in some meaningful way I find it hard to imagine _real_ functional interactive programming. Thi"},{"id":11669295,"author":"wheatBread","month":"2016-05","story":"A Farewell to FRP","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own talk relating Elm to similar-looking reactive approaches.","quote":"I talk about the relation between Elm and that sort of stuff in","text":"Definitely not ignoring. I talk about the relation between Elm and that sort of stuff in [VIDEO] Lots of things look similar in this area that are not. I also offer an analysis of \"higher order\" observables in [LINK] , and the big difference is the underlying thread architecture you get using these two. I hope I'll be able to make these sorts of things clearer in time!"},{"id":10562752,"author":"nv-vn","month":"2015-11","story":"Writing code doesn't mix well with oration","stance":"reference","why":"Example of effective live demo showing Elm debugging.","quote":"it works well in this demo","text":"I think that live coding can serve as a really good tool if you do it right and it applies directly to what you're doing. For example, it works well in this demo [VIDEO] because it's a demo of how Elm can help you think about what's going on and debug your code on the spot. This presentation would have been a lot more boring if it lacked a live demo."},{"id":10065955,"author":"jarpineh","month":"2015-08","story":"A simpler web architecture using React, Flux, CSP, and FRP concepts","stance":"reference","why":"Elm creator's talk on the same FRP topic.","quote":"There is also Elm's creator's presentation at Strange Loop last year of the same topic","text":"Higher level concepts in area so rife with incidental complexity like programming tend to get lost in the details of the implementation. Patterns and abstract concepts like FRP can only be understood by with implementation. Ideas tend to bend before working code, then again with an example app you enthusiastically write for your blog, and then again when someone bases her next project on that (not to mention HN discussions...). I like to learn thing this way, and not care that much about what the alphabet soup entails. Try to find what is a proper JavaScript MVC framework. And then write the first one yourself ;) I'm currently reading Clojure Reactive Programming, which differentiates from FRP using another term, CES for Compositional Event Systems, and goes to some length into history of these concepts, like higher order FRP, First Order FRP, Arrowized FRP, and of course Observer pattern and Data Flow programming. There is also Elm's creator's presentation at Strange Loop last year of the same topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10107248,"author":"ghuntley","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask HN: Why web frameworks are becoming so complex?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to rebut claim web pioneered functional UI paradigms.","quote":"","text":"> The web community pioneered UI development paradigms involving > functional programming techniques and immutable data Ugh, No. [LINK] and [VIDEO]"},{"id":10107945,"author":"goldenkey","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask HN: Why web frameworks are becoming so complex?","stance":"reference","why":"Resource from the Elm creator on functional reactive programming.","quote":"I appreciate your resources, namely the video by the creator of Elm,","text":"I appreciate your resources, namely the video by the creator of Elm, [VIDEO] . However, I agree with above poster. I remember developing my first windows app by doing a main event loop. It's been that way for a long time. Only recently are we doing more asychronous callback / bound type mechanisms of reaction. And still not as strong as the FLP stuff mentioned in the video. Not as much functional stuff, just functional paradigms. I personally really like the perfection of static input/output transformation graphs. But yeah..it's not been that way for a while and I agree with OP, the web being so event/input/signal based, has spurred the movement for better ways of reactive programming."}],"r":[["QZy4Yml3LTY",1],["fNpsgTIpODA",1],["_8bhtEgB8Mo",1],["ftcIcn8AmSY",1],["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1],["krB0enBeSiE",1]]},"PQmSUHhP3ug":{"wy":"HN cites it as the benchmark lap record and recommends it even to non-car fans for its sheer speed and precision.","q":{"x":"If anyone hasn't seen this, I highly recommend it, even if you're not a car fan.","i":45485218,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48001804,"author":"TacticalCoder","month":"2026-05","story":"Porsche will contest Laguna Seca in historic colors of the Apple Computer livery","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Porsche's record 5:19 Nordschleife lap, a must-know video.","quote":"Any car lover who doesn't know that vid can safely watch it.","text":"Here's Porsche really at its best, on the Nurburgring's Nordschleiffe in 2018, which is arguably the most complicated and diverse track on the planet doing a cool 5m19s (in 2024 a Mercedes AMG GT One was a full 1min10s slower, for example): [VIDEO] Any car lover who doesn't know that vid can safely watch it."},{"id":45485218,"author":"lossolo","month":"2025-10","story":"BYD builds fastest car","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Record 5:19 lap, worth watching even for non-car fans.","quote":"If anyone hasn't seen this, I highly recommend it, even if you're not a car fan.","text":"> Porsche 919 Hybrid EVO did it in 5:19 If anyone hasn't seen this, I highly recommend it, even if you're not a car fan. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25974011,"author":"RaceWon","month":"2021-01","story":"Tesla's new steering yoke isn't retro, it's a safety risk","stance":"rec","why":"Astonishing record lap showing precision driving.","quote":"Here's a Great video shot at the Nürburgring showing an astonishing lap record","text":">> Anyway, big rigs have loose steering because they need precision at highway speeds. The opposite is actually true. Big rigs have loose steering so that the driver will Not jack knife the truck in a panic situation. A tighter steering rack is Much more precise. Street tires, and especially racing tires, work best (have the most grip) at very narrow slip angles. Here's a Wikipedia about the \"friction circle\" as racers call it. \" [LINK] \" Here's a Great video shot at the Nürburgring showing an astonishing lap record of the Porsche 919 Hybrid race car, precision personified... Enjoy! \" [VIDEO] \""},{"id":19407593,"author":"RaceWon","month":"2019-03","story":"Formula 1: The secret aerodynamicist reveals design concepts","stance":"rec","why":"Realistically conveys the speed of a Porsche 919 at the Nurburgring.","quote":"portays the quick factor fairly realistically","text":">I almost feel stupid but I never realized they take the corners THAT fast. Holy shit. Respect. That's insane. The camera angles Rarely do the cars justice in protraying their speed (though its gotten better). There's a great vid on HN with a Porsche 919 sportscar at the Nurbugring that portays the quick factor fairly realistically (you can quibble about the various diffrences between a Sportscar and an F1 car in terms of capabilities--both are other worldy quick, and fast). I like to post that vid when talk here turns to self-driving cars, but I digress; F1 pilots are simply amazing. The cars are too. \" [VIDEO]"},{"id":17891002,"author":"RaceWon","month":"2018-09","story":"Apple self-driving car in accident: California DMV filing","stance":"rec","why":"Porsche 919 record lap showing extreme human driving skill.","quote":"And it is ridiculous...","text":"I was going to post earlier that IMO the level they should be aiming at is that of a brilliant racer, otherwise who needs them. There was a vid on here recently of the Porsche 919 beating the lap record at the Ring, and one of the comments was \"I don't have the balls to drive that fast in a video game\" such is the skill level of humans at the wheel-a few to be sure, but still and all there it is. Here's the vid btw. And it is ridiculous... \" [VIDEO]"},{"id":21856140,"author":"kart23","month":"2019-12","story":"Autonomous DeLorean drives sideways to move forward","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the current lap record holder.","quote":"Theres no way a tesla roadster is beating the current record holder, the 919 evo.","text":"Theres no way a tesla roadster is beating the current record holder, the 919 evo. The roadster would be magnitudes heavier, plus it wouldn't have the aero for the downforce. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21340298,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2019-10","story":"Tesla Q3 Financial Results","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the overall Nurburgring lap record.","quote":"the overall record is 5:19 held by the Porsche 919 Evo","text":"> it will be pretty much destroyed next year by the Tesla S with plaid mode Why don't Tesla's current cars destroy the Taycan? I think it's fair to say that at this time Tesla's a year behind Porsche in the performance EV stakes. Here are some of the Taycan's performance test results which Tesla has so far been unable to match: - 24 hour endurance run. 3,425 kms covered in 24 hours: [LINK] - Onboard video of their 7:42 run at the Nurburgring: [LINK] - Acceleration and handling comparison to the Model S P100D: [LINK] - Model S Plaid's Nurburgring results: [LINK] And no onboard video yet from any of Tesla's Nurburgring runs unfortunately. It's also disappointing that Tesla isn't bringing any of its current cars to the Nurburgring. > the new roadster will literally lap it That seems unlikely. To literally lap the Taycan the new Roadster will need to achieve a 3:50 lap time. The current electric lap record is 6:05 held by the Volkswagen ID.R: [LINK] And the overall record is 5:19 held by the Porsche 919 Evo: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aSEQfqNYNAc",1],["SxdOUGdseq4",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["_YWMGuh15nE",1],["fwcl17Q0bpk",1],["plVk4NVIUh8",1],["1OEu9C51K2A",1]]},"oP3c1h8v2ZQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as a charming, funny way to see story structure and a source for the idea that plots have shapes.","q":{"x":"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks","i":25078089,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25078089,"author":"bmitc","month":"2020-11","story":"Kurt Vonnegut, The Art of Fiction No. 64 (1977)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Vonnegut's charming talk on story shapes","quote":"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks","text":"Vonnegut is really special. I always like coming back to his Shape of Stories talks: [VIDEO] Here's a longer one: [LINK] It's interesting to see the difference in him with age. He seems to giggle more as an older man."},{"id":4924582,"author":"baconner","month":"2012-12","story":"Programmer creates 800,000 books algorithmically, starts selling them on Amazon","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Vonnegut graphing emotional shapes of stories","quote":"It reminds me of this great video of Vonnegut graphing the emotional shape of stories.","text":"Agreed. It's a shame to think about all the interesting things that might be getting built instead. Programming a novel generator is a great example. What a fantastic challenge being overlooked. It reminds me of this great video of Vonnegut graphing the emotional shape of stories. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28426120,"author":"jonahx","month":"2021-09","story":"Chekhov's Gun","stance":"rec","why":"Vonnegut graphing famous story plots","quote":"Vonnegut drawing graphs of famous story plots","text":"Vonnegut drawing graphs of famous story plots: [VIDEO] I saw him do this bit in person in the early 90s and never forgot it."},{"id":9574719,"author":"meesterdude","month":"2015-05","story":"How to Write with Style (1985)","stance":"rec","why":"Vonnegut explains the shape of stories","quote":"I really love this video of his, on the shape of a story","text":"i read this and liked it, only after I scrolled up did I realize it was Kurt Vonnegut. Also, I really love this video of his, on the shape of a story [VIDEO] As a side note I found the formatting, or lack of formatting, interesting. It felt very much like a document, or a piece of paper, than a web page. That surprised me."},{"id":3482974,"author":"ZenPsycho","month":"2012-01","story":"Dear Tech Community, We Have A Communication Problem","stance":"rec","why":"Vonnegut explains story shapes","quote":"see this Kurt Vonnegut video where he explains story shapes","text":"It's not about logic vs. illogic. Logic is important to understand for a technical person- if they do not understand logic, their programs do not work, and their bugs cannot be fixed. But normal humans do not respond to logic. Normal humans respond to stories, and this is what tech people are really BAD at. You know who is good at telling stories? The entertainment industry, and politicians. What is a story? A linear sequence of cause and effect, with some kind of overall shape. see this Kurt Vonnegut video where he explains story shapes [VIDEO] If you can construct a compelling narrative- which is empathetic with the listener enough that you know they will be able to insert themselves into the story, and imagine that they are them main character, then you win- that one battle. If you try to program a person like they are a computer, by simply listing a series of what you feel are logical statements, you will come across as condescending, and self absorbed. You will fail. When you notice that they are not following you, and you interpret this as \"Oh they must be missing a library. var TCPIP = import(\"internets\").tcpip;\" you just make the problem worse."},{"id":33409787,"author":"CharlesW","month":"2022-10","story":"There are only five media stories","stance":"reference","why":"Vonnegut's story shapes: man in hole, boy gets girl","quote":"(Shapes: Man in Hole, Boy Gets Girl, Cinderella)","text":"\"Kurt Vonnegut on the Shapes of Stories\": [VIDEO] (Shapes: Man in Hole, Boy Gets Girl, Cinderella)"},{"id":27752780,"author":"bmitc","month":"2021-07","story":"No More Movies","stance":"reference","why":"One of Vonnegut's lectures presenting story shapes","quote":"There are a couple of lectures posted on YouTube.","text":"That's what I meant by presentations of his shapes of stories. There are a couple of lectures posted on YouTube. [VIDEO] [LINK] The thinking originated in his (rejected) master's thesis at the University of Chicago. Here are some photos of some of the graphs: [LINK] Even there, in an incomplete copy, there are seven unique story shapes. And there's a major difference between saying here are some shapes of some stories and here are the only story shapes."},{"id":13231763,"author":"roymurdock","month":"2016-12","story":"The Music of the Future","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for stories having shapes","quote":"","text":"> It's odd to consider music written like this - where snippets of a piece are written in all sorts of conflicting tempos, and then finally assembled during \"editing\" into something cohesive (although I guess this is how the Norwegian megapop songwriters kind of do this). Sample-heavy (hip-hop, house) musical genres have been doing this for a while. They'll take old 90 BPM records, sample em, pitch em/speed em up, or cut samples in such a way that they fit into new time signatures, keys, tempos, etc. The really good producers will take a bunch of samples and mash em up into a new work. I think a lot of popular art forms have an internal tempo, whether their creators are conscious of structuring the tempo or not. All stories (music, movies, books, plays) have \"shapes\" [1] which incorporate a component of tempo. It would be interesting to make it explicit though for an art form where language rather than music is the main form of expression. Musicals are a happy medium here, a completely time-based dinner party or play might end up more like Jeopardy and less like free-flowing art. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11989873,"author":"jfoutz","month":"2016-06","story":"The emotional arcs of stories are dominated by six basic shapes","stance":"reference","why":"Vonnegut lecture on story shapes","quote":"the talk is funny though, i saw him give a much longer version.","text":"It actually sounds a lot like the old Vonnegut talk. [VIDEO] non video description [LINK] the talk is funny though, i saw him give a much longer version."}],"r":[["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["-J_xL4IGhJA",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1],["pOmXiapfCs8",1],["v7Mt0GYHU9A",1]]},"UjZYMI1zB9s":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an engaging look at the life of pro Dota players and the early rise of esports, though one commenter notes it is somewhat promotional.","q":{"x":"I've enjoyed this documentary a lot.","i":32827311,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32827311,"author":"przefur","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Follows three Dota players before the first million dollar tournament","quote":"I've enjoyed this documentary a lot.","text":"Free to Play (2014) [1] I've enjoyed this documentary a lot. It follow three DOTA players and theirs life prior to 'The International', a first million dollar e-sport tournament. What caught my attention the most, is that, despite the three main characters (Dendi - Ukraine, Fear - USA, hyhy - Singapore) come from very different backgrounds and cultures, they treat the game almost the same - as a way of living. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31175980,"author":"CollinEMac","month":"2022-04","story":"Esports stars have shorter careers than NFL players","stance":"rec","why":"Valve documentary on pro Dota 2 players","quote":"it's kind of more of a commercial but it's still pretty good if you're interested in esports","text":"Valve made a documentary about pro DOTA 2 players a whopping 8 years ago (it doesn't seem that long ago at all). I bring it up because I remember at some point they mention that your reaction time drastically starts to decrease around the late 20's or so. I'd imagine that may also have something to do with it for fast-paced games at least. Here's the documentary (it's kind of more of a commercial but it's still pretty good if you're interested in esports) [VIDEO]"},{"id":9986830,"author":"nindalf","month":"2015-08","story":"A Pakistani Teen Gets Rich in E-Sports","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary following three Dota players","quote":"Free to Play, a documentary about 3 Dota players","text":"I've been watching this game for years, and I can say with confidence that Sumail is the real deal. He has skills that few can match. Right now he's playing the middle of the biggest tournament in e-sports history and he's about 10 games away from winning $6 mil (of which he'll keep $1m). The tournament, organised by Valve has a prize pool that's mostly crowd funded, so its likely to be a steady source of income for the top players of Dota 2 for a few years at least. I guess we'll know by next Saturday what Sumail's fate is. For more info * Free to Play, a documentary about 3 Dota players - [VIDEO] [75 mins] * Dota 2, a game that's free to play - [LINK] * The International tournament - [LINK] If anyone has any questions about this I'll be happy to answer :)"},{"id":7729547,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2014-05","story":"The International – Dota 2 Championships","stance":"rec","why":"Documents life of top-tier Dota players","quote":"Valve made a movie called \"Free to Play\" which documents what life is like for top-tier Dota players","text":"The prize pool for the upcoming Dota 2 tournament is now the largest prize pool ever in esports history, which is why I'm submitting it to HN. It's an interesting new phenomenon, because it seems to indicate that esports may finally be taking off. Valve basically crowdfunded their tournament prize pool. They started out by offering an initial $1.6M prize pool, but gave every dota 2 player the option of buying a \"compendium\" for $10, and each compendium increases the prize pool by $2.50. That means Valve makes $7.50 per compendium sold. I just ran the math, and as of right now there have been ($3,365,563 - $1,600,000)/$2.50 = 706,255 compendiums purchased. Since Valve earns $7.50 per compendium, that means they just made $5.3M in a couple days. And they're set to make even more next year, since the Dota 2 playerbase has some nice exponential growth: [LINK] I'm not sure there are any articles about this yet, because this news is so new. I wish I had a decent article to submit to HN instead of this link. But this is certainly newsworthy, so it's a matter of time before you start hearing other news sources talking about it. EDIT: If you're looking to see what a Dota tournament is like, here are two great matches from last year: Game 1 [LINK] Game 3 [LINK] The production quality is so good! EDIT2: Also, Valve made a movie called \"Free to Play\" which documents what life is like for top-tier Dota players: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7606825,"author":"xfalcox","month":"2014-04","story":"Steam Gauge: Steam’s most popular games","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary about professional Dota players","quote":"I really recommend watching this","text":"It runs natively on linux, it's free and bugs, but not source code, are hosted on github. I really recommend watching this [VIDEO] just make some popcorn before."}],"r":[["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["VPBH1eW28mo",1],["LLCF7vPanrY",1],["n1QEj09Pe6k",1],["_Qd3erAPI9w",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["8T6ClX-y2AE",1]]},"nYkdrAPrdcw":{"wy":"HN points to it as the original talk that explains the motivation behind React, Flux and Redux, and that sold some commenters on React.","q":{"x":"these talks, they are the ones that sold me on React","i":36147485,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36147485,"author":"yazaddaruvala","month":"2023-06","story":"React is 10 years old","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that convinced commenter to adopt React","quote":"Finally if you haven't before these talks, they are the ones that sold me on React","text":"I'll try one last time: > Sorry none of this makes any sense to me. If you're not familiar with the MVC variants (MVVM, MVP, MVI, MOVE)[0][1] we should start there. Next you should read through some common mistakes of Backbone.js like applications [LINK] and some criticisms of MVC on HackerNews in 2012[0]. Finally if you haven't before these talks, they are the ones that sold me on React: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [VIDEO] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":42334348,"author":"phailhaus","month":"2024-12","story":"React 19","stance":"rec","why":"Flux architecture versus MVC explanation","quote":"Facebook gave a great talk on Flux architecture vs MVC ten years ago that you should check out here","text":"If you have state changes within your render cycle, then you can't write pure functions anymore, you have a stateful class. And if you have a stateful class, then React can't control when you render, because it can't know when you update a variable. So the onus is on you to decide when your component needs to re-render. This is extremely easy to screw up. You will end up in scenarios where the UI does not match the underlying state, just because the two have gone out of sync. React inverts the MVC paradigm and says \"no, you don't get to decide when to render.\" So if you want to update state, React will manage that for you and queue up the render. With React, your UI and state literally cannot go out of sync (except for refs), and all the data dependencies are managed for you. Facebook gave a great talk on Flux architecture vs MVC ten years ago that you should check out here: [VIDEO] > what's the point of the setXXX() functions? Why not just make them constant explicitly Look at the code again: it is explicitly constant! `const [myState, setMyState] = useState(0)`. `myState` is a const, and will literally not change its value for the entire render cycle. There is no mutable state to manage, no race conditions. You just tell React what to render based on what the value is now, and React injects the current value for you. The first time, `myState` is 0 since that's the initial value. Every subsequent render, React will give you the latest value. You can pretend everything is con"},{"id":36130831,"author":"franciscop","month":"2023-05","story":"React is 10 years old","stance":"rec","why":"Flux talk explains philosophy behind Redux","quote":"something highly recommended to learn the philosophy behind Redux","text":"Exactly, this is the same as saying Redux has two methodologies, the \"setter system\" and the \"event system\"; since it wasn't that well documented initially doesn't mean that the setter way was ever recommended (it's not). In fact if you watched the Flux youtube talk, something highly recommended to learn the philosophy behind Redux, it was pretty clear. Edit: here's the video (from 10:20) [VIDEO]"},{"id":14709267,"author":"danesparza","month":"2017-07","story":"How to optimize React applications that use Redux","stance":"rec","why":"Facebook announcement videos that explain React and Flux","quote":"the original Facebook engineering announcement videos helped me understand React and Flux tremendously","text":"Redux is an implementation of Flux. I don't use Redux, but the original Facebook engineering announcement videos helped me understand React and Flux tremendously: [VIDEO] If you're talking about Redux specifically -- I agree with you. I think it's ugly and confusing."},{"id":7721261,"author":"rattray","month":"2014-05","story":"Flux Application Architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Flux concepts clearly, starting around the 10 minute mark","quote":"Jing does a terrific job explaining in my opinion.","text":"Video seems to start discussion of Flux just after the 10 minute mark. Direct Link: [VIDEO] EDIT: So far, the video is much more helpful to me in terms of bringing the concepts of Flux to life. Jing does a terrific job explaining in my opinion."},{"id":31983123,"author":"acemarke","month":"2022-07","story":"UIs are not pure functions of the model (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Date of Flux announcement versus React","quote":"was first announced a year later, in 2014","text":"> React is a child of the Flux architecture philosophy FWIW, this isn't actually the case. React was first announced in 2013: - [LINK] The \"Flux Architecture\" was first announced a year later, in 2014: - [VIDEO] React's original influences were things like Facebook's internal PHP-based UI definition layers: [LINK]"},{"id":26540957,"author":"franciscop","month":"2021-03","story":"Show HN: SimpleR State – simple state management for React","stance":"reference","why":"Flux architecture for controlled data flow","quote":"See from 10:20","text":"It formalizes how data is created, accessed and modified. Instead of multiple places trying to modify some specific data ad-hoc and with possible collisions/changes, now you have a mores strict way of dealing with data. Basically the Flux architecture. See from 10:20: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20631382,"author":"alexanderchr","month":"2019-08","story":"Facebook Hit by Apple’s Crackdown on Messaging Feature","stance":"reference","why":"Facebook's message-count bug as motivation for Flux","quote":"Interestingly one of the reasons they created react/flux was to fix it","text":"I don't think it's intentional. Facebook has had this bug for several years. Interestingly one of the reasons they created react/flux was to fix it: [VIDEO] (15 minute mark)"},{"id":8524547,"author":"liscovich","month":"2014-10","story":"Meteor hits 1.0","stance":"reference","why":"Flux intro criticizing two-way data binding at scale","quote":"two-way data binding creates a major challenge for scaling applications","text":"I wonder if the criticism that the Flux team at Facebook has directed at two-way data binding is also applicable to Meteor. Jing Chen is her intro of Flux ( [VIDEO] ) says that two-way data binding creates a major challenge for scaling applications because views can trigger changes in data, and data changes can trigger cascading changes in views, which makes it very difficult to predict the behavior of the app as a whole. Wouldn't Meteor face the same challenges? In other words, if you believe that the Flux design pattern (which involves a central dispatcher as the only entity capable of updating the data) is sound, shouldn't you stay away from Meteor's model when building large applications? Or am I missing something?"},{"id":41033416,"author":"Zanfa","month":"2024-07","story":"Web Crap Has Taken Control","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They still experience the exact same state inconsistency bug as described [0] during React announcement","text":"Apparently Facebook can't do it with React either. They still experience the exact same state inconsistency bug as described [0] during React announcement as rationale for using React. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30745336,"author":"Zanfa","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Why is the Instagram desktop website so unreliable?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the exact same unread messages counter bug from 2014 that they used as a case-study for the React/Flux announcement video[1]","text":"That would definitely explain why facebook.com still has had the exact same unread messages counter bug from 2014 that they used as a case-study for the React/Flux announcement video[1]. They just don't care. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fpbOEoRrHyU",1],["OoJsPvmFixU",1],["iCI3B5eT9NA",1],["nIoXOplUvAw",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1],["-DX3vJiqxm4",1]]},"7y1xJAVZxXg":{"wy":"HN cites it in threads on churches, tax exemption and religious fundraising, as the go-to explainer on seed faith televangelism.","q":{"x":"John Oliver did an excellent piece on a new crop of so-called \"seed faith\" televangelists","i":11646139,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":14293869,"author":"soheil","month":"2017-05","story":"John Oliver pleads with viewers to revive net neutrality fight","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of Oliver's incredible shows","quote":"Some of his shows were incredible a few years back","text":"Not sure why you're getting so many down votes, but I completely agree with you. Some of his shows were incredible a few years back, two that come to mind: Televangelists[1] and Debt Buyers[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11646139,"author":"downandout","month":"2016-05","story":"Homeless alone","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Exposes 'seed faith' televangelists who fleece poor people","quote":"John Oliver did an excellent piece on a new crop of so-called \"seed faith\" televangelists","text":"Many people believe in the mistaken \"no pain, no gain\" philosophy. People seem to believe that somehow the universe will recognize their sacrifice and reward them accordingly. John Oliver did an excellent piece on a new crop of so-called \"seed faith\" televangelists that have popped up to fleece poor people that believe in this philosophy [1]. The reality, of course, is the opposite. The most successful people I have known have all started their businesses while their lives were relatively stable. That doesn't mean they started off rich, but it does mean they weren't spending all of their time trying to figure out where they were going to sleep or where their next meal was coming from. These days, it is wholly unnecessary to quit your job or move to SV to make a startup happen, and doing so is counterproductive in almost all cases. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13906148,"author":"linuxkerneldev","month":"2017-03","story":"Engineer Converts Van into Laundromat, Offers Free Loads to Homeless","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of how proselytizing victimizes the poorest","quote":"I believe John Oliver had an excellent analysis","text":"> what's the economic return on proselytizing? Interesting question. I did a quick check. It looks to be incredibly profitable, at least for main stream religious organizations (which tend to also be tax free). The two top links for googling \"profit of church\" are: Eg: From CNN: \" 1. The Vatican Bank has $8 billion in assets The Vatican Bank, which has about $8 billion in assets, has often been at the center of scandal and corruption since it was founded in 1942. Pope Benedict began the process of cleaning the bank up, and Francis has continued that work. \" [LINK] \" THE Sydney Catholic archdiocese controls funds worth more than $1.2 billion and has regularly made multi-million dollar tax-free profits. The royal commission into child sex abuse heard the archdiocese banked surpluses of between $7.7 million and $44 million between 2004 and 2007, a period during which the Catholic church was aggressively defending a claim for $100,000 brought against it by former altar boy John Ellis. \" [LINK] I believe John Oliver had an excellent analysis of how proselytizing tends to victimize the poorest section of society. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10526391,"author":"snake117","month":"2015-11","story":"GoFundMe Gone Wild","stance":"rec","why":"Explains televangelists' 'seed faith' fundraising","quote":"check out this video from Last Week Tonight","text":"This reminded me of something similar: televangelists asking people for \"seed faiths\". If you haven't heard about this yet, check out this video from Last Week Tonight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15359794,"author":"stevenwoo","month":"2017-09","story":"Anthony Levandowski, self driving car whiz who fell from grace","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver mocking how easy it is to be a church","quote":"John Oliver famously made fun of this on a segment.","text":"There pretty much is no such thing as an illegitimate church in the USA. The IRS is too scared to touch that anymore. John Oliver famously made fun of this on a segment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10810669,"author":"ivanca","month":"2015-12","story":"Scientology says it's received $5.7M from Google in ad grants","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"All religion orgs are considered non-profit; the funny thing is that \"religion\" is purposefully vaguely defined by the IRS, related: Jhon Oliver televangelist bit [VIDEO] Organized religions including televangelists are one of the best business around, in a more cynical world I think there would be VCs and accelerators for creating new of them."},{"id":10096513,"author":"masklinn","month":"2015-08","story":"LLVM Foundation Granted 501(c)(3) Nonprofit Status","stance":"reference","why":"Lists requirements for a church per the IRS","quote":"the requirements for a church John Oliver listed during his show[1][2] are not exactly stringent","text":"Considering [LINK] is legal[0] and the requirements for a church John Oliver listed during his show[1][2] are not exactly stringent, it would probably work. [0] which you know because the phone number is 1-800-THIS-IS-LEGAL, it's a real phone number too: [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] straight outta the IRS: [LINK]"},{"id":16189877,"author":"ddebernardy","month":"2018-01","story":"List of oldest companies: Before 1300","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"some \"church\" related activities are hardly not-for-profits","text":"Churches are some of the wealthiest organizations out there: [LINK] And let's get serious, some \"church\" related activities are hardly not-for-profits: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15511063,"author":"jdmichal","month":"2017-10","story":"Why the trial by ordeal was actually an effective test of guilt","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the \"seed faith\" movement [1] needs to be nuked from orbit.","text":"Agree that we're pretty much in line. Your arguments echo those you might hear from a \"dissenter\" type in the \"spiritual but not religious\" movement [0]: [LINK] > ... personally I think that 'men of the cloth' are criminals of the worst order, they willingly and knowingly spread mental viruses that cause great harm to society. I think this is probably the place where we differ the most. I think the people you discuss certainly exist. I think there's also people in those positions for the right reasons. As usual with any potential stereotype, it's important to look at individuals as individuals. (That said, the \"seed faith\" movement [1] needs to be nuked from orbit.) [0] Not that I'm aligning you to the movement. I'm just noting the similarity. [1] [VIDEO] --- On the aside of my usage of \"DAE\", in this particular case it's a bit of a tongue-in-cheek reference along the lines of \"preaching to the choir\". That is, I don't think it would be a stretch to say that any argument presented as \"rationality vs dogma\" would have a population heavily leaning towards the former in this forum."}],"r":[["Z2DU0qLfPIY",1],["kBdfcR-8hEY",1]]},"QT0I-sdoSXU":{"wy":"It is the go-to short explainer HN links when voting system alternatives, MMP or German-style elections come up.","q":{"x":"CGP Grey has a great video on it","i":21327398,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":48592725,"author":"sfRattan","month":"2026-06","story":"Show HN: Gerrymandle - Daily puzzle game where you redraw electoral districts","stance":"rec","why":"Good explanation of German mixed-member proportional voting.","quote":"CGP Grey has a good YouTube video explaining it, probably better than I have here","text":"Germany uses mixed-member proportional representation. You cast two votes in each Federal election: one vote for a registered political party at the federal level and one vote for among a list of local candidates from those parties. The ballots famously have the phrase \"You have 2 voices/votes\" (\"Sie haben 2 Stimmen\") on the top [1]. The seats in the Bundestag are parceled out in proportion to shares of the national vote among federally recognized parties, and each party has a list of candidates who may or may not get a seat from that party's resulting allotment, based in part on the popularity of those candidates in their local list elections. The math of it is a bit more complicated than that, but it works quite well to completely sidestep the gerrymandering problem. The flipside is that parties must be registered federally to participate, and this gives the government a great deal of implicit power to exclude parties it doesn't like. CGP Grey has a good YouTube video explaining it, probably better than I have here [2]. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21327398,"author":"cgrealy","month":"2019-10","story":"Ranked-choice voting is on the ballot in New York City","stance":"rec","why":"Great explanation of New Zealand's MMP voting.","quote":"CGP Grey has a great video on it","text":"New Zealand uses Mixed Member Proportional (MMP) voting. IMO, it's the most representative voting system, precisely because it encourages smaller parties, and essentially forces coalitions. It's a bit complicated, but people are very used to it now. CGP Grey has a great video on it [VIDEO]"},{"id":18507505,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2018-11","story":"The Second Half of Watergate Was Bigger, Worse, and Forgotten by the Public","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant explainer on mixed-member proportional representation.","quote":"Relevant videos (IMO)","text":"Elections are supposed to be bloodless revolutions. If you can't effect change through the electoral system, then the non violent solution is to revolutionize how we vote. Relevant videos (IMO) First Past The Post Voting - [LINK] Range Voting - [LINK] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK] Alternative Vote - [LINK] Mixed-Member Proportional Representation - [VIDEO] Bonus video This video will make you angry - [LINK]"},{"id":16837832,"author":"namlem","month":"2018-04","story":"EU copyright proposal could undermine the use of Creative Commons licenses","stance":"rec","why":"Explains mixed member proportional representation well.","quote":"which is explained well in this video","text":"New Zealand has mixed member proportional representation, which is explained well in this video: [VIDEO] The US's political system is extremely antiquated, and is not at all appropriate for a post-industrial economy. We do have a few nice things though, like a very strong protection for speech. Arguably too strong right now though, as it gives corporations nearly unlimited rights to influence elections."},{"id":13697545,"author":"LoonyBalloony","month":"2017-02","story":"Robots Aren’t Killing the American Dream","stance":"rec","why":"One of several videos on alternatives to first past the post.","quote":"I'll post some videos from people smarter then me","text":"Elections were (IMO) meant to be bloodless revolutions. its just the 1% has captured both primary political parties. Also, the flawed first past the post electoral system discourages competition (IMO to keep it cheaper for the 1% to own the whole process). I'll post some videos from people smarter then me: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":42947927,"author":"kuschku","month":"2025-02","story":"They Thought They Were Free: The Germans, 1933-45 (1955)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for mixed-member proportional system.","quote":"","text":"(Note: I'm not an american) > Whether it’s Biden’s fault or not is irrelevant > again, whether that was Trump’s doing or not is irrelevant For democracy to work, you need voters with a good enough civics education to understand how the different parts of government interact, and a trustworthy media providing factual news so voters can understand the dilemmas their politicians are facing. If what you're saying is right, and American voters are unable or unwilling to understand how their vote and current events combine to cause certain outcomes, doesn't that mean democracy in the US cannot possibly work? What you're describing sounds closer to a popularity contest or a sports competition than politics. ________________________ Personally, I believe a major part of this issue is caused by the political system and media landscape of the US. In a two-party system as in the US, the legislative only represents half the population (as bipartisan efforts are rare), leading to resentment among the other half. In a mixed-member proportional system[1], as in Germany, New Zealand and many Scandinavian countries, you end up with many more different parties in parliament (Germany currently has 8 [2]). For any law to pass, you need multiple parties to work together, which allows more voters to be represented. Similarly, while in Germany newspapers and public broadcasters still provide high-quality news (less than 20% of the population distrust the public broadcasters and major newspapers as "},{"id":42948032,"author":"kuschku","month":"2025-02","story":"They Thought They Were Free: The Germans, 1933-45 (1955)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains mixed-member-proportional voting.","quote":"mixed-member-proportional voting ( )","text":"Or you could switch to a different voting system which allows more than two parties, allowing people to make more nuanced choices? There are even voting systems which are perfectly representative (as a popular vote would be) and ensure all states get a voice (the goal of the electoral college). Say, a bicameral system. One chamber allocating each state a fixed number of voices, ensuring each state has a voice, and a second chamber with mixed-member-proportional voting ( [VIDEO] ), which allows perfectly proportional majorities in this chamber while giving each district a representative (in the process also fixing gerrymandering). In fact, it was actually this system, designed by the US, that the allied countries chose for Germany after World War II."},{"id":27739773,"author":"dijit","month":"2021-07","story":"Indiscriminate messaging and chatcontrol: Last chance to protest","stance":"reference","why":"Explains D'Hondt-style proportional allocation used in the EU.","quote":"CGP Grey did some videos on these","text":"> the EU is not democratic in the first place EU Election system is _literally_ one of the fairest systems in existence[0] ensuring that every party get some measure of proportional representation. Compared to English First Past the Post (minority rule, and 2 party systems) or Scotlands Single-transferrable vote (which is still winner take all); but D'Hondts method means that people can be safe in the knowledge they'll be represented and it's proportional to the input. It's not \"perfect\" but it's _literally_ the best humans have come up with. [0]: [LINK] [0.1]: [LINK] CGP Grey did some videos on these: FPTP (England): [LINK] STV (Scotland): [LINK] D'Hondt-esque (EU): [VIDEO]"},{"id":19147100,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2019-02","story":"A tiny Swiss company thinks it can help stop climate change","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as an alternative voting system.","quote":"","text":"Reform the electoral system to achieve the representation we all deserve. The electoral system we use now: [LINK] Alternatives: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] We can start introducing these to everyone at the state and local level to build momentum for electoral reform at the federal level. A good old fashioned general strike until we get what we need from our \"representatives\"."},{"id":19212613,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2019-02","story":"U.S. Supreme Court Puts Limits on Police Power to Seize Private Property","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among voting system explainers.","quote":"Mixed-Member Proportional Representation -","text":"How important is this privilege if I can maliciously control how you vote? Now imagine the good we can do with real electoral reform (Democrats idea of repealing citizens United is a meager start, but weak lip service to real reform) What we have now - First Past The Post Voting - [LINK] Range Voting - [LINK] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK] Alternative Vote - [LINK] Mixed-Member Proportional Representation - [VIDEO] Electoral reform is just step 1, something we can all come together for. Something no one could possibly be against. How about if I control who can be voted for? Of course it matters - [LINK] bonus video: This video will make you angry - [LINK]"},{"id":18240473,"author":"thelasthuman","month":"2018-10","story":"China May Have $5.8T in Hidden Debt with ‘Titanic’ Risks","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among explainers of voting systems.","quote":"Mixed-Member Proportional Representation -","text":"The real problem is both main stream political parties don't represent around 45% of the country. First Past The Post Voting- [LINK] Range Voting- [LINK] Single Transferable Vote - [LINK] Alternative Vote - [LINK] Mixed-Member Proportional Representation - [VIDEO]"},{"id":6635744,"author":"TheLegace","month":"2013-10","story":"9/11 Suspects Can’t Mention being Tortured during Trial because Classified","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for mixed proportional representation platform.","quote":"","text":"I agree it's the problem in Canada as well. For the first time a third party was able to come ahead of one of the two parties, but this had disastrous effects as it provided the opposition party a majority government and overall weakened the power of the center-left and left. And right now I have to decide if my vote will mean anything if I vote for the third party. The third party's platform will be mixed proportional representation. Which I hope we see one day so maybe this won't be such an issue any more. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4994408,"author":"dkhenry","month":"2013-01","story":"Hollingsworth for US Senate 2014, implementing direct democracy","stance":"reference","why":"Link for mixed-member proportional system.","quote":"","text":"I think we could come together with both Ideas and make a system that works. I like some aspects of your plan , but I would want to see a few modifications. Your extending into voting systems as well and I don't necessarily think your doing it the best way , but i _love_ the idea your putting forth. Edit: So i realise why I like aspects of your proposal. Because it has a few of the principles of the Mixed-Member proportional [0] System mixed into it. I like MMP I really like it. I think that combined with a radical expansion of the number of representatives ( I think we should be closer to 100k then 500k, maybe as low as 50k ) would allow us to do many amazing things as a country. Also I would toss this wrinkle in. MMP is done at a state level and only state level political parties are recognized, and they are now official. They must follow rules to elect their leadership. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4839507,"author":"mmphosis","month":"2012-11","story":"Voting is a Sham Mathematically Speaking","stance":"reference","why":"Explains mixed member proportional representation.","quote":"Mixed Member Proportional (MMP) Representation solves some of the problems.","text":"Mixed Member Proportional (MMP) Representation solves some of the problems. [VIDEO] The problem with MMP is when the parties choose the ranking of their list of representatives. I think it would be even better if rather than use a party generated list, instead the representatives are determined by people's votes."}],"r":[["l8XOZJkozfI",2],["e3GFG0sXIig",2],["VNvzZfsC13o",1],["ovJcsL7vyrk",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1]]},"eqQBubilSXU":{"wy":"HN considers it an outstanding explanation of why drivers miss motorcycles and cyclists, linked in threads on visibility and junction crashes.","q":{"x":"This is an outstanding vid on the whole subject illustrating the problem and some avoidance methods.","i":21157222,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21157222,"author":"imglorp","month":"2019-10","story":"Lethal memory fail: Why drivers see, and then forget motorcyclists","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Illustrates the visibility problem and avoidance methods","quote":"This is an outstanding vid on the whole subject illustrating the problem and some avoidance methods.","text":"There's a second factor at work beyond memory - it's the human visual perceptive system. Bikes are not always moving across the visual field, and they aren't fitting the pattern of a bigger single-color smooth object so it's effectively camouflage. This is an outstanding vid on the whole subject illustrating the problem and some avoidance methods. This should be taught in rider training but it's not (US). [VIDEO]"},{"id":16114829,"author":"imglorp","month":"2018-01","story":"A Type of Road Junction that Kills Cyclists","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains perception psychology behind drivers failing to see motorcyclists","quote":"this excellent video goes a little further into some perception psychology about camouflage","text":"This is also of high interest to motorcyclists who even have a name for the phenomena: SMIDSY (Sorry, Mate I Didn't See You). OP was focused on the A pillar and some other effects, while this excellent video goes a little further into some perception psychology about camouflage and how we perceive moving objects across backgrounds. You can ignore the motorcycle stuff but the explanation is enlightening. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28271176,"author":"IgorPartola","month":"2021-08","story":"A DIY E-bike Conversion on the Cheap","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how little visible cyclists are despite high-viz gear","quote":"It’ll show you just how little visibility you’ll have","text":"Have you ever seen videos of how invisible you look despite all the high viz stuff? In some cases it becomes urban camo. Take a look at this video, especially at 2:30 or so. It’ll show you just how little visibility you’ll have: [VIDEO] Your best bet is to have all your clothing match your bike, Power Rangers style, to increase your chances. And lots and lots of very bright lights. One little handlebar mounted light is going to look like a reflection or a flashlight."},{"id":24352648,"author":"kingaillas","month":"2020-09","story":"Airline pilots landing at LAX report “a guy in jetpack” flying alongside them","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the optical illusion behind SMIDSY and countermeasures","quote":"That video covers the optical illusion that leads to it and what motorcycles can do.","text":">it's always \"Sorry mate I didn't see you\". Ah, the SMIDSY: [VIDEO] That video covers the optical illusion that leads to it and what motorcycles can do."},{"id":36389090,"author":"imglorp","month":"2023-06","story":"Invisibility Training for Motorcyclists [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Complementary material on the SMIDSY problem with a visibility manoeuvre","quote":"Complimentary material about the SMIDSY problem (sorry, mate, I didn't see you) from someone else","text":"Complimentary material about the SMIDSY problem (sorry, mate, I didn't see you) from someone else, including a visibility manoeuver f9 doesn't mention. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["EOwxxsPaogY",1],["wM8Xli2KTzI",1]]},"y7HrM-fk_Rc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a free, serious introduction to digital and computational photography from a professor who helped build the early Pixel cameras.","q":{"x":"It's short and fun, I recommend it to all HN readers.","i":43985557,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48040037,"author":"solarkraft","month":"2026-05","story":"What makes a good smartphone camera?","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture series on computational photography by a Pixel camera pioneer.","quote":"interesting lecture series about computational photography by Marc Levoy, who worked on the earlier Pixel cameras","text":"To expand on the HDR example: There’s this interesting lecture series about computational photography by Marc Levoy, who worked on the earlier Pixel cameras: [VIDEO] From what I remember, the core thesis is “take a lot of pictures and take the best parts”, which works for a surprising number of cases."},{"id":43985557,"author":"asafira","month":"2025-05","story":"What is HDR, anyway?","stance":"rec","why":"A serious introduction to digital photography from Stanford course.","quote":"if you want a more serious introduction to digital photography, I recommend the lectures by Marc Levoy from his Stanford course","text":"I did my PhD in Atomic, Molecular, and Optical (AMO) physics, and despite \"optical\" being part of that I realized midway that I didn't know enough about how regular cameras worked! It didn't take very long to learn, and it turned out to be extremely important in the work I did during the early days at Waymo and later at Motional. I wanted to pass along this fun video from several years ago that discusses HDR: [LINK] . It's short and fun, I recommend it to all HN readers. Separately, if you want a more serious introduction to digital photography, I recommend the lectures by Marc Levoy from his Stanford course: [VIDEO] . I believe he runs his own group at Adobe now after leading a successful effort at Google making their pixel cameras the best in the industry for a couple of years. (And then everyone more-or-less caught up, just like with most tech improvements in the history of smartphones)."},{"id":34596644,"author":"sys32768","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Stanford CS 178 digital photography lecture series recorded at Google.","quote":"one of 18 videos representing lectures on digital photography, from a version of my Stanford course CS 178","text":"Marc Levoy's Lectures on Digital Photography: [VIDEO] >This is one of 18 videos representing lectures on digital photography, from a version of my Stanford course CS 178 that was recorded at Google in Spring 2016."},{"id":24797531,"author":"deadmutex","month":"2020-10","story":"Sony cameras can now be used as webcams for Mac","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent course where Levoy explains why camera makers shunned computational photography.","quote":"this excellent course shared by Google","text":"> No google pixel / iOS quality level HDR & night sight modes Marc Levoy (the brains behind Google's amazing Computational Photography technology), said that he had gone to the Camera companies before and they rejected his proposals. Camera companies were too afraid that someone was going to take a photo, and it would end up looking bad, and result in people returning their camera. Source: Somewhere in this excellent course shared by Google: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21259761,"author":"johnmaguire2013","month":"2019-10","story":"Google Announces Pixel 4","stance":"rec","why":"Free Stanford lecture series on digital photography by a Google researcher.","quote":"Marc Levoy, who spoke at the Google event today is a Professor Emeritus at Stanford","text":"The star tracking feature of the astrophotography mode definitely has me interested. I'm disappointed to see the continued lack of battery capacity on the smaller phone though. While I'd love a larger battery, I'm one of the unfortunate ones whose small hands can already barely reach across my Pixel 2's phone screen -- much less the top section. I'm also incredibly excited about the face unlocking feature -- I owned a Pixel Xs for about a week before returning it, and face unlock was my absolute favorite feature. It's very seamless especially compared to fingerprint unlock. As an aside, Marc Levoy, who spoke at the Google event today is a Professor Emeritus at Stanford, and has a great series titled \"Lectures on Digital Photography\" available for free on Youtube: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bkQJdaGGVM8",1],["qJEWOTZnFeg",1],["7FeqF1-Z1g0",1],["PGv5BqNL164",1]]},"Eghl19elKk8":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, clear introduction to these two concepts for making sense of current politics.","q":{"x":"You will love this video (12m)","i":39821896,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":43648209,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2025-04","story":"Trump temporarily drops tariffs to 10% for most countries","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short talk on politics of eternity","quote":"You should watch this (12min).","text":"[VIDEO] You should watch this (12min). You just expressed the \"politics of eternity.\" You should check out Timothy Snyder in general to see what historians are saying about our current times."},{"id":39821896,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2024-03","story":"Ask HN: What is a HN unpopular opinion that you hold?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains politics of inevitability and democracy as action","quote":"You will love this video (12m)","text":"Timothy Snyder, author of On Tyranny , likes to restate the idea of US exceptionalism as both the politics of inevitability and democracy is not something you are, it is something you do . Many people wrongly think that democracy is an inevitable result of capitalism, but if that's true then there is nothing extra you have to do to remain democratic other than participate in capitalism. If freedom is an inevitable result of being an American, then there is nothing you have to do to be free as long as you are American. If peace between two countries is an inevitable result of both countries \"having a McDonalds\", there is nothing extra you need to do to achieve peace except trade. If you want freedom, peace, or governance that represents you, you can't just be something, you actually have to hold beliefs and conduct yourself in a way conducive to those outcomes. You will love this video (12m): [VIDEO] (edited to make some statements more correct)"},{"id":22904666,"author":"jadbox","month":"2020-04","story":"Assuming Responsibility for Time","stance":"rec","why":"Pairs with article on time and history","quote":"I'd recommend pairing this article with this video with Timothy Snyder","text":"I'd recommend pairing this article with this video with Timothy Snyder on Politics of Eternity, Politics of Inevitability: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43607923,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2025-04","story":"U.S. stock futures tumble indicating another plummet on Wall Street","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The jury is still out on whether what you are saying is hyperbole The president of the Heritage Foundation said “we are in the process of the second American Revolution, which will remain bloodless if the left allows it to be.” [LINK] -- [LINK] This is a direct quote from a major institution behind the current administration that is well known to be a major force for choosing judicial candidates for the GOP. I understand it feels like hyperbole, but that's not because it's hyperbolic, it's because it's hard to accept. Accepting it would cause a lot of grief and grief is disabling. Accepting it creates a sense of responsibility or a sense of helplessness, neither of which feel good. Accepting it means that we aren't just in a bad situation, we are in a literal emergency. It means action is necessary now. A secretary of defense and a chairman of the joint chiefs of staff have said he is a threat to the constitution and the country: [LINK] [LINK] 4 prominent republicans have Sieg Heiled including musk and bannon: [LINK] [LINK] Here's the head of the OMB saying he wants to put government workers in trauma: [LINK] [LINK] These are primary sources... This isn't hyperbole. > We can't assume the worst-case outcome despite the serious danger of that outcome should it occur. We don't have to assume the scenario to ask if it is possible. What prevents that scenario? That is the real problem. Without rule of law, there is nothing that stops it. The problem isn't that one particular sto"},{"id":17949870,"author":"ajuc","month":"2018-09","story":"I survived the Warsaw ghetto. Here are the lessons I’d like to pass on","stance":"reference","why":"Defines politics of inevitability and eternity","quote":"See \"politics of inevitability\" and \"politics of eternity\", as defined by Timothy Snyder.","text":"> People like to imagine that there is some kind of corrective force to history, some invisible hand that will, inevitably, push things into balance and restore normality. See \"politics of inevitability\" and \"politics of eternity\", as defined by Timothy Snyder. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["9tocssf3w80",1],["d0nERTFo-Sk",1],["GTQnarzmTOc",1]]},"N-yALPEpV4w":{"wy":"HN commenters credit it with changing their minds on nuclear power and cite it for the downsides of renewables.","q":{"x":"it's what changed my mind and therefore in my opinion the best delivery of the argument I'm aware of.","i":21112217,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21112217,"author":"0xfaded","month":"2019-09","story":"Cascade reactions use nanoparticles to convert CO2 to complex molecules","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues solar and wind are low density; favors nuclear.","quote":"it's what changed my mind and therefore in my opinion the best delivery of the argument I'm aware of.","text":"I recently watched a TED talk which changed my opinion of renewables from \"maybe\" to \"no\". Basically he argues that solar and wind are low density energy sources that require significant resources to capture, which all become waste after 25 years. Meanwhile we have nuclear which provides consistent base load, and for very good reasons we have gotten good at containing all the waste/pollution produced. I'm now of the opinion we should be on a sprint towards fusion, investing heavily in other nuclear technologies, e.g. thorium, along the way. Sorry to leave a video link, but it's what changed my mind and therefore in my opinion the best delivery of the argument I'm aware of. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19875341,"author":"mikemotherwell","month":"2019-05","story":"In Australia, Coal Remains King","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Strengthened the commenter's pro-nuclear view.","quote":"really changed my mind on this.","text":"> Australia, with it's plentiful sunshine and wide open spaces It's funny, but that sounds an AWFUL lot like the Terra Nullius argument used to disposes Indigenous people of their land. Australia's \"wide open spaces\" are home to flora and fauna that are unique and worth preserving. The idea we'd want vast swaths of solar destroying the natural environment - one that is unique and wonderful - is odd to me. [VIDEO] really changed my mind on this. I was pro-nuclear before seeing it, but am even more so after."},{"id":24149665,"author":"kinjba1","month":"2020-08","story":"Air pollution is worse than we thought","stance":"rec","why":"Downsides of renewables such as land use and storage.","quote":"Here are a couple TED talks showing the downside of renewable energy","text":"No energy source [that we've discovered so far] is perfect. All have downsides. Sadly, living in denial of that fact is more popular than ever, but that's a separate topic. Here are a couple TED talks showing the downside of renewable energy - such as the enormous land usage and reliable energy storage needed to compete with nuclear or fossil. Why renewables can’t save the planet [VIDEO] A reality check on renewables [LINK]"},{"id":24748079,"author":"corn13read","month":"2020-10","story":"We Need a Nuclear New Deal, Not a Green New Deal","stance":"reference","why":"Supports thorium and liquid salt nuclear.","quote":"Liquid Salt and Thorium are the best wins we have today until we figure out fusion.","text":"Liquid Salt and Thorium are the best wins we have today until we figure out fusion. [VIDEO] Simple facts."},{"id":19566441,"author":"nestorD","month":"2019-04","story":"The Third Phase of Clean Energy Will Be Most Disruptive Yet","stance":"reference","why":"Gas companies promote renewables since gas complements them.","quote":"gas compagny such as Total are actually promoting renewable energies","text":"Watching the following TED talk, I was surprised to discover that gas compagny such as Total are actually promoting renewable energies because they currently need to be complemented and gas is the main solution used for that purpose. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["tJoe_daP0DE",1],["E0W1ZZYIV8o",1]]},"0rR9IaXH1M0":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny but surprisingly persuasive outsider take on gun control, mainly in gun-related threads.","q":{"x":"I recommend listening to what Jim Jefferies had to say about gun control.","i":16750311,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16750311,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2018-04","story":"Google maps shows location of San Bruno shooting","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Comedic but convincing arguments on gun control.","quote":"I recommend listening to what Jim Jefferies had to say about gun control.","text":"Whenever I hear that brought up as an argument, I recommend listening to what Jim Jefferies had to say about gun control. Despite Jim Jefferies being a comedian and phrasing his arguments in a comical, comedic way, I've yet to see anyone give a convincing counter-argument, especially to the \"you're bringing a gun to a drone fight\" part. Jim Jefferies on Gun Control, part 1: [VIDEO] Jim Jefferies on Gun Control, part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":31514884,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2022-05","story":"Mental illness, mass shootings, and the politics of American firearms (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Australian comedian's arguments on US gun control.","quote":"Jim Jefferies (an Australian comedian living in the States) on gun control","text":"Round and round we go... As a foreigner, it's appalling to see every excuse imaginable trotted out every time this happens. Don't think you're fooling anyone. You're just making things up on the spot for the sake of pretending to have a rational argument, like an intelligent adult would in your place. But I will eat my shorts if you personally have spent even an hour doing the research before making statements about \"what this would do\", or \"that wouldn't work\", or whatever. I don't mean clicking \"like\" on a Facebook post by a fellow NRA member. Mean literally reading through scientific publications, or looking at statistics published by third parties that are unaffiliated with any United States organisation. Or just Googling to see what the other 96% of the human race does with guns and laws. \"\"No Way to Prevent This\", Says Only Nation Where This Regularly Happens\": [LINK] Jim Jefferies (an Australian comedian living in the States) on gun control: Part #1: [VIDEO] Part #2: [LINK] Can you counter every single one of his points ? Really? With facts ? That you can link to in a scientific paper that wasn't paid for by a gun lobby in the states? 19 children just died. Please take this a tiny bit seriously and don't just click the reply link to blurt out something in a public forum that can be trivially shown to be false..."},{"id":12947505,"author":"ux-app","month":"2016-11","story":"Ask HN: Have you done / plan to do any basic “prepping” (tinned food etc)","stance":"rec","why":"Honest comedic take on guns and violence.","quote":"And finally here's a pretty honest take on the matter from a comedian.","text":"No worries :) > seems a bit disingenuous to highlight suicide, while at the same time not including injury, rape, or loss of property that result from home invasions. Nothing disingenuous about it. In fact, statistically speaking, the situation is even worse. Introducing a gun into an already violent encounter significantly increases the risk of injury and death. There is tonnes of evidence to support this. Here is one, very specific study that indirectly confirms exactly this. [LINK] And here's another fascinating study PREDICTORS OF FIREARM USE AND EFFECTS OF WEAPONRY ON VICTIM INJURY IN VIOLENT CRIME [LINK] It's a long read, but well worth it if you are really interested in what the literature has to say about the often surprising ways in which guns affect the outcomes of violent crime. And finally here's a pretty honest take on the matter from a comedian. NSFW for language [VIDEO] The humor may not be to your taste, but the points made are fair"},{"id":11282291,"author":"marklgr","month":"2016-03","story":"The Next Front in the New Crypto Wars: WhatsApp","stance":"reference","why":"Comedian's point about rationalizing gun ownership.","quote":"guns are fun, people enjoy having them and shooting","text":"> \"decide what I want first and rationalize it later\" Incidentally, this is Jim Jefferies point[1] about guns: guns are fun, people enjoy having them and shooting, so they rationalize with evil governements threats from centuries ago. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30466218,"author":"EdwardDiego","month":"2022-02","story":"We're choosing Rust, and not Go, C++, or Node.js","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"fuck off, I like Rust","text":"Yeah, I feel like you could just rephrase Jim Jefferie's classic comment on gun control[0] and replace this entire blog post with \"fuck off, I like Rust\". [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"5Dw3aKbw5Wo":{"wy":"HN points to it in Atmos and spatial audio threads as a knowledgeable critique of why the format rarely delivers.","q":{"x":"I found this video by Benn Jordan very enlightening about the whole Atmos nonsense","i":37337578,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36675218,"author":"philovivero","month":"2023-07","story":"Brian Eno Albums Available in Dolby Atmos and Spatial for the First Time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Audio engineer explains why Atmos rarely works well","quote":"This is a smart, motivated audio engineer talking about Atmos.","text":"Someone else posted this down in the bottom of a comment thread somewhere, but I think it deserves to be at the top level: [VIDEO] This is a smart, motivated audio engineer talking about Atmos. Quick summary: it's awful when it works perfectly, and it almost never works perfectly, in fact, it rarely works at all. Good luck trying to get Atmos working, and even if this was a completely open standard, would you really bother?"},{"id":39096160,"author":"spamizbad","month":"2024-01","story":"Apple Music will now pay 10% higher royalties to artists for Spatial Audio music","stance":"rec","why":"Explains situation and current limitations of Atmos","quote":"Benn Jordan's video from earlier last year does a good job of explaining the situation","text":"I imagine it's seen as a value-add by Apple and Dolby. Studios are forking off massive sums to build-out Atmos rigs. A savvy artists may ask for it because it helps you on Apple Music. As for the consumer experience, I feel like Benn Jordan's video from earlier last year does a good job of explaining the situation and its current limitations: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37337578,"author":"TonyTrapp","month":"2023-08","story":"Dolby Atmos will use your TV to expand living room speaker setups","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Atmos isn't worth it","quote":"I found this video by Benn Jordan very enlightening about the whole Atmos nonsense","text":"I found this video by Benn Jordan very enlightening about the whole Atmos nonsense: \"Gaslighting Your Fans w/ Dolby ATMOS\" [VIDEO] TL;DW: It's just not worth it. (but do watch it to understand why)"},{"id":36680080,"author":"Intermernet","month":"2023-07","story":"Brian Eno Albums Available in Dolby Atmos and Spatial for the First Time","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting critique of Atmos by Benn Jordan","quote":"Interesting video from Benn Jordan at","text":"You can get very cheap digital to digital audio hardware that can convert any multi-channel audio stream (DRM or otherwise) to straight multi-channel PCM. I personally, occasionally, use an RME Babyface Pro (not cheap) for this purpose, but there are many options available. You can even do this purely in software. On Windows or Linux look at Jack. On MacOS I guarantee it's available. As an aside, Atmos is generally lossy. It's actually a terrible format for music distribution. It's only popular due to marketing. Interesting video from Benn Jordan at [VIDEO]"},{"id":36682436,"author":"harry8","month":"2023-07","story":"Brian Eno Albums Available in Dolby Atmos and Spatial for the First Time","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[LINK] tl;dr New surround tech with added height channels. Great. I can imagine there could be uses. Like any new tech before you've analysed it, could be good, could be trash. Additional surround height channels cannot possibly be as good as 2 channels, properly recorded and mixed to drive stereo headphones for generating 3D sound. This is key. You can't beat headphones for 3D sound. If you can't understand why then the conclusions which follow will seem even more incomprehensible, grandparents involvement or no. Best way to meet that impasse head on is to go get your headphones and find some older 3D audio and listen to its quality. Or make some, like I did in the 1980s. It's really great stuff, good fun and can be used creatively and with elegance. I don't doubt for a second Eno could do something worthwhile with it. And could have in the 1980s on the 2 tracks of an LP while instructing the listener to use headphones had he so chosen. Adding height channels to surround cannot make better 3d audio than you could achieve with headphones. So I'm putting Eno down because this is a marketing claim that is garbage. He could have done top notch, first rate, headphone 3D sound on his albums at any time in the past, did he even try? Has he said? He's releasing stuff using $some_new_tech (atmos) for which you need to buy a bunch of kit and justifying it claiming 3D is wonderful. And it is! And he never needed that new tech to take advantage of it and could very, very easily, right n"}],"r":[["UiS27feX8o0",1],["XJ97iXQrqzw",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["qybUFnY7Y8w",1],["9pOiOhxujsE",1]]},"FfDa8tR25dk":{"wy":"Recommended as a moving, first-person account of the people who dealt with the worst of the Chernobyl cleanup.","q":{"x":"Chernobyl 3828 is about the people","i":11502048,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11502048,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-04","story":"Famous Photo of Chernobyl's Most Radioactive Material Was a Selfie","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary about the people who cleaned the Chernobyl roof.","quote":"Chernobyl 3828 is about the people","text":"One of the most impressive documentaries I've ever seen is \"Chernobyl 3828\"[1]. It is about the 3828 \"liquidators\" (cleanup crew) - euphemistically called the \"bio robots\" - that had to clean up the worst section of roof above the reactor. Maximum allowed dose was two minutes exposure to that section the roof. It has some of the same footage as the sequence in your link to t=3430, but there is a key difference: Chernobyl 3828 is about the people . The psychology of the cleanup was complicated. Some people ran while others faced the danger and accepted a \"red badge of courage\" because if they didn't, someone else would have to go in their place . [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20109169,"author":"effie","month":"2019-06","story":"HBO's Chernobyl may yet become the highest rated TV Show ever","stance":"rec","why":"Personal-viewpoint account of cleanup from Starodumov and Bordakov","quote":"There are some great documents on youtube from these different points of view","text":"I understand and partly agree. The geeky stuff was very limited and the narrative and exposition was somewhat dramatized. I am also not sure if the real characters really acted towards the state in such a brave unassailable way as depicted... Still I think the show adopts a valid viewpoint and gives a powerful and needed message about a corrupt totalitarian system. But you are right that telling the story from the point of view of organizational challenges or from a point of view of a real person putting their life in danger in taking part in the effort would be very interesting too. There are some great documents on youtube from these different points of view, e.g. the story told by Starodumov and Bordakov: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13629036,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2017-02","story":"High radiation readings at Fukushima’s No. 2 reactor complicate robot probe","stance":"rec","why":"Ukrainian documentary on roof cleanup by two-minute-shift liquidators","quote":"Anybody that isn't familiar with the \"liquidators\" should watch Chernobyl 3828","text":"> Chernobyl-style \"bio-robot\" \"liquidators\" Anybody that isn't familiar with the \"liquidators\" should watch Chernobyl 3828 , a Ukrainian documentary about the cleanup of the worst part of the roof with comments by the people currently working their two-minute shifts as a \"bio-robot\". [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20061589,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2019-05","story":"They Fictionalize Nuclear Disasters Like Chernobyl Because They Kill So Few","stance":"reference","why":"Firsthand accounts and footage of roof cleanup liquidators","quote":"In their own words[1]","text":"The people that were very unfortunately necessary that deserve to be remembered are the 3828 \"liquidators\" that cleaned up the radioactive graphite (mixed with bits of reactor fuel/waste) that covered the roof above the reactor. In their own words[1]: > [the remote control bulldozer] has overridden a graphite block with it's right caterpillar track, and now was sitting on 12,000 Roentgen fragment. [...] Starodumov was already thinking about something else... what all of us have already known: robots failed, and the extra radioactive zones will have to be cleaned by men. > The shift lasted only two minutes. [...] Here, the unthinkable background radiation of nearly 8000 Roentgens. 20 meters away from us is the naked muzzle of the destroyed reactor. [...] With each succeeding group, with each fragment removed, the background radiation will reduce. This means the risk for the next group will already be lower. [1] (this includes footage of the cleanup) [VIDEO]"},{"id":10942915,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2016-01","story":"2015 “smashed” 2014’s global temperature record","stance":"reference","why":"Liquidators' own account of the roof cleanup.","quote":"Their story - in their own words[3] - is something I will never forget.","text":"> There is still a lot of that material I never said there wasn't. The cleanup is ongoing and will take a very long time, but it still isn't \"destroyed for good\". At least the caesium eventually goes away. The Pb, Hg, and As in the 1.1 billion gallons of fly ash slurry that the Tennessee Valley Authority spilled[0] over homes and into the Emory and Clinch rivers doesn't have a half life. > economy and agriculture The tsunami also did incredible damage to the economy and agriculture of Japan. > Can you claim without flinching that this will never ever happen? Absolutely. Even the idiotic RBMK design can be run safely after being modified to fix the void coefficient. I suggest actually reading my link[1] above, which explain in great detail the problems in the design of that reactor. Even with those problems, it took incredible effort[2] to trigger the steam explosion. > 'heroes of Chernobyl' I'm give a lot of respect and thanks to those that had to deal with Chernobyl's immediate aftermath and the 600,000 liquidators that were part of the cleanup afterwords. In particular, the 3828 people that cleaned up the worst section of roof in two minute shifts. Their story - in their own words[3] - is something I will never forget. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10725202,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-12","story":"Irradiated: The hidden legacy of 70 years of atomic weaponry","stance":"reference","why":"Tribute to liquidators who cleaned the Chernobyl roof.","quote":"I thank the 3828 liquidators that cleaned up the worst of the Chernobyl roof, two minutes at a time.","text":"I thank the 3828 liquidators that cleaned up the worst of the Chernobyl roof, two minutes at a time. [VIDEO] > died for nothing While that is certainly true, it's worth remembering that most are still alive, though many are now having trouble in the healthcare system thanks to poor CCCP records (or finding those records now)."},{"id":10132508,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-08","story":"Where Are Chernobyl’s Children?","stance":"reference","why":"Related Chernobyl material in a long nuclear discussion.","quote":"","text":"Ever since it became easier to get into the Chernobyl area, there has been an increase in people trying to dramatize it. I suggest watching this short video with Tom Scott, about his recent visit, where they try to inject a bit of reality into the situation: a lot of looted/stripped buildings that have stood abandoned for decades. It's impressive, but doesn't have a lot to do with radiation or the reactor. [LINK] There are certainly a lot of areas where you shouldn't linger for extended periods of time. The reactor accident (and how the situation was handled afterwords[1]) was absolutely terrible. That said, it is important to remember that - even accounting for the Chernobyl disaster (and the utterly insignificant radiological [3] disaster in Fukushima) - nuclear power is still the only viable option if we want to have energy in the future, as it is the cleanest . Everything else pollutes more and only a few technologies even come close to making enough power. Unfortunately, the Hiroshima Syndrome[4] is epidemic, and the current wave of \"Chernobyl disaster theatre\" is only making these fears worse. We need to find better ways to educate people and counter the decades of misinformation and fear. The HN crowd like to \"disrupt\" things, so may I suggest finding ways to disrupt the common fears about radiation? [1] My greatest respect and thanks to the people - the \"liquidators\" - who had to be the \"bio-robots\" that cleaned up the worst areas when the mechanical robots failed fro"},{"id":8670517,"author":"acqq","month":"2014-11","story":"Chernobyl’s Hot Mess, “the Elephant’s Foot,” Is Still Lethal (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Liquidators cleaning the contaminated Chernobyl reactor roof.","quote":"Related video, also workers at Chernobyl, but another piece of the reactor: the roof.","text":"Related video, also workers at Chernobyl, but another piece of the reactor: the roof. [VIDEO] \"Military people call such places \"FRONTLINE\", liquidators who worked at the Chornobyl nuclear station called it \"ROOF COATING\". It was the most contaminated, and therefore the most dangerous, place in the zone. The remains of the roof coating of the 4th reactor. The operation on decontaminating the roof lasted more than five months. We will tell about only two days.\""}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"M8F5hXqS-Ac":{"wy":"HN recommends the channel for understanding why North American road design makes cycling dangerous and how the Dutch approach differs.","q":{"x":"Watch the bicycle infrastructure get better as he get out into the Amsterdam suburbs.","i":28325117,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28334519,"author":"tdeck","month":"2021-08","story":"“Does induced demand apply to bike lanes?” and other questions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compares a Canadian and Dutch journey, showing how safe Dutch cycling is.","quote":"I've really been inspired lately by watching the videos on this channel about urban design in the Netherlands","text":"> The best option is to just take the lane and follow the rules of the road for drivers of vehicles. You get noticed and you don't have conflicts at intersections. You don't need to be physically fit to do it (other than be physically capable of riding a bike). Most people won't be comfortable doing this for the simple reason that they'll be going slower than surrounding traffic and it will piss of drivers, which is scary when you're on a bike and they have two tons of metal right beside you. If the safest way to do something is mildly terrifying and generally viewed as antagonistic, we can't be surprised that most people will avoid doing that thing. I've really been inspired lately by watching the videos on this channel about urban design in the Netherlands: [LINK] I didn't realize how much urban cycling raises my blood pressure until I watched this video that compares a journey in Canada to a journey in the Netherlands, it's hard to imagine cycling being so safe and easy outside a college campus: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33768362,"author":"gcanyon","month":"2022-11","story":"The Exceptionally American Problem of Rising Roadway Deaths","stance":"rec","why":"Channel covering road, pedestrian and bike safety extensively.","quote":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively.","text":"Not Just Bikes on YouTube covers road/pedestrian/bike safety extensively. [LINK] Specific videos: Crossing the street should be safer: [LINK] How to quickly build a cycling city: [LINK] Wasting money on car infrastructure: [LINK] The wrong way to set speed limits: [LINK] Safe cycling: [VIDEO] \"Stroads\" are dangerous: [LINK]"},{"id":29883649,"author":"noahtallen","month":"2022-01","story":"Tesla Model S Goes 752 Miles with a Startup's Prototype Battery","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why North American road design makes cycling dangerous.","quote":"This channel is helpful in understanding the problem","text":"Cycling is normally very dangerous in North America (outside of certain urban cores and small towns), due to the road design and zoning laws. This channel is helpful in understanding the problem: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28325117,"author":"Gwypaas","month":"2021-08","story":"“Does induced demand apply to bike lanes?” and other questions","stance":"rec","why":"Compares Amsterdam and Calgary cycling; infrastructure improves in suburbs.","quote":"Watch the bicycle infrastructure get better as he get out into the Amsterdam suburbs.","text":"Watch the bicycle infrastructure get better as he get out into the Amsterdam suburbs. Cycling showdown, Amsterdam vs. Calgary [VIDEO]"},{"id":28571219,"author":"lolpython","month":"2021-09","story":"Renting a car will be a pain until at least 2022","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Most things we take for granted like being able to get a week's worth of groceries home in one trip or going to enjoy a restaurant that specializes in a niche style, or buying cheap goods at a warehouse store, or being able to get to a hospital within half an hour all require automobile infrastructure It's possible to do these things without a car if your city has prioritized making that a reality. The exact mode depends on the city. Some examples: * In the Netherlands, cycling infrastructure is strong enough that you can bike to a big box store outside the city and load up your bike with what you need [0]. You can fit a week's worth of goods in a cargo bike [1] if that's your style. There is also an argument to be made that you don't need to buy groceries weekly if your city is dense and you have a store nearby. * In Pittsburgh, there are flyer (fast track) busses that will take you to malls in the northern car-oriented suburbs as well as light rail to the southern suburbs. So you can reach most niche stores in the metro area by bus/light rail. Although it's pedestrian hostile in the northern burbs. In the city proper, bus coverage is good enough to get basically anywhere including all the niche restaurants. Though light rail is more frequent and pleasant. Bus frequency and reliability is just OK, not great. Could be improved by changing priorities. My point here is that Pittsburgh's urban form is not great but they still came up with transit solutions that work for averag"},{"id":28605678,"author":"Symbiote","month":"2021-09","story":"Trailerduck is a smart e-bike trailer","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"there's no way you'll be going fast through the centre of Amsterdam","text":"No, 25km/h is the normal maximum speed for a normal person cycling. > On my road bike I sustain 20 mph for most of the ride. A few people manage 32km/h in the Netherlands or Denmark, but only if they have a long commute through the suburbs or a rural area, often for recreation/leisure/exercise. In towns, other people aren't expecting Lance Armstrong to appear, and can step/swerve into your path. You can see from [1] (at the linked point) that there's no way you'll be going fast through the centre of Amsterdam. The path isn't wide enough, there are far too many other cyclists and pedestrians, and the car drivers yielding at the junctions would be startled, at best. The same at 4m32s (a park). At 9m37s it looks like the kind of place you'd get some road/fast cyclists (in fact the narration says so). People who want to go as fast as you would usually select a different route, where possible. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5TkIe60y2GI",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1],["v3y8AIEX_dU",1],["Ds-v2-qyCc8",1],["sI-1YNAmWlk",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["VQKMoT-6XSg",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["S0qjK3TWZE8",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["pLPpl2ISKTg",1],["oqu5DjzOBF8",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["MBT1OK6VAIU",1],["sg2j7mZ9-2Y",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1],["Uhx-26GfCBU",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1]]},"meHYBhcpdvQ":{"wy":"Offered as a well-researched counterpoint in threads complaining about car dealerships and direct sales.","q":{"x":"Here is a well researched youtube video on the subject.","i":33607407,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33607407,"author":"somat","month":"2022-11","story":"Tesla finds a loophole in states where dealerships are forbidden: Tribal lands","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well researched video on dealership model.","quote":"Here is a well researched youtube video on the subject.","text":"Here is a well researched youtube video on the subject. [VIDEO] And on that subject. What may be the best channel on youtube, amazingly well researched, written, shot, presented... and it's a(incredulous tone) motorcycle channel?!"},{"id":32319167,"author":"somerandomqaguy","month":"2022-08","story":"Ford CEO Had Enough: Dealers Raising Prices Need to Stop or Else [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Other side of the dealership model, from motorcycle industry.","quote":"Just as a look at the other side","text":"Just as a look at the other side: [VIDEO] - The Stealership Trap - Why We Need the Dealership \"Rip Off\" This is for motorcycles but the jist of it is that the situation with dealerships is convenient to motorcycle manufacturers. I suspect it's semi applicable to cars as well."},{"id":31602982,"author":"dharmab","month":"2022-06","story":"Ford CEO says EVs will be sold 100% online with nonnegotiable price","stance":"rec","why":"Argues dealerships help manufacturers' finances and manage risk.","quote":"This recent video on motorcycle dealerships argues that dealerships are beneficial","text":"This recent video on motorcycle dealerships argues that dealerships are beneficial for the financial arms of manufacturers and also manage risk due to warranty/recall work and sales flops. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40923185,"author":"somerandomqaguy","month":"2024-07","story":"New technology and old tactics have made buying a car a death march of deception","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why motorcycle dealerships work this way.","quote":"FortNine released a video on why motorcycle dealerships work this way","text":"It's mostly on the car manufacturers. Most of them seem to want the dealership model nowadays. There's no such laws in place requiring independent dealerships in Canada. Aside from the usual direct sales car companies, Genesis dealerships in Canada are all owned by Genesis Canada and I can order a car direct from Genesis. Yet most of the big names in cars operate with independent dealerships, as well as practically every motorcycle manufacturer as far as I know. In fact, Mercedes-Benz Canada just sold off all their Toronto dealerships to a AWIN/Zanchin [1] and AFAIK it wasn't because they were forced to. I don't have any real insight into why this is the case. Most I can say is that if direct sales was so lucrative for car makers, one has to wonder why they didn't take advantage of it here. That being said, FortNine released a video on why motorcycle dealerships work this way; I suspect a lot of it applies to cars as well: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["DlTq8DbRs4k",1],["N2jZryt607U",1],["usSfgHGEGxQ",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["NY66qpMFOYo",1],["eOti3kKWX-c",1],["i9i47sgi-V4",1],["tMP5Pbx8I4s",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["O61WihMRdjM",1]]},"yvDCzhbjYWs":{"wy":"Recommended as a favorite tech talk showing how huge data availability changes how problems are approached.","q":{"x":"Norvig does an especially great job in making you truly appreciate the seismic impact that the availability of massive quantities of data can have","i":12640863,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12640863,"author":"ChicagoBoy11","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Conveys the seismic impact of massive data availability on problem thinking.","quote":"Norvig does an especially great job in making you truly appreciate the seismic impact that the availability of massive quantities of data can have","text":"Peter Norvig on the \"Unreasonable Effectiveness of Data\" [VIDEO] I think it is so easy for us to discuss the impact of big data and quickly get into the weeds, but I think in this talk Norvig does an especially great job in making you truly appreciate the seismic impact that the availability of massive quantities of data can have on your way to think about problems. This is one of the first things I ever saw of him, and I've been in love ever since."},{"id":5045829,"author":"gpcz","month":"2013-01","story":"Data trumps everything","stance":"rec","why":"Examples of simple algorithms with lots of data.","quote":"If you're looking for more examples and information about using simple algorithms with lots of data, I recommend Peter Norvig's","text":"If you're looking for more examples and information about using simple algorithms with lots of data, I recommend Peter Norvig's \"The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Data\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":3913433,"author":"stiff","month":"2012-05","story":"Beware The Hype Over Big Data Analytics","stance":"rec","why":"Nice presentation on the effectiveness of data.","quote":"You can watch a nice presentation from Peter Norvig about the unreasonable effectiveness of data","text":"I am not sure if I disagree with the central thesis of the article, but the argumentation seems rather weak in many places, for example he says: Let's take the example of the number of people who walk into a store and buy a certain product. By studying 10 customers you can get an estimate of the probability of the purchase and predict sales; by studying 100 customers you get a better estimate. By analyzing 1000 customers you have a very good estimate. You could study a million customers, but the results are unlikely to be vastly different from the results of analyzing 1000 customers and probably not worth the expense. This makes sense only for very low-dimensional data sets, but most real-life problems are very high-dimensional and in high-dimensional spaces a million customers (or any other number of samples you might reasonably expect to gather) might still very well be only a small sample. So, there are situations where because of various constraints you will never be able to get to this level where more data stops yielding better results. You can watch a nice presentation from Peter Norvig about the unreasonable effectiveness of data: [VIDEO] Or read about the well-known curse of dimensionality: [LINK]"},{"id":47149844,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-02","story":"Half million 'Words with Spaces' missing from dictionaries","stance":"reference","why":"Norvig's data talk linked as related to word segmentation.","quote":"","text":"That happens often with domain names, but then you get expertsexchange.com, penisland.net, whorepresents.com, therapistfinder.com, a Dutch pre-match analysis site voorspel.nl, or a site about the game overspel.nl. Peter Norvig - The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Data [VIDEO]"},{"id":10581062,"author":"wslh","month":"2015-11","story":"Google Open-Sourcing TensorFlow Shows AI's Future Is Data, Not Code","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier source for the data-over-code idea.","quote":"This is old news","text":"This is old news: Peter Norvig, The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Data (2011): [VIDEO] and a previous article from 2009: [LINK]"}],"r":[["a1zDuOPkMSw",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["aAb7hSCtvGw",1]]},"-gGLvg0n-uY":{"wy":"HN users post it as a meme or satirical comment in threads about AI regulation, age verification and online identity.","q":{"x":"which watching is in my opinion worth your time","i":44150911,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":49265047,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2026-08","story":"How Claude marks AI-generated content","stance":"rec","why":"Meme video arguing AI will force global digital ID.","quote":"This is a meme video but I think it hits the nail on the head.","text":"This is a meme video but I think it hits the nail on the head. TLDR: AI will force global online digital ID for everyone that uses the internet for the exact reason you mentioned. And that would forever change free speech forever allowing the powers that be to put the genie \"back in the bottle\" so to speak. link: Raiden Warned About AI Censorship - [VIDEO]"},{"id":44954276,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2025-08","story":"Porn censorship is going to destroy the internet","stance":"rec","why":"Video on AI being used to break the internet.","quote":"Great video on how AI is being actively used to break the internet.","text":"Correct, and it's to put the genii back in the bottle so to speak. Great video on how AI is being actively used to break the internet. Cause a problem, provide the solution. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44150911,"author":"aleph_minus_one","month":"2025-06","story":"The Future of Comments Is Lies, I Guess","stance":"rec","why":"Meme video about AI forcing identity verification.","quote":"which watching is in my opinion worth your time","text":"> I think that, ultimately, systems that humans use to interact on the internet will have to ditch anonymity. Relevant meme video (which watching is in my opinion worth your time): Raiden Warned About AI Censorship - MGS2 Codec Call (2023 Version) [VIDEO]"},{"id":47123505,"author":"amiga386","month":"2026-02","story":"The Age Verification Trap, Verifying age undermines everyone's data protection","stance":"reference","why":"Fictional video marrying Metal Gear Solid 2's plot with current events.","quote":"this fictional video thematically marries Metal Gear Solid 2's plot with current events","text":"You're not thinking more than one step ahead. If you let a third party define who \"has ID\", \"is human\", etc. you give that third party control over you. You already gave control of your attention away to the sites who host the UGC, now you also give away control of your sense of reality. At any point they can tell a real human what they can and can't say, and if they go against their masters, their \"real human\" status is revoked, because you trust the platform and not the person. If we want to go full conspiritard, we could accuse those of wanting to control speech to be the financial backers of those flooding social media with AI slop: [VIDEO] -- this fictional video thematically marries Metal Gear Solid 2's plot with current events: \"perfect AI speech, audio and video synthesis will drown out reality [...] That is when we will present our solution: mandatory digital identity verification for all humans at all times\""},{"id":44954253,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2025-08","story":"Porn censorship is going to destroy the internet","stance":"reference","why":"Video on controlling AI via a global ID system.","quote":"Related video about the need to control AI with a global ID system","text":"Related video about the need to control AI with a global ID system to control \"misinformation and disinformation\" as it will be imposable to tell what is real and what is fake. We are getting hit from all sides. You will be tracked and it will be used against you. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43926983,"author":"aleph_minus_one","month":"2025-05","story":"Ghost students are creating problems for California colleges","stance":"reference","why":"Meme video on AI and censorship.","quote":"meme video concerning related topics","text":"Relevant (in my opinion \"deep\") meme video concerning related topics: Raiden Warned About AI Censorship - MGS2 Codec Call (2023 Version) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38071626,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2023-10","story":"Biden to Issue First Regulations on Artificial Intelligence Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Meme video said to capture the manufactured AI crisis.","quote":"This meme video incapsulates this perfectly.","text":"All joking aside I firmly believe that this “crisis” is manufactured or at least heavily influenced by those that want to shut down the internet and free communications. Up until now they have been unsuccessful. Copyright infringement, hate speech, misinformation, disinformation, child exploitation, deep fakes, none have worked to garner support. Now we have an existential threat. Video, audio, text, nothin is off limits and soon it will be in real time (note: the GOV tries to stay 25 years ahead of the private sector). This meme video incapsulates this perfectly. [VIDEO] Mark my words, in five years or less we will be begging the governments of earth to implement permanent global real time tracking for every man woman and child on earth. Privacy is dead. And WE killed it."},{"id":38071821,"author":"normalaccess","month":"2023-10","story":"Biden to Issue First Regulations on Artificial Intelligence Systems","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the knock-on effects of AI.","quote":"","text":"I’ve posted this elsewhere in this thread but the consequences of AI have HUGE knock on effects. [VIDEO] [LINK] Once global ID is cracked then global social credit can gain some traction. Etc…"},{"id":37102169,"author":"neets","month":"2023-08","story":"DoD Announces Establishment of Generative AI Task Force","stance":"reference","why":"Meme video offered as context for AI regulation.","quote":"I think this video[1] belongs here for","text":"I think this video[1] belongs here for \"context\". [1] [Raiden Warned About AI Censorship - MGS2 Codec Call (2023 Version) - YouTube]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":35257026,"author":"mgdlbp","month":"2023-03","story":"It’s Game over on Vocal Deepfakes","stance":"reference","why":"Speculative fiction about AI and censorship.","quote":"Relevant speculative fiction that tops the current /wsg/ thread","text":"You can search for it given the keywords... [LINK] Relevant speculative fiction that tops the current /wsg/ thread: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44151326,"author":"safety1st","month":"2025-06","story":"The Future of Comments Is Lies, I Guess","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Time to go full blown conspiracy theory mode","text":"Time to go full blown conspiracy theory mode [VIDEO] 1) Even telephone calls will become totally untrustworthy --> 2) Mandatory digital identity verification for all humans, at all times --> 3) Total control and censorship, the end of what we think of as the Internet today."},{"id":37732136,"author":"richbell","month":"2023-10","story":"Tom Hanks says that’s not him selling dental insurance in Instagram ads","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is corny, but it summarizes the idea quite well.","text":"Requiring digital signatures is the first step in the alleged conspiracy to censor AI (and the Internet as a whole). This is corny, but it summarizes the idea quite well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36120143,"author":"bakugo","month":"2023-05","story":"HBO Max new Captcha system","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"provide your ID when you connect to the Internet, and we will guarantee you are a human","text":"> We will soon need this, and I feel government will gladly present a solution: provide your ID when you connect to the Internet, and we will guarantee you are a human. Relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35004801,"author":"dane-pgp","month":"2023-03","story":"Germany opposes EU plans for client-side scanning","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Controlling the output of generative AI technology is simple.","text":"\"Controlling the output of generative AI technology is simple. We will create context for its use. First we will censor any use related to social taboos. Then we will censor anything else that we desire. If anyone complains, we will accuse them of wanting to engage in or promote social taboos.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":35049878,"author":"sinity","month":"2023-03","story":"Europe's big tech bill is coming to fruition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Then we'll censor anything we desire.","text":"> And who decides which information is valid and which isn't? [VIDEO] Hehe > Who are you to decide what's misinformation anyway? > That sounds like something misinformation terrorist would say. ... > First, we'll censor any use related to social taboos. Then we'll censor anything we desire. If anyone complains, we'll accuse them of wanting to engage in and promote social taboos."}],"r":[["JxBg4sMusIg",1],["mII9NZ8MMVM",1]]},"14wqBA5Q1yc":{"wy":"Cited as a landmark of speedrunning, where a trick once limited to tool-assisted runs was pulled off live on hardware.","q":{"x":"his brief videos give a really nice introduction to the topic that should be accessible to most people","i":9792946,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":16992337,"author":"qz3","month":"2018-05","story":"MrWint's GBC Pokémon: Yellow Version “Arbitrary Code Execution” (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Frame-perfect glitch run warping to the credits","quote":"absolutely unbelievable.","text":"There was a time where is discovered the speedrun and tas communities and binge-watched everything it has to offer. I was constantly amazed by what the players achieved, and I especially liked the ones where the gap between tas and human player closes. SethBling made an elaborate, frame-perfect glitch exploit run of Super Mario World where he warps from the first level to the end credits - absolutely unbelievable. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9792946,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-06","story":"Hacking Super Mario World to Warp to the Credits [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Live credits warp on real SNES with pixel-perfect setup","quote":"pulled off the impossible and did the credit warp on an actual SNES, live","text":"I believe it's been posted before, but sethbling (of vanilla-minecraft-hacks fame) pulled off the impossible and did the credit warp on an actual SNES, live[1]. As this video very-nicely explains, this required incredible amounts of pixel-perfect accuracy. Lining up the line of koopa shells on exact x-offsets and very narrow heights mid jump (!) to write the landing-sled - using a controller, realtime - is utterly insane. As a side note sethbling's latest project is teaching[2] (using neuro-evolution) his machine learning bot to play SMW[2], SMB1[3], and Mario Kart (original)[4]. While he machine learning is simple by today's standards, his brief videos give a really nice introduction to the topic that should be accessible to most people. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":33380213,"author":"NobodyNada","month":"2022-10","story":"Beating Super Mario Bros 3 in 19 frames (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a historically significant human-performed run","quote":"Super Mario World (RTA, 2015)","text":"Some highlights/historically significant examples of this: Super Mario World (TAS, 2014): [LINK] Super Mario Bros. 3 (TAS, 2014): [LINK] - The same run performed RTA (i.e. by a human using a real controller) at AGDQ 2015: [LINK] Super Mario World (RTA, 2015): [VIDEO] Super Mario World \"total control\" (RTA, 2015): [LINK] Pokemon Yellow total control (TAS, 2017): [LINK] The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time (RTA, 2019-ish?): [LINK] Ocarina of Time total control/\"Triforce%\" (TAS, 2022): [LINK] (explanation: [LINK] )"},{"id":29364067,"author":"alexose","month":"2021-11","story":"Writing a Fuzzer for NES Games","stance":"reference","why":"Example of next-level retro speedrunning","quote":"I realized that speedrunning retro games is truly next-level these days.","text":"Ever since I saw someone beat Super Mario World by reprogramming the game from within the game itself ( [VIDEO] ), I realized that speedrunning retro games is truly next-level these days."},{"id":9529859,"author":"duskwuff","month":"2015-05","story":"Gamer beats both Mega Man X and Mega Man X2 using the same controller","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a TAS trick later done live","quote":"it's not the first time a speedrunning trick has made its way from TAS into","text":"Oh, sure. Still, it's not the first time a speedrunning trick has made its way from TAS into \"live\" runs. For instance, the SMW credits skip: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"W71BTkUbdqE":{"wy":"The go-to reference in monorepo and trunk-based development discussions, recommended as a firsthand account of how Google manages source control.","q":{"x":"I'd recommend this video that describes it.","i":13514652,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":31292094,"author":"kyrra","month":"2022-05","story":"Google is 2B lines of code and it's all in one place (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on Google's source control","quote":"There's also a YouTube video which is linked from the wired article that is good.","text":"Googler opinions are my own. I also highly recommend this ACM article talking about our source control from 2016. [LINK] There's also a YouTube video which is linked from the wired article that is good. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13514652,"author":"kyrra","month":"2017-01","story":"Trunk-Based Development","stance":"rec","why":"Describes Google's custom in-house source control","quote":"I'd recommend this video that describes it.","text":"Google doesn't use perforce anymore. They have a custom in-house SCM that has various interfaces wrapped around it. I'd recommend this video that describes it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12189292,"author":"twotwotwo","month":"2016-07","story":"Go Packaging Proposal Process","stance":"rec","why":"Rachel Potvin on Google's single-version third-party code policy","quote":"There is a great talk about some of this by Rachel Potvin","text":"It's common practice for folks building Go binaries (but not libraries) to check in the code for their dependencies. So whenever you build Docker, you get the same thing unless Docker made a change (either by changing Docker itself or explicitly checking in an upgrade to a dep). There are a few nice things about it; vendoring means you won't have a dependency on other repos or archives being available, and if you're a binary maintainer you can see source changes in your own code and that of your dependencies side-by-side if you're, say, investigating a bug that came up between certain revisions. The not-so-nice things are pretty much what you'd think. There isn't a command to say \"give me upgrades, but not ones that the author flagged as breaking changes\". If you're writing a _library_ that is depended on by other _libraries_ there is no one conventional way to safely do breaking changes, so there's a lot of effort to avoid those changes plus sometimes hacks like different repo paths for different versions. Part of the history is that, internally, Google's got one version of third-party code in the latest rev of their source control tree, by company policy. You upgrade something for one, you upgrade it for the whole codebase. Sounds daunting, but they have lots of testing, canary servers, and so on to catch problems. There is a great talk about some of this by Rachel Potvin (can't recall if it covers the third_party/ stuff specifically): [VIDEO] So Go's initial model didn't c"},{"id":19303168,"author":"svat","month":"2019-03","story":"Case Study: Monolithic Repository at Google (2018) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This paper from last year surveys Google “engineers who have experience with both monolithic repos and multiple, per-project repos” to see what exactly they like and dislike about monorepos. The results match my experience, and I find the paper well-written (especially like the “Threats to Validity” section that says what biases may have affected the results; more papers and arguments should have them IMO). Older reading on monorepos that I'm aware of: • Dan Luu, Advantages of monorepos (2015) [LINK] • Why Google Stores Billions of Lines of Code in a Single Repository (2015): [LINK] (HTML) / [LINK] (PDF) / [VIDEO] (video) This paper is from the “Software Engineering in Practice” track of the ICSE 2018 conference: • [LINK]"},{"id":18823119,"author":"weinzierl","month":"2019-01","story":"Software Engineering at Google (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Potvin states branching at Google is exceedingly rare","quote":"Googler Rachel Potvin made an even stronger statement in her presentation about","text":"What I don't understand is how they accomplish larger collaborative changes. The paper says: \"Almost all development occurs at the 'head' of the repository, not on branches.\" Googler Rachel Potvin made an even stronger statement in her presentation about \"The Motivation for a Monolithic Codebase\" [1]: \"Branching for development at Google is exceedingly rare [..]\" In the related ACM paper she published with Josh Levenberg there is the statement that: \"Development on branches is unusual and not well supported at Google, though branches are typically used for releases.\" I my world when we have to make a bigger change we create a branch and only merge it into the trunk when it is good enough to be integrated. The branch enables us to work on that change together. I don't understand how they do this at google. As far as I understand in their model they either have to - give up on collaboration and always have just a single developer work on a change. - share code by other means. - check in unfinished work to the trunk for collaboration and constantly break trunk. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11993246,"author":"valarauca1","month":"2016-06","story":"Why Google Stores Billions of Lines of Code in a Single Repository","stance":"reference","why":"Google's 2015 talk on its single repository","quote":"Google gave a talk at the @scale conference in 2015 about this very topic.","text":"Google gave a talk at the @scale conference in 2015 about this very topic. You can watch it here (30 minutes): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Uj-rK8V-rik",1],["BpsMkLaEiOY",1]]},"n068fel-W9I":{"wy":"Recommended as a fascinating insider account of how the F-22's software makes it easier to fly than a Cessna.","q":{"x":"the talk is that good!","i":29151134,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29151134,"author":"khazhoux","month":"2021-11","story":"Pilot Says Too Many Jumpers Outside Led to King Air Spin","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Test pilot explains F-22 is easier to fly than a Cessna.","quote":"the talk is that good!","text":"I recently learned that this is an issue that bomber pilots / airplanes must deal with, when they deploy their payloads. Here is a video of a test pilot giving a talk at MIT on the F-22 and its advanced flying capabilities which --as he puts it-- makes flying an F-22 actually easier than a Cessna. [VIDEO] Apologies in advance for you losing an hour -- the talk is that good!"},{"id":27376159,"author":"mikehollinger","month":"2021-06","story":"An F-35 Pilot Explains Why the Jet's Bad Press Misses the Point","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Test pilot on F-22 flight control tradeoffs and software help.","quote":"there's a great hour-long lecture [1] by Colonel Randy Gordon","text":"Slightly related, but there's a great hour-long lecture [1] by Colonel Randy Gordon, who's a test pilot, discussing the flight control systems for the F-22, contrasting it (a bit) to other jets, and even to a Cessna. He talks about the tradeoffs between human factors, mechanical engineering, software engineering, and shows how the software systems help the pilot. It's well worth the hour if you're an aviation geek. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30965647,"author":"roeles","month":"2022-04","story":"A naked skydive inspired a way to keep pilots oriented in flight","stance":"reference","why":"Shows F-22 handling engine failures automatically.","quote":"the F-22 appears to handle stuff like engine failures for you[1]","text":"Interestingly, the F-22 appears to handle stuff like engine failures for you[1]. Perhaps it's inertia of regulations and training. Radically automating airplanes involves training loads of people. There's also certification, which is different for military aircraft than for civilian aircraft. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["sSfejgwbDQ8",1],["FzF1KySHmUA",1]]},"zU7Lww-sBPg":{"wy":"Recommended by some as a blunt expert critique of origin-of-life research, while others question its claims and length.","q":{"x":"Here's a talk by one of the world’s top synthetic organic chemists offering a blunt assessment of the current state of origin of life research","i":20588935,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":31014648,"author":"freediver","month":"2022-04","story":"Human brain compresses working memories into low-res ‘summaries’","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture on origin of life described as eye-opening.","quote":"I can recommend watching James Tour on this very topic [1]","text":"> Afaik we are very close to artificially creating living cells. This is one recent example [1]. I beg to differ. It may look impressive on the surface, just like GPT-3 looks impressive on the surface, but is far from the real thing. It is just another extension of the ladder to the Moon. The effort described in the article is nowhere near a living cell. It lacks protein building and DNA/RNA mechanisms. They basically describe a group of nanomotors. I can recommend watching James Tour on this very topic [1] and Stephen Meyer on the related topic of intelligent design [2]. Those two lectures were eye-opening for me learning more about this field. Note: both of them are self-confessed theist scientists which to me did not represent a problem (my viewes are agnostic, and it only made it more interesting as you rarely get to hear different views about these matters than pop-sci). > The consciousness part is similar to AGI. It is not clear what you mean by that. One thing is to build computer code and then have it manifest 'intelligence'. Whole another thing is doing same with organic matter that can not be 'programmed', even if we knew how to do it (let alone there is no evidence that 'programming' is responsible for consciousness at all to begin with). This is also known as 'hard problem of consciousness' and David Chalmers is considered one of the leading experts in the field [3]. Basically smartest scientists in the world are clueless about this and do not know even where to b"},{"id":25146697,"author":"friendlybus","month":"2020-11","story":"I should have loved biology","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series showing how far biology is from explaining life's origin.","quote":"James Tour has a great series on origin of life that shows in the hard tasks of applying biology we are way off knowing how any of this works.","text":"Oh, well the 'current' argument is that it's unlikely for a bunch of reasons. - cambrian explosion of life is seen as unlikely given that changing random genes or dna mutations has such a low success rate in generating new life, that an explosion of life happening all at once seems out of step with the math. - we don't know how to create life at all. adding up bits and pieces of molecules into cells and onwards can be screwed up at any point in the process and has to start over from square one to succeed. people in the lab can't even get it right and they can write down their progress and adapt unlike nature. - self-organizing molecules and natural forces guided towards life haven't been seen yet to exist in nature, it has no expressed intent or will to self-organize into life. randomness and infinite time just isn't enough I don't see the evidence that local minima and maxima changes in body shape, size, ect, act to completely change the species of an animal. There's a long debate running from the 1980s on this stuff, it's just getting views on youtube more recently and becoming properly structured and scientific arguments instead of just conjecture. The stephen meyer 'intelligent design', david berlinski 'doubting darwin', ect arguments. The hoover institute did a piece a few years ago on youtube. James Tour has a great series on origin of life that shows in the hard tasks of applying biology we are way off knowing how any of this works. [VIDEO] The counter arguments to ste"},{"id":20588935,"author":"taejavu","month":"2019-08","story":"A chemical clue to how life started on Earth","stance":"reference","why":"Blunt assessment of origin-of-life research by a top synthetic chemist.","quote":"Here's a talk by one of the world’s top synthetic organic chemists offering a blunt assessment of the current state of origin of life research","text":"Here's a talk by one of the world’s top synthetic organic chemists offering a blunt assessment of the current state of origin of life research: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20590091,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2019-08","story":"A chemical clue to how life started on Earth","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's a long talk (1h). Which are the main points?","text":"From the first comment: > \"amino acids don't hook together by themselves\" [VIDEO] This is a very weird remark considering that the article posted here is about how amino acids hook together by themselves, and which amino acids hook in the correct way and which hook in the incorrect way. --- It's a long talk (1h). Which are the main points? I only skimmed, so I hope I have a not very unfair selection: * We don't know the details about how life begun. I agree. I think we all agree. * Most press articles (and many research article) are overhyped. I agree. Can I get a bot to post that in every scientific post in HN? * The recipes to build organic molecules are complex, with many steps that are not posible to find in the wild (somewhat like [VIDEO] , and many slides with technical details after that) I agree. The problem is that the recipe for the natural formation of some compounds is something like \"Get a big pool like the Mediterranean, kill all the bacterias so they don't eat the mix of chemical compounds, wait a million years. And then you will get only an efficiency of 0.0000001.\" And probably nobody is sure that it is the correct recipe, perhaps it need some metal as catalyzer, perhaps light, perhaps darkness, perhaps lack of oxygen, ... So the recipes he show in the slides are about how to get the same compounds in a lab, preferably in some flask that you can put over a table, waiting only a few hour, with something like a 10% or 1% of efficiency. So you must use weird con"}],"r":[["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["h6fcK_fRYaI",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["eQ2OZKitRwc",1]]},"3MghYhf-GhU":{"wy":"Recommended as the definitive retrospective on 3dfx, with first-hand stories about engineering hacks and design philosophy.","q":{"x":"This is a really great retrospective on 3dfx from four of the founders.","i":35027350,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35027350,"author":"djmips","month":"2023-03","story":"3dfx: So powerful it’s kind of ridiculous","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Retrospective on 3dfx from four founders","quote":"This is a really great retrospective on 3dfx from four of the founders.","text":"This is a really great retrospective on 3dfx from four of the founders. 3dfx Oral History Panel with Ross Smith, Scott Sellers, Gary Tarolli, and Gordon Campbell (Computer History Museum) [VIDEO]"},{"id":35028547,"author":"aldrich","month":"2023-03","story":"3dfx: So powerful it’s kind of ridiculous","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Key 3dfx people on clever engineering hacks to squeeze silicon","quote":"I would highly recommend watching the 3dfx Oral History Panel video from the Computer History Museum","text":"History is funny because at that time in the 90s there was a company called Bitboys Oy. That company was founded by some Finnish demoscene members and was developing a series of graphics cards, Pyramid3D and Glaze3D, with a programmable pipeline around 1997-1999 [1]. This was at around 5 years before the first commercial shader capable card was released. Even though Wikipedia classifies it as vaporware, there are prototype cards and manuals floating around showing that these cards were in fact designed and contained programmable pixel shaders, notably: - The Pyramid3D GPU datasheet: [LINK] - The pitch deck: [LINK] - The hardware reference manual: [LINK] (shows even more internals!) (As far the companies go: VLSI Solution Oy / TriTech / Bitboys Oy were all related here.) They unfortunately busted before they could release anything, due to a wrong bet in memory type choice (RDRAM, I think) and letting their architecture rely on that, then running out of money, perhaps some other problems. In the end their assets were bought by ATI. As for 3dfx, I would highly recommend watching the 3dfx Oral History Panel video from the Computer History Museum with 4 key people involved in 3dfx at the time [2]. Its quite fun as it shows how 3dfx got ahead of the curve by using very clever engineering hacks and tricks to get more out of the silicon and data buses. It also suggests that their strategy was explicitly about squeezing as much performance out of the hardware, and making sacrifices (q"},{"id":16376971,"author":"rasz","month":"2018-02","story":"Fall of Voodoo","stance":"rec","why":"Panel with 3dfx founders who left Silicon Graphics","quote":"You might also like watching Computer History Museums","text":"You might also like watching Computer History Museums \"3dfx Oral History Panel with Ross Smith, Scott Sellers, Gary Tarolli, and Gordon Campbell\" [VIDEO] Ross Smith, Scott Sellers and Gary Tarolli were 3dfx founders, they left Silicon Graphics to do this startup."},{"id":14310655,"author":"rasz","month":"2017-05","story":"Volta: Advanced Data Center GPU","stance":"reference","why":"3dfx founders' design philosophy of simple, cheap graphics","quote":"The whole point of Voodoo 1 was making it as simple and cheap as possible","text":"Creators of Voodoo (3dfx = Gary Tarolli, Scott Sellers) came from the world of fully programmable GPUs. Silicon Graphics workstations had full T&L since ~1988 ( [LINK] ). The whole point of Voodoo 1 was making it as simple and cheap as possible by removing all the advanced features and calculating geometry/lighting on the CPU. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["rEoc0YoALt0",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1],["CUjt36SD3h8",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["fKSSY1gzCEs",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["8b7erU_DOfE",1],["bJ5ppf0po3k",1],["xn76r0JxqNM",1]]},"7VG_s2PCH_c":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible, funny explanation of why vaccines matter.","q":{"x":"Last Week Tonight has a nice episode on Vaccines","i":21989861,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":21989861,"author":"rahuldottech","month":"2020-01","story":"DR Congo measles: More than 6k dead in world's worst outbreak","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why vaccines matter and why taking it slow is bad.","quote":"Last Week Tonight has a nice episode on Vaccines","text":"Last Week Tonight has a nice episode on Vaccines: [VIDEO] It explains why vaccines are important, why \"taking it slow\" with vaccines is a bad approach, and other important stuff. Please vaccinate yourself and your kids."},{"id":17684584,"author":"hjek","month":"2018-08","story":"Ask HN: What is going on?","stance":"rec","why":"Good Last Week Tonight episode on vaccines.","quote":"Last Week Tonight has quite a good episode on vaccines","text":"Yes, sometimes explanations and reasonable arguments can just derail conversations completely. For preventing that, I find David Burns' disarming technique[0] really useful. It is about \"finding truth in what the other person is saying, even if it seems blatantly wrong, or illogical, or exaggerated.\" His podcast is amazing. Perhaps part of what your room mate is saying is that she is concerned about the well-being of her (future) children? Surely that's truly good. She's also interested in avoiding certain medical treatments that are excessive or unnecessary, which is also good. Don't know about the Holocaust-denying colleague, but sometimes xenophones have a fear of losing their job to someone else, which could leave them unable to economically support themselves or their family, and maybe they really enjoy the job they have. If your colleague talks about you being indoctrinated, then he must have a high regard of the truth (which is good), and if he's interested in how indoctrination works, he may even be interested in reading about Chomsky's propaganda model[1]. Of course, this disarming stuff is only useful if you actually want to prevent conflict with other people, and it's totally legitimate to have a conflict with anti-vaxers and Holocaust deniers. But also, you live and work together, and constant conflicts may not get you nor them anywhere but down. Also, Last Week Tonight has quite a good episode on vaccines[2] (but showing that to your roomie might not calm things "},{"id":27657883,"author":"MattGaiser","month":"2021-06","story":"Surfside official told residents building was safe, despite engineer's warning","stance":"reference","why":"Comment about scientists understating how certain they are.","quote":"Reminds me of this comment about how scientists sometimes understate how certain they are","text":"Would that normally be in such a document? Plenty of engineering type documents I have read don't lay out threats in plain English nor do they try to add emotional urgency to them. Reminds me of this comment about how scientists sometimes understate how certain they are: [VIDEO] ."}],"r":[["Zk11vI-7czE",1],["ao2GL3NAWQU",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["YdHTlUGN1zw",1]]},"B5FxFfymI4g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an amazing, clear explanation of why incumbents move upmarket and lose to low-end challengers.","q":{"x":"Here's part of a great talk from Christensen describing the same logic but in the Steel industry","i":7395695,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7108723,"author":"dandare","month":"2014-01","story":"Lenovo Agrees to Buy IBM Server Business for $2.3 Billion","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Christensen explains the steel mini-mill disruption example","quote":"Clayton Christensen's amazing talk on Disruptive Innovation","text":"It immediately reminded me of the mini-mills example from Clayton Christensen's amazing talk on Disruptive Innovation [VIDEO]"},{"id":7395695,"author":"reqres","month":"2014-03","story":"The Meteoric Rise of DigitalOcean","stance":"rec","why":"Christensen explains incumbents moving upmarket and losing to low-end challengers","quote":"Here's part of a great talk from Christensen describing the same logic but in the Steel industry","text":"The logic you present is correct from the incumbent's perspective. But followed to its logical conclusion, it leads to the downfall of the incumbent according to Christensen's model of disruptive innovation. The incumbents will continually cater upmarket to their higher value customers while the challenger picks up more and more low value customers. But there's only so far upmarket you can go until you run out of customers and that's when the low end challenger has completely consumed the incumbent. I think much of this thread is a testament to the lower value customers being ignored. Things about how DO is much less complex, simpler and as a result cheaper. Here's part of a great talk from Christensen describing the same logic but in the Steel industry: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14610081,"author":"cornflake","month":"2017-06","story":"14 People Make 500K Tons of Steel a Year in Austria","stance":"reference","why":"Christensen's breakdown of steel industry economics","quote":"were broken down really well by Harvard professor Clayton Christensen in his writings and this clip","text":"The economics behind this disruptive innovation in the steel industry were broken down really well by Harvard professor Clayton Christensen in his writings and this clip: [VIDEO]"}]},"HMYiktO0D64":{"wy":"HN commenters share it as a rare, atmospheric look at what working at an early computing center was actually like, including how code was submitted and processed.","q":{"x":"This is one of my favorites, about people submitting their code to get processed at their computing center","i":39657770,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39657770,"author":"pram","month":"2024-03","story":"Bell Labs: An Institute of Creative Technology (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How code was submitted and processed at the computing center.","quote":"This is one of my favorites, about people submitting their code to get processed at their computing center","text":"ATT has a bunch of Bell Labs stuff on their Youtube channel, it's all a pro watch. This is one of my favorites, about people submitting their code to get processed at their computing center: [VIDEO] Watching it made me realize a lot of batch-esque things like Hadoop and Spark aren't too far off, just more automated."},{"id":30941751,"author":"cube00","month":"2022-04","story":"The State of Fortran","stance":"rec","why":"Training video showing what the Bell Labs computing center was like.","quote":"These two training videos from the Bell Labs Computing Centre are a very interesting insight into what it was like.","text":"These two training videos from the Bell Labs Computing Centre are a very interesting insight into what it was like. Part 1 [VIDEO] Part 2 [LINK]"},{"id":36750151,"author":"st_goliath","month":"2023-07","story":"Bell Labs Holmdel Complex","stance":"reference","why":"1973 training video for Holmdel computing services.","quote":"they uploaded a 1973 era training video for the computing services","text":"Fascinating to see a couple recent photos and find out what happened to this place. The Bell Labs Holmdel Complex is something that I find myself unexpectedly stumbling over on the Internet every few years or so. First time some circa 10 years ago, when they uploaded a 1973 era training video for the computing services to `AT&T tech channel`: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":36750384,"author":"js2","month":"2023-07","story":"Bell Labs Holmdel Complex","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Raquel Welch taped up on the System 370","text":"Raquel Welch taped up on the System 370: [VIDEO] I think it's this photo: [LINK] Her photo is the only personal item I see anywhere in the two videos."},{"id":17384056,"author":"unimpressive","month":"2018-06","story":"Ted Nelson struggles with uncomprehending radio interviewer (1979) [audio]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The funny thing is that early on in this interview the host tells Nelson that he's the only one saying anything about using computers to store and retrieve text. But that's simply not true, certainly not in 1979. For example, John McCarthy had been describing what largely came to be our digital literary future as early as 1970: [LINK] Even within the contemporary environment a significant fraction of computer use was not 'number crunching', but the sorting, retrieval, and printing of essentially textual information. (i.e, records) Records are tiny documents, which to me makes this early portion where Ted accepts the point without argument a large missed opportunity to get the point across. [VIDEO]"}]},"Ox5P7QyL774":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical demo of Self's integrated, direct-manipulation environment whenever prototypal inheritance or live programming comes up.","q":{"x":"This is a good demo of what could be done in the Self environment","i":12471590,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":22120877,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2020-01","story":"Organizing Programs Without Classes (1991) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows programming and graphics closely interwoven in Self.","quote":"Related, and worth a watch","text":"Related, and worth a watch: [VIDEO] It's a video about the Self language, which I think was designed using the principles in this paper. I like it because (like Oberon) it shows that programming and graphics can be much more closely interwoven than we're used to. They were doing it in the 90's. Then we lost it somewhere along the way by trying to make everything big and user-facing."},{"id":12471590,"author":"Fr0styMatt88","month":"2016-09","story":"In the Beginning Was the Command Line (1999)","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of what can be done in the Self environment.","quote":"This is a good demo of what could be done in the Self environment","text":"I'd point to the work done on tangible UIs and programming environments for that. For example, the work on tangible UIs eg- Self: [LINK] This is a good demo of what could be done in the Self environment: [VIDEO] GUI and non-programmability are tangential things. I don't think they're implicitly bound together."},{"id":41650338,"author":"jecel","month":"2024-09","story":"Launch HN: Haystack (YC S24) – Visualize and edit code on an infinite canvas","stance":"reference","why":"Sun's 1995 video on the Kansas environment in Self.","quote":"Here is a video about it from Sun in 1995","text":"This reminds me of the \"Kansas\" environment in the Self programming language. Here is a video about it from Sun in 1995: [VIDEO] More interesting search features were added after this video was made."},{"id":38826794,"author":"trashburger","month":"2023-12","story":"Ambient Co-Presence","stance":"reference","why":"Official Sun movie showing Self's shared visual environment.","quote":"Here's an official movie released by Sun Microsystems","text":"This existed in the 90s! The Self programming language[0] took Smalltalk's existing visual approach and extended it to be a full visual environment with interactive animations and physical metaphors, giving you a literal \"desktop\" to work on. Later versions of Self also supported working on a single \"world\" over the network, where the other users' objects and \"hands\" (cursors) would be visible to you. Here's an official movie released by Sun Microsystems: [VIDEO] [0]: [LINK]"},{"id":27805961,"author":"andai","month":"2021-07","story":"Julia and the Reincarnation of Lisp (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Asks for resources on Self, cited as source for prototypal inheritance.","quote":"does anyone have any resources or stories about Self?","text":"I've heard it described as \"Scheme in C's clothing.\" I remember reading something about how that's a misconception, but I can't recall where. On that note, does anyone have any resources or stories about Self? [0] (The language from which JS inherited prototypal inheritance.) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23494062,"author":"zaiste","month":"2020-06","story":"Show HN: Kretes – Build full-stack applications in TypeScript and PostgreSQL","stance":"reference","why":"Example of integrated editor, tooling and runtime environment.","quote":"I'd like to tightly combine a semi-visual programming editor, the tooling and the runtime/infrastructure into one","text":"I'd like to tightly combine a semi-visual programming editor, the tooling and the runtime/infrastructure into one «thing» as shown in this video [1]. You write a bit of code, you drag and drop another bit, and almost instantly it becomes «online» - the compilation / the deployment process are somehow hidden and the configuration is minimal. It just works, so you can focus on solving the actual business needs of your application - but that's a long term vision... ;) I must mention DarkLang [2] that seems to have a similar goal, but a different approach: Kretes builds on top of VS Code, and uses one of the most popular programming languages while DarkLang builds not only their editor from scratch, but they also introduced a new programming language. I find their project ambitious and inspiring - a potential game changer imho [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":22649555,"author":"zaiste","month":"2020-03","story":"Ask HN: What projects are you working on now?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The project draws inspiration from Self [2]","text":"I'm creating a Rails/Django equivalent in TypeScript called Huncwot [1]. The project draws inspiration from Self [2] and aims to combine a framework, an editor and infrastructure into a unified programming environment. In other words, I'm on a quest for the Holy Grail of Programming. ;) [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7920840,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-06","story":"Web Starter Kit","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Self [VIDEO] Xerox Star User Interface [LINK] [LINK] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [LINK] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK] Warren Teitelman work at Xerox PARC [LINK] The Mesa/Cedar programming environment [LINK] Steve Jobs internal demo of NeXTSTEP 3, [LINK] Alan Kay on Xerox PARC work [LINK] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":7565751,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2014-04","story":"Shadow DOM","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Mainly videos Self [VIDEO] Xerox Star User Interface [LINK] [LINK] Tektronix 4404 Smalltalk [LINK] The Smalltalk-80 Programming System [LINK] The Final Demonstration of the Xerox 'Star' computer [LINK] Symbolics Hypertext Document [LINK]"},{"id":6631673,"author":"muglug","month":"2013-10","story":"Self: the movie","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube copy of the Self movie.","quote":"Here it is on YouTube (Quicktime movie didn't have sound for me). Quality isn't as high, though.","text":"Here it is on YouTube (Quicktime movie didn't have sound for me). Quality isn't as high, though. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AbgsfeGvg3E",1],["GNO_CYUjMK8",1],["7ttG90raCNo",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1],["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["PYylPRX6z4Q",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["NxvQPzrg2Wg",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["fCn8zs912OE",1],["Ggz_gd--UO0",1],["DpXy041BIlA",1],["S7TUe5w6RHo",1]]},"Tml94je2edk":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a clear account of the trade-offs between static and dynamic typing and why the industry is swinging back to types.","q":{"x":"It gives the major reasons why one would prefer one over the other","i":36590025,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36590025,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2023-07","story":"Dynamic Languages Strike Back (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues static types can match dynamic languages' benefits, not vice versa","quote":"It gives the major reasons why one would prefer one over the other","text":"I think the suitable followup to this is the Richard Feldman talk, \"Why Static Typing Came Back.\"[1] It gives the major reasons why one would prefer one over the other, and makes the case that static types can provide most of the actual benefits of dynamic languages, but the reverse is not true. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37598303,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2023-09","story":"Strong arrows: a new approach to gradual typing","stance":"rec","why":"Argues static typing recovers dynamic typing's advantages","quote":"Rich Feldman has a good talk on this, entitled Why Static Typing Came Back[1].","text":"Yeah, Rich Feldman has a good talk on this, entitled Why Static Typing Came Back[1]. It makes a good argument that static typing can recover most of the advantages traditionally accounted to dynamic typing. Rapid iteration you can get from incremental compilers, being concise you can get from powerful type inference, etc. Conversely, the advantages of static typing (largely boiling down to increased reliability) cannot easily be captured by dynamically typed languages. Gradual typing, particularly successful by TypeScript, might be an interesting middle ground, or it might just be a reflection of the dynamic history of JS. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36649394,"author":"dansalvato","month":"2023-07","story":"Why Use Pascal?","stance":"rec","why":"Why dynamic languages rose in the 90s and typing is returning","quote":"The talk makes a lot of other cool points about how development has changed in the past decade or two","text":"I saw a talk that touched on this recently: [VIDEO] He explains that dynamically-typed languages like Python, Ruby, and JS became popular in the 90s because they offered a fast feedback loop for website building, and they didn't need an IDE or compilers, which were slow (and often not free). It ended up not being worth trading your development time for the performance increase when all your users were connecting via 56k modem anyway. The talk makes a lot of other cool points about how development has changed in the past decade or two, and why the trend is moving back to static typing."},{"id":40131095,"author":"zogrodea","month":"2024-04","story":"The last days of my type checker for JavaScript","stance":"reference","why":"Cited from memory about Dart abandoning gradual typing","quote":"My only reference is a memory of this talk by Richard Feldman","text":"I believe Dart was originally gradually typed, and then abandoned the gradual typing for strict-typing-by-default? That doesn't quite sound properly type-safe to me although still safer than how Javascript is by default. My only reference is a memory of this talk by Richard Feldman but I might be wrong or misremembering. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["4YTfxresvS8",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["dEaecUuEqfc",1],["iD9xk3SDSYc",1]]},"X29lF43mUlo":{"wy":"HN brings it up as comic relief and as a funny take when immunity, dirt and germs come up.","q":{"x":"You've reminded me of this George Carlin rant","i":33219271,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":25840673,"author":"iodiocracynow","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: Anyone else burnt out due to extended lockdown and work-from-home?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Carlin on germs helps with lockdown burnout","quote":"I deal with burnout watching videos from george Carlin on germs and virus","text":"I deal with burnout watching videos from george Carlin on germs and virus... 10y old what a wise man [VIDEO]"},{"id":33219271,"author":"pengaru","month":"2022-10","story":"Mud boosts the immune system","stance":"rec","why":"Carlin rant on germs and immunity relevant to mud","quote":"You've reminded me of this George Carlin rant","text":"You've reminded me of this George Carlin rant: [VIDEO] Edit: Usually I regret scrolling down to youtube comments, not this time: > This is not a comedy, this is a TED talk."},{"id":18075389,"author":"peterwwillis","month":"2018-09","story":"Walmart Requires Lettuce, Spinach Suppliers to Join Blockchain","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Alternate solution: do not announce small foodbourne illness outbreaks. In the article's example, 250 people got sick from a lettuce outbreak, and everyone stopped buying lettuce, hitting the whole industry. But 5 million people get sick from Norovirus alone in the US every year. Leafy vegetables like lettuce are the most common source of food illness. Telling people about a minor outbreak is just letting them freak out for no good reason. We should be educating people better about foodbourne illness so they can respond without freaking out, and so they don't get sick so damn much. And the media should stop freaking people out. 250 people out of 350 million getting sick, and not even dying? It's a minor risk! 35,000 die in traffic every year, and these people are freaked out by some lettuce. Take a fuckin chance, would ya please? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JFaOYYSxSEA",1],["c2lgEyNaop4",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["jK0BkTPUGQY",1],["tzr7hRXcwkw",1],["VFFlO7yBIBM",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1]]},"ieb1lmm9xHk":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as a vivid demonstration of learning by quieting the conscious mind, and recommend it alongside the book.","q":{"x":"This video of Timothy Gallwey teaching a woman how to play tennis. In 5 minutes.","i":12528900,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12528900,"author":"andy_ppp","month":"2016-09","story":"The Inner Game of Everything: 1974 Tennis Book Is Still a Sensation","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gallwey teaches a woman tennis in five minutes.","quote":"This video of Timothy Gallwey teaching a woman how to play tennis. In 5 minutes.","text":"[VIDEO] This video of Timothy Gallwey teaching a woman how to play tennis. In 5 minutes. The book is one of the best I've ever read (go get it now), it's about mental focus, improvement by not trying and about not judging your results. I was sitting in front of a really difficult problem earlier today but I kept persisting at it even though at points I got frustrated (\"I'm not sure I'm smart enough to do this...\") I managed to stop thinking for a bit and try again without understanding the code fully and it ended up working. I'm not saying being intuitive always works with programming but sometimes if you feel out of your depth it can really help push you through."},{"id":10623598,"author":"leoc","month":"2015-11","story":"How the 'Quiet Eye' Technique Makes Athletes More Coordinated","stance":"rec","why":"TV feature on Inner Game of Tennis, including keeping eyes on ball.","quote":"Obligatory Inner Game of Tennis TV feature","text":"Obligatory Inner Game of Tennis TV feature: [VIDEO] and specifically related to keeping the eyes on the ball: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9619335,"author":"deepnet","month":"2015-05","story":"Consciousness Began When the Gods Stopped Speaking","stance":"reference","why":"Gallwey distracts the conscious mind so learning can occur.","quote":"As a teacher he tries to distract the conscious mind so learning can take place.","text":"Conversely the conscious mind can also make it harder to learn things. I refer to the \"Inner Game of Tennis\" by Tim Gallwey [VIDEO] As a teacher he tries to distract the conscious mind so learning can take place."}],"r":[["oaDTiWaYfcM",1],["u4ZoJKF_VuA",1],["vq2nnJ4g6N0",1],["fWqBmmPQP40",1],["QRaACa1Mrd4",1]]},"2SdGkkp1aq8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a showcase of what skilled demoscene programmers can squeeze out of extremely limited hardware.","q":{"x":"I like that the demo effects are used here to tell a story in addition to being a pure display of coding prowess.","i":1975073,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":10516221,"author":"msarnoff","month":"2015-11","story":"Software developers describing their work in 1973 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"VIC-20 demo from an awesome demoscene creator.","quote":"The video was posted by an awesome demoscene dude named viznut. Check out the rest of his channel.","text":"The video was posted by an awesome demoscene dude named viznut. Check out the rest of his channel. Experimental music from very short C programs: [LINK] Robotic Liberation (VIC-20 demo): [VIDEO]"},{"id":1975073,"author":"Leynos","month":"2010-12","story":"Incredible demos on the Commodore Vic-20","stance":"rec","why":"VIC-20 demo using effects to tell a story.","quote":"I like that the demo effects are used here to tell a story in addition to being a pure display of coding prowess.","text":"Videos of some of the demos described here: Robotic Warrior [LINK] Robotic Liberation [VIDEO] Impossiblator [LINK] I like that the demo effects are used here to tell a story in addition to being a pure display of coding prowess."},{"id":4888108,"author":"neumann_alfred","month":"2012-12","story":"Microsoft's DroidRage Twitter campaign goes painfully wrong","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"NOBODY expects the robotic liberation! [1] [1] [VIDEO] (I am aware this will rightfully get modded down but sometimes a man has to do what a man has to do)"}],"r":[["ZsRRCnque2E",1],["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["xx7Lfh5SKUQ",1]]},"2wq9x2QcZN0":{"wy":"HN refers to it for Weinstein's views on advanced math and physics and academia's problems, though some find him a bit kooky.","q":{"x":"Eric Weinstein (a bit kooky) explains his view on the trenches of advanced mathematics/physics","i":20907123,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20907123,"author":"abledon","month":"2019-09","story":"Even Physicists Don’t Understand Quantum Mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Weinstein on advanced math/physics and academia's problems","quote":"Eric Weinstein (a bit kooky) explains his view on the trenches of advanced mathematics/physics","text":"In this Lex Freidman interview, Eric Weinstein (a bit kooky) explains his view on the trenches of advanced mathematics/physics: [VIDEO] He goes into depths about the problems right now in academia"},{"id":19627214,"author":"cronix","month":"2019-04","story":"Nearly half of young millennials get thousands in support from parents","stance":"rec","why":"Interview about AI, ending on job displacement","quote":"Here's a good one from Eric Weinstein. The whole interview is good (about AI)","text":"You'd probably be interested in watching Ray Dalios recent interview: [LINK] Here's a good one from Eric Weinstein. The whole interview is good (about AI), but at the end he discusses this kind of issue (time is bookmarked): [VIDEO] This is part of a good lecture by Dr. Jordan Peterson on IQ, AI, and how AI is and will continue to displace people who will have no means to work to support themselves: [LINK]"},{"id":19459491,"author":"adaptiveValleys","month":"2019-03","story":"Revolutionary Ideas in Science, Math, and Society [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Weinstein discusses distrust of Pinker's optimism","quote":"At 33:45 of the podcast after he gives the kinetic-potential energy analogy to describe his distrust of Pinker's optimism.","text":"He doesn't consider himself a physicist either.[1] [1] [VIDEO] At 33:45 of the podcast after he gives the kinetic-potential energy analogy to describe his distrust of Pinker's optimism."}],"r":[["-q7ZVXOU3kM",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["plcc6E-E1uU",1],["uqKGREZs6-w",1]]},"3pvpNKUPbIY":{"wy":"Pointed to for the founder's own account of Instagram's pivot, early scaling on Django with a small team, and avoiding overengineering.","q":{"x":"you can hear about it from the founder in this really good Lex Fridman podcast","i":32743565,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32743565,"author":"joegahona","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: Which startups have the most interesting pivot stories?","stance":"rec","why":"Founder tells Instagram's pivot story","quote":"you can hear about it from the founder in this really good Lex Fridman podcast","text":"Thank you for sharing this -- I didn't realize this existed. Instagram is covered in your link, and you can hear about it from the founder in this really good Lex Fridman podcast: [VIDEO] These lists always kind of remind me of popular bands that changed music over time. The most drastic \"pivot\" I've ever come across in a known band is Ministry, who went from a benign new-wave Depeche Mode clone in 1983 [1] to a pseudo-Cabaret Voltaire in 1986 [2], then to full-on metal by 1991 [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":29330105,"author":"melenaboija","month":"2021-11","story":"Overengineering can kill a product","stance":"rec","why":"Instagram co-founder on early platform days and overengineering","quote":"Interesting interview of Lex Friedman to Kevin Systrom co-founder of instagram","text":"Interesting interview of Lex Friedman to Kevin Systrom co-founder of instagram where he talks about the beginning of the platform and overengineering. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31338698,"author":"hnrodey","month":"2022-05","story":"Many popular websites see what you type before you hit submit","stance":"reference","why":"Early Instagram silently uploaded photos before posting to feel instant","quote":"Instagram silently uploaded your photo before you actually officially posted","text":"In the early days, Instagram silently uploaded your photo before you actually officially posted as a way to improve the \"speed\" of the user experience. So go ahead and add your filters, the text for the post, etc. because then the moment you clicked Post the app presented like it happened immediately. All for the UX. Source: Kevin Systrom on Lex Fridman Podcast [VIDEO]"},{"id":29403073,"author":"BoumTAC","month":"2021-12","story":"Some thoughts on microservices","stance":"reference","why":"Instagram scaled to 50 million users on Django with a small team","quote":"scale up to 50 millions users with a small team of developers","text":"I watch the other day, the Lex Fridman podcast with Kevin Systrom, founder of Instagram. He talks about the scaling issue; they were using Django at that time and scale up to 50 millions users with a small team of developers. I do not think a majority of companies have more than 50 millions users and absolutely need to go full microservices. [VIDEO]"}]},"DUGGJpn2_zY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the primary source on Sony's ECDSA nonce-reuse blunder and the history of console cracking.","q":{"x":"I recommend watching the Console Hacking talk at 27c3 by the fail0verflow team","i":46456260,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":46456260,"author":"xav_authentique","month":"2026-01","story":"Sony PS5 ROM keys leaked – jailbreaking could be made easier with BootROM codes","stance":"rec","why":"fail0verflow explain Sony's cryptography mistake","quote":"I recommend watching the Console Hacking talk at 27c3 by the fail0verflow team","text":"If anyone is interested in the cryptography mistake that Sony made I recommend watching the Console Hacking talk at 27c3 by the fail0verflow team: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21732322,"author":"DCKing","month":"2019-12","story":"The rise and fall of the PlayStation supercomputers","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on how the PS3 was cracked","quote":"This video is really worth watching if you're interested in this stuff","text":"This doesn't take anything away from Sony's decision making, but removing OtherOS never really helped them \"get good security\". The PlayStation 3 was cracked wide open [1] without OtherOS and because people were pissed at its removal. On Playstation 3s released in the first 3.5 years of its lifespan you can get full hypervisor compromise without any hardware mods, and you can yet again run OtherOS on the latest firmware. Now with less restrictions than before. Newer PS3s (starting with CECH-25xx) have 'only' been partially compromised. They can't run OtherOS, but they can still run homebrew and piracy despite Sony taking OtherOS away. Maybe it's a bit of solace that this stuff only punished Sony. But inevitably, the lesson they learnt is \"we'll never experiment with this stuff again\". [1]: This video is really worth watching if you're interested in this stuff: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46757267,"author":"CursedSilicon","month":"2026-01","story":"Open-source self-driving for 325 car models from 27 brands","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as historical evidence of the PS3 hypervisor hack timeline","quote":"","text":"I'm a frankly rather disgusted at the comparison (or suggestion of) using an LLM to correct the record. So instead I used the 20 watt \"LLM\" behind my eyeballs running on snacks to reaffirm my memory as someone who was there. Going through old news articles and WayBackMachine where needed. Sadly your own blog was privated and not archived, which makes some things difficult. But news reposts from other sources were helpful! Back in Jan 2010, Digital Foundry did an excellent cover of your work on the PS3's hypervisor attack [1] Grabbing some choice quotes from that article: - \"the all-important decryption keys are held entirely in the SPU and can't be read by Hotz's new Hypervisor calls\" - \"The other security element is the so-called root key within the CELL itself. It's the master key to everything the PS3 processes at the very lowest level, and according to publicly available IBM documentation, it is never copied into main RAM, again making its retrieval challenging. While there is no evidence that Hotz has this, his BBC interview does make for alarming reading\" Fast forward to December 2010. 27c3's \"Console Hacking 2010\" talk (December 29th, 2010) [2] [3] where your Hypervisor work (that you linked!) is mentioned at 4:25 or so. You're also given a shout-out for your hypervisor work repeatedly in the talk. With the link you provided described at 18:25. Described as \"really unreliable\" and \"eh whatever\" due to requiring hardware modification and only granting rudimentary hyperv"},{"id":44112398,"author":"tgma","month":"2025-05","story":"There Is No Diffie-Hellman but Elliptic Curve Diffie-Hellman","stance":"reference","why":"Shows PlayStation's ECDSA nonce reuse failure","quote":"","text":"I believe the patent issue was by far the dominant friction for adoption in 2000s. On the NIST curve problem, well, maybe FUD, but evidently, they indeed backdoored the elliptic curve-based random number generator, so I would say some distrust is warranted. Irrespective of the curve issue, ed25519/x25519 is superior and has other nice properties like not catastrophically breaking if you can't generate a unique random \"k\" for ECDSA as PlayStation discovered the hard way[1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"ITTxTCz4Ums":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a liberating account of present-moment awareness and mindfulness.","q":{"x":"I think this is a liberating truth about the human mind.","i":13787582,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13787582,"author":"finkin1","month":"2017-03","story":"You May Want to Marry My Husband","stance":"rec","why":"Sam Harris on living in the present moment","quote":"I think this is a liberating truth about the human mind.","text":"\"There are ways to really live in the present moment. What's the alternative? It is always now. However much you feel you may need to plan for the future, to anticipate it, to mitigate risks, the reality of your life is now. This may sound trite... but it's the truth... As a matter of conscious experience, the reality of your life is always now. I think this is a liberating truth about the human mind. In fact, I think there is nothing more important to understand about your mind than that if you want to be happy in this world. The past is a memory. It's a thought arising in the present. The future is merely anticipated, it is another thought arising now. What we truly have is this moment. And this. And we spend most of our lives forgetting this truth. Repudiating it. Fleeing it. Overlooking it. And the horror is that we succeed. We manage to never really connect with the present moment and find fulfillment there because we are continually hoping to become happy in the future, and the future never arrives.\" - Sam Harris ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":9884064,"author":"__m","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: Do you get impatient when learning new things?","stance":"rec","why":"Mindfulness meditation to reduce pressure when learning","quote":"Maybe Mindfulness meditation can help you","text":"I would try to reduce the pressure. Are you genuinely interested in the things you need to learn? Doing the exercises will significantly increase the things you'll be able to recall. Maybe Mindfulness meditation can help you [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4163349,"author":"tokenadult","month":"2012-06","story":"Why Meditation Doesn't Directly Increase Happiness","stance":"reference","why":"Sam Harris lecture on atheist attitude to death with guided meditation","quote":"The video lecture is on the subject of the atheist attitude toward death","text":"On 25 June 2012, Jerry Coyne's Why Evolution Is True website recommended a LONG video of a lecture by Sam Harris, a meditation practitioner, from April’s Global Atheist Conference in Melbourne. The video lecture is on the subject of the atheist attitude toward death, and Harris leads his audience through a mindfulness meditation exercise as part of the video. [VIDEO] I watched the whole video, and participated in the mindfulness meditation exercise. I have to say that after listening to the whole lecture, I have the same misgivings Sam Harris has about being removed from any thoughts or judgments about the external world. There is genuine suffering in the world that it is important to do something about. There is a tension between getting in touch with the present moment and using a meaningful part of each day to solve problems and help our fellow human beings. Having lived in a predominantly Buddhist society during two different three-year stays as that society transformed from Third World colonialized poverty to First World free and democratic modernity, I'm not at all sure that most people would be more happy if most people were doing more mindfulness meditation."}]},"ZZs2HUW9tDA":{"wy":"Shared as a favorite game-development anecdote with a clear technical explanation.","q":{"x":"For those who don't know, Burton explains this in depth here","i":28814356,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38372033,"author":"LeonenTheDK","month":"2023-11","story":"Why does the moon change size when you snipe it in GTA?","stance":"rec","why":"Sonic 3D's secret level select screen as a dev anecdote.","quote":"One of my favourites is Sonic 3D's secret level select screen","text":"I love little game dev anecdotes such as these. Interesting relics of the development process. One of my favourites is Sonic 3D's secret level select screen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28814356,"author":"LaserDiscMan","month":"2021-10","story":"The Coding Secrets Hidden in “Sonic the Hedgehog” [video]","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth explanation of the Sonic 3D secret level select.","quote":"For those who don't know, Burton explains this in depth here","text":"For those who don't know, Burton explains this in depth here: [VIDEO] Another one of my favorite ones describes copy protection via intentionally destroyed floppy sectors: [LINK]"},{"id":37565023,"author":"theevilsharpie","month":"2023-09","story":"Mythbusters: Wing Commander I Edition","stance":"reference","why":"Sonic 3D level select was actually a custom crash handler.","quote":"it was a custom crash handler that the devs created that trapped all of the main CPU's error interrupts and redirected to a level select screen.","text":"This reminds me of a hack that Traveller's Tales implemented for Sonic 3D Blast on the Sega Genesis/Megadrive. If you physically wiggle or hit the game cartridge enough while the the console is powered on with the game inserted in it, a \"secret level select\" screen pops up where you can select a level. It was long considered to be an easter egg, but actually, it was a custom crash handler that the devs created that trapped all of the main CPU's error interrupts and redirected to a level select screen. They did this to avoid having their game build rejected by Sega's QA team and delaying the game's release. (Sega's QA testing was a multi-week process, and a crash would mean the build getting rejected and having to start the process over again.) When people were wiggling the game cart, they were inadvertently unplugging the cart enough for the processor to throw an error. Source (3-minute YouTube video): [VIDEO] The linked \"Coding Secrets\" YouTube channels has other videos details similar hacks, if you're interested in going down that rabbit hole. It was wild the things these devs had to do to get games to work within the hardware and business constraints of the time."}],"r":[["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1]]},"nTFEUsudhfs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an inspiring pitch for video-based, flipped-classroom learning.","q":{"x":"Watching Khan's TED talk[2] is pretty inspiring.","i":2455230,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":3849534,"author":"stevenkovar","month":"2012-04","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"Khan explains the teacher dashboard and a perspective on learning","quote":"It's a great perspective on learning and (in the context of the topic I wrote about) failure.","text":"Here's a great video of Salman Khan explaining that segment of the teacher dashboard at TED. It's a great perspective on learning and (in the context of the topic I wrote about) failure. [VIDEO]"},{"id":2455230,"author":"statenjason","month":"2011-04","story":"What math (and programming) teachers should know about memory","stance":"rec","why":"Salman Khan on flipping classrooms with video lessons","quote":"Watching Khan's TED talk[2] is pretty inspiring.","text":"> We spend an hour learning a concept, and only practice using it when we get home. This is why I'm a fan of what the Khan Academy[1] is striving to do. It flips this pattern around, with more instruction at home and practice when with the instructor. The teacher can then spend less time introducing a topic and instead working through the process with individual students. Watching Khan's TED talk[2] is pretty inspiring. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3117305,"author":"thy","month":"2011-10","story":"Why Finland’s schools are great (by doing what we don’t).","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"ok, khan's struct is well-known.","text":"Brazil here, I always have been trying to get excited with subjects that i learn. Thats when it gets easier to be an autodidact. So, my way to get things cool its to visualize them. from my entire hight school i've been doing that, for example: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] yet, still doing that on computer college [LINK] on hight school days, i even got an friend on C++ who used to compete with me. not really compete, because we would won nothing for it. instead, we colaborate. because when you compete, you can just bring your 'enemy down', to get better than him. ok, khan's struct is well-known. [VIDEO] I really always wanted to make an website where you could just do apps, basead on your classes, doodling or whatever. if i do that, i would aim on education, website internally. really running for it. and, wolfram's about programming + education [LINK] cheers!"}]},"nxeMoaxUpWk":{"wy":"Linked as the clear explainer on rotational gravity and its Coriolis oddities.","q":{"x":"Scott Manley has a great video on artificial gravity in space","i":32068779,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32068779,"author":"deepdriver","month":"2022-07","story":"Japanese researchers propose a concept for making artificial gravity on the moon","stance":"rec","why":"Covers artificial gravity concepts including early Soviet centrifuge tests in space.","quote":"Scott Manley has a great video on artificial gravity in space","text":"YouTuber Scott Manley has a great video on artificial gravity in space, including concepts that resemble the article's proposal: [VIDEO] Before I watched the video, I didn't know the Soviet Union tested a (small) centrifuge for artificial gravity in space back in the 70s: [LINK]"},{"id":29553976,"author":"samwillis","month":"2021-12","story":"NASA's new sleeping bags could prevent eyeball 'squashing' on the ISS","stance":"rec","why":"Shows rotating artificial gravity is feasible at ISS scale, radius as small as 40m.","quote":"it’s a good and informative watch","text":"Scott Manley did a video looking at this, it’s a good and informative watch: [VIDEO] The gist is that is actually doable at the same(ish) scale as the ISS. Bigger is better but we can do it at a radius as little at 40m."},{"id":44402725,"author":"MobiusHorizons","month":"2025-06","story":"Starcloud can’t put a data centre in space at $8.2M in one Starship","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant discussion of rotating gravity and its effects on movement.","quote":"Relevant Scott Manley video","text":"Relevant Scott Manley video: [VIDEO] Relevant tom Scott video: [LINK] There is a lot of research, but it’s not as simple as operating under real gravity. Makes many movements harder and can result in getting sick."},{"id":40274764,"author":"EGreg","month":"2024-05","story":"Drug resistant bacteria found on ISS mutating to become functionally distinct","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Coriolis problems of rotation-based gravity.","quote":"See 6 minutes in","text":"Why not just use rotation to generate artificial gravity via centrifugal force? I guess the answer could be that the sideways motion will drive you crazy due to coriolis forces? See 6 minutes in [VIDEO] But they can just substitute windows with monitors that compensate for the view. Incidentally, the viewscreens on Star Trek etc. always had the ship right side up, so they probably compensated exactly in this way :)"},{"id":32068506,"author":"throwaway290","month":"2022-07","story":"Japanese researchers propose a concept for making artificial gravity on the moon","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses Coriolis effect and oddities of rotational gravity.","quote":"Coriolis effect and other issues that make rotational \"gravity\" feel really weird","text":"Does this address the Coriolis effect and other issues that make rotational \"gravity\" feel really weird compared to actual gravity (6:58 [VIDEO] )?"}],"r":[["fbTrLyqL_nw",1]]},"rUg_ukBwsyo":{"wy":"Cited in steam car threads as a well-produced view of how a Doble works and how it solved startup in 1925.","q":{"x":"The video has great production and really shows the starting and driving processes","i":40532210,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40532210,"author":"snaily","month":"2024-05","story":"Steam's Last Stand","stance":"rec","why":"Great production showing starting and driving a Doble steam car","quote":"The video has great production and really shows the starting and driving processes","text":"Jay Leno has a Doble car (mentioned in the article as a 1920s attempt at a \"user-friendly\" steam car revival). The video has great production and really shows the starting and driving processes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39694386,"author":"FrustratedMonky","month":"2024-03","story":"Howard Hughes modified a 1925 Doble steam car to reach 133 MPH (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Cutaway of the Doble engine showing moving parts","quote":"This Jay Leno video is actually good.","text":"anybody have diagrams of this engine? Picture [LINK] This video opens the hood, and shows cross sections of engine. And is technical focus. [LINK] This Jay Leno video is actually good. Has cutaway of the engine to show the moving parts. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40547550,"author":"somat","month":"2024-06","story":"Steam's Last Stand","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how Doble solved steam car startup in 1925","quote":"Here is how they solved it in 1925.","text":"Here is how they solved it in 1925. I feel with our many modern advancements we could do much better. [VIDEO] That said, as neat as steam cars are, there is a reason internal combustion engines won. Probably because they are smaller, lighter and most importantly cheaper."},{"id":40537281,"author":"somat","month":"2024-05","story":"Steam's Last Stand","stance":"reference","why":"Doble as high point of steam car technology","quote":"perhaps the high point of steam car technology before that personal motive power route was abandoned.","text":"The stanly was a much earlier tech, perhaps the best car of it's generation. But compare that to the doble, perhaps the high point of steam car technology before that personal motive power route was abandoned. [VIDEO] Now admittedly the doble was a high end luxury item, But you can see how it could be possible to have a every mans steam car."},{"id":16130746,"author":"archgoon","month":"2018-01","story":"Wood gas vehicles: firewood in the fuel tank","stance":"reference","why":"Jay Leno driving a Doble steam car","quote":"I found this video of Jay Leno driving one.","text":"Thanks; I had never heard of the Doble Steam cars until now. I found this video of Jay Leno driving one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10066486,"author":"dalke","month":"2015-08","story":"Saving 25,000 Manuals","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions a handbook that came with a 1925 steam car","quote":"you can hear mention of the manual that came with a 1925 steam car.","text":"1930s isn't exactly ancient history. By 1930, for example, we had commercial aviation, a long-distance phone network, and experimental television stations. Of course we also had vacuum cleaners, steam engines, automobiles, combines, and all sorts of other things that needed manuals. At [VIDEO] you can hear mention of the manual that came with a 1925 steam car. (\"In the handbook it says 'Things for your man to do every day.'\") To be concrete, here's a copy of the instruction manual for a 51-P Crosley radio receiver from 1924: [LINK] . A \"Manual of wireless telegraphy (radio) for the use of naval electricians\" from 1915: [LINK] . And \"A practical guide for the use of the Edison phonograph\" from 1892: [LINK]"}],"r":[["ZLWMQLMiTPk",1],["O-2tpwW0kmU",1]]},"v68zYyaEmEA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the go-to explanation of optimal Wordle openers like CRANE and a friendly introduction to information theory, also relevant to Mastermind.","q":{"x":"still worth a watch.","i":37161709,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37161709,"author":"bnycum","month":"2023-08","story":"Mastermind Solver","stance":"rec","why":"Information-theory approach to Wordle, with a followup video.","quote":"still worth a watch.","text":"Reminds me of the 3Blue1Brown video about Wordle, and then it's followup video. I guess that's kind of a spoiler, but still worth a watch. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30234887,"author":"dowakin","month":"2022-02","story":"Play Wordle from Your Terminal","stance":"rec","why":"Explains optimal Wordle opening guesses like crane via information theory.","quote":"check out recent video from 3Blue1Brown","text":"If you wonder why in the demo author uses `crane` as first guess - check out recent video from 3Blue1Brown [VIDEO]"},{"id":38980453,"author":"bschne","month":"2024-01","story":"How to solve the \"Mastermind\" guessing game? (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Applies information theory to Wordle, similar to Mastermind.","quote":"has a video applying information theory to solving Wordle","text":"ICYMI, 3blue1brown (Grant Sanderson) has a video applying information theory to solving Wordle, which is conceptually similar to mastermind -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":38675447,"author":"pstuart","month":"2023-12","story":"Seven Things We Learned Analyzing 515M Wordles","stance":"reference","why":"Argues for CRANE as the optimal opener.","quote":"that CRANE is mathematically optimal","text":"Interesting that SLATE is the apparent optimum opener. I found a youtube that \"showed\" that CRANE is mathematically optimal: [VIDEO] The second guess (if a low hit count) fleshes out the remaining vowels. My average is 4, followed by 3."},{"id":30926826,"author":"sargstuff","month":"2022-04","story":"Λ-2D: Exploration of Drawing as Programming Language, Ideas from Lambda Calculus","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential Wordle reference in a rambling comment.","quote":"","text":"Warning: Bit of a 'Hitch hikers guide to the galaxy\"/conway game of life yak shave for a reply. Nice start to providing a simple way to visually interact/experiment/manipulate different multiple Neural network concepts by loading/transforming any traditional code (working or not) & reshuffling/tesselating the structual representation of the code to generate a turing tape path via color execution paths. Switching to pi calculus could provide the theory to support coroutine/coprossing/ threading\" multiple interacting tape paths/color combinations/path diffs/scaled recusion to perform assembly language instructions. Raster graphics without the yak ( [LINK] ) Feed back between tape & \"traditional code\" as neural network layer(s)! Flexiable code (via tessilations) without changing the actual imported source code! So, complete svg (tape interactions) web page as code source on how to interact with web page, ( [LINK] ) what's displayed/played, documentation & usage manual included within code structure! Unicode permits implied piping rich kern font without all the tech fluff. Unicode fails because allows infinate multics and is there for k&r incomplete. But, this approach is way more dimensional/colorful than unicode/tech stuff. ?? can one's code unintentially provide a wordle problem & solution in one package ?? ( [VIDEO] ) ?? turing complete fourier transforms [LINK] toolkit concepts: Neural networks / analog stuff : [LINK] perceptrons: [LINK] spreadsheets (: [LINK] (turing spread"}]},"xJ6Ym719urg":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a long, engaging introduction to Stamets and his fungi work.","q":{"x":"I recommend listening/watching the JRE episode with him","i":24578636,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24667554,"author":"cmsefton","month":"2020-10","story":"“Fungi Can Teach Us a New Way of Looking at the World”","stance":"rec","why":"A great talk by Stamets that inspired fungi reading.","quote":"Listened to a great talk by Paul Stamets on Jo Rogan's show","text":"Am currently reading Sheldrake's book, Entangled Life. Highly recommend it [LINK] Came across it because I've always enjoyed mushroom spotting in the nearby forests, and decided to explore them in greater detail. Listened to a great talk by Paul Stamets on Jo Rogan's show [VIDEO] Decided to look for a book to read, and Sheldrake's book popped up. So much of it has been fascinating, and it's a very easy read, too. It's incredible how these organisms raise so many questions about life, from panspermia to questions about individuality. Well worth a read!"},{"id":24578636,"author":"eluketronic","month":"2020-09","story":"The Fungal Evangelist Who Would Save the Bees","stance":"rec","why":"Long interview with Paul Stamets on fungi.","quote":"I recommend listening/watching the JRE episode with him","text":"For those interested in learning more about Stamets and his fungal endeavors, I recommend listening/watching the JRE episode with him ( [VIDEO] ) as well as the Tim Ferris Show episode ( [LINK] ). Fascinating individual."},{"id":27031798,"author":"delano","month":"2021-05","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (May 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Quantified Citizen | Senior Mobile Engineer | Senior Full Stack Engineer | Vancouver, BC | REMOTE | Full-Time Quantified Citizen is a Canadian-based mobile health startup developing solutions to disrupt the clinical and health product research markets. We empower professional researchers to easily create their own scientific studies, while building a movement towards more timely insights into the latest health research for citizens of all kinds, with a particular emphasis on psychedelic research and therapy. We are hiring experienced engineers to help us build out our mobile and web app products. We love product-minded engineers that are comfortable both managing a team and working independently, and love to contribute to product decisions. Our tech stack is built from the ground up on Elixir/Phoenix and Flutter/Dart. We launched our flagship mobile research app [1] in November 2019 on the Joe Rogan Experience [2] and have since had over 35,000 iOS app installs with a 4.8 star rating. Among our flagship studies, we've partnered with world-famous mycologist Paul Stamets [3] on a UBC research ethics approved 12,000+ participant correlational study [4] on the effects of microdosing psychedelic substances on cognitive performance and mental health. We've also partnered with award-winning cinematographer Louie Schwartzberg on a 5,000 participant Gratitude Study. Apply here: [LINK] More information about working at QC: [LINK] -- [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LIN"}]},"2JM2oImb9Qg":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever highly divisible numbers matter, such as sharding, base 12, time units and music.","q":{"x":"Here is a GREAT video on these 'anti-prime' numbers","i":17085153,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17085153,"author":"Balgair","month":"2018-05","story":"The fifth hyperfactorial: 5⁵×4⁴×3³×2²×1¹=86400000 milliseconds is exactly 1 day","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains anti-prime (highly composite) numbers.","quote":"Here is a GREAT video on these 'anti-prime' numbers","text":"Numbers like 24, 60, and 360 are 'highly composite numbers'. Here is a GREAT video on these 'anti-prime' numbers : [VIDEO] These anti-prime numbers were studied first by Ramanujan (of course) and conditions are that: 1) The prime factors must be consecutive (2^n X 3^m not 2^n X 5^m) 2) The powers of the prime factors must be deceasing as the prime number increases (2^4 X 3^3 not 2^1 X 3^5) 3) The numbers end with the the highest prime to the highest prime (2^4 X 3^1 not 2^3 X 3^2). Except for the numbers 2 and 4. More here: [LINK]"},{"id":39000578,"author":"willis936","month":"2024-01","story":"Decimal Time","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that 12 is highly factorable.","quote":"12 is a highly factorable number and 10 is not.","text":"They're all magical because they are small. 12 is a highly factorable number and 10 is not. Ancient Greece was not ignorant of this fact. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32644498,"author":"nikeee","month":"2022-08","story":"Why 12 notes in Western music?","stance":"reference","why":"Numberphile calls highly composite numbers anti-primes.","quote":"Numberphile [1] calls them \"Anti-Primes\".","text":"These numbers are called \"Highly Composite Numbers\" [0]. Basically, it is a series of numbers where each number has more factors than the number before it (and is the first number with that number of factors). As you hinted, they are especially useful if your number system does not have fractions or decimal places and you still want to divide things. You may recognize the beginning of the series: 1 2 4 6 12 24 36 48 60 120 180 240 360 720 Numberphile [1] calls them \"Anti-Primes\". [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28777442,"author":"infogulch","month":"2021-10","story":"Lessons learned from sharding Postgres at Notion","stance":"reference","why":"Numberphile video on anti-primes, numbers with more factors than smaller ones.","quote":"Numberphile made a video about \"highly composite numbers\" / \"anti-primes\", which are numbers that have more factors than all smaller numbers.","text":"Numberphile made a video about \"highly composite numbers\" / \"anti-primes\", which are numbers that have more factors than all smaller numbers. [VIDEO] There is an oeis sequence of them that starts \"1, 2, 4, 6, 12, 24, 36, 48, 60, 120, 180, 240, 360, 720, 840...\", which notably does not include 480. [LINK]"}],"r":[["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["EACkiMRT0pc",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["FkKPsLxgpuY",1],["-k3mVnRlQLU",1],["qJZ1Ez28C-A",1]]},"KT18KJouHWg":{"wy":"HN points to it as the go-to explanation of how police can identify anyone through relatives' DNA tests, in privacy and genetics threads.","q":{"x":"If you haven't watched the video Veritasium did about the Golden State Killer, I highly recommend it.","i":30341128,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30341128,"author":"falcolas","month":"2022-02","story":"San Francisco police linked a woman to a crime using DNA from her rape exam","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Details how police can reach anyone's DNA through relatives in databases.","quote":"If you haven't watched the video Veritasium did about the Golden State Killer, I highly recommend it.","text":"DNA is a genie that's flown far and free from their bottle. Even if you are not in the system directly (from, say, 23&me, rape kits, arrests, etc), someone within 2-3 degrees of separation on your family tree is. It's enough for you to be identified in most cases. If you haven't watched the video Veritasium did about the Golden State Killer, I highly recommend it. It details quite nicely how if anybody has your DNA sample, the police have access to it. [VIDEO] Bonus points for noticing how many of these company reps who tout \"nothing to hide\" like protecting their own privacy."},{"id":38623476,"author":"rocho","month":"2023-12","story":"23andMe frantically changed its terms of service to prevent customers from suing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Even my brother and I share only ~50% DNA. This is completely false. Any two random humans have more than 99% overlap by virtue of being the same species. It's even higher for brothers. We also share around 90% DNA with cats, dogs and elephants. [LINK] > I'm not too worried about it because it's never a 100% overlap. This doesn't make sense. If they were equal, you'd be the same person except for environmental differences. Many applications don't need equal DNAs. E.g. [VIDEO] > About insurance companies, they're legally forbidden to use such data. This is a very weak argument. There's a long history of companies doing illegal things, and even if it's illegal today it doesn't mean it'll be illegal tomorrow."},{"id":36242425,"author":"morebortplates","month":"2023-06","story":"We can ID people from DNA that shows up in environmental studies","stance":"reference","why":"Video about the Golden State Killer case.","quote":"Video by Veritassium about the case","text":"That's exactly how the so called Golden State Killer was caught. [LINK] Video by Veritassium about the case: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30344797,"author":"afiori","month":"2022-02","story":"San Francisco police linked a woman to a crime using DNA from her rape exam","stance":"reference","why":"Shows identification from remote relatives' DNA via family trees.","quote":"","text":"Finding people from remote relatives and medical inferences. If someone has access to enough nicely distributed DNA samples they can correctly identify you from your DNA even if you are not in any database only based on your family tree. Varitasium video for reference: [VIDEO] This is generally time consuming and done to identify criminals but it is very simple."},{"id":29085045,"author":"donatj","month":"2021-11","story":"Facebook plans to shut down its facial recognition system","stance":"reference","why":"Shows people can be identified via distant relatives' DNA.","quote":"Basically they can find you from your DNA, even if only a couple very distant relatives ever gave theirs.","text":"If you don't like that, you're going to hate this. [VIDEO] Basically they can find you from your DNA, even if only a couple very distant relatives ever gave theirs."},{"id":29104972,"author":"aaron695","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: Have you done a DNA test for health reasons and learned anything useful?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Not directly useful to me. In hind-site I guess obvious. All the solutions you should do anyway. Lung cancer, any cancer, Alzheimer's, heart disease - Don't smoke, filter your air, don't drink (to much), lower stress, eat healthy, have good relationships with people. My skin cancer was low, should I wear sunscreen less? Maybe it breaks some peoples self denial or gives them a kick in the ass? Definitely do it though a Chinese company if you can. Keep it out of the hands of governments that control you. Probably you are screwed anyway on this - \"How 1 Person Can Expose The Identities of 1000\" - Veritasium - [VIDEO] I think it's good to get control of your body. Maybe it's busy work. Maybe it's not."},{"id":29149902,"author":"timmytokyo","month":"2021-11","story":"All Those 23andMe Spit Tests Were Part of a Bigger Plan","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Except that this kind of person is related to other people who might have different views on such consent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28778026,"author":"johncessna","month":"2021-10","story":"Investigators say they've finally identified the Zodiac Killer","stance":"reference","why":"Video on techniques used to find the Golden State Killer.","quote":"I just finished watching Veritasium's [1] latest video which featured the techniques for finding the Golden State Killer","text":"Weird, I just finished watching Veritasium's [1] latest video which featured the techniques for finding the Golden State Killer, and saw this one come across the feed. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28796211,"author":"nextaccountic","month":"2021-10","story":"Why kidney failure led me to found an Health Tech Startup","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how relatives' DNA tests let police match you.","quote":"Here's a video how law enforcement can get a match on a dna database through any of your relatives that did a dna test there.","text":"Do they store the dna in a database that law enforcement could, perhaps with a subpoena, have access to? Here's a video how law enforcement can get a match on a dna database through any of your relatives that did a dna test there. [VIDEO] This kind of thing creates a chilling effect in which people avoid getting the medical help they need just to not contribute with a permanent genetic database. This kind of stuff is exactly why HIPAA exists. Also: why did they give you a summary, but gave the doctors a full report? Could you get the full report if you asked them?"}],"r":[["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["v19cu2hj2Ms",1],["SCAGc-rkIfo",1]]},"eECjjLNAOd4":{"wy":"Shared as the definitive retelling of one of the dumbest online arguments ever.","q":{"x":"Jon Bois did an amazing video about this one","i":43698734,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43698734,"author":"wolrah","month":"2025-04","story":"4chan Sharty Hack And Janitor Email Leak","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jon Bois on the dumbest online argument","quote":"Jon Bois did an amazing video about this one","text":"> Obligatory post about the dumbest argument to ever be had online Jon Bois did an amazing video about this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27121553,"author":"1_player","month":"2021-05","story":"●●|●●●●●|●●|●","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Body builders try to figure out how many days are in a week, summary dramatised here","text":"LEMMiNO has a nice 18 minutes documentary and summary on Cicada 3301: [LINK] -- Some \"internet lore\" off the top of my head: * Body builders try to figure out how many days are in a week, summary dramatised here: [VIDEO] * Time Cube: [LINK] -- EDIT: speaking of SilvaGunner, I've never heard this name before, I found two mentions of it on two unrelated YouTube videos in the past 3 hours. Incredible. Baader–Meinhof strikes again."},{"id":32715248,"author":"smcl","month":"2022-09","story":"I tricked myself into working out 3 times per week","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"you don't count the day you're on when you're counting days","text":"Hm, if they switched the schedule up a little and had a workout every other day (with a rest day in between) they could have been working out 4-5 times per week. If anyone's unsure about the maths on that then don't worry, \"you don't count the day you're on when you're counting days \" ;-) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-5wpm-gesOY",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1]]},"txZq7Laz7_4":{"wy":"HN commenters say it convinced them to drop other layout approaches and embrace Grid, and cite its advice on fallbacks for older browsers.","q":{"x":"it has convinced me to stop using other layouts/frameworks and just embrace grid","i":17161956,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17161956,"author":"rsoto","month":"2018-05","story":"Best Practices with CSS Grid Layout","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced commenter to embrace CSS grid over other layouts","quote":"it has convinced me to stop using other layouts/frameworks and just embrace grid","text":"I'm reading the comments and I think there's a lot of misunderstanding about what CSS Grid is. Mostly because the spec is big and there's not just one way to do things, so it gets mixed with flexbox and tables. I recommend watching last year's Morten's CSS grid presentation[1] as it has convinced me to stop using other layouts/frameworks and just embrace grid, which is truly amazing and it has been a blast working with it and making responsive websites without worrying too much about the markup. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18756663,"author":"BlackMonday","month":"2018-12","story":"CSS grid garden","stance":"reference","why":"Serve mobile layout to browsers lacking grid","quote":"a mobile layout like described in the video","text":"If possible you could alternatively have send her (every browser which doesn't support full grid) a mobile layout like described in the video: [VIDEO] > From this follows a simple but surprising truth: The lack of support for CSS grid in an old browser should not affect the experience of the visitor, but rather just make the experience different. > If you agree with this (and you should), there is no reason you can’t use CSS grid today! > Here’s how that approach could work in practice. Rather than using fallbacks and shims to ensure a design and layout look the same across all browsers, we’d provide the mobile vertical single-column layout to all browsers and then serve up advanced functionality to those browsers and viewport widths that can take advantage of them. It might sound like progressive enhancement, but it’s actually more of an accessibility-centric approach enabled by a mental shift. [LINK]"},{"id":17620826,"author":"keth","month":"2018-07","story":"Show HN: Layoutit – An interactive CSS Grid generator","stance":"reference","why":"Suggests shipping mobile layout for non-grid browsers","quote":"it was suggested to just ship a mobile layout for browsers that don't support css grid","text":"I remember a video + blog post about this, and it was suggested to just ship a mobile layout for browsers that don't support css grid. At least for personal sites that seemed reasonable. EDIT: [VIDEO]"}]},"-zt57TWkTF4":{"wy":"HN cites it as concrete data on how a big YouTube channel diversifies revenue and how little comes from AdSense.","q":{"x":"Linus tech tips did an enlightening breakdown video of their earnings","i":27282026,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27282026,"author":"dan1234","month":"2021-05","story":"YouTube Will Monetize Videos Without Paying Content Creators","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown showing YouTube ads are about 25% of revenue.","quote":"Linus tech tips did an enlightening breakdown video of their earnings","text":"Linus tech tips did an enlightening breakdown video of their earnings and YouTube ads make up around 25% of their revenue. I think they have to upload to YouTube because that’s where their audience is (IIRC it’s the world’s second largest search engine). There are lots of channels that I’ve only discovered as related videos to other videos I’ve watched. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43398915,"author":"mrweasel","month":"2025-03","story":"Block YouTube ads on AppleTV by decrypting and stripping ads from Profobuf (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"YouTube Premium revenue is larger than expected, under 20% of total.","quote":"Linus highlighted was that YouTube Premium revenue was much larger than most would expect.","text":"Linus tech tips had a break down of their income. One thing Linus highlighted was that YouTube Premium revenue was much larger than most would expect. See [VIDEO] (it's a little under 20% of their total revenue from YouTube)."},{"id":37978652,"author":"ziml77","month":"2023-10","story":"Dave Cutler: The Secret History of Microsoft Windows [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Premium views are worth more than ad-supported views.","quote":"","text":"I know I heard a rough number at some point of the value of a Premium view vs an ad supported view. Can't find it without digging, but here's a source for them being a good amount more valuable [VIDEO]"},{"id":34227034,"author":"lathiat","month":"2023-01","story":"My YouTube Earnings","stance":"reference","why":"Detailed video of the channel's published earnings.","quote":"Linus Tech Tips has published their earnings for the past couple of years.","text":"That used to be the case but it changed at some point I think about 2 years ago - couldn't find a quick reference to exactly when it changed but I remember seeing quite a few channels start publishing their earnings once it changed. This quora post quotes the terms: [LINK] \"HOWEVER, YOU MAY ACCURATELY DISCLOSE THE AMOUNT OF GOOGLE’S GROSS PAYMENTS TO YOU PURSUANT TO THE PROGRAM\" Linus Tech Tips has published their earnings for the past couple of years. One detailed video here: [VIDEO] Linus also posted his adsense of ~$4.6million for the last 9 months a couple of days ago: [LINK]"},{"id":33085385,"author":"faller_slive","month":"2022-10","story":"YouTube locks 4K playback behind Premium subscriptions in latest test","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Linus Tech Tips only made 26% of their revenue from YouTube AdSense in 2020.","text":"I suppose it depends on the size of the channel. If you have more subscribers than you can negotiate higher pay for in video ad placements. From channels that I have heard talk about it, google ads make up a minority of their revenue. Perhaps it is not the most representative example but Linus Tech Tips only made 26% of their revenue from YouTube AdSense in 2020. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31817552,"author":"kadoban","month":"2022-06","story":"Contra Wirecutter on the IKEA air purifier","stance":"reference","why":"Summary of the channel's stance on sponsors and funding.","quote":"is probably a good summary on Linus's thoughts on sponsors/funding.","text":"> Their opinion changes depending on who is sponsoring them, no? No, to the extent humanly possible. They have reviews, which are not sponsored by anyone having anything to do with the product being reviewed. Those are their actual opinions and evaluations of products. Separately they have sponsored showcase videos, or whatever they call them, where they won't say anything untrue, but they're just showing off whatever it is, not really evaluating it deeply. These videos are _very_ obviously different, and very obviously labelled. LTT has top tier policies and openness about these issues compared to really anyone on youtube or anywhere. More anecdotally, I've seen them give takes on previous or current sponsors that were _very_ not in their financial best interests. If sponsors are paying for easy coverage, they're not getting their money's worth. > Well, no, it would work way better if he had no bias. That would just be lying. There are no humans without biases. [VIDEO] is probably a good summary on Linus's thoughts on sponsors/funding. If you trust what he says or not is up to you. I do though."},{"id":30667163,"author":"old_man_jenkins","month":"2022-03","story":"Vanced has been discontinued","stance":"reference","why":"Premium viewers contribute more to creators than ad viewers.","quote":"","text":"While YouTube still keeps a lot of your money when you pay for premium, you actually contribute more towards a creator if you watch with premium rather than ads[0]. [0] [VIDEO] (Timestamp 6:50)"},{"id":28586648,"author":"judge2020","month":"2021-09","story":"Bye YouTube, Hello PeerTube [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A lot of people see the usefulness of PeerTube but don't move because the creators they watch are on YouTube. It's the same for the creators, though - they can't move off of YouTube entirely, because none of their viewers will go to a PeerTube instance just to watch that one creator. Thus nobody moves to PT unless there are other factors (like extremist creators moving because their videos keep getting removed by YT). Another major problem that prevents creators from hosting on PeerTube is that there are no ads - many see direct-from-YouTube revenue driven by Premium views/Ad views generate a significant portion of their income. Even Linus Tech Tips, with their audience of gamers who mostly running an ad blocker, sees YouTube generate 26% of their profit[0], almost exactly as much as they get from sponsors spots in their videos. If everyone were to move to PT, we'd either need an intermediary for ads, or only creators with audiences that could afford to personally finance them (via Patreon or similar) would be able to survive and make a living off of running their channel. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28589095,"author":"mastazi","month":"2021-09","story":"Bye YouTube, Hello PeerTube [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Ads is a stable revenue stream for these content creators. Ads are a revenue stream mostly for the platform e.g. YouTube itself. You can have a look at this[1] or any other \"how my YouTube channel makes money\" video to see that's the case. In-video ads, on the other hand, are indeed a significant revenue stream for creators, but those have nothing to do with the platform and everything to do with the single creator. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27672034,"author":"novok","month":"2021-06","story":"The 'Fuck You' Pattern","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"So I turned off adblock, but I see no ads, even though you have some ad domains. Is imgflip pro the way you make money only? I tried again and all I saw is one banner ad, sometimes. Do you have more ads more frequently, or is it just a relatively minimal amount in general? What is the revenue split between pro & ads? For linus for example, it's about %26 youtube ads, [VIDEO] How do you deal with moderation? Is it automated, I see you even have comments? Or is it just report buttons and your takedown form? How is the support load? What is your tech stack? PHP? Python? C#? Java? Golang?"},{"id":25709062,"author":"judge2020","month":"2021-01","story":"PeerTube v3","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That's kind of an issue then - YouTube is only partly (probably a single-digit-percent given they dropped the direct share feature[0]) about sharing videos with friends - most people stick around for the recommendations, ie. the endless stream of interesting/intriguing/funny content. Most of that content currently dominating YT (at least, at the quantity that it's produced) is only possible due to these creators making YouTube their full-time job and getting paid to do it. Linus Tech Tips (which also runs Techquickie and Techlinked) attributes 26% of their total revenue to YT Ads[1], and the other categories (like merchandise/affiliate links) aren't something everyone can do if their videos aren't about products available on Amazon or can't grow their merch at the rate LTT does. If PT is just about sharing videos, then you might as well run a nextcloud instance instead since 'no ads' is a huge downside to PT for creators. 0: [LINK] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24766356,"author":"slimginz","month":"2020-10","story":"4000° Plasma Lightsaber Build (Retractable Blade) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how a large channel diversifies revenue streams.","quote":"Linus Tech Tips actually just released a video[1] on how they make money","text":"Not quite the same since it's a much larger team but Linus Tech Tips actually just released a video[1] on how they make money and it shows that diversifying your revenue stream as much as possible is key to be successful on YouTube. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24937540,"author":"drewg123","month":"2020-10","story":"Alphabet announces Q3 2020 earnings","stance":"reference","why":"Pie chart of channel revenue: ads 26%, promos 27%.","quote":"The time code takes you to a pie chart breaking down their revenue.","text":"I always thought creators made roughly as much or more from the promos they embed in the videos. See the LTT video about how they make money: [VIDEO] The time code takes you to a pie chart breaking down their revenue. YT ads are 26% and in-video promos are 27%"}]},"1-vcErOPofQ":{"wy":"Brought up whenever latency, light speed or the size of a nanosecond comes up, as the classic physical demonstration.","q":{"x":"this seminal scene between the great, late Grace Hopper and David Letterman","i":15890761,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15890761,"author":"bouvin","month":"2017-12","story":"The fifth hyperfactorial: 5⁵ × 4⁴ × 3³ × 2² × 1¹ milliseconds is exactly 1 day","stance":"rec","why":"Grace Hopper shows Letterman the foot-per-nanosecond wire demonstration","quote":"this seminal scene between the great, late Grace Hopper and David Letterman","text":"No mention of the foot per nanosecond is complete without this seminal scene between the great, late Grace Hopper and David Letterman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17573272,"author":"adrianN","month":"2018-07","story":"Traveling to the Sun: Why Won't Parker Solar Probe Melt?","stance":"reference","why":"Grace Hopper illustrating that a light-nanosecond is about one foot","quote":"A lightnanosecond is about 30cm, or 1 foot.","text":"Lightnanoseconds are a useful measure in everyday life as well. A lightnanosecond is about 30cm, or 1 foot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12988483,"author":"baldfat","month":"2016-11","story":"SpaceX plans worldwide satellite Internet with low latency, gigabit speed","stance":"reference","why":"Grace Hopper explains a nanosecond as the distance light travels in a foot","quote":"Grace Hopper telling David Letterman what a nano second is!","text":"Latency Math (Almost 100ms faster then current technology) EDIT: This is only one way ALSO Grace Hopper telling David Letterman what a nano second is! [VIDEO] 1 Nanosecond = distance light travels one foot 1 mile = 5260 nanoseconds (In a vacuum) Current Satellite Internet = 22,000 miles or 115720000 Nano Seconds = 115.72 ms SpaceX Low Orbit = 715 to 823 Miles 823 miles = 4328980 nano seconds = 4.3 ms"}]},"3t6L-FlfeaI":{"wy":"Used as a shorthand joke for Google-scale bureaucracy and the difficulty of simple tasks at large companies.","q":{"x":"here's the video where it originated","i":29032656,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41021312,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2024-07","story":"Google URL Shortener links will no longer be available","stance":"rec","why":"Satire of Google infrastructure circa 2010","quote":"Have you seen the Broccoli Man short?","text":"Ha ha ha. Have you seen the Broccoli Man short? And that was a satire of what Google's infrastructure looked like in 2010 . [VIDEO]"},{"id":43876078,"author":"anenefan","month":"2025-05","story":"Ask HN: Cloud Costs What am I missing?","stance":"reference","why":"2010 video on frustrating file storage","quote":"","text":"It's way over my head but aware it already was quite intricate and frustrating [1] [1a] at times. Finding a good stable online file storage area is getting harder, especially the last few years where people have been caught out when fires or other catastrophes [2] occur. [1] [VIDEO] [video - 2010] [1a] [LINK] *A more universal link to a mp4 (15 megs) or mkv (5 megs) download as per the original youtube link since imposed restrictions youtube likes to impose on some people from time to time. [2] [LINK]"},{"id":43525690,"author":"alexpotato","month":"2025-03","story":"The Mediocrity of Modern Google","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Google's big-company dysfunction","quote":"","text":"A couple of things have struck me about Google as an outsider: - Learning that they had 100K+ employees - Seeing resumes from people who had spent 5+ years at Google and had a project list with what I would expect from <1 year at a hedge fund or <3 years at a bank - Learning about the \"I want to just serve 5 Terabytes\" video/story [0] - Hiring people away from Google who were, for example, network engineers who turned out to be great SREs with good programming skills - They just kill off greats apps that people enjoy with little to no notice (granted, keeping stuff around forever ala AWS is its own issue) In other words, they became a big corporate behemoth and lost a lot of their \"spark\". Which is sad b/c I remember the early days of using Google search, Google maps etc where it felt like we were all living in the future. Sure, they are breaking ground in the AI space but 100% agree that it feels like they are on the downward trajectory. (although other firms have been here and come back so let's see...) 0 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":41080973,"author":"hintymad","month":"2024-07","story":"Fear of over-engineering has killed engineering altogether","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates simple tasks getting stalled by organizational overhead","quote":"","text":"Maybe fear killed engineering. Even in a decently managed company, I can see so many engineering effort gets stalled, compromised, bastardized, or killed. Like your manager asks you to get sign-offs from 10 different orgs. Like you want to do a POC, yet somehow an infra team demanded that you integrate with their system even though your targeted users didn't give a shit. Like you just want to submit a workflow to Temporal Cloud yet a product manager demands that you create an abstraction because he is afraid of X and Y. Like you just want to serve 5TB of data, and you got sucked into this: [VIDEO] . I'm not sure how companies battle this kind of fear. It looks Amazon's Working Backwards, Netflix's Freedom and Responsibility, Uber's Let Builders Build more or less can counter such fear, but ultimately it's about finding the right people who have good sense of product and who strive to make progress."},{"id":40273448,"author":"huseyinkeles","month":"2024-05","story":"Microsoft CTO: Thoughts on OpenAI (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"2010s video showing similar issues back then","quote":"This video [0] was from 2010s, and seems like they had similar issues even back then.","text":"This video [0] was from 2010s, and seems like they had similar issues even back then. (never worked at Google) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39831736,"author":"choppaface","month":"2024-03","story":"Why Google failed to make GPT-3","stance":"reference","why":"Google's internal resource marketplaces","quote":"Google has had complex \"marketplaces\" around company resources for years","text":"There are only so many TPUs available and they're kept at 100% utilization. Google has had complex \"marketplaces\" around company resources for years: [VIDEO] It's a cultural value, like playing board games."},{"id":34385570,"author":"moandcompany","month":"2023-01","story":"Internal document explains why Google has become slow and bureaucratic","stance":"reference","why":"Humorous take on why simple things are hard at Google","quote":"Refer to the red panda that merely wants to serve 5TB of data.","text":"I am a Xoogler. The source document/presentation (\"Coordination Headwinds\") and idea are many years old, and from what I recall were not aimed to be critical of the company and its organization, rather to give its employee readers a feeling of solace and understanding for why getting seemingly small things done can feel overly hard and slow… a less humorous, and less SWE-specific version of \"Broccoliman\" if you will. Refer to the red panda that merely wants to serve 5TB of data. - [VIDEO] - [LINK] I’ll try to continue using the organism-metaphor in my commentary. While the “slime mold” story provides a convenient and charitable explanation of Google's bureaucracy and dysfunctions, another explanation is: - As an organism, Google's executive decision-making functions are severely impaired by lack of accurate information, dysfunction of its middle-management “organs” or tiers, and impedance mismatches arising from genetic encodings or mental models associated with the slime-mold idea. - Despite being on a mission to “organize the world's information and make it universally accessible and useful,” Google is far less successful at doing this with its own information. These characteristics are not unique to Google, but some of Google’s cultural history makes this a particularly challenging problem. It may be convenient to think of Google as a slime-mold organism, and in an individual contributor’s day-to-day engineering activities it may very well be, but as a corporation it is no"},{"id":33753766,"author":"hansvm","month":"2022-11","story":"I/O is no longer the bottleneck","stance":"reference","why":"Google-scale expectations even for small services","quote":"","text":"Yeah, when I was there I saw plenty of <1% optimizations saving REDACTED gobs of money, and people were rewarded for it. I don't think it's applicable to most teams though. Imagine a foo/bar/widget app that only serves 20B people (obvious exaggeration to illustrate the point) and is only necessary up to a few hundred times per day. You can handle that sort of traffic on a laptop on my home router and still have enough hootzpah left to stream netflix. I mean, you are Google, and you need to do something better than that [0], but the hardware for your project is going to be negligible compared to other concerns unless you're doing FHE or video transcoding or something extraordinarily expensive. Walk that backward to, how many teams have 20B users or are doing extraordinarily expensive things? I don't have any clue, but when you look at public examples of cheap things that never got much traction and probably had a suite of engineers [1], I'd imagine it's not everyone in any case. You're probably mostly looking at people with enough seniority to be able to choose to work on core code affecting most services. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":29481070,"author":"sangnoir","month":"2021-12","story":"AWS us-east-1 outage","stance":"reference","why":"Reminder that multi-region rigor comes with costs","quote":"unyielding multi-region rigor comes with costs","text":"> At a different (unnamed) FAANG I'm guessing Google, on the basis of the recently published (to the public) \"I just want to serve 5TB\"[1] video. If it isn't Google, then the broccoli man video is still a cogent reminder that unyielding multi-region rigor comes with costs. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29032656,"author":"benley","month":"2021-10","story":"I don’t know how to count that low","stance":"reference","why":"Original source of the phrase about counting that low","quote":"here's the video where it originated","text":"Regarding the phrase \"I don't know how to count that low\", here's the video where it originated. Frankly I'm astonished this hasn't been shared before, and I hope I don't get in trouble for posting it (it's like 11 years old, surely nobody cares at this point, right?) [VIDEO]"},{"id":46554030,"author":"anymouse123456","month":"2026-01","story":"The Jeff Dean Facts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My other favorite internal internal meme from that era was the Broccoli Man video","text":"My other favorite internal internal meme from that era was the Broccoli Man video, \"I just want to serve 5 Terabytes.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":46224547,"author":"kevindamm","month":"2025-12","story":"Getting a Gemini API key is an exercise in frustration","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Broccoli Man! classic","text":"Broccoli Man! classic [VIDEO] (2010) To be fair, a lot of this changed after that video became a meme.. but I'd bet that the broccoli man template is still trending on memegen"},{"id":43992771,"author":"dijit","month":"2025-05","story":"Working on complex systems: What I learned working at Google","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This is probably the most accurate statement possible.","text":"This is probably the most accurate statement possible. “I just want to store 5TiB somewhere” “Ha! Did you book multiple bigtable cells” [VIDEO]"},{"id":40833820,"author":"lesuorac","month":"2024-06","story":"Ask HN: What is the best code base you ever worked on?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"To quote broccoli man, \"i've forgotten how to count that low","text":"millions sounds like a lot but it's like just 3 employees. To quote broccoli man, \"i've forgotten how to count that low\" [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36253607,"author":"jedmeyers","month":"2023-06","story":"Google cuts office space in Bay Area by more than a million square feet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the Google guy might have tried to spend time scaling a product that will have five concurrent users","text":"You might have had 20 years of experience building products that don't need to scale for millions of concurrent users, if you consider all \"purely internal architectural changes\" to be lower priority than the customer facing work just because it's purely internal. And the Google guy might have tried to spend time scaling a product that will have five concurrent users, because \"this is Google\" ( [VIDEO] ). It's all about perspective, and CEO thought the other perspective was more valuable."},{"id":31382982,"author":"codethief","month":"2022-05","story":"Monarch: Google’s Planet-Scale In-Memory Time Series Database","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the hilarious \"I just want to serve 5 terabytes\" video[0]","text":"The first time I heard about Monarch was in discussions about the hilarious \"I just want to serve 5 terabytes\" video[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31485607,"author":"ma2rten","month":"2022-05","story":"Imagen, a text-to-image diffusion model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"People don't want to deal with broccoli man [1].","text":"You mean like: [LINK] This is common in the research PA. People don't want to deal with broccoli man [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30059020,"author":"teddyh","month":"2022-01","story":"The Curse of NixOS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Noone has borgmon readability.","text":"> The worst is, of course, GCL/borgcfg, the (turing complete) configuration language for Google's internal job scheduling software. “Noone has borgmon readability.” — Broccoli Man ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":46050237,"author":"PlanksVariable","month":"2025-11","story":"Broccoli Man, Remastered","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the overtly robotic voices added to the humor of the original","text":"Have you ever watched the original? It most certainly did not involve real people talking ( [VIDEO] ). In fact, the overtly robotic voices added to the humor of the original, IMO. It was lost in this translation."},{"id":41066968,"author":"pas","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Am I crazy or is Android development awful?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's the classic \"I would like to serve 5 terabytes\"[0] Google problem.","text":"Ridiculously low-quality documentation, a build and development system that puts the local Rube Goldberg competition winner to shame, constant churn, total deafness or willful ignoring of wishes of dev ... and so on have nothing to do with what happened 20 years ago. It's the classic \"I would like to serve 5 terabytes\"[0] Google problem. (Their engineering culture reeks of this. See the tools they have released. Angular, Bazel, Go, Kubernetes, etc. The last one escaped the usual decline of incompetence because the community showed up. Go has GOPATH. Even in a language designed for \"simplicity\" initially getting it set up had this roadblock.) And no doubt the roots of the problem are manifold, but it seems all stemming from ongoing management incompetence (creation of shareholder value[1] maaaybe excluded, though I am very curious what kind of inane metrics the Android SDK team is whipped to chase). [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"}],"r":[["WlT3UhC7NwQ",1]]},"6xWXRk3yaSw":{"wy":"Mostly cited in discussions of Fisker's troubles and of whether a negative review can sink a car company.","m":[{"id":39650425,"author":"Arnavion","month":"2024-03","story":"MKBHD, Fisker, and \"the worst car ever reviewed\"","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed review of everything wrong with the Fisker Ocean.","quote":"Watch it yourself","text":"It's a 20min video and a little over half of it is everything that's bad, so it's too long to summarize. Watch it yourself: [VIDEO] 8:10 onwards. Most of them could be fixed by software, though the problem with the steering buttons being too close to the hand grip (14:11) may not be?"},{"id":46975595,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2026-02","story":"Rivian R2: Electric Mid-Size SUV","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"MKBHD has gone into quality issues at times: see for example Cybertruck [1] and Fisker Ocean [2].","text":"> None of these car reviewers ever take into account build quality and customer issues. Because most car reviewers' job is to explain new releases. Most issues arise after time, which reviewers generally don't get. MKBHD has gone into quality issues at times: see for example Cybertruck [1] and Fisker Ocean [2]. In their Q&A videos, the couple that does Motormouth [3] due mention reliability when asked for recommendations. There are sources for reliability assessments, like J. D. Power and Consumer Reports. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":41654404,"author":"nozzlegear","month":"2024-09","story":"Mkbsd: Download all the wallpapers in MKBHD's \"Panels\" app for free","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"a review of the Fisker Ocean², lead to accusations that he killed the company behind the car due to the negative review.","text":"How does what you said jive with, for example, his review of the Humane AI Pin¹, where he reviewed the product so negatively that he received flak on social media for being too harsh and careless with his influence. Another video of his, a review of the Fisker Ocean², lead to accusations that he killed the company behind the car due to the negative review. ¹ MKBHD: The Worst Product I've Ever Reviewed... For Now [LINK] ² Auto Focus: This is the Worst Car I've Ever Reviewed [VIDEO] ³ MKBHD: Do Bad Reviews Kill Companies? [LINK]"},{"id":39920782,"author":"stoorafa","month":"2024-04","story":"Fisker's Ocean SUV investigated for doors that won't open","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Also got a bad review from MKBHD on YouTube, which can’t help","text":"Their cars recently significantly dropped in price, and it seems clear why [1]. Also got a bad review from MKBHD on YouTube, which can’t help[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["1K5oDtVAYzk",1],["aO3JgPUJ6iQ",1]]},"8q71hrwUcu0":{"wy":"HN cites it in discussions of occupational licensing and rent-seeking as a libertarian argument.","q":{"x":"I think Milton Friedman had some great (albeit biased) lectures","i":11055107,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11055107,"author":"EGreg","month":"2016-02","story":"The Rent-Seeking Is Too Damn High","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman's libertarian analysis of occupational licensing","quote":"I think Milton Friedman had some great (albeit biased) lectures","text":"I think Milton Friedman had some great (albeit biased) lectures in which he analyzed these issues from a consequentialist libertarian point of view: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21302694,"author":"snagglegaggle","month":"2019-10","story":"When Medical Debt Collectors Decide Who Gets Arrested","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on how regulation leads to central planning","quote":"He explains how regulation eventually results in a centrally planned setup","text":"Careful, \"for-profit\" healthcare has a lot of government controls on it. Single payer is only obvious after you add a lot of controls to the market. See [VIDEO] and the more complete [LINK] . He explains how regulation eventually results in a centrally planned setup that is less efficient than what used to happen."},{"id":9121807,"author":"tolmasky","month":"2015-02","story":"Engineering ethics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The issues discussed in the post are about pretty malicious acts, basically writing spyware and malware. I'm not sure how a Professional Engineer exam will really address this. We are mixing in a lot of unrelated things (tests, apprenticeship, etc) to guise what this actually is: a club where existing members are the gate keepers for new members. This is the circular part, it serves the purposes of the members, not the customers -- notice how the users and customers don't figure into any of the 4 steps you mentioned in licensure at all -- they aren't empowered, they are now \"protected\" by the very same set of people we determined needing wrangling in in the first place. Aside from the trivial chicken and egg problems (who decides the initial PEs? who decides the first exam?), we have the very real concern that the most connected current software engineers in government are precisely the ones that are probably currently breaking the code of ethics (writing malware for national security purposes). This highlights the issue with \"self judgment\" and makes me incredibly skeptical of the opinions of such a potential licensing committee. Software engineering is not mechanical engineering. There's very little known \"for sure\", and it doesn't compare to the kind of experience we have building a bridge (there are people who can make really good arguments that mutability is \"unsafe\" for customers, or that strcpy vs strncpy, or hell, C being used at all). This makes this area ripe for ab"}],"r":[["ZMByI4s-D-Y",1],["PHe0bXAIuk0",1],["qOP2V_np2c0",1],["xoVJKj8lcNQ",1],["_fHfgU8oMSo",1],["ipRvjS7q1DI",1]]},"9KR2N_Q8ep8":{"wy":"Cited in car-safety threads as evidence that Tesla optimizes for real-world crash data rather than just standardized crash tests.","q":{"x":"What they claim to do there is to use their real world data to try to optimize for real crashs and not for crash tests.","i":29190678,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28860834,"author":"throwawaysea","month":"2021-10","story":"Tesla unveils its new crash safety tech based on collecting real-world data","stance":"rec","why":"Tesla uses real crash data and simulation to tune safety systems","quote":"shows a very cool approach to safety.","text":"For those who don't have the time, the video this article is summarizing ( [VIDEO] ) shows a very cool approach to safety. Tesla takes real-life crash data from their cars, charts the various ways in which collisions take place, tunes crash responses (like airbag deployment and seatbelt restraint) for each situation based on simulations, and produces a more optimally safe car as a result. They also are able to validate safety of passengers virtually using this method. This video includes a crash test they did of a non-standard crash that isn't covered by regulatory bodies but isn't uncommon in real life driving, and they use it to prove that their simulation approach works. My only concern is that Tesla builds all this on a vast body of data they collect from their drivers. This feels like a privacy problem, and I wish they would make this type of program opt-in."},{"id":30184684,"author":"maxdo","month":"2022-02","story":"Normalized crash data shows Autopilot is much less safe than Tesla claims","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sure, [LINK] [VIDEO] Tesla roll out cars in generations. So just take a $40k Model 3/Y platform was introduced in 2017 and compare it with any car been introduced that year. My friend bought a BMW in 2019 for similar money, it doesn't have half of that. Maybe you can have them but you have to buy extra packages for extra money. Tesla don't sell safety features for extra price. The only extra feature is Autopilot. But hardware, camera's , lane departure avoidance, blind spot collision warning etc in every car. You can even take such a simple feature as forward collision warning system. When you drive it is super accurate it uses the same data and models from Autopilot to predict when there is a crash. If your slowing down dynamic is not good or the car in front/back change it dynamics it will warn you immediately. Only this feature saved me several times from collision. I'm not a good driver. As I said in 2022 there are many car brands that catching up in some cases they doing better. And this is good. Human is a bad, constantly distracted driver. Technology is the only way to make our life safer."},{"id":29644590,"author":"ricardobeat","month":"2021-12","story":"2021-22 Tesla Model Y earns highest IIHS accolade","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla talks about its safety program","quote":"They've release a video recently talking about their safety program","text":"You're probably imagining something like the ID4 test: [LINK] or how the Model 3 fared in 2019: [LINK] They seem to have improved this scenario while not lowering any of the other test scores, hard to consider it 'cheating'. The XC40 has a very similar result with the car skipping the barrier (and also earned the highest mark): [LINK] Volvo and Tesla are the only two automakers that have their own crash labs. They've release a video recently talking about their safety program: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29190678,"author":"Dunedan","month":"2021-11","story":"How do cars fare in crash tests they're not specifically optimized for? (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla's Crash Lab using real-world data","quote":"What they claim to do there is to use their real world data to try to optimize for real crashs and not for crash tests.","text":"A month ago Tesla published a video showing their Crash Lab: [VIDEO] What they claim to do there is to use their real world data to try to optimize for real crashs and not for crash tests."},{"id":28998586,"author":"panick21_","month":"2021-10","story":"Hertz orders 100k Teslas","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla designs for real-world crash data rather than tests","quote":"They literally have data and video of every crash and update their cars to deal with real world crashes.","text":"Its the opposite. Tesla actually specifically does not design for Tests only. They literally have data and video of every crash and update their cars to deal with real world crashes. See for example: [VIDEO] Teardown of the cars have reviled that they are very sturdy built, maybe even overbuilt at points."}],"r":[["bpAi70WWBlw",1],["WijoL3Hy_Bw",1]]},"AE1gaNO9nj0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the evidence that inductive EV charging can match wired charging in efficiency.","q":{"x":"You should check out this video where wireless charging beats wired charging in end to end efficiency","i":41327087,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41327087,"author":"audunw","month":"2024-08","story":"No more trip hazards: safe in-pavement electric car charging system pilot","stance":"rec","why":"Wireless charging beats wired in end-to-end efficiency","quote":"You should check out this video where wireless charging beats wired charging in end to end efficiency","text":"Wireless charging can be surprisingly efficient. You should check out this video where wireless charging beats wired charging in end to end efficiency: [VIDEO] In that case it’s probably more due to a particularly inefficient DC fast charger. (It’s been a long time since I watched it so I might not remember the details right.) In the end it doesn’t have to be exactly as efficient as wired charging, and probably never will be for a good implementation of a wired charger. It only has to be so close that you don’t care about the difference. I don’t think the inverse square law is very relevant on these scales. Electromagnetic radiation can be focused."},{"id":45228991,"author":"breve","month":"2025-09","story":"Porsche demonstrates inductive EV charging at IAA","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence wireless can match wired charging efficiency","quote":"Wireless charging can be as efficient as wired charging","text":"> There's zero chance this will make it into a production vehicle Wireless charging is in production. Here's one example: [LINK] Wireless charging can be as efficient as wired charging: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38425638,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2023-11","story":"Tesla launches Supercharger congestion fee at $1 per min at 90% charge","stance":"reference","why":"Shows wireless EV charging can be efficient","quote":"Wireless charging for EVs can be efficient","text":"> If there only was a system that could automatically disconnect Or just use wireless charging, then there are no cables. Wireless charging for EVs can be efficient: [VIDEO] And wired charging is not as efficient as you might think: [LINK]"},{"id":37494464,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2023-09","story":"Germany Will Force 80% of Gas Stations to Install EV Chargers","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that wireless EV charging is very efficient","quote":"It's very efficient","text":"> How does that work for people who live in cities? With curbside charging, and wireless charging will be convenient once that's rolled out. No cables, just park over the charging plate. It's not like wireless phone charging. It's very efficient: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30894411,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2022-04","story":"How close is wireless power technology?","stance":"reference","why":"Test of wireless EV charging efficiency","quote":"Efficient wireless charging is on the way for EVs","text":"Efficient wireless charging is on the way for EVs: [LINK] [VIDEO] No cables or charging posts mean lower maintenance costs and tidier parking spaces."}],"r":[["fuFlMtZmvY0",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["rQhzEnWCgHA",1]]},"C-52AI_ojyQ":{"wy":"HN links it whenever a thread hits the ambiguity of whether a billion is 10^9 or 10^12.","q":{"x":"explains it all quite nicely.","i":7299387,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7299387,"author":"Toucan","month":"2014-02","story":"Has goto fail been fixed yet?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains short versus long scale billion definitions","quote":"explains it all quite nicely.","text":"I don't think that's right. 10^6 is a million in both languages. 10^9 used to be a milliard in British English but is no longer used. It is now referred to as a billion. 10^12 used to be a billion in British English but is now referred to as a trillion. Edit: [VIDEO] explains it all quite nicely."},{"id":42552639,"author":"throw0101d","month":"2024-12","story":"Fun facts about SQLite","stance":"reference","why":"Long versus short scale billions","quote":"","text":"> Since SQLite is used extensively in every smartphone, and there are more than 4.0 billion (4.0e9) smartphones in active use, each holding hundreds of SQLite database files, it is seems likely that there are over one trillion (1e12) SQLite databases in active use. I find the scientific notation of the counts amusing given the sometimes different meanings of \"billion\" and \"trillion\" (especially with English as a second language): * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK] (bonus: on French numbers)"},{"id":32674582,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2022-09","story":"OptiFi Program Incident Report","stance":"reference","why":"Long versus short scale billions","quote":"See \"How big is a billion?\" from Numberphile","text":"MM is for mille-mille (French?), thousand-thousand, so 1000-1000 or 1-000-000. See \"How big is a billion?\" from Numberphile : * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":32364393,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-08","story":"Italy, Intel close to $5B deal for chip factory","stance":"reference","why":"Long versus short scale billions","quote":"See \"How big is a billion?\" by Numberphile","text":"See \"How big is a billion?\" by Numberphile : * [VIDEO] * [LINK] (Given a choice, I would go with the long system, but sadly that doesn't seem to be the timeline we live in.)"},{"id":31069539,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-04","story":"As the scale of science expands, so does the language of prefixes","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses long and short scales, milliards and myriads","quote":"See also the video \"How big is a billion?\" by the Numberphile channel","text":"See also the video \"How big is a billion?\" by the Numberphile channel: > We discuss millions, billions, trillions and centillions is this film about the long and short scales. We also touch on quadrillions, sextillions, milliards, billiards and the Greek myriad. * [VIDEO] Does a \"billion\" have 9 zeros or 12 zeros? * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":24098887,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-08","story":"The ‘-ize’ have it (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Long and short number scales for billion","quote":"Is \"billion\" a thousand million (10^9) or a million million (10^12)? Numberphile video","text":"> What other spelling idiosyncrasies are there that I can get on my high horse about? Long and short number scales? * [LINK] Is \"billion\" a thousand million (10^9) or a million million (10^12)? Numberphile video: * [VIDEO] TL;DR: billion = bi-million = two of 10^6 = 10^{6x2} = 10^12. trillion = tri-million = 10^{6x3} = 10^18, and not 10^12. quad rillion, pent …."},{"id":17675870,"author":"pwinnski","month":"2018-08","story":"Apple is worth over $1B,000","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A thousand-billion is the old way of referring to numbers, but the UK switch to the American style in the 1970s. Strangely, the old style of billion, thousand-billion, trillion, thousand-trillion, quadrillion, etc, makes much more sense. What is a billion two of, anyway? What is a trillion three of? And so on? [VIDEO]"}]},"CgASBVMyVFI":{"wy":"Mostly quoted or linked as a witty example of Lehrer's satire, often as a joke in unrelated threads.","q":{"x":"National brotherhood week is a good example","i":16775531,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16775531,"author":"0xdeadbeefbabe","month":"2018-04","story":"Tom Lehrer at 90: a life of scientific satire","stance":"rec","why":"Good example of Lehrer's satire","quote":"National brotherhood week is a good example","text":"National brotherhood week is a good example: [VIDEO] You don't need to know any history, but it probably helps if you went to a public school or had siblings."},{"id":34025367,"author":"leoh","month":"2022-12","story":"Tom Lehrer has released all of his songs into the public domain","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"National Brotherhood Week,","text":"Oh the Protestants hate the Catholics And the Catholics hate the Protestants And the Hindus hate the Muslims And everybody hates the Jews — National Brotherhood Week, [VIDEO]"},{"id":7439255,"author":"PhasmaFelis","month":"2014-03","story":"Sony announces Project Morpheus, a virtual reality headset coming to PS4","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"all of my folks hate all of your folks, it's American as apple pie.","text":"\"My computer is more powerful than your console, so that means it's more fun! Can't you see that I'm having more fun than you?!? Hey! Stop having fun right now!\" Yeah, shitty PC ports of console games are a problem, I'll totally agree with that. Of course, they're also a tiny percentage of all PC games. It's not like there's not other stuff for you to play. And whose fault is it that there are shitty PC ports? Well, the publishers looking for a quick buck--oh no, silly me, it's the console gamers ! Their gamepad-clutching fingers are pulling the strings of a Machiavellian global network designed expressly to ruin your fun! Jesus Christ. The Xbox fans hate the Sony fans, and the Windows fans hate the Apple fans, and the PC fans hate the console fans, oh, the black folks hate the white folks, and the white folks hate the black folks, all of my folks hate all of your folks, it's American as apple pie. [VIDEO] There's gotta be someone I can blame for my burnt toast and my broken shoelace. Get your shit together and get worked up about something that matters. And in the mean time, play some games, have fun, and stop stressing so much!"}],"r":[["dF_-jQc53jw",1]]},"EFRUL7vKdU8":{"wy":"Recommended whenever a thread asks how dangerous it is to fall into a reactor or fuel pool.","q":{"x":"XKCD has a good video about this.","i":45725015,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45713323,"author":"seanmcdirmid","month":"2025-10","story":"A worker fell into a nuclear reactor pool","stance":"rec","why":"Answers how dangerous falling in a reactor pool is","quote":"It’s still a good video.","text":"Doesn’t it depend on how far they sink? I’m pretty sure xkcd did a comic on this. Oh, it’s a video, here it is: [VIDEO] Edit: ok, I’m probably the fiftieth person to point this out. It’s still a good video."},{"id":45725015,"author":"benmathes","month":"2025-10","story":"A worker fell into a nuclear reactor pool","stance":"rec","why":"Top of pool water is remarkably safe","quote":"XKCD has a good video about this.","text":"XKCD has a good video about this. The top of the water is remarkably safe, and a life vest would keep you up there! [VIDEO]"},{"id":42207636,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2024-11","story":"The meme-ification of the “Demon Core”","stance":"reference","why":"Consequences of swimming in a nuclear fuel pool","quote":"xkcd published a What If? video about the consequences of swimming in a nuclear fuel pool","text":"> He took a dip in a pool with a functioning nuclear pile xkcd published a What If? video about the consequences of swimming in a nuclear fuel pool: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41045539,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2024-07","story":"A New Specialized Train Is Ready to Haul Nuclear Waste","stance":"reference","why":"Nuclear storage pool safety","quote":"","text":"> Revell River is a watershed for local communities, and South Bruce is a farming community. Thus, expect to be sued by dozens of folks... Not “if” but “when” the site inevitably leaks like the identical facilities in the US and Germany. Given that the storage will be several hundred metres below the surface and the water table of (e.g.) the Bruce area is at ~35m: * [LINK] Not sure what such an incident would really do. Especially since the Bruce nuclear plant is already storaging spent fuel on-site for about forty years, so it's not much different if that location is picked. > If it was such a great safe idea, why not install it in downtown New York or Washington? Good jobs program right... ;) Sure. Things are currently held on-site at nuclear plants, and they can continue to do so indefinitely IMHO. I'm in Toronto, ~50km from the Pickering plant, and I'd be fine with that. I good friend of mine has lived in Pickering all his life and doesn't give it a second thought. Heck, if OPG wants to build a spent fuel pool in my backyard I'd be happy to accept it: free year-round \"ground-source\" heating/cooling for me. * [LINK] * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["iGbC5j4pG9w",1]]},"JZshZp-cxKg":{"wy":"HN mentions it as a fondly remembered classic of children's educational television.","q":{"x":"The legendery Pinball Number Count, with music by the Pointer Sisters","i":27111239,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27111239,"author":"jeffwass","month":"2021-05","story":"'Sesame Street': from radical experiment to beloved TV mainstay","stance":"rec","why":"Classic Pinball Number Count with Pointer Sisters music","quote":"The legendery Pinball Number Count, with music by the Pointer Sisters","text":"I've always wondered how many classic Sesame Street sketches were made by artists and animators while tripping on psychadelics. All the sketches below are ones I've seen when I was little. The legendery Pinball Number Count, with music by the Pointer Sisters : [VIDEO] Totally tripped-out alphabet, and I mean totally tripped-out : [LINK] Multi-armed guru counting to twenty a few times including in Spanish : [LINK] Little kid gets lost in a weird neighborhood : [LINK]"},{"id":48084337,"author":"vunderba","month":"2026-05","story":"Space Cadet Pinball on Linux","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There have been a few attempts at this. I think the most well-known one is probably this one [1]. While we’re at it, I’d love to see a physical version of the seseame street pinbal table [2], though that one might be a bit more ambitious. :) [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18252777,"author":"TomMckenny","month":"2018-10","story":"Caroll Spinney Leaves ‘Sesame Street’ After Nearly 50 Years","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I grew up on it too but a full generation earlier. Even now when some unlikely coincidence surfaces the number 12, sometimes this song[0] still pops into my head. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1]]},"KRvv0QdruMQ":{"wy":"HN brings it up in threads on ancient civilizations and detecting past technology in geology.","q":{"x":"I enjoyed Kurzgesagt's video [0] about our ability to sense ancient aliens.","i":38834315,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38834315,"author":"Nzen","month":"2024-01","story":"Pushing It Back","stance":"rec","why":"Video about our ability to sense ancient aliens.","quote":"I enjoyed Kurzgesagt's video [0] about our ability to sense ancient aliens.","text":"Along the same lines as the pbs video, I enjoyed Kurzgesagt's video [0] about our ability to sense ancient aliens. [0] [VIDEO] 12 minutes"},{"id":40235394,"author":"berniedurfee","month":"2024-05","story":"New findings point to an Earth-like environment on ancient Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Advanced civilizations could have existed with little evidence remaining.","quote":"Kurzgesagt did a video on this.","text":"Kurzgesagt did a video on this. Though they discuss how advanced civilizations could have existed on Earth and we might not even know it because there wouldn’t be much left and what was left might be so unusual that we might chalk it up to natural phenomena. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38671236,"author":"robbomacrae","month":"2023-12","story":"Would it be possible to detect an industrial civilization in geological record?","stance":"reference","why":"Covers challenges of detecting past civilizations in geological record.","quote":"it acknowledges the challenges in detecting such evidence due to the limited and complex nature of the geological record.","text":"Kurzgesagt also: [VIDEO] And just like the paper, it acknowledges the challenges in detecting such evidence due to the limited and complex nature of the geological record."}],"r":[["zuKV2DI9-Jg",1],["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",1],["JTfhYyTuT44",1]]},"LA-gNoxSLCE":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a source for details of Cloudflare's architecture, such as LuaJIT-based request inspection and request volumes.","q":{"x":"I discovered a moderately old video from CloudFlare hiding in there, and had a rather big TIL moment.","i":10821326,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10821326,"author":"i336_","month":"2016-01","story":"Ask HN: Whats your favorite HN thread from 2015?","stance":"rec","why":"Reveals every Cloudflare request passes through LuaJIT attack detection.","quote":"I discovered a moderately old video from CloudFlare hiding in there, and had a rather big TIL moment.","text":"I discovered a moderately old video from CloudFlare hiding in there, and had a rather big TIL moment. It's one thing to read \"LuaJIT is one of the fastest dynamic languages\" etc blah blah on [LINK] ... ...it's quite another to learn that EVERY SINGLE CLOUDFLARE REQUEST - all 4 million/sec of them - goes through a LuaJIT-based attack detection system. Oh. Video: [VIDEO] (from [LINK] ) Found in the linked thread here: [LINK]"},{"id":17342851,"author":"exikyut","month":"2018-06","story":"Ask HN: If I Close My Data Centers, What About the People/Jobs Lost?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Cloudflare getting systems built by Quanta.","quote":"","text":"> Even putting aside that the computer tech part of the business isn't their core competency, custom hardware is hard. Hmmm. Good point. (Maybe I scared OP away, heh.) > Is there an example of any large company, other than Google, who has mastered this? Well, I certainly don't know. I've heard Cloudflare gets their systems made, for some definition of \"from scratch\", by Quanta. Presumably there's a bit of a decision-making process in there, and presumably Quanta handles everything related to the \"why is the devkit sample broken again #51383\" side of things and ships a more or less finished product (maybe even with finished enclosures). My reference point for this knowledge is [VIDEO] (2015). Regarding SAS, maybe past a certain ordering quantity (or anything similar that would reveal a massive operation) the market would expect certain behavior like requesting value-adds, ordering with only one supplier, or other things that would generate lock-in. I of course have absolutely no idea [if this sort of thinking is reasonable]. Your points about commodity at scale sound quite reasonable. (You clearly have more experience than I do, which is kind of zero, heh.) As for suspicion about summaries and conclusions, well, (tangential example) the radial graph on [LINK] (the one from Forrester) doesn't show GitHub :D Yeah, building own hardware is sadly still within the realm of over-specialization at this point. I guess this is because 4+-layer PCB printing is still eye-watering so the "},{"id":13767227,"author":"Artemis2","month":"2017-03","story":"Quantifying the Impact of “Cloudbleed”","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Cloudflare request rate figures.","quote":"","text":"For reference, two years ago Cloudflare was serving more than three million requests per second[0]. I can only imagine this figure has gone up a lot. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11595678,"author":"i336_","month":"2016-04","story":"Hello Lua","stance":"reference","why":"Details Cloudflare's hardware and logging stack.","quote":"Thousands of regular expressions\" is mentioned verbally in the video around ~11:25.","text":"You make incredibly fair points and I don't have the data to answer what you've raised. This could be lack of research or the fact that CF hasn't released it. I expect a 20/80 non-researched/non-available mix. My understanding is that the WAF detects trivial stuff like SQL injection and XSS, and the video also mentions it detected ShellShock and so forth (yeah, this was a while ago). \"Thousands of regular expressions\" is mentioned verbally in the video around ~11:25. That's where I got that from. There are some bits mentioned about the hardware around 19:30: - E5-2630v3 (16 cores, 32 w/ HT which is turned off), 128GB RAM, 10Gbps - Custom built by Quanta: 4 nodes per 2U (each node is like half-width and 1U high), redundant 2xPSU in the middle - 40 machines run Kafka storing 400TB of log data (ingest @ 15Gbps), 50TB disk per node - 5 machines run CitusDB (sharded PostgreSQL) to power realtime graph generation, 12TB SSD per node - 100+ machines for realtime analytics running Go (and presumably LuaJIT) - LuaJIT seems to be the realtime \"hmm, interesting\" flagging system, while Go is used for in-depth intelligent analysis that's (I assume) slightly behind realtime. Also, the video is actually over here - [VIDEO] --- I'm curious to understand what tail latency is, and very interested to learn more about language latency and performance in general. What subjects would you recommend I learn about / read? (I would describe myself as interested from a hobbyist-intermediate standpont, b"},{"id":9867113,"author":"mtourne","month":"2015-07","story":"OpenResty – A fast web app server by extending Nginx","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Cloudflare's move to Lua in Nginx.","quote":"More on this from dotScale 2015","text":"Rewriting the hodge podge of C, Perl and other custom bits of Nginx almost entirely in Lua and hiring its maintainer Yichun in the process where important steps for scaling up the CloudFlare's CDN and make sure each node is running as hot as possible. More on this from dotScale 2015: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9780914,"author":"mdaniel","month":"2015-06","story":"How CloudFlare extracts a signal from 10 trillion log lines each month [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"What is the deal with the recent \"thedotpost.com\" envelope for perfectly valid YouTube URLs? I want to flag this post just for that. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hNRO7lno_DM",1],["6orsmFndx_o",1]]},"RkTvDjhImwo":{"wy":"Cited as an example of how much surprising behavior can hide in seemingly simple circuits, in hardware backdoor discussions.","q":{"x":"don't underestimate what can be hidden in seemingly simple places","i":25201136,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25201136,"author":"tzs","month":"2020-11","story":"MyNOR: Single board computer that uses a single NOR gate as its ALU","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how much can hide in simple circuits","quote":"don't underestimate what can be hidden in seemingly simple places","text":"It's not quite a backdoor, but don't underestimate what can be hidden in seemingly simple places: [VIDEO] Here's how that is doing it: [LINK] He's got several more, including this one which doesn't even use switches. You just tap the wire to toggle the LEDs: [LINK] I have no idea how that one works."},{"id":17286570,"author":"wanderingjew","month":"2018-06","story":"“All accredited journalists at the #KimTrumpSummit get a free USB fan.”","stance":"reference","why":"Best example of disguising components","quote":"this [1] is probably the best example.","text":"I'm not going to say 'no', but I would highly doubt it. If you're wondering what the 'state of the art' in disguising components to look like other components is, this [1] is probably the best example. It's a guy that turned a LED into an LED and an inductor, and put transistors inside of switches. What you're proposing -- basically putting a USB-capable microcontroller inside something that looks like resistor -- isn't impossible. I can imagine that it could be done, but I would have no idea how. You would probably go with a very small (physically) microcontroller, maybe one of the SOT-23-6 PIC or AVR packages. I don't think it's possible to write a USB stack for those, and you only have 1-2kB to implement a BadUSB [2] sort of thing. It might be possible, but I doubt it. Still, that size of package could be disguised as a (large) SMD resistor, although the PCB would look odd with the addition of extra traces. To be honest, it really wouldn't make sense to disguise a microcontroller as passive components. It's already been demonstrated journalists will gladly plug random USB devices into their computer. If you wanted the device to just pass a cursory investigation, you'd just put a glob of black epoxy over the USB port. Hiding a microcontroller as a pair of resistors might be possible, but it's far more effort than what is really needed. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":16953784,"author":"garaetjjte","month":"2018-04","story":"'Demonically Clever' Backdoor Hides Inside Computer Chip","stance":"reference","why":"Series LED circuit hiding clever filters","quote":"I have seen fun videos with seemingly simple circuits with LEDs and switches connected in series and they behaved completely differently","text":"Obviously not at SMD scale, but I have seen fun videos with seemingly simple circuits with LEDs and switches connected in series and they behaved completely differently (eg. each switch connected in series controlled other LED). It turned out that switches and LEDs have been modified with clever frequency generators and filter circuits. edit: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["TLm7Q92xMjQ",1],["94ed4v_T6YM",1],["K4JhruinbWc",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["gJ5KV3rzuag",1],["jyQwgBAaBag",1],["DovunOxlY1k",1],["5uiroWBkdFY",1],["4d0EGIt1SPc",1],["p45T7U5oi9Q",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HF-9Dy6iB_4",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1],["r-A78RgMhZU",1],["04ERSb06dOg",1]]},"U03XXzcThGU":{"wy":"HN points to it for insight into how the Caves of Qud developer thinks about game engines.","q":{"x":"If you're interested in how Brian thinks take a look at this video","i":37554710,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37554710,"author":"edem","month":"2023-09","story":"Brian Bucklew Porting Caves of Qud from Unity to Godot","stance":"rec","why":"Insight into how Caves of Qud's developer thinks about engines.","quote":"If you're interested in how Brian thinks take a look at this video","text":"If you're interested in how Brian thinks take a look at this video: [VIDEO] I've learned a lot from this back in the day!"},{"id":42410169,"author":"cdiamand","month":"2024-12","story":"Mapping all 13.8 gigapixels of the Caves of Qud world map","stance":"reference","why":"Developers explain technical design behind Caves of Qud.","quote":"the developers have peeled back the curtain on how they've created parts of the game","text":"I come back to it from time to time and play for a week. It looks like it's been getting more accessible over time, with a new UI update having been rolled out recently. There's definitely a certain mindset that helps make it more enjoyable. Imagination being key! From the technical side, the developers have peeled back the curtain on how they've created parts of the game: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30885604,"author":"and0","month":"2022-04","story":"OpenMW becomes a general-purpose engine for isometric RPGs","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on data-driven, event-driven engine design in Caves of Qud.","quote":"inspired by Brian Bucklew","text":"I'm pretty far into a project to create a Typescript framework for turn and tile-based (think roguelikes) client/server games: [LINK] . Mix of entity-component and event-driven design (inspired by Brian Bucklew [VIDEO] and Thomas Biksup [LINK] , though looking now I'm surprised I landed on a similar but imo more robust event system as I was only looking at EC pattern at the time). Typescript means entity factories and their components can be fully written in the language rather than XML per the videos above, and people can for example distribute a package that is basically DnD5e or Pathfinder rulesets, custom world generators, or anything else to be plugged into a published game. Typical Minecraft \"infinite\" world data structure, handles binding up event system to component listeners automatically, (will soon) handles serialization, listeners are async generators ala Redux Saga for neat inversion of control and advanced patterns as needed. It doesn't have any specific frontends or clients (APIs and react hooks+utils exist but not super advanced yet, and a small WebGL renderer + react component), it's more a collection of objects that recursively process state updates in response to a user input until it finishes (like a classic console roguelike), and produce a serialized stream of events clients of any kind can listen to, completely agnostic to how any client may choose to represent it, so you could easily make a DM interface vs an interface for players connected to the same"}]},"Vn2vdQZhs0w":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence that DisplayPort cable labeling is unreliable and that even major brands can fail, and for its explanation of version differences.","q":{"x":"Good and interesting video to explain the version diffs in spec","i":36702056,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36702056,"author":"unstatusthequo","month":"2023-07","story":"Displayport: A Better Video Interface","stance":"rec","why":"Good, interesting explanation of DisplayPort version differences.","quote":"Good and interesting video to explain the version diffs in spec","text":"Good and interesting video to explain the version diffs in spec: [VIDEO] And even better, a calculator based on your monitor capabilities that helps you choose: [LINK] I ended up with a $15 2.0 10 foot cable from Monoprice. Fit my ultra wide Samsung G9 pixel pushing needs just fine."},{"id":32695715,"author":"rektide","month":"2022-09","story":"USB4 v2 is also 120 Gbps","stance":"reference","why":"Example of in-depth tech reviews to trust over marketplaces.","quote":"Linus's in-depth tech reviews","text":"Exactly. Yes. I'd also just say this is a low information environment. Traditionally, we'd rely on the media as a line of defense. Product reviews would tell us who to trust, what the good deals are. We trusted the media to review & find problems & surface issues in the past. But those institutions have undergone enormous revenue crunch, and there's very few left who can really go in depth & do the deep, thorough, intelligent exploration we ought trust them for. Online marketplaces in the world today are bedlam, and there are few trusted authorities. This is a general world condition, but with tech it's extra bad because when things don't work, we almost ever know why/who to blame, it's all just frustrating wizard-shit that's broken & not going, & we are left bereft utterly impotent in the face of these high tech system's mysterious failures. Honestly, I'd prefer not paying for USB-IF certification on my cables, and I don't care that none of the official logos designating certain compliances are met are embossed on my cables. But I also am a geek who will go spend 3 hours to find Benson (alas no longer active) or Linus's in-depth tech reviews[1], and who doesn't mind trying some cables (buy a couple different ones at a time), finding some not working, & returning those, to save a couple bucks at some hassle. For most people: just buy certified gear. [1] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":32628340,"author":"cainxinth","month":"2022-08","story":"Tell HN: Most DisplayPort cables aren't sold with correct information","stance":"reference","why":"Tested many video cables; major brands' cables failed.","quote":"Lots of cables from major suppliers like Monoprice failed their tests.","text":"LTT got a TotalPhase cable tester and ran tests on a whole bunch of video cables. Lots of cables from major suppliers like Monoprice failed their tests. Here’s the video for DisplayPort: [VIDEO]"}]},"ZH2wGpEZVgE":{"wy":"HN cites it as the standard walkthrough of Mario's implicit teaching and as an example of hiding the tutorial inside the problem.","q":{"x":"To get a sense for this, watch this video [1] (from 7 years ago) walking through the design of level 1-1 of SMB.","i":27556321,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27556321,"author":"jader201","month":"2021-06","story":"SNES – Super Mario World Widescreen","stance":"rec","why":"Walkthrough of Mario level 1-1 design","quote":"To get a sense for this, watch this video [1] (from 7 years ago) walking through the design of level 1-1 of SMB.","text":"Was thinking the same thing. The Nintendo designers were always so meticulous about the design of the levels, especially what’s visible to a player at any given point. To get a sense for this, watch this video [1] (from 7 years ago) walking through the design of level 1-1 of SMB. It’s crazy what (likely) went into planning the design of each level of the various Mario platformer games. But like OP, this is strictly looking at it from a gameplay perspective, ignoring the impressive technical feet that this is. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35923528,"author":"iamwil","month":"2023-05","story":"The Legend of Zelda: Tears of the Kingdom Release","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Mario level design analyses listed","quote":"does analysis of how Nintendo designs their Mario levels.","text":"Yes, it's rare, but they have. Game maker's toolkit has one on how they solved their open world problem for BotW: - [LINK] And at GDC, they talked about their Chemistry engine for BotW: - [LINK] - [LINK] Game historian has some tidbits of how they made certain design decisions for their successful games Mario Kart: - [LINK] Super Mario World: - [LINK] Super Mario Bro 3: - [LINK] GMT, Snowman, and Extra credit does analysis of how Nintendo designs their Mario levels. - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [VIDEO] Generally, it follows the ramp up and down of the Hero's journey in storytelling. 1. Introduce a new mechanic in a safe area where it's impossible to die. 2. Increase the challenge with the mechanic by adding variations 3. Ramp up challenge even more by combining with previously learned mechanic 4. Have periods of rest inbetween the challenges."},{"id":27555036,"author":"zeta0134","month":"2021-06","story":"The UX of video game tutorials","stance":"reference","why":"World 1-1 as famous tutorial","quote":"Possibly the most famous video game tutorial of all time has got to be World 1-1 from the original Super Mario Bros.","text":"Possibly the most famous video game tutorial of all time has got to be World 1-1 from the original Super Mario Bros. [VIDEO] And really, most of the later Mario games execute this fairly well, with some (sunny) exceptions here and there. Nintendo is unusually skilled at introducing a new mechanic in a way that feels like organic play, and slowly ramping up the complexity in a way that will leave most players feeling like natural masters by the end of the level, without realizing that the level was leading them by the nose the entire time."},{"id":23026288,"author":"derefr","month":"2020-04","story":"Some school districts plan to end the year early, call remote learning too tough","stance":"reference","why":"Example of hiding the tutorial in the problem","quote":"Hide the tutorial for the problem","text":"Proper teaching looks less like what most teachers do, and more like what (good) game designers do. • Hide the tutorial for the problem, in the \"shape\" of the problem, so the student won't even realize they're being taught something. (See e.g. [VIDEO] ) • Make the student/player feel a need for a new tool, before the tool is introduced. Have them try (and perhaps fail) to solve the problem without the tool first. (See the \"keys and doors\" discussion in [LINK] ) • For each new concept, present its lesson as a series of spaced-chunk challenges, where each chunk ramps the difficulty up to a climax and then down again to a gentle denouement. Give the learner's mind a chance to feel a sense of mastery over already-understood concepts, not just the continual stress of new challenges. (See [LINK] or [LINK] ) • Gate problems behind proof of mastery of previous micro-skills, not just in the form of obvious tests, but also in the form of multi-step problems for new knowledge which can't even be approached without also having another prerequisite skill. Ensure that it becomes impossible to \"leave behind\" a foundational skill—i.e. ensure that the learner will get stuck without the new tool, and so realize they need to backtrack to pick up that tool, rather than flailing uselessly at the next problem. • Meter and visualize progress. Map acquisition of micro-skills directly to some sort of measurement the student can look at (a level number; a tech tree; a skill grid), to feel that they're"},{"id":13875681,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-03","story":"The new legend of Zelda is a toy box of delights","stance":"reference","why":"Nintendo's implicit steering in level 1-1","quote":"The implicit steering is one of the things that Nintendo is famous for though.","text":"> You can definitely feel the 'steering' if you're looking out for it The implicit steering is one of the things that Nintendo is famous for though. At least among game designers: [VIDEO] What the video doesn't point out is that if you don't run to the right, the timing of the jump to avoid the Goomba makes you hit that first question mark block by accident."},{"id":11203874,"author":"CM30","month":"2016-03","story":"Intro.js – Step-by-step guide and feature introduction for your website","stance":"reference","why":"Mario taught through level design","quote":"games like Super Mario Bros was designed to teach you through level design","text":"It's certainly a nice enough script for what it does, and I can see it being useful in some instances, but... a lot of this 'feature tour/tutorial' stuff strikes me as more of an excuse to not worry about ease of use. A good site design should be intuitive in of itself, not something that requires a guided tour to figure out. It's a bit like the modern gaming world to be honest. In the old days, games like Super Mario Bros was designed to teach you through level design, so you'd figure out how things worked without getting a huge info box thrown in your face. Like this: [VIDEO] On the other hand, games since got obsessed with tutorials detailing every minute thing about the title, leading to such over the top insanity as this 'help DVD' included with Super Mario Galaxy 2: [LINK] These tutorials on sites and in mobile apps remind me all too much of the latter. It's like 'we won't use our product to teach people in a natural way, so we'll put in a guide for complete dummies instead'."}]},"boB6qu5dcCw":{"wy":"Linked as a quick demonstration of the ion plane in threads about silent aircraft and flying cars.","q":{"x":"if you want to see how it works and see it flying here is a YouTube link","i":31932764,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31932764,"author":"c0n5pir4cy","month":"2022-06","story":"MIT engineers fly first-ever plane with no moving parts (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how the ion plane works and flies.","quote":"if you want to see how it works and see it flying here is a YouTube link","text":"Older news as other people have mentioned, if you want to see how it works and see it flying here is a YouTube link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23376183,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2020-06","story":"UVB radiation was the Devonian-Carboniferous extinction kill mechanism","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Don't ion drives have ozone as a side-effect? Balloons with solar-powered ion drives might do the trick then - they don't even need to be lighter than air, the ion drive should give it enough thrust [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":20642177,"author":"qqn","month":"2019-08","story":"Flying cars","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"near-silent aircraft without moving parts","text":"Thanks for the commentary guys, it’s been fascinating churning over all this even more. Thoughts: - Safety is the same issue as always, only it’s being ironed out faster than before. Controlled vertical landings, 3D space, and redundancy help a lot. I don’t foresee many flying high enough to get really hurt. Maybe things like undercarriage cushions or redirected waterways underneath major traffic flows could help even more. - Noise cancellation tech is already out there, as well as near-silent aircraft without moving parts: [VIDEO] - Main arteries will be less condensed, but much more, much smaller traffic will appear and that’s going to be a new problem. The death knell for intracity public transport? - Well-made points about “actual” flying cars not actually being used. Maybe it would have been the same for cars if someone made motorized horses instead of the automobile? Thank you for all the resources. I really liked the random touchdown point, underlining how exact our tech already is. I've polished the post quite a bit, adding a lot of these extra things to it. If anyone can clarify the paper bag problem @brownbat mentioned and what @blunte meant by \"s/trans/tele\" I'd really appreciate it."},{"id":18504568,"author":"hnphillipj","month":"2018-11","story":"Silent and Simple Ion Engine Powers a Plane with No Moving Parts","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's 5m wide","text":"[VIDEO] > But it is not a viable propulsion source. At least not on Earth for things larger than a meter or so. It's 5m wide"}]},"c5QF2HjHLSE":{"wy":"HN draws on it for practical decision-making and problem-framing techniques that apply well beyond Clojure.","q":{"x":"A good approach to making decisions in a team context (also works for personal use) is explained by Rich Hickey here","i":41888376,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41744673,"author":"jauntywundrkind","month":"2024-10","story":"Holocron is an object storage based leader election library","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey on choosing good words and avoiding nicknames","quote":"Rich Hickey has a section on using good words, on not using nicknames, in his (as ever wonderful) Clojure Conj 2023 talk Design in Practice,","text":"Rich Hickey has a section on using good words, on not using nicknames, in his (as ever wonderful) Clojure Conj 2023 talk Design in Practice, [VIDEO]"},{"id":41888376,"author":"erichocean","month":"2024-10","story":"Focus on decisions, not tasks","stance":"rec","why":"Hickey's talk on making decisions in a team","quote":"A good approach to making decisions in a team context (also works for personal use) is explained by Rich Hickey here","text":"A good approach to making decisions in a team context (also works for personal use) is explained by Rich Hickey here: [VIDEO] The talks is called \"Design in Practice\" but is really about making decisions."},{"id":38695851,"author":"jugjug","month":"2023-12","story":"Volkswagen Will Bring Back Physical Buttons in New Cars","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey argues problem statements must not be missing features","quote":"We do not have feature X\" is never a valid problem statement.","text":"Reminds on Rich Hickey ranting about touch screens in cars [1] in Design in Practice: > We do not have feature X\" is never a valid problem statement. If you need proof of this you only need to look at a modern car which has a touch screen where no one said, \"I need to slide my finger on some random piece of glass to a precise point to set my blower in my car while I am driving.\" No one has ever said that, right? But somebody did say, \"We need touch screens because young people will never buy our cars.\" This is what happens when you are not talking about the problem. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36400194,"author":"nivertech","month":"2023-06","story":"Ask HN: What team tools do you use in your startup to organize information?","stance":"reference","why":"Rich Hickey's decision matrix approach in design talk","quote":"Decision Matrix (almost as old as Periodic Table;), which was recently popularized by Rich Hickey in his talk Design in Practice[1,2]","text":"I experienced this in Scrum planning, daily standups, and other rituals were done with Excel shared via Zoom (or similar) meetings. The Scrum Master would ask each team member a question (e.g. estimation, etc.) and then fill the Excel while online, wasting everyone's time. Same with planning, there will be some basic template for a sprint, and it will be filled with the estimations/Jira tickets during the online meeting! Given said that, there are some valid uses for shared spreadsheets in Product/SDLC. One method I used is specific for data engineering projects: the product fills Excel/Google Spreadsheet with the example input, output shapes in the form of tables, and the developers asking question while they arriving to common spec. Another is called Decision Matrix (almost as old as Periodic Table;), which was recently popularized by Rich Hickey in his talk Design in Practice[1,2]. Another valid usecase for shared spreadsheets is Use Cases table. --- [1] [VIDEO] [2] slide #31 [LINK]"}],"r":[["tAACYA1Mwv4",1],["FlQYU3m1e80",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["oaDTiWaYfcM",1]]},"g8JzXEoT9LI":{"wy":"HN people mention it as where they first heard of mouth taping and DIY CPAP-style fixes for sleep apnea.","q":{"x":"Essential Craftsman on Youtube is where I first heard about mouth taping","i":37634651,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34588183,"author":"ghostbrainalpha","month":"2023-01","story":"Show HN: I'm a doctor and made a responsive breathing app for stress and anxiety","stance":"rec","why":"DIY CPAP and mouth taping approach for sleep apnea","quote":"Watch this guys two videos on a DIY CPAP.","text":"You can actually cut a little slit in the mouth tape, so that you won't panic. Breathing a straw sized amount of air through the mouth if you need to, will prevent suffocation anxiety, but also limit your mouth breathing enough that your nose does most of the work. Watch this guys two videos on a DIY CPAP. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44098016,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2025-05","story":"Sleep apnea pill shows striking success in large clinical trial","stance":"reference","why":"Series on forcing nose breathing during sleep","quote":"like Essential Craftsman discussed in his series of videos","text":"Has anyone tried the \"hostage tape\" or similar to force breathing through your nose like Essential Craftsman discussed in his series of videos [VIDEO]"},{"id":37634651,"author":"linsomniac","month":"2023-09","story":"Mouth Taping","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the commenter's introduction to mouth taping and nasal strips","quote":"Essential Craftsman on Youtube is where I first heard about mouth taping","text":"Essential Craftsman on Youtube is where I first heard about mouth taping, he just uses a \"GrifGrips Sports Tape\", which I was interested to try until I realized it's not really compatible with my beard... I sleep only \"ok\", been trying a lot of things to improve it. I have borderline apnea (5.2 events per hour, they start treating at 5), and my Garmin watch puts my SpO2 average around 90, which is maybe a little low for sleeping, but my doctor isn't worried about that. I was hoping the tape would up my \"sleep score\" a little, but again I haven't tried it. Essential Craftsman did say he tried the nasal strips and they increased his SpO2 a couple percent. I've tried them over the last week and while it seems easier to breathe through my nose with them, they didn't translate into any increased SpO2 over night according to my watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34953602,"author":"Ralo","month":"2023-02","story":"Poor Sleep Drove Me Insane, and My Long Path to Recovery","stance":"reference","why":"DIY sleep apnea fix update","quote":"","text":"I started getting sleep issues during the beginning of university and during a bodybuilding phase but I treated it as \"that's what life is, you don't sleep. Welcome to adulthood.\" after growing up with an over weight and extreme snorer father who was very much on the stance of \"shut up and be tired, that's what life is\" By this point I was already getting to where it clearly wasn't normal and it's extremely affecting my life but I was so weak and exhausted that daily chores felt like I was carrying 20lbs weights on my arms. After years of broken sleep, it completely shipwrecked my schooling and gym routines. This lasted 3-4 years I thought it was backpain ruining my sleep, since I would wake up 3x a night with a sore neck and back. I decided to check with a doctor. This did nothing, they told me I was too young to have these issue and just needed to fix my routine. I tried some basic tips, fixed sleeping times, new bed, new office chair. etc. Didn't change a thing. I ended up learning that I snore. Being single all of university, I never heard this. I started looking into this on my own and it was an absolute eye opener [0][1][2]. I never realized how important nose breathing is. It's not at all the same as just breathing through you mouth. I always had issues breathing through my nose when sleeping and always just breathed through my mouth. I would wake up with a completely dry mouth. This is bad for your teeth, it changes your jaw structure, and allows you to choke during s"}]},"jErqdRE5zpQ":{"wy":"HN uses it as a quick look at what using a Canon Cat was actually like, in threads on Raskin, command interfaces and early Macintosh history.","q":{"x":"a great demonstration of what it would be like to use one now","i":40267363,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40267363,"author":"watersb","month":"2024-05","story":"Preliminary Macintosh Business Plan (12 July 1981) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Short informal demo showing what using a Cat is like","quote":"a great demonstration of what it would be like to use one now","text":"Wow, of course.. didn't think of video. That video is great, a VCR tape collection of corporate demos by Jef Raskin's company, apparently for investors and resellers. YouTube suggested a short, informal demo posted only 10 years ago, about 6 minutes: [VIDEO] A bit less familiar with it so a quick and dirty video I suppose but a great demonstration of what it would be like to use one now. Essential history! Thanks!"},{"id":29384816,"author":"kragen","month":"2021-11","story":"Command palettes: how typing commands became the norm again","stance":"reference","why":"Another Canon Cat video","quote":"Thanks for the video! I had never seen it.","text":"Right, The Humane Environment, following on his work with the Canon Cat and the Macintosh, before Steve Jobs took over the project and made it into a better Lisa. Later they renamed THE to the more googlable \"Archy\", a pun on RCHI, the Raskin Center for the Humane Interface, shortly before Jef's surprising death from pancreatic cancer. Jef's son Aza unfortunately hasn't kept Jef's stuff online, and he also invented infinite scroll websites. Nice guy though. LEAP was Emacs incremental-search, but on the Cat the idea was to use it to replace all cursor movement except \"creep\" left and right arrows. Thanks for the video! I had never seen it. YT suggested [VIDEO] , another Cat video. [LINK] shows a brief clip of the Forth interpreter."},{"id":22042903,"author":"marttt","month":"2020-01","story":"Leap Technology (keyboard vs. mouse on a Canon Cat machine, ca 1987)","stance":"reference","why":"More on the Canon Cat","quote":"More on the Canon Cat","text":"More on the Canon Cat: [VIDEO] [LINK] There are also a few previous HN discussions on this machine. EDIT: I just have to post this lovely scene from the original video separately. This is about human-computer interaction after all, isn't it! [LINK] Aren't we actually approaching an age where this kind of (almost-)single-purpose machines might be making a comeback? The focus this model seems to allow or enforce on the user feels kind of... healthy."},{"id":12509392,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-09","story":"Restoring YC's Xerox Alto: how our boot disk was trashed with random data","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of the Canon Cat text editing machine","quote":"Video demo: [2]","text":"Jef Raskin was passionate about user interface design, but had a completely different approach. The realization of Raskin's thinking is the Canon Cat.[1] Video demo: [2] It's a machine for text editing with the minimal number of keystrokes. There are special \"Leap\" keys, and a \"Front\" shift for extra characters on keytop fronts. There's no pointing device. It's an elegant concept, but nothing one would think of as a GUI today. It's more like something that would appeal to Markdown enthusiasts. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IJiHDmyhE1A",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1]]},"jeghGhVdt9s":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever gallium or liquid metal comes up, as a vivid demonstration of gallium destroying aluminum.","q":{"x":"you should watch his video where he breaks a lock by simply applying Gallium to a lock","i":24057964,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24057964,"author":"jansan","month":"2020-08","story":"The World of Competitive Lockpicking","stance":"rec","why":"Gallium destroying an aluminum lock.","quote":"you should watch his video where he breaks a lock by simply applying Gallium to a lock","text":"If you have never heard about Gallium destroying Aluminium, you should watch his video where he breaks a lock by simply applying Gallium to a lock: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41167408,"author":"Animats","month":"2024-08","story":"Replacing Liquid Metal on an Asus Zephyrus G15's CPU","stance":"reference","why":"Shows gallium attacking aluminum.","quote":"this is what happens when a small amount of gallium contacts aluminum.[1]","text":"Gallium? That's only liquid down to 30C. And it expands when heated. So what happens when your laptop is off and drops to room temperature? Worse, this is what happens when a small amount of gallium contacts aluminum.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29988111,"author":"laurent92","month":"2022-01","story":"Direct conversion of CO2 to solid carbon by Gallium-based liquid metals","stance":"reference","why":"Gallium destroys an aluminum padlock.","quote":"Funny for a metal that seems to flow like Terminator and can melt an aluminum padlock, see LPL","text":"Funny for a metal that seems to flow like Terminator and can melt an aluminum padlock, see LPL: [VIDEO] (It’s doesn’t melt it exactly, it destroys the structure because Gallium atoms are very small and insert themselves through)."},{"id":28278648,"author":"oneplane","month":"2021-08","story":"Phones containing magnets inhibit implantable cardioverter defibrillators","stance":"reference","why":"Gallium surprisingly destroys sturdy metal.","quote":"That reminds me of this LPL video about Gallium","text":"Now that I read that, it seems obvious that static fields and RF wouldn't interact like that... Thanks! The video is very well done (for a semi-pro it seems?), it's one of those things where people (including me) might think \"oh yeah magnets, I know about those\" but you actually don't, or at last not at the scale that you might need. That reminds me of this LPL video about Gallium: [VIDEO] where you might think: oh yeah, metals, those are sturdy blocks that you need to weld or saw."},{"id":24708342,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2020-10","story":"PS5 Teardown [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Inspired the commenter to buy gallium.","quote":"I bought some gallium to play with after seeing this video of it on a lock -","text":"Cheers, hadn't heard of that alloy before. I bought some gallium to play with after seeing this video of it on a lock - [VIDEO]"},{"id":24709275,"author":"013a","month":"2020-10","story":"PS5 Teardown [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Few things I noticed: - That stand is a crazy piece of unnecessary engineering just to make the customer's life a wee little bit easier. Very cool. - Removable side-panels. This was strongly theorized from previous leaks, but I think this is the first time we've gotten an official video showing them. The aftermarket for these will be huge, allowing really well integrated customization. It also makes Sony's \"limited edition consoles\" they release for big games far cheaper to build, and maybe even accessible to people who don't want to replace their whole console (if Sony releases the plates as a separate/pre-order/etc purchase). - The NVME and fan are both easily accessible without voiding warranty. They've advertised that NVME slot before; PCIE4 is required, and it has to match or exceed the internal SSD's raw read performance in order to be supported, which is 5.5Gbps. Nearly every current-gen NVME SSD is too slow, which may lead to some confusion, but I also expect Sony will have some PS5 branded SSDs right in the Best Buy gaming accessories isle so hopefully too many people don't get burned by this. Definitely prefer it to Microsoft's proprietary strategy. - Single cooling fan. There's a ton of empty space inside the console, so it doesn't seem impossible that they could have found room for a second fan, but they must feel confident about this one (massive) fan's ability to cool that (massive) heatsink. Judging by the design though, it looks like it has a lot of surface ar"}],"r":[["bfN2JWifLCY",1]]},"n_6p-1J551Y":{"wy":"Brought up whenever reaction wheels or self-balancing cubes come up, with the jump-up maneuver singled out as the coolest part.","q":{"x":"I always thought that was the coolest part of the Cubli project.","i":39336884,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39336884,"author":"lh7777","month":"2024-02","story":"I designed a cube that balances itself on a corner","stance":"rec","why":"Cubli jump-up maneuver is the coolest part","quote":"I always thought that was the coolest part of the Cubli project.","text":"> I plan to add mechanical brakes to the reaction wheels...This enables jump-up manoeuvers which in turn enable the cube to get to its equilibrium position on its own. Looking forward to this -- I always thought that was the coolest part of the Cubli project. Here's a video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39341811,"author":"jrussino","month":"2024-02","story":"I designed a cube that balances itself on a corner","stance":"reference","why":"Old Cubli videos chain jumping, balancing and falling into locomotion","quote":"they go further, chaining together \"jumping up\", balancing, and controlled falling to demonstrate locomotion!","text":"In the old Cubli videos they go further, chaining together \"jumping up\", balancing, and controlled falling to demonstrate locomotion! [VIDEO]"},{"id":14796220,"author":"venning","month":"2017-07","story":"Japan's zero-gravity space drone sends first pictures from ISS","stance":"reference","why":"Reaction wheels with braking transfer momentum to the body","quote":"the reaction wheels spin up while keeping total angular momentum at zero","text":"From a related video: the reaction wheels spin up while keeping total angular momentum at zero, then apply a rapid (magnetic) brake to transfer the momentum to the body (from the wheels). I'm assuming if you do that precisely enough (and in three dimensions), you can generate linear movement. EDIT: I'm probably very wrong about the movement. EDIT: The video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12142541,"author":"justifier","month":"2016-07","story":"The Science of the Missing Gravitational Waves","stance":"reference","why":"Gyroscopic reaction wheels shifting center of mass","quote":"","text":"> Consider the Bullet Cluster, which is two colliding galactic clusters. The gravitational center of mass is offset from the visible center of mass. This offset cannot be explained as potential energy of visible matter. There must be something else, invisible and massive, i.e. dark matter. i'd argue another possible explanation to add to your i.e. was addressed by the gp gp> then galaxies are not moving in the way we currently think they are. the movement of gyroscopes can alter a system's center of mass [VIDEO]"}]},"q2gO4DKVpa8":{"wy":"HN brings it up in inequality, UBI and backlash threads as an early warning from a rich insider.","q":{"x":"Nick Hanauer understood this fourteen years ago.","i":47802096,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":18187809,"author":"anon49124","month":"2018-10","story":"Universal Basic Income Is Silicon Valley’s Latest Scam","stance":"rec","why":"Argues pitchforks will come for the wealthy without redistribution","quote":"Otherwise, pitchforks will be coming for the 1%.","text":"The big thing UBI can do is help people whom want and desire to get out of suffering and miserable circumstances to higher level of productivity, lifestyle and tax generation. It's a no-brainer. Otherwise, pitchforks will be coming for the 1%. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47724489,"author":"jyounker","month":"2026-04","story":"Molotov Cocktail Is Hurled at Home of OpenAI CEO Sam Altman","stance":"reference","why":"Hanauer warning about widening inequality","quote":"Nick Hanauer has been talking about this for over ten years","text":"It's due to the widening inequality. Nick Hanauer has been talking about this for over ten years: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47802096,"author":"i_think_so","month":"2026-04","story":"We gave an AI a 3 year retail lease and asked it to make a profit","stance":"reference","why":"Hanauer's early warning about social unrest","quote":"Nick Hanauer understood this fourteen years ago.","text":"...and in America there are more guns than humans, and more potentially unemployed white collar workers than the police, military, and national guard combined. Nick Hanauer understood this fourteen years ago. Very few others did. And despite him spending his own time and money to explain it in simple English, nobody in his peer group wanted to hear it -- his TED talk on the subject ... took several years before it was published. Just a coincidence, I'm sure. FA (for a decade or so) FO, I guess? [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] than humans, and more potentially unemployed white collar workers than the police, military and national guard combined."},{"id":43252039,"author":"untwerp","month":"2025-03","story":"Inheriting is becoming nearly as important as working","stance":"reference","why":"Wealthy have long been warned of backlash","quote":"They have been well aware of it. See Beware, fellow plutocrats, the pitchforks are coming","text":"> The hyper-wealthy seem oblivious to the fact that you can't both be a trillionaire that's causing societal economic desperation and live in a society where people politely do their business and leave you in peace. They have been well aware of it. See Beware, fellow plutocrats, the pitchforks are coming [VIDEO] We seem to be in the bread and circus phase for the time"},{"id":22535999,"author":"eliomattia","month":"2020-03","story":"Pay/receive Universal Basic Income without middlemen","stance":"reference","why":"Example of wealthy people open to contributing","quote":"","text":"Interesting! Government(-backed) UBI is the ultimate goal. But how to make it happen? First, why has it not happened already? All the data indicate that it's good. A no-brainer, then? Well, - It's expensive - Governments vest financial power in businesses, not in themselves. End results? 1) A very oiled mechanism for income concentration, no questions asked 2) No mechanism for income distribution (!) 3) Blaming the government for not providing UBI already 4) No UBI, be it voluntary or government 5) Many people, who in fact want to contribute towards it, cannot. Why not start by creating that mechanism for income distribution, then? A quote about Sam Altman[1]: «Altman suggests researching Universal Basic Income. (Y Combinator has already begun work on a UBI project in Oakland.) “We should set a goal of eliminating poverty in the country,” Altman writes on his website. “I’m not yet sure what a reasonable timeframe for this goal is, but I do feel a moral obligation to figure out how to do it.” Unions aren’t working, Altman says, and wages are stagnant, so we need something better, even though he isn’t sure what that is yet.» Some examples of people who would like to contribute, yet cannot: - Nick Hanauer[2,3] - Letter for a wealth tax[4], not exactly on income but on wealth, yet indicative of the supply - The Giving Pledge[5] - Yusaku Maezawa[6] - Andrew Yang[7,8] has just brought together donors who are pledging millions for UBI - Patriotic Millionaires[9] - The World Economic"}]},"tCRPUv8V22o":{"wy":"Linked in threads about algorithmic and demoscene music as the canonical introduction to bytebeat.","q":{"x":"Surprisingly complex music from very simple modular arithmetic and bit shifting.","i":39661839,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39661839,"author":"isoprophlex","month":"2024-03","story":"Mandala: A little plaground for testing pixel logic patterns","stance":"rec","why":"Complex music from simple modular arithmetic and bit shifting.","quote":"Surprisingly complex music from very simple modular arithmetic and bit shifting.","text":"See also the 1D version of this, rendered as audio: \"bytebeat\" music. Surprisingly complex music from very simple modular arithmetic and bit shifting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22853941,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-04","story":"Conway's Game of Life on Dec PDP-7, Type 340 Display, & Am Radio ByteBeat Music","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Bytebeat is algorithmic music created from one line of code","text":"Listening to a PDP-7 with an AM radio isn't exactly ByteBeat (since you're technically only allowed one line of code), but it sure sounds a lot like it: Meet Bytebeat: A Brand New Electronic Music Genre [LINK] >Become a slave to the algorhythm. >Heard about the latest computer music genre sweeping the internet? It’s called “bytebeat” and if this comes as news to you, don’t despair, the thing’s only about five months old, so you’re still ahead of the curve. Bytebeat is algorithmic music created from one line of code and was discovered/invented while Finnish low-tech artist and programmer Ville-Matias Heikkila (aka Viznut) and his friends were experimenting with the computer programming language C code and creating one line formulas that could produce an audio output (see below). Bytebeat: Music from very short programs - the 3rd iteration [VIDEO] Bytebeat - Music from math formulas [LINK]"},{"id":13687365,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-02","story":"The Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: A Visual Approach (2015) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I have seen this in the wild! It's used all over the place in the demo-scene.","text":"> Points near the origin have dark colors, with the color assigned to a complex number z approaching black as z approaches 0. Points far from the origin are light, with the color of z approaching white as |z| approaches infinity. Every complex number has a different color in this picture, so a complex number can be uniquely specified by giving its color. So I was wondering if this approach could also be useful in the context of floats and integers. The implementation becomes simpler in many ways (we only have a finite number of possible values to consider). For example, start with a unique mapping of 8-bit integers to a continuous colour ramp. I suppose the easiest option would be to start in the top left, then go from left to right, top to bottom, resulting in a 16x16 image. If we then take a formula that also returns an 8-bit value, map every possible input to an output, and show the results as an image, it should make it easy to see where we have an integer overflow, for example (if we see sudden jumps from very bright to dark or the other way around). Then I realised: I have seen this in the wild! It's used all over the place in the demo-scene. For example: [VIDEO] [LINK] Aside, this also reminds me of Gustafson's closure plots for comparing his number encoding to standard floating points, although it's not really the same thing: [LINK]"},{"id":7911308,"author":"vegedor","month":"2014-06","story":"Show HN: Wavepot – Digital audio workstation of the web","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"i feel like this belongs here","text":">I'm glad you like it. The sound is generated purely by a JavaScript function returning sample values i feel like this belongs here: [VIDEO] - not in a DAW kinda sense, though"},{"id":5606336,"author":"laumars","month":"2013-04","story":"Experimental one-line algorithmic music","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"one of my favorite tracks from this scene was generated that way[2]","text":"I think the theory behind this is a great deal more interesting than that video lets on. The idea started out as simple C programs piping characters to a UNIX audio device. But it's grown since then, now there's a web page that lets you create audio with Javascript expressions[1] (one of my favorite tracks from this scene was generated that way[2]). There's some good blog posts about the origins of this[3], and music theory behind it[4]; both of which will go into more detail than I'd care to for a HN comment[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] (5m 13s) [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["aNR4n0i2ZlM",1],["My4RA5I0FKs",1]]},"1_OXM4mr_i0":{"wy":"HN cites it as the standard demonstration of superheated water, in threads about microwaving water and tea.","q":{"x":"Mythbusters video of an example of superheated water","i":31043703,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31043703,"author":"noasaservice","month":"2022-04","story":"Employee’s unwanted birthday party nets him $450k after lawsuit","stance":"reference","why":"Example of superheated water.","quote":"Mythbusters video of an example of superheated water","text":"I seem to remember as well that the coffee was reported as instantly boiling when sugar was added. If that's the case (and no reason to disbelieve the plaintiff), that's a superheated liquid. In other words, it is a liquid above its own boiling point and waiting for a cavitation event (something for bubbles to hold on to) to instantly boil. And this seems to coincide with the coffee explosion and burns from the plantiff. Mythbusters video of an example of superheated water: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23646543,"author":"shaftway","month":"2020-06","story":"US woman sparks transatlantic tea war with brutal online brew","stance":"reference","why":"Mythbusters tests superheated water.","quote":"Mythbusters took a look","text":"I thought one of the reasons not to use a microwave is that it's way to easy for the water to get superheated. Especially in a ceramic mug that is smooth on the inside, with no nucleation sites for the water to form steam it can get way over boiling point fairly easily. Then when you put something in (like a teabag or sugar) you're dropping a ton of nucleation sites in and the water will flash to steam, occasionally exploding the mug. But maybe that's just an urban legend. /shruggie Mythbusters took a look: [VIDEO] Could you use a small saucepan and bring water to the desired temperature if you don't want to use a microwave or teapot? Maybe a small coffee maker without any coffee in it, just to get the water up to the temperature you want."},{"id":33500472,"author":"sltkr","month":"2022-11","story":"Barilla passive cooker","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"but note that they use distilled water (which nobody drinks) in a Pyrex container (which nobody uses).","text":"It's something to be aware of, but in practice it's very difficult to superheat water at home. For superheating to occur, you need very pure water in a smooth container with no imperfections. Tap water in most places cannot be superheated because it contains too many minerals, and most ceramic cups aren't smooth enough to prevent boiling. If you're making tea, an easy way to prevent superheating is to drop the bag in the water before you heat it. Another way is to put a wooden stirrer in the cup. There's a mythbuster video here where they show water being superheated: [VIDEO] , but note that they use distilled water (which nobody drinks) in a Pyrex container (which nobody uses). And they actually use a mug of tap water as a control."}]},"1nBx-37c3c8":{"wy":"HN cites it as a well-known clip on how structural incentives and hiring shape media bias without conspiracy.","q":{"x":"if you believed something different, you wouldn't be sitting where you're sitting","i":21657857,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":38263196,"author":"conception","month":"2023-11","story":"EU Parliament Decides That Your Private Messages Must Not Be Scanned","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky's point applied to lobbying.","quote":"a key aspect that I think Noam put well here","text":"I think there is a theme in this thread that misses a key aspect that I think Noam put well here - [VIDEO] I think the idea that if an opposing idea went to an NRA friend and offered more money, in almost all cases they would refuse because the lobbying dollars aren’t to convince but to support someone who asked believes what you do. In some cases, probably like crypto laws, a politician might think “sure I don’t care or have an opinion, you bought some donations and I don’t hate your opinion so I’ll help out”, but that is a lot different than “you pay me x and I vote y.” I think taking the agency out of the politicians hands, in most cases, is the wrong perspective."},{"id":33460236,"author":"pannSun","month":"2022-11","story":"Vox journo says NYT tech reporters had mandate to not cover big tech positively","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky quote on hiring like-minded people.","quote":"By hiring only the like-minded, top-down decisions can appear organic.","text":"By hiring only the like-minded, top-down decisions can appear organic. In the words of Chomsky: I don't say you're self-censoring. I'm sure you believe everything you're saying. But what I'm saying is if you believed something different, you wouldn't be sitting where you're sitting. - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31947962,"author":"ahelwer","month":"2022-07","story":"The Pentagon uses a secret program to wage proxy wars","stance":"reference","why":"Famous Chomsky clip on structural incentives shaping what journalists believe.","quote":"there's a famous clip of Chomsky making this point here","text":"The point of analyzing structural incentives is that you don't need a conspiracy. Everyone involved actually believes they are fully and truthfully discharging their duties and fighting the good fight. None of them are walking around plotting how to pull the wool over the public's eyes. They wouldn't be hired if they believed otherwise; there's a famous clip of Chomsky making this point here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23834704,"author":"rbecker","month":"2020-07","story":"Resignation Letter","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Even ten times that many journalists will do you no good, when what they report is filtered according to some pre-concieved view of the world (or the journalists themselves are only hired if they share that view: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":21657857,"author":"mistermann","month":"2019-11","story":"How can you know what news is real?","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky explains how editorial bias works through hiring, without conspiracy.","quote":"if you believed something different, you wouldn't be sitting where you're sitting","text":"A skeptical mind should immediately ask \"wait, that sounds like a bit of a conspiracy theory, am I to believe Jeff Bezos is acting as an editor for the newspaper he owns?\" Which is a perfectly valid question. Noam Chomsky explains to a BBC journalist how it can be accomplished much more cleverly: [VIDEO] Reporter: How can you know that I'm self-censoring, how can you know that a journalist is self-censoring? Chomsky: I'm not saying you're self-censoring, I'm sure you believe everything you say. But what I'm saying is, if you believed something different, you wouldn't be sitting where you're sitting."}]},"2T0OUIW89II":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a defense of Bitcoin's energy use, offered as a rebuttal to claims that mining is wasteful.","m":[{"id":26150857,"author":"oraphalous","month":"2021-02","story":"Mining Bitcoin with pencil and paper: 0.67 hashes per day (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Bitcoin community does consider energy consumption.","quote":"smart people in the bitcoin space NEVER spent any time thinking about energy consumption","text":"\"and many enthusiasts don't seem to understand enough about Bitcoin to even realize it.\" Yeah because - smart people in the bitcoin space NEVER spent any time thinking about energy consumption. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25320652,"author":"siwatanejo","month":"2020-12","story":"Bitcoin still makes no sense","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a source debunking Bitcoin energy waste claims.","quote":"Debunked.","text":"> The second one is that it makes illegal activities easier, and at a larger scale. It's quite common right now for companies to be coerced pay millions of dollars to anonymous criminals, all enabled with cryptocurrencies. For the vast majority of businesses/individuals, it is governments and banking cartels the ones stealing your money (via inflation and fractional reserve respectively) without recourse. Switching to a world in which, instead of all of us, but only a few, are stolen money, is a bit better. > The most prominent negative effect is the huge energy waste, which doesn't help when we are trying to cut on our carbon footprint Debunked. Sources: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":17477071,"author":"craigc","month":"2018-07","story":"Is the Bitcoin network an oligarchy?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Based on your reply, I am fairly convinced you have already made up your mind so it is somewhat pointless to spend my time trying to convince you otherwise, but I will say a couple things. For one, while our civilized world is built on trust and you may trust Banks, Governments, Tech companies etc, the majority of millennials do not trust Banks or Governments. Numbers outside the US are even lower. For more information about this I would recommend watching this presentation: [LINK] I believe money is something that you should be able to have full control and ownership of. Governments and banks can lock or seize accounts/funds for any reason. Banks can limit deposit/withdrawal/transfer amounts. I don’t think that right now this is a big problem, but in the future, imagine banks getting hacked and funds stolen or even a bank run. There are a lot of scenarios where I can see our financial system failing. If inflation rates increase then it will drive more people towards things like Gold and Bitcoin. Look at what has been going on in Venezuela. As far as the electricity use, please consider taking a look at this article: [LINK] It also links to a couple of other sources that show how gold mining, paper currency/minting, and electricity used from the banking system all use orders of magnitude more electricity than Bitcoin mining does. If ultimately Bitcoin can replace banks or even gold, then it will have done the world a favor. Most miners also use renewable energy such as hydro "},{"id":17190515,"author":"craigc","month":"2018-05","story":"How Futures Trading Changed Bitcoin Prices","stance":"reference","why":"Offered in defense of Bitcoin's energy use.","quote":"I would also encourage you to watch this","text":"> It can be double-spent Well aware of this fact, but it has never happened partially because Bitcoin is the largest and most secure blockchain. Regular currencies can be double spent too, but rely on trusted intermediaries to step in and reverse the fraudulent transactions. In a way that is part of what makes Bitcoin so remarkable. That you can transact without having to depend on intermediaries. > It can not, therefore, represent an economy that expands in real value as it grows to include more people engaging in more trade. No, the value is in the fact that what you hold represents a piece of a limited number of coins that will ever exist. Gold does not increase in value because people are actively spending it to purchase coffee. It is a hedge against inflation. As more US dollars enter the economy and are used for transactions, the value of a single dollar decreases. > The network presently costs the Earth as much energy as the entire energy usage of the country of Denmark. I am not going to defend the energy usage and say that that is good, but the energy usage is part of the reason the network is secure and why there hasn’t been a 51% attack. FWIW the traditional economy: banks, vaults, armored vehicles, money printing, etc uses up far more energy. I would also encourage you to watch this: [VIDEO] . Bitcoin actually has the power to introduce innovations to produce cheaper, alternative energy due to mining competition. > A transaction can take \"anywhere from 30 minutes "}]},"5QQdNbvSGok":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference in threads about confusing error messages and printers.","m":[{"id":32338238,"author":"deckard1","month":"2022-08","story":"Cut the cutesy errors","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Or can read like PC LOAD LETTER[1] to others.","text":"> but they do seem to becoming more prevalent. I think prevalence is important here. Everyone seems to be injecting cuteness into everything today. Blog posts or README files overflowing with emojiis and meme images. I'd really like to banish the rocket emoji. The metaphor is exhausted. The corporation-as-a-friend is everywhere. Interestingly, some errors started out life as deadly serious and only became humorous later. \"lp0 on fire\". Printers used to really catch fire. Unix really was suggesting that maybe you want to look into that. The message still exists in Linux (as far as I know), but today seems rather funny to some people. Or can read like PC LOAD LETTER[1] to others. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29360882,"author":"dylan604","month":"2021-11","story":"Why Paper Jams Persist (2018)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] My 2 least favorite things about computers: fonts and printing. Even in 2021, printing still sucks. Sure, we've decided we want to print on so many different types of printing surfaces, that modern printer manufactures probably dream of the days only printing on white paper. Makes me wish for a laser printer with an option to use dot matrix fan folded style paper feed. At least you know it's going to pull straight."},{"id":11233734,"author":"DanBC","month":"2016-03","story":"DOD officials say autonomous killing machines deserve a look","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"PC LOAD LETTER","text":"> * Robots do not act cruelly unless specifically instructed. Well, sure, but have you used computers recently? It's often a fucking awful experience and while the computer clearly isn't trying to make your life frustrating that's often the result. PC LOAD LETTER [VIDEO] [LINK] Do we really want the people who make printers to make autonomous killing machines?"}]},"74Ig_QKndvE":{"wy":"Cited in Prolog threads as a rare documentary on the language's origins, with proper subtitles despite being in French.","q":{"x":"A recently released documentary talks a bit about these developments","i":31214915,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35664752,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2023-04","story":"Why did Prolog lose steam? (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Colmerauer originated Constraint Logic Programming","quote":"it was Alain Colmerauer, co-creator of Prolog, who also came up with the idea of Constraint Logic Programming","text":"Nice! >> As I understand it, Prolog itself is CLP(H), that is CLP over the Herbrand terms (that is, atoms and functors and stuff). That's an interesting way to see it. Quite an unorthodox one, mind. I learned recently that it was Alain Colmerauer, co-creator of Prolog, who also came up with the idea of Constraint Logic Programming, specifically to address Prolog's er difficulties with numbers and arithmetic. See: [VIDEO] I was recently asked a question about the use of Peano notation in some stuff I was working on. I didn't think of it that way at the time, but one big advantage of Peano notation compared to is/2 is that it is... well, in a nutshell it's declarative integer arithmetic, just like CLP(FD). Boy I hate saying jargon-y things like that. But Peano numbers as Prolog terms in predicate's heads can be backtracked-over, and unwound when recursion unwinds, just like you do in your rle2/2 above. The downside is that they get hard to read once they get too big. Then again, you can easily do this sort of thing: %! peano(?Arabic,?Peano) is nondet. % % Convert between Arabic and Peano number notations. % % Use this program to quickly convert between Peano notation and % Arabic notation. % % Accepted modes are (+,-) and (-,+). In mode (?,?) this predicate % returns only 0 deterministically. % % Examples: % == % ?- peano(1,P). % P = s(0). % % ?- peano(I,s(0)). % I = 1. % % ?- peano(N,P). % N = P, P = 0. % == % peano(N,P):- peano(P,0,N). peano(0,N,N):- !. peano(s(P),N,Acc):- su"},{"id":34232391,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2023-01","story":"Prolog at Work","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary with Kowalski interview on Colmerauer and Prolog's origins","quote":"this documentary (in French) about Alain Colmerauer","text":"Thanks for trying to correct me, if I'm wrong (but thanks anyway). What I write is the impression I've formed after reading various sources on the history of Prolog, over the years. Some of them are even by Kowalski himself and my impression that he was the \"neat\" and Colmerauer the \"scruffy\" comes from his comments on their relation. I certainly could have misunderstood his comments, or be misremembering them. Unfortunately it will take me some time to dig out the specific sources and check my assumptions [1]. One such recent source is this documentary (in French) about Alain Colmerauer by a lady who shares his surname (a relative, I'm guessing): [VIDEO] I've pointed the link to just before the start of an interview with Kowalski where he says (in English) what I transfer above, that Colmerauer was more practically minded and coming from a computer science side point of view while he (Kowalski) was more theoretically minded and coming from a mathematical logic point of view, and also that this tension created an \"alchemy\" that would not have transpired otherwise. I watched this documentary very recently and I guess it coloured my comment above. More generally, it's easy to find discussion of the \"pure\" subset of Prolog and about its purported advantages compared to the rest. I'd have to go back to those textbooks to be sure, but I think I first encountered this idea in The Art of Prolog (O'Keefe) or \"The Craft of Prolog\" (Sterling & Shapiro), or probably both. And I have hea"},{"id":31214915,"author":"triska","month":"2022-04","story":"Natural Language Processing with Prolog in the IBM Watson System (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on Colmerauer and the origins of Prolog","quote":"A recently released documentary talks a bit about these developments","text":"Prolog is brilliantly useful for text processing and reasoning about strings, mostly due to its built-in grammar formalism called Definite Clause Grammars (DCGs). In fact, Prolog originates from Q-systems which were specifically designed to process language via grammar rules: [LINK] The \"Q\" stands for Québec, where Alain Colmerauer developed this formalism which eventually led to Prolog. A recently released documentary talks a bit about these developments: [VIDEO] It is interesting that the very first Prolog systems, Prolog 0 and Marseille Prolog, represented strings as lists of characters, then the Edinburgh tradition used lists of codes (in the implementation defined encoding), and all Prolog systems that have become available in the last 5 years (Scryer Prolog, Tau Prolog, Trealla Prolog and ichiban/prolog) are again using lists of characters to make string processing as convenient and readable as originally intended. For example, we can use definite clause grammars in Scryer Prolog to find occurrences of the same character twice in immediate succession in a string: ?- phrase((...,[C,C],...), \"Hello, world!\"). C = l ; false. In this string, the character 'l' is the only such letter."},{"id":31215100,"author":"funstuff007","month":"2022-04","story":"Natural Language Processing with Prolog in the IBM Watson System (2011)","stance":"reference","why":"French documentary with proper subtitles","quote":"the doc is en Francais. However, it does have legit subtitles","text":"> [VIDEO] Bon jour, the doc is en Francais. However, it does have legit subtitles (i.e. not auto-generated)."}]},"8vQmTZTq7nw":{"wy":"HN cites it as the example of physically trucking data when network transfer is impractical.","q":{"x":"You can transfer up to 100PB per Snowmobile, a 45-foot long rugged sized shipping container, pulled by a semi-trailer truck.","i":18976724,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":40034104,"author":"Animats","month":"2024-04","story":"WD Ultrastar Transporter: Briefcase with 368TB of NVMe SSD, 128GB RAM, 1300W PSU","stance":"reference","why":"AWS Snowmobile for the biggest data transfers","quote":"for those really big transfers, the \"Snowmobile\".","text":"AWS has a whole line of these, their \"Snow\" product line.[1] There's the \"Snowcone\", the \"Snowball\", and, for those really big transfers, the \"Snowmobile\".[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18976724,"author":"pizza","month":"2019-01","story":"Twitter migrates data to Google Cloud","stance":"reference","why":"AWS presentation of Snowmobile truck for moving exabyte-scale data","quote":"You can transfer up to 100PB per Snowmobile, a 45-foot long rugged sized shipping container, pulled by a semi-trailer truck.","text":"The scale of these megacorps is crazy! I wonder how you even plan to move that much data. I know that Amazon has a service called Snowmobile for stuff like this. They probably did something similar here. \"You can transfer up to 100PB per Snowmobile, a 45-foot long rugged sized shipping container, pulled by a semi-trailer truck.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":15373673,"author":"sillysaurus3","month":"2017-09","story":"Wozniak and Copps: Ending net neutrality will end the Internet as we know it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] \"Never underestimate the bandwidth of a station wagon full of tapes barreling down the highway at 70mph.\""}]},"D-On0DGcDlc":{"wy":"Used in bike-lock discussions as evidence that the LiteLok is weak, and a reminder that LPL reviews are a trusted benchmark for lock security.","m":[{"id":23046593,"author":"krn","month":"2020-05","story":"France offers each cyclist €50 for bike repairs once lockdown ends","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a LitLok cut in 16 seconds.","quote":"LitLok cut in 16 seconds","text":"> Because the average bike price is going up it also becomes interesting to steal parts. A decent handlebar + shifters are easy to sell individually. That's an interesting point. Doesn't that make a folding full-size bike the only real solution? Something like Changebike DF-611MB: [LINK] > There are a few new lock types which are supposed to work better against angle grinders like Tex-lock or LitLok. After a quick search... Tex-lock cut in 32 seconds: [LINK] LitLok cut in 16 seconds: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35231937,"author":"howling","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm skeptical of any bike lock's security that is not reviewed by LPL.","text":"This is the same company that creates the touted highly secure LiteLok Gold that LPL cuts in 16s. [VIDEO] I'm skeptical of any bike lock's security that is not reviewed by LPL."},{"id":24403679,"author":"crote","month":"2020-09","story":"Threat modelling case study: bicycles","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The kevlar fibers don't really work in practice.","text":"The kevlar fibers don't really work in practice. Turns out you can just use a pair of shears or a boltcutter. Examples: [LINK] [VIDEO]"}]},"F7pYHN9iC9I":{"wy":"Cited as a vivid demonstration of how much personal data is findable and shared online, in privacy and tracking threads.","m":[{"id":26608325,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2021-03","story":"How can you tell if someone is lying?","stance":"reference","why":"Mind reader video as a warning about data surveillance.","quote":"","text":"The notion that behavioural tics can betray lying (or truth-telling) is also part of the kayfabye / stage dressing of truth-determination and vetting. If you have a set of props (polygraph, truth-teller, various actual or fabricated observations, etc.) that you can present as a plausible basis for claiming someone is lying, then you can apply more severe psychological pressure, possibly getting them to break and confess, though often also simply forcing a false confession, a very real hazard. (See various accounts suggesting that friendly-demeanor interrogation is far more useful.) In the account I detailed in an earlier comment on this thread, I'd ... let slip to people who I thought might feed back my counterpart ... that I was in touch with some people who were incredibly good at hacking online information, and dug for (and revealed) a few details I'd turned up ... which actually were based on reverse-engineering a database structure and assessing the contents. (One ... glorious personal weakness of the adversary revealed some critical information I'd been looking for, in particular.) I suspect that message did find its way back, as my adversary was caught entirely unawares. The net effects weren't entirely unlike a YouTube video on an \"amazing mind reader\" (actually warning of data surveillance and privacy ... on behalf of a bank): [VIDEO] And the CIA's Crypto AG operation, in which a firm allegedly selling products to encrypt Telex communications had been back-doored for"},{"id":11266698,"author":"kelseydh","month":"2016-03","story":"Trackers","stance":"reference","why":"Mind reader demo showing data sharing risks.","quote":"That reminds me a lot of this video","text":"That reminds me a lot of this video: [VIDEO] Would be great to make one similar combining the two concepts in regards to sharing phone data."},{"id":4593221,"author":"fruchtose","month":"2012-09","story":"Dave The Incredible Mindreader – How Does He Do It?","stance":"reference","why":"Direct link to the video behind a blogspam article.","quote":"Just go here for the video that the link pads with meaningless text","text":"This link nothing but blogspam. Just go here for the video that the link pads with meaningless text: [VIDEO]"}]},"FZ6lZJWL_Xk":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a primary source for what Tesla and Musk said about Autopilot and automatic charging at the time.","m":[{"id":8819692,"author":"beltex","month":"2014-12","story":"Tesla Is Working on Robotic Snakes That Emerge from the Wall to Charge Your Car","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Musk's snake charger remark","quote":"have the charge connector plug itself in.","text":"This was originally mentioned by Elon back in October at the launch of the Dual Motor Model S and Autopilot event. It was in reference to the cars new ability to park itself. “Something I’d like to do, which I think many of our engineers will be hearing this in real-time, is have the charge connector plug itself in. Like an articulating, like sort of a snake, like Metal Gear Solid or something.” [VIDEO]"},{"id":8439352,"author":"dave1619","month":"2014-10","story":"Watch This Tesla Drive Itself at the Press of a Button [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I did a test ride of a P85D last night. It was breathtaking. The car explodes (silently) of course like a rocket. It feel much, much faster than a regular P85. Here's the test ride I took: [LINK] It's difficult to explain in just words how fast 3.2 seconds is and I had a difficult time grasping it myself until I actually sat in a Tesla P85D and experienced it going from 0-60 in a blazing quick three seconds. It felt like a completely different (and much quicker) car than the previous single-motor P85. The car accelerates so quickly with so much force that I found myself in disbelief and awe. Tesla truly engineered an amazing new version of the Model S. This new dual-motor version of the Model S is available to order in the performance version and will be available in the 60kwh and 85kwh version vehicles in February. Overall, having an AWD dual-motor version expands the appeal of the Model S to more market segments, especially those in colder areas. And the addition of a dual-motor performance version catapults the P85D into the realm of a true super car. Also in an unexpected and shocking move, Elon Musk announced Tesla's Autopilot hardware at last night's event. Most people were expecting some trivial driver assist features like lane departure warning and adaptive cruise control. Further, in previous interviews Elon had alluded to Autopilot being ready by the end of 2015. But in last night's announcement, Elon outlined a 4-part Autopilot system that would allow for driving t"},{"id":36143501,"author":"stefan_","month":"2023-05","story":"Tesla Confirms Automated Driving Systems Were Engaged During Fatal Crash","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Toyota never claimed any of this stuff.","text":"Tesla Unveils Dual Motor And Autopilot, Oct 10, 2014: [VIDEO] \"from zero miles an hour to 155 miles an hour and so it'll detect if there's a car in your blind spot if you've got a highway barrier on one side if there's something you might you know move into in any way possible\" Toyota never claimed any of this stuff."}]},"FaOSCASqLsE":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly invoke it as a joke when discussing meetings, management and workplace absurdity.","m":[{"id":47428579,"author":"Supermancho","month":"2026-03","story":"Tech hobbyist makes shoulder-mounted guided missile prototype with $96 in parts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Watching the video I immediately thought of the SNL skit","text":"Watching the video I immediately thought of the SNL skit: [VIDEO] If I am understanding what I saw, for all the work on the canards, the propellant runs out immediately leaving it to tumble in air, That being said, I agree that it's a prototype and all that entails. I agree that it can (and probably will be) improved upon."},{"id":34235434,"author":"ourmandave","month":"2023-01","story":"Shopify cancels all recurring meetings with over two people “in perpetuity”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This reminds me of SNL's Undercover Boss Starkiller Base","text":"This reminds me of SNL's Undercover Boss Starkiller Base ~ Kylo Ren [VIDEO] Kylo Ren as Matt: \"He said Kylo Ren has an 8-pack. That Kylo Ren is shredded.\""},{"id":15804612,"author":"saluki","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: When is 2FA not enough?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Dude 2FA straight up sucks!","text":"Dude 2FA straight up sucks! Undercover Boss Star Killer Base Edition [VIDEO] 2FA is a weak link, it's been proven it's easy to social engineer access to your phone account/number. Once someone has your phone they will receive all your 2FA notifications and once they can reset your email password with it they have the keys to the castle. Plus if you lose your phone, then you're locked out, without your printable one time use keys, that you probably didn't print. There has to be something better."}]},"FzDBiUemCSY":{"wy":"HN shares it as an amusing glimpse of Jobs before he was polished on camera.","q":{"x":"The part where they put a giant white earbud in his ear is priceless","i":2199090,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":14386369,"author":"Udo_Schmitz","month":"2017-05","story":"Nightline Interview with Steve Jobs (1981) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies source interview of this pre-show clip","quote":"The Interview that starts at 5:50 seems to be the one where this pre-show clip is from","text":"The Interview that starts at 5:50 seems to be the one where this pre-show clip is from: [VIDEO] CNN dates that video to 1978 though: [LINK]"},{"id":3695238,"author":"nostromo","month":"2012-03","story":"Paul Graham consistently says ‘um’ ~7 times per minute","stance":"reference","why":"Example that even natural speakers get nervous","quote":"","text":"> I wondered if he was a little nervous, but that didn’t seem likely for someone in his position. All sorts of people can get nervous speaking in public, including people that are thought of as being 'natural' public speakers like Steve Jobs ( [VIDEO] ) and Sam Harris ( [LINK] ), as well as billionaires ( [LINK] ) and CEOs ( [LINK] )."},{"id":2199090,"author":"mayank","month":"2011-02","story":"Video: 24 year-old Steve Jobs prepping for his first TV appearance (1978)","stance":"reference","why":"Amusing moment with a giant white earbud","quote":"The part where they put a giant white earbud in his ear is priceless","text":"The part where they put a giant white earbud in his ear is priceless, especially when he looks somewhat suspiciously at it. EDIT: direct link: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35243380,"author":"HopenHeyHi","month":"2023-03","story":"Richard Stallman's Ted talk in Steve Jobs' style/voice (elevenlabs.io/ChatGPT)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"His first TV appearance","text":"Steve Wozniak: Where's your beard? Steve Jobs: In the bathroom sink. I shaved it off. Steve Wozniak: Well, how come? Steve Jobs: 'Cause banks don't like beards. -- His first TV appearance: [VIDEO] Reportedly, Steve used to be a smelly bearded hippie."}]},"NCvKXgp-Awo":{"wy":"Cited as a cringe-worthy example of corporate culture and bad corporate branding.","m":[{"id":28783416,"author":"tgv","month":"2021-10","story":"Canyon.mid","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"If you haven't heard it, first prepare some ear bleach before you click.","text":"The infinite four chord inspiration sound is quite dreadful (shame on you, Hans Zimmer), but I do hope we never revert to the times of KPMG, we're strong as can be: [VIDEO] . If you haven't heard it, first prepare some ear bleach before you click."},{"id":27110306,"author":"bitwize","month":"2021-05","story":"Why does every advert look the same? Corporate Memphis","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Corporate Memphis is about as funky as the KPMG theme song","text":"Tim Burton's movies often have aesthetic mini-manifestos embedded in them. For example, Beetlejuice represented the reverse of the classic \"ghost story\", one in which the ghosts were sweet, earnest, and identifiable while the living people were horrid and monstrous. So for the Maitlands, Burton chose the aesthetic of their traditional Victorian Connecticut home with all its understated ornateness. The building has \"character\", which the young couple wish to preserve. The Deetzes (except for Lydia) are represented by... Memphis. And the fact that they strip the home down and completely remodel it into a Memphis mishmash indicates how horrible and destructive they are. That said, OG Memphis at least has an element of fun and funkiness to it. Think the opening titles of Saved by the Bell . Corporate Memphis is about as funky as the KPMG theme song: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17883665,"author":"mav3r1ck","month":"2018-08","story":"Mutiny at the big five is part of the future of work","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it is beyond hilariously bad","text":">Remember the KPMG anthem that leaked circa 2000? Actually, no I don't, never heard of this until now, but it is beyond hilariously bad, assuming this [0] was the real version. It gets better, KPMG tried to claim that another website cannot link to them with their permission [1], now _that_ is hilarious. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":22293560,"author":"bitwize","month":"2020-02","story":"The Disneyfication of a University","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I'm getting flashbacks to the cringe I felt the first time I heard the KPMG song","text":"Oh man, I'm getting flashbacks to the cringe I felt the first time I heard the KPMG song: [VIDEO] It's no coincidence that corporate anthems like this sound similar to the Scientology hymn We Stand Tall: [LINK]"}]},"RqvuEal2P2E":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of creators' complaints about YouTube copyright blocks on music instruction videos.","q":{"x":"Rick Beato's channel has several rants about this, and here's one of them","i":24573291,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":38940973,"author":"ChrisMarshallNY","month":"2024-01","story":"Investigating Rock Radio with Rick Beato","stance":"reference","why":"Beato rant on copyright takedowns.","quote":"Rick Beato is pretty cool. I remember a rant, he did about copyright takedown crap[0]","text":"Rick Beato is pretty cool. I remember a rant, he did about copyright takedown crap[0] (He's actually done a bunch of them). > Making videos is really hard. This has been my experience, which is why I tend to write longform, instead of producing videos (which I know would be a lot more popular). [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27055802,"author":"lovelyviking","month":"2021-05","story":"Piano teacher gets copyright claim for Beethoven’s Moonlight Sonata [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Another creator's anecdote about blocked videos.","quote":"Here is another anedcdote from one of the famous YouTube creators","text":"You basically use one single anecdote while YouTube ... Here is another anedcdote from one of the famous YouTube creators: I Confronted the People That BLOCKED My Video (Rant) [VIDEO] I Testified Before the SENATE On BLOCKERS | Here's What Happened [LINK]"},{"id":24573291,"author":"tshaddox","month":"2020-09","story":"They're deleting my channel, but they don't know why? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Beato rant on copyright blocks of music instruction videos.","quote":"Rick Beato's channel has several rants about this, and here's one of them","text":"Music instruction channels on YouTube know that this may technically be true but simply does not matter. Rick Beato's channel has several rants about this, and here's one of them: [VIDEO] Apparently you can have YouTube videos blocked or demonetized for even mentioning the name of a song from some very famous artists who have very active legal teams."}]},"TbwlC2B-BIg":{"wy":"Used as a funny reference for realistic expectations about travel and whether it fixes your life.","q":{"x":"this SNL sketch about a tour operator that tries to set realistic expectations about what going to Italy can or can't do for your life","i":36096590,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36096590,"author":"qgin","month":"2023-05","story":"Experiences Won’t Make You Happier Than Possessions","stance":"reference","why":"SNL sketch on realistic expectations of what travel can fix","quote":"this SNL sketch about a tour operator that tries to set realistic expectations about what going to Italy can or can't do for your life","text":"Definitely important to define terms. Seems like in this case experiences = thrill-seeking or \"bought experiences\". For that type of thing, I'm reminded of this SNL sketch about a tour operator that tries to set realistic expectations about what going to Italy can or can't do for your life [VIDEO] > There's a lot a vacation can do. Help you unwind. See some different-looking squirrels. But it cannot fix deeper issues like how you behave in group settings or your general baseline mood. That is a job for incremental lifestyle changes sustained over time. We can give you the zipline. We cannot give you the ability to say \"wheeee\" and mean it."},{"id":20751472,"author":"_hardwaregeek","month":"2019-08","story":"Why we lie about being retired","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it's kinda like the recent SNL skit Romano Tours","text":"I'm young and nowhere close to retiring, but I already have friends who have bought into the whole retire early train. Except, it's kinda like the recent SNL skit Romano Tours^[1]. If you're sad now, you're gonna be sad then. Retiring isn't gonna fix their problems. They're not going to be suddenly happy. And even if everything goes right (no kids, no health problems, high income, low cost of living) and they retire \"early\", that's still going to be in their late 30's, early 40's. And then what? My friends claim they'll figure something out, but if they've lived 40 years without any sense of self actualization, I'm skeptical that they'd suddenly find themselves. I'm not saying one should work a meaningless job instead of retiring, but I don't get waiting for decades to find one's purpose in life. Might as well wait for Godot. And if retiring early doesn't work out, one can end up like the main character in Ikiru: 30 years of perfect attendance and absolutely nothing to show for it. But hey, maybe that's just my anxiety about starting out adult life. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43071174,"author":"titanomachy","month":"2025-02","story":"50 Years of Travel Tips","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this classic SNL skit","text":"My version of his advice would be \"if a stranger invites you to a party, don't turn them down.\" Also, your comment reminds me of this classic SNL skit: [VIDEO] \"If you're sad now, and you get on a plane to Italy, the 'you' in Italy will still be the same sad you from before! Does that make sense?\""},{"id":41609438,"author":"MichaelRo","month":"2024-09","story":"Working in the office 5 days/week to build company culture is a myth: PwC report","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the classic SNL sketch \"Romano Tours","text":"To the people complaining that they can't make friends or maintain meaningful relationships outside of physical in-office contact, I wanna point them out to the classic SNL sketch \"Romano Tours\" where Adam Sandler is a tourism operator selling trips to Italy: [VIDEO] Me, I have zero issues bonding with remote colleagues but then again, I have local friends too with whom I connect mostly through telecommunication tools (like WhatsApp) and occasionally meet in person. Quoting from the SNL sketch, and I can't stress this enough: \"If you are sad where you are and then you get on a plane to Italy, in Italy there will be the same sad you from before\"."},{"id":39138602,"author":"daswerth","month":"2024-01","story":"I'm a Digital Nomad. It's Not as Fun as It Looks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this all-time great SNL skit: Romano Tours","text":"Reminds me of this all-time great SNL skit: Romano Tours ( [VIDEO] ) \"We can take you on a hike... We cannot turn you into somebody who likes hiking.\""}]},"ZbFM3rn4ldo":{"wy":"HN cites it whenever someone worries that explanation or analysis spoils wonder, as a compact statement that knowledge deepens appreciation.","q":{"x":"being analytical about the world makes its beauty shine more, not less","i":33597415,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33597415,"author":"latexr","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Do you recall any book or course that made a topic finally click?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on analysis making nature's beauty shine more.","quote":"being analytical about the world makes its beauty shine more, not less","text":"> The seemingly random, yet beautiful grain, in wood. Or the way tree leaves rustle in the wind.. etc etc, you can see an immense beauty and goodness, not possible without a divine, all-giving power. That is quite the statement on the nature of reality, yet it’s entirely based on personal opinion. You don’t know from observation if the “beauty and goodness” of the world is impossible without a divine power, you choose to believe so. I also pay close attention to the natural world and my relationships, yet don’t feel the slightest inclination to believe in a higher power. If anything, I share Richard Feynman’s view that being analytical about the world makes its beauty shine more, not less: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30047801,"author":"Strilanc","month":"2022-01","story":"The clock does not measure time; it produces it","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman's view that understanding only adds to appreciation.","quote":"","text":"This is really not the article for me. It's written by someone who, I infer, agrees that Newton \"Destroyed the poetry of the rainbow by reducing it to a prism.\" [1]. And that is so alien to my experience of understanding things and defining things that I fail to relate to the text. I'm very much on the \"it only adds\" side of things [2][3]. That being said, consider a meeting that happens \"on the second Tuesday of each month\". The number of days between each meeting is so haphazard and ridiculous... if you rebooted human society, that definition would not survive unchanged. There's a clear example of the spandrels in our time keeping system reaching out and affecting the actions we take. 1: [LINK] (search for text \"prism\") 2: [LINK] 3: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20231907,"author":"cmsefton","month":"2019-06","story":"Where Has the Magic Gone?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how knowing how something works doesn't diminish its beauty.","quote":"It's not a loss at all, it's really just familiarity","text":"I'm reminded of Feynman's discussion about The Beauty of the Flower [VIDEO] Replace the word beauty with magic, and you get a similar point with regards to the \"loss\" of magic. It's not a loss at all, it's really just familiarity; \"knowing\" how it works doesn't make it any less magical (or beautiful). My suggestion to recapture that magic, is to delve further into the things you don't understand or know, and to ask further questions to unravel deeper layers, rather than continually having to use the knowledge you already have. It's worth recognizing that you can still appreciate that \"magic\", despite having looked behind the curtain to see how it works."},{"id":13903733,"author":"npgatech","month":"2017-03","story":"Appreciating Art with Algorithms","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how understanding adds to the beauty of a flower.","quote":"which does a better job of explaining my point","text":"Scott's TEDx presentation says otherwise[1] - he was pretty much against Chuck's allegations to the point where he did a tongue-in-cheek presentation. > It's still obviously a Chuck Close filter that acts in a way that completely erases/ignores the materiality, labour, and individual creativity that Close put into his paintings. I disagree. In no way does that ignore Chuck's materiality, labour and individual creativity. In fact, it sheds more light on the process (which is very procedural and art critics would argue it is very \"mechanical\"). There is a tremendous amount of creativity in figuring out, as the OP has done, the underlying algorithmic beauty of replicating Chuck's works. Your definition of creative endeavor (materiality, labour) is profoundly limiting and very \"physical\". I feel the opposite - OP's algorithm is way more interesting than Chuck's \"materiality\" and \"labour\". I will resort to Feynman's clip on 'Beauty of Flowers'[2], which does a better job of explaining my point. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"_QST1ZAJ29o":{"wy":"Cited in Amiga threads as the source for the Warhol live-painting moment and the launch event.","q":{"x":"Warhol paints Debbie on stage","i":41165102,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46754805,"author":"vaxman","month":"2026-01","story":"The year is 1993, The Amiga is everything for me","stance":"reference","why":"Amiga's 1985 launch event","quote":"","text":"I mean, some people show up to the blow out party three years after it ended, sure. That they're still talking about it 33 years later gives you an indication of what the main event must have been like [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":44825149,"author":"trembolram","month":"2025-08","story":"40 Years of the Amiga","stance":"reference","why":"Amiga launch event with famous demos like bouncing ball","quote":"The event also included other famous demos, such as the bouncing ball.","text":"The Mandrill was part of the launch event of Amiga in 1985 at the Lincoln Center. The event also included other famous demos, such as the bouncing ball. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41165102,"author":"virgulino","month":"2024-08","story":"Andy Warhol's lost Amiga art found","stance":"reference","why":"Warhol painting Debbie Harry at the 1985 Amiga launch","quote":"Warhol paints Debbie on stage","text":"\"The World Premiere of the Amiga (1985, Andy Warhol, Debbie Harry): Possibly wanting to one-up the Apple Macintosh launch in 1984, the Amiga 1000 debuted at a black-tie event held at the Vivian Beaumont Theater at Lincoln Center in New York City on July 23rd, 1985. (...)\" Warhol paints Debbie on stage: [VIDEO]"}]},"_o-HcG8QxPc":{"wy":"HN cites it as a prime example of FreeBSD tuned for extreme network performance at massive scale.","q":{"x":"being actively used to run the largest streaming service on the planet makes you pretty relevant.","i":30386167,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":41568176,"author":"voytec","month":"2024-09","story":"FreeBSD 13.4-Release","stance":"reference","why":"Netflix tuning FreeBSD for high-bandwidth networking","quote":"They hack FreeBSD[0] to achieve cool numbers over and over[1][2].","text":"Your comment suggests that FreeBSD is a thing of a past and no longer capable of \"quality networking\", due to poor WiFi support. I can only agree with the poor WiFi support part. But WiFi is by no means something you can judge networking stack dedicated for servers. FreeBSD was never designed for consumer hardware networking. There were a few \"desktop distros\" but all have more or less died, and the few FreeBSD-based \"storage distros\" moved to Linux after FreeBSD changed its upstream for ZFS. And I'm saying this as someone who made a lot of effort to use FreeBSD on laptop over the years, and a current user of 15.0-CURRENT on ThinkPad. But FreeBSD was and is THE choice for high-bandwidth wired networking. Netflix is both an early adopter and an active contributor to FreeBSD's networking codebase. They hack FreeBSD[0] to achieve cool numbers over and over[1][2]. Intel's device mentioned by you - AX200 - works \"fine\" on my ThinkPad, for a few months now. The device was on supported hardware list for more than a year prior, but the actual driver wasn't covering all vendor/device id pairs (different flavors of hardware or behavior all known as \"the same\" chip; this is a major problem with consumer devies since chip outages started). That said, by \"fine\" I mean that I can use the card and finally can connect to 5GHz networks and avoid disruptions related to 2.4GHz congestion. But FreeBSD is still incapable of utilizing speeds offered by modern WiFi specifications. Even with 5GHz co"},{"id":32523697,"author":"qwertox","month":"2022-08","story":"Serving Netflix Video Traffic at 800Gb/s and Beyond [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Video accompanying earlier slides discussion","quote":"This was the video which was posted back then alongside the slides","text":"Discussion of the same presentation 11 months ago, when the title was 400GB/s. [LINK] This was the video which was posted back then alongside the slides: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30386167,"author":"breakfastduck","month":"2022-02","story":"From macOS to FreeBSD","stance":"reference","why":"FreeBSD runs Netflix's streaming at scale","quote":"being actively used to run the largest streaming service on the planet makes you pretty relevant.","text":"I'd say being actively used to run the largest streaming service on the planet makes you pretty relevant. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28788517,"author":"drewg123","month":"2021-10","story":"NUMA Siloing in the FreeBSD Network Stack (2019) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker's follow-up talk on serving video at 400G","quote":"This is my EuroBSDcon talk from 2 years ago.","text":"This is my EuroBSDcon talk from 2 years ago. The more recent one (about getting to 400G) is up now at [VIDEO]"}]},"fjsSr3z5nVk":{"wy":"HN mentions it only as a joke or shorthand for the urge to destroy a frustrating printer or device.","m":[{"id":29643914,"author":"hnlmorg","month":"2021-12","story":"Hidden Networks in TP-Link Routers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"went full on Office Space[1] on it","text":"I'm not at all surprised by this article because I recently purchased a new TP-Link router (Archer something IIRC) and it's quite honestly the worst router I've ever run. I goes down several times a day, dropping all network traffic that isn't TCP. Thus DHCP, UDP DNS and ICMP echo all fail. My old router is > 5 years old Asus (possibly as much as 10 years old!!) and literally held together with electricians tape. I've had an array of physical failures due to age from the antenna no longer standing up to the power button no longer functioning and I've used electricians tape to fix all of them. But for the stuff that matters it still works extraordinary well. So I figured it was only a matter of time before that router died completely and I'd need a new one. And I figured it has been so many years since I last upgraded that my new device should be amazing in comparison. But man was I wrong. After getting kicked out of an important Zoom call last week (and weeks of issues and debugging too) I finally lost my temper, unhooked the router and went full on Office Space[1] on it. I'm now back to running the old router. A router that's older than both of my kids. A router that had earned its retirement. I'm going to replace it with a Draytek device in the new year. Given I'm mostly working from home these days I want something reliable. But I'm also thinking Draytek will last me another ~10 years so it will work out cheaper than the TP-Link crap. In fact I'll never buy another TP-Link"},{"id":23760328,"author":"Pick-A-Hill2019","month":"2020-07","story":"You need 15 Pieces of Flair (1999)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the infamous printer smashing scene","text":"When I first saw the title it reminded me of the film Office Space and turns out that it is indeed from the 1999 film featuring Jennifer Aniston and of course the infamous printer smashing scene ( [VIDEO] , clean SFW version) and Family Guy's take on it [LINK]"},{"id":10348685,"author":"brento","month":"2015-10","story":"Dealing with Noise in an Open Office Setting","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I often fantasized about taking a baseball bat to it, like they did to a printer on Office Space","text":"I once worked as a developer at a newspaper that had an open floor plan. The biggest issue was the darn emergency scanner radio squawking in the background all day! I often fantasized about taking a baseball bat to it, like they did to a printer on Office Space ( [VIDEO] ). It's one of the reasons I no longer work for a newspaper."}]},"hgOzYZDrXL0":{"wy":"It is the reference for Mu, Standard Chartered's strict Haskell, and for the practical case that strictness makes debugging easier.","q":{"x":"So strictness is really fantastic, I must say. Stack traces work.","i":16345695,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":35812641,"author":"foldr","month":"2023-05","story":"Haskell in Production: Standard Chartered","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> their own dialect of Haskell called Mu. [emphasis mine] >Mu has a strict runtime, which makes program performance easier to analyse and predict – but prevents us from just copy-pasting some Haskell libraries that rely crucially on lazy evaluation. [emphasis mine] If Mu were an entirely different language then you wouldn't be able to copy-paste any Haskell libraries. See also e.g. here: [LINK] , [VIDEO]"},{"id":16345695,"author":"chillee","month":"2018-02","story":"Exploring ReasonML and functional programming","stance":"reference","why":"Augustsson on strictness versus laziness in Mu","quote":"So strictness is really fantastic, I must say. Stack traces work.","text":"I mean, if you want a direct quote from Lennart Augustsson, here's a quick transcription of the relevant parts of the relevant video. [VIDEO] > So strictness is really fantastic, I must say. Stack traces work. When something goes wrong, you can say \"so you call things from there to there to there\". It's the single most time saving thing from Mu compared to Haskell, because things do go wrong from now and then, and it's so great to know exactly what went wrong... And strict languages have what I call resource composition. So if you understand the complexity of 2 parts you can stick them together and nothing weird happens. They add up. That's not true in lazy languages. And debugging, it's sensible in strict languages. And exceptions, they actually work! Strictness is great. It's terrible, also. So it has nice resource composition but it doesn't compose nicely semantically. So laziness has some good things as well. I think laziness is a much nicer way of programming except for these pesky resources. You definitely need some lazy functions, even C has lazy functions. And then he goes on to talk about the ways that they allow laziness in a strict language, and when they need laziness. Screenshots of relevant slides. Considering that the report was pretty much a summary of the talks given at CUFP, I don't know why you would cast doubt on its legitimacy. [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":12901514,"author":"wz1000","month":"2016-11","story":"A Look at How Traders and Economists Are Using the Julia Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"Standard Chartered's Haskell Excel interop story","quote":"","text":"IIRC, Standard Chartered achieved this by initially adding Haskell interop to Excel[0] and then moving to a custom GUI solution to replace Excel altogether. [0]- [VIDEO]"},{"id":10265394,"author":"gmfawcett","month":"2015-09","story":"Analyzing Three Space Leaks in Haskell","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote covering Mu, Standard Chartered's strict Haskell dialect","quote":"there is a strict dialect of Haskell called Mu, owned by Standard Chartered","text":"While it's not publicly available, there is a strict dialect of Haskell called Mu, owned by Standard Chartered. See [VIDEO] . It would be nice if the rest of us could play with it, but so it goes."}]},"igh9iO5BxBo":{"wy":"HN shares it as a joke in threads about English profanity and non-native speakers.","m":[{"id":47844829,"author":"em-bee","month":"2026-04","story":"NASA had to train Apollo 11's astronauts to not use profanity","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"i am not offended by swearwords, i am annoyed by them. it makes absolutely no sense to me to use a term that suggests sexual intercourse to describe anything else but that act. likewise it makes no sense that someone is told to have said sexual intercourse as a way of suffering. not only because there should be no connection, but also because the whole idea that sexual intercourse is something you should suffer from is completely alien to me and also demonstrates a very skewed view on what it is supposed to be about. it proliferates the idea that sexual intercourse is something bad. i understand that the use of that word in that context should be seen as completely disconnected, but that only works if the original meaning is no longer in use. like the original meaning of the word gay. using gay as a swearword annoys me just as much. and it hurts the people who identify themselves as such. if you want to use swearwords, use those that are objectively bad in all variants of their meaning. like shit. but here we have the reverse problem. shit is good: [VIDEO] so maybe we can find new terms that we all can agree on are bad, like SUV-driver ;-)"},{"id":27230308,"author":"irjustin","month":"2021-05","story":"Native speakers are hard to understand in a lingua franca situation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Ismo, the comedian sums it up in great style","text":"Ismo, the comedian sums it up in great style[0] [1]. \"I've got shit to do\" means anything but actually going to the bathroom. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":21146117,"author":"josefresco","month":"2019-10","story":"Is “Ass” the Most Complicated Word in English?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"This was my favorite.","text":"I'll just leave this here: [VIDEO] He also does a \"clean\" version for television, and I think he's done a version for \"ass\" as well. This was my favorite."}]},"j0z4FweCy4M":{"wy":"Treated as a primary source on how Tesla builds its self-driving stack, cited by timestamp for transformers, synthetic data, Dojo and the humanoid bot.","q":{"x":"Here's Tesla's AI day presentation on how they are going to use AI in their planning system","i":29146300,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":42885723,"author":"gzer0","month":"2025-01","story":"TopoNets: High performing vision and language models with brain-like topography","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla's architecture talk linked as source.","quote":"Link to the 2021 Tesla AI day talk","text":"I spent time working with Andrej and the rest of the FSD team back in 2020/2021, and we had plenty of conversations on how human visual processing maps onto our neural network architectures. Our approach—transformer-based attention blocks, multi-scale feature extraction, and temporal fusion—mirrors elements of the biological visual cortex (retina → LGN → V1 → V2 → V4 → IT) which break down raw inputs and integrate them over time. It’s amazing how closely this synthetic perceptual pipeline parallels the way our own brains interpret the world. The key insight we discovered was that explicitly enforcing brain-like topographic organization (as some academic work attempts - such as this one here) isn't necessary - what matters is having the right functional components that parallel biological visual processing. Our experience showed that the key elements of biological visual processing - like hierarchical feature extraction and temporal integration - emerge naturally when you build architectures that have to solve real visual tasks. The brain's organization serves its function, not the other way around. This was validated by the real-world performance of our synthetic visual cortex in the Tesla FSD stack. Link to the 2021 Tesla AI day talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35615772,"author":"yreg","month":"2023-04","story":"Synthetic Data from Diffusion Models Improves ImageNet Classification","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Tesla's use of synthetic data for training.","quote":"","text":"I believe Tesla uses synthetic data for FSD/Autopilot training as well. Not sure what's the ratio between synthetic data and real videos, but it seems that they plan to rely more onto the first group in the future. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34883448,"author":"petilon","month":"2023-02","story":"Software 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Musk quote discouraging machine learning use.","quote":"","text":"Machine Learning and Neural Networks are not applicable for solving all kinds of problems. In fact, in Tesla AI Day Elon Musk remarked that \"I discourage the use of machine learning... unless you have to use machine learning, don't do it\" [1]. Getting machine learning to work right is hard. If it can be avoided it should be. So to cast this as Software 1.0 vs 2.0 doesn't make sense. There is a class of problems where neural networks work better. Everywhere else we will continue to use traditional code. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33400580,"author":"113245","month":"2022-10","story":"Why Tesla removed radar and ultrasonic sensors [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Details on Tesla's task-specific neural networks.","quote":"go into some detail about this. They use multiple networks that are dedicated to specific tasks.","text":"The Tesla AI day videos [1] go into some detail about this. They use multiple networks that are dedicated to specific tasks. [1] [VIDEO] (2021), [LINK] (2022)"},{"id":30644506,"author":"yazaddaruvala","month":"2022-03","story":"U.S. eliminates human controls requirement for fully automated vehicles","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for synthetic training data generation.","quote":"","text":"[LINK] This is a rather intuitive mathematical proof that neural nets can emulate all functions in many dimensions. Knowing integrals (Calc 102) would help. With this information it’s either that self-driving is eventually possible or human brains are not mathematically representable (i.e. something akin to a soul). Meanwhile, the data collection/storage is getting better (all Teslas are continually testing FSD and reporting failure cases, synthetic generation of “good and unique” perfectly labeled data[0] is only a year or two “old”). Sensors/cameras are improving (and have a lot more room to improve). The compute hardware is getting better and cheaper (analog computers[1], TSMC 2nm and beyond, Apple/Google/Teslas neural cores, etc). Additionally, given Waymo is already in production without humans in PHX it’s arguable that we already have “autonomous vehicles”. We now just need them to improve slightly and for Waymo or similar to test in the top 25 cities in the US. Which is mostly a matter of capital and time not technology limits. Maybe weather will be an issue for a few more years/decades - but even if Waymo only operated in Summer, I’d be happy to reduce my Lyft costs - and with enough data and maybe better sensors the neural nets will be able to handle weather too. Especially if the car just goes slow. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":30063781,"author":"joakleaf","month":"2022-01","story":"Meta Unveils New AI Supercomputer","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla's presentation cited as less convincing comparison to Cruise.","quote":"","text":"Cruise actually consider both social dynamics and uncertainty (i.e. what can hide behind an obstacle, or where are pedestrians/bikes/cars likely to move to). If you are interested in self-driving cars, I can highly recommend their presentation from November 2021: [LINK] For me it felt more convincing than Tesla's (a few months prior); [VIDEO]"},{"id":29564811,"author":"lazysheepherd","month":"2021-12","story":"I Can’t See You but I’m Not Blind","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla FSD predictions shown as analogy for unstable mental imagery.","quote":"old and new Tesla FSD predictions which are sampled here","text":"> The \"images\" are part information. This. It is a mashup of both. I can clearly visualize many 3D objects in my mind, as well as zoom, pan, scale, rotate, recolor, animate, distort them, etc. But it is not purely visual, because I often \"see\" opposite side of a cube momentarily, which I should not be able to from this perspective. So, it is not a rendering engine. It is not physically, spatially and temporally stable. Stability is lost especially as your focus shifts (e.g. \"seeing\" behind objects as your attention slips there) The best way I know to describe this is by old and new Tesla FSD predictions which are sampled here: [VIDEO] Improved version is how people talk about visualization, but the old unstable version is how (at least for me) it mostly is."},{"id":29142181,"author":"JoshTko","month":"2021-11","story":"Zillow just gave us a look at machine learning's future","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"ML only beats humans in very, very narrow scenarios.","text":"This is a poor use case for ML. You'd only have a couple hundred examples for a neighborhood with maybe two dozen variables. The model will also miss key variables that that there aren't datasets for but would be apparent upon a visit. ML only beats humans in very, very narrow scenarios. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29146300,"author":"lostdog","month":"2021-11","story":"GPT-3 is no longer the only game in town","stance":"reference","why":"How Tesla uses AI in planning because traditional methods do not scale","quote":"Here's Tesla's AI day presentation on how they are going to use AI in their planning system","text":"Here's Tesla's AI day presentation on how they are going to use AI in their planning system because traditional methods aren't scalable enough: [VIDEO] (1:20:15). You can see hints of this in presentations from the other self-driving companies. Boston Dynamics was definitely an exception for a long time. Certainly the perception systems that they are adding use ML, but you are right that they use expressly use classical methods for their control system."},{"id":28971582,"author":"jhgg","month":"2021-10","story":"Dutch forensic lab says it has decoded Tesla's driving data","stance":"reference","why":"Musk opening saying Tesla has deep AI activity","quote":"Tesla is much more than an automotive company, and we have lots of deep AI activity","text":"I mean in the same event that you list, Elon literally opens with: \"Tesla is much more than an automotive company, and we have lots of deep AI activity...\" I think it is fair to say that a company that is literally inventing its own AI training supercomputer is probably an AI company as well :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":28245861,"author":"bko","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla AI Day Live Stream [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Tesla humanoid bot announcement at 2:05:11","quote":"The newly announced Tesla humanoid bot is around 2:05:11.","text":"The newly announced Tesla humanoid bot is around 2:05:11. Expected prototype in 2022. The demo was an obvious troll but it looks pretty exciting. I'd rather have resources put into actual innovation than learning how to increase the likelihood someone will click a button by 0.01% [VIDEO]"},{"id":28282950,"author":"gpm","month":"2021-08","story":"John Carmack visits Starbase","stance":"reference","why":"Musk describing controlling the humanoid robot by speaking","quote":"when describing how it would be optimal to be able to control Tesla's humanoid robot","text":"Elon's been giving some lip service to closer-to-GI capabilities anyways, e.g. when describing how it would be optimal to be able to control Tesla's humanoid robot by saying \"please go to the store and get me the following groceries\"... [VIDEO] Whether Tesla wants to fund serious work towards that may be a different question (but they do seem to be serious about at least building the robot)."},{"id":28303785,"author":"dexter89_kp3","month":"2021-08","story":"Do vision transformers see like convolutional neural networks?","stance":"reference","why":"Karpathy on using transformers to map image space to vector space","quote":"Karpathy spoke about it in on Tesla AI day.","text":"They do. Karpathy spoke about it in on Tesla AI day. They use it for transforming image space to a vector space. See: [VIDEO] (timestamp 54.40 onwards)"},{"id":28303865,"author":"gamegoblin","month":"2021-08","story":"Do vision transformers see like convolutional neural networks?","stance":"reference","why":"Transformers used higher in stack, regnets on raw camera input","quote":"Transformers mentioned on the slide at timestamp 1:00:18.","text":"In Karpathy’s recent AI day presentation he specifically stated they use transformers. But not on the raw camera input — they use regnets for that. The transformers come higher up the stack: [VIDEO] Transformers mentioned on the slide at timestamp 1:00:18."},{"id":28303872,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2021-08","story":"Do vision transformers see like convolutional neural networks?","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp where Tesla discusses transformers","quote":"Fascinating. I guess transformers are efficient.","text":"Can you provide a more specific timestamp? 54.40 doesn't seem to mention anything about transformers, and \"onwards\" is two hours. I'd be really surprised if they use transformers due to how computationally expensive they are for anything involving vision. EDIT: Found it. 1h: [VIDEO] Fascinating. I guess transformers are efficient."},{"id":28304463,"author":"tandem5000","month":"2021-08","story":"Do vision transformers see like convolutional neural networks?","stance":"reference","why":"Bird's-eye-view prediction with attention, and unstable lane predictions","quote":"their predictions seem surprisingly temporally unstable","text":"They use the key-value lookup/routing mechanism from Transformers to predict pixel-wise labels in bird view (lane, car, obstacle, intersection etc.). The motivation here is that some of the predictions may temporarily be occluded, so for predicting these occluded areas it may be particularly helpful to attend to remote regions in the input images which requires long-range dependencies that highly depend on the input itself (e.g. on whether there is an occlusion), which is exactly where the key-value mechanism excels. Not sure they even process past camera frames at this point. They only mention that later in the pipline they have an LSTM-like NN incorporating past camera frames (Schmidhuber will be proud!!). Edit: A random observation which just occurred to me is that their predictions seem surprisingly temporally unstable. Observe, for example, the lane layout wildly changing while it drives makes a left-turn at the intersection ( [VIDEO] ). You can use the comma and period keys to step through the video frame-by-frame."},{"id":28339476,"author":"Dunedan","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla Packs 50B Transistors onto D1 Dojo Chip","stance":"reference","why":"Musk discouraging unnecessary machine learning use in Q&A","quote":"Unless you have to use machine learning, don't do it.","text":"> […] AI has a massive value to every aspect of the company's work. That's also just wrong. During the recent \"Tesla AI Day\", when asked during Q/A, Elon Musk specifically mentioned that they intentionally use machine learning only for very few cases: Q: \"Is Tesla using machine learning within its manufacturing, design or any other engineering processes?\" Elon: \"I discourage use of machine learning, because it's really difficult. Unless you have to use machine learning, don't do it. It's usually a red flag when somebody is saying 'We wanna use machine learning to solve this task'. I'm like: That sounds like bullshit. 99.9% of the time you don't need it.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":28370353,"author":"thesausageking","month":"2021-08","story":"The Tesla Dojo Chip Is Impressive, but There Are Some Major Technical Issues","stance":"reference","why":"Q&A on compiler and software challenges for Dojo hardware","quote":"The Q&A section on their compiler and software that the author links to is very interesting","text":"The Q&A section on their compiler and software that the author links to is very interesting: [VIDEO] It sounds like they're going to have write a ton of custom software in order to use this hardware at scale. And, based on the team being speechless when asked a follow up question, it doesn't sound like they know (yet) how they're going to solve this. Nvidia gets a lot of credit for their hardware advances, but what really what their chips work so well for deep learning was the huge software stack they created around CUDA. Underestimating the software investment required has plagued a lot of AI chip startups. It doesn't sound like Tesla is immune to this."},{"id":32089617,"author":"espadrine","month":"2022-07","story":"Andrej Karpathy leaves Tesla","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they only had a simple architecture for a very long time","text":"Waymo has superior performance based on their historical statistics. It makes sense, since their lidar sensors capture more of the environment, and directly in 3D. Their AI also seems better QA’ed. The Tesla AI Day[0] surprised me as it showed they only had a simple architecture for a very long time, simply feeding barely processed camera pixels to a DNN and hoping for the best with little more than supervised learning off human feeds. Their big claim to glory was that they rearchitectured it to produce a 2D map of the environment… which I thought they had years ago, and is still a far cry from the 3D modeling that is needed. After all, sure, we humans only take two video feeds as input… But we can appreciate from it the position, intent, and trajectory of a wealth of elements around us, with reasonable probability estimates for multiple possibilities, sometimes pertaining to things that are invisible, such as kids crossing from nowhere when near a school. Cruise also seems to have better tech; they had a barely-watched 2h30 description of their systems[1] which shows they do create a richer environment, evaluate the routing of many objects, and train their systems on a very realistic simulation, not just supervised training, which means it can learn from very low-probability events. They have a whole segment on including the probability that unseen cars may travel from perpendicular roads; Tesla’s creeping hit-or-miss are well-documented on Youtube. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":28241912,"author":"ipsum2","month":"2021-08","story":"Tesla AI Day [video]","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"is illegally changing lanes in an intersection","text":"I don't know if anyone else caught this, but their \"optimal trajectory\" planned path is illegally changing lanes in an intersection, at -1:26:00: [VIDEO] . Pretty funny."}]},"nFZGpES-St8":{"wy":"Brought up whenever medical device security or open-source firmware in implants is discussed.","q":{"x":"on her experience trying to navigate the issue of getting a pacemaker-defibrilator, including fairly serious security concerns","i":18440992,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18440992,"author":"microcolonel","month":"2018-11","story":"FDA isn't doing enough to prevent medical device hacking, HHS report says","stance":"reference","why":"Sandler's experience getting a pacemaker-defibrillator and its security concerns.","quote":"on her experience trying to navigate the issue of getting a pacemaker-defibrilator, including fairly serious security concerns","text":"Karen Sandler, then (2011) Executive Director of the GNOME Foundation on her experience trying to navigate the issue of getting a pacemaker-defibrilator, including fairly serious security concerns (device functioning can be affected by easily spoofed, unauthenticated radio communications): [VIDEO]"},{"id":5997552,"author":"microcolonel","month":"2013-07","story":"A New Cyber Concern: Hack Attacks on Medical Devices","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the medical device security concern is old, not new.","quote":"not a new \"cyber\" concern, a very old concern","text":"[VIDEO] << not a new \"cyber\" concern, a very old concern, with a new scientific american article."},{"id":3959655,"author":"mikegerwitz","month":"2012-05","story":"Is an open-source pacemaker safer than closed-source?","stance":"reference","why":"Sandler's OSCON 2011 talk on closed-source pacemaker software concerns.","quote":"Karen Sandler of the GNOME Foundation discussed an issue similar to this nearly a year ago at OSCON (2011)","text":"Karen Sandler of the GNOME Foundation discussed an issue similar to this nearly a year ago at OSCON (2011): [VIDEO] Indeed, the first thing that came to mind when I saw this presentation was RMS' comment, as mentioned in this article."},{"id":3607725,"author":"notm","month":"2012-02","story":"Wireless drug-delivery microchips, more consistent than injections","stance":"reference","why":"Karen Sandler on risks of proprietary software in implanted medical devices.","quote":"Relevant","text":"I feel uneasy with body implanted microchips running proprietary software that can be accessed wirelessly. Am I the only one? Sure no, ask Karen Sandler. Relevant: [VIDEO]"}]},"rERApU26PcA":{"wy":"Used as a joke or a tongue-in-cheek reference in security and authentication threads.","m":[{"id":36718740,"author":"NoZebra120vClip","month":"2023-07","story":"Hype Will Turn Your Security Key into Junk","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I will not be silent until authentication works like this","text":"I will not be silent until authentication works like this: [LINK] Or until it works like this: [VIDEO] And then logging in will look exactly like this: [LINK]"},{"id":30366418,"author":"outworlder","month":"2022-02","story":"Some neurons are active when adding, others when subtracting","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That might explain why Data's lockout code in that particular TNG episode has so many \"7\" on it :)","text":"That might explain why Data's lockout code in that particular TNG episode has so many \"7\" on it :) One, seven, three, four, six, seven, three, two, one, four, seven, six, Charlie, three, two, seven, eight, nine, seven, seven, seven, six, four, three, Tango, seven, three, two, Victor, seven, three, one, one, seven, eight, eight, eight, seven, three, two, four, seven, six, seven, eight, nine, seven, six, four, three, seven, six. Lock. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28091760,"author":"France_is_bacon","month":"2021-08","story":"Mozilla: *Privacy Not Included","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"From my understanding, the only real way to create a password is to make it really, really long. Like, \"Sometimes 10 people will get together on Friday night. Wow!\" Or whatever you want. Makes it easy to remember. Personally I just usually string a bunch of random numbers and upper/lower/special characters - 40 of them. Just like this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34914538,"author":"EGreg","month":"2023-02","story":"I Broke into a Bank Account with an AI-Generated Voice","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is bad and insecure. Look at this","text":"This is bad and insecure. Look at this: [VIDEO] Same thing with “pay with your face” they tried in China. They all are susceptible to replay attacks. It is garbage! The only secure way to authorize actions is to have a device in your possession which you unlock with your password and/or biometrics — and use THAT device to sign interactive challenges or ZK-SNARKS. The device could stores keys in a secure enclave, but regardless, the keys should never leave the device. The ability to export private keys (as with ethereum and bitcoin wallets) is insecure, too. Look how 1password does it: local key plus master password. Far better than LastPass (master password only). Also you should have notifications whenever a new device authenticates, or when you are trying to establish new recurring payments at a great rate than you would allow otherwise. Then you need to confirm from other devices. That is the scheme that works universally and the further you depart from that, the more ridiculous hacks you get."}]},"sOnqjkJTMaA":{"wy":"HN mentions it only in passing, as an example of high-quality 4K video, a musical reference, or part of Quincy Jones's legacy.","m":[{"id":42051948,"author":"seanhunter","month":"2024-11","story":"Quincy Jones has died","stance":"reference","why":"Thriller as one of Quincy Jones's productions","quote":"thriller-the best selling album of all time.","text":"He's the guy who produced this [LINK] \"Yah mo Be there\" here because it's one of my favourites and this [VIDEO] (thriller-the best selling album of all time. Billie Jean etc. ALl Quincy. But he also did this [LINK] \"Come fly with me\" from \"Sinatra at the Sands\". Yes that arrangement was Quincy. and this [LINK] \"It's my party and I'll cry if I want to\" Wait wut? Yes. Quincy. and this [LINK] Dinah Washington. \"Mad about the boy\". Yup. Quincy and \"We are the world\", and The brothers johnson and some stuff with Miles Davis and Sarah Vaughn and many many others. It's absolutely absurd that one person did that range of things."},{"id":37215187,"author":"jxramos","month":"2023-08","story":"The Mystery of an Abandoned Cabinet Full of Kodachrome Slides in San Francisco","stance":"reference","why":"Thriller in 4K as example of high-quality video","quote":"We do have thriller in 4k","text":"yah, I'm speaking of mostly TV productions, music videos, and that level of video material. We do have thriller in 4k, other productions too [VIDEO] . Now those are interesting examples of digital cameras you provide above; definitely worth sharing and bringing to the table. Now that I think of it, I think video recordings existed in the 70s too? Like how exactly were TV shows broadcast anyways now that I think about it. The Abba remastered stuff is where my association got set [LINK] , that stuff is incredible."},{"id":16475528,"author":"itp","month":"2018-02","story":"Donald Glover Can't Save You","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Michael Jackson demonstrating a 2 and 4 clap","quote":"MJ even demonstrates a 2&4 clap during the song.","text":"Fascinating to me that you picked Thriller as an example, as MJ even demonstrates a 2&4 clap during the song. [VIDEO] Curious what you mean by half the claps? Would this involve clapping only on every fourth beat, only on every second beat, or am I completely misunderstanding?"}]},"uASUHbFEhWY":{"wy":"Posted as a joke in threads about AR headsets, wearables and Apple's future products.","m":[{"id":36658910,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2023-07","story":"Apple Vision Pro Headset to Launch in US Stores by Appointment","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory Futurama","text":"> “Do you have an appointment?” No. “Oh, it’ll be about 45 minutes before someone can help you.” Ah, yes, the Ferrari customer experience. You need to be selected by the manufacturer to be allowed to buy their product. It makes the product feel rare and exclusive, and the customer feel \"special\", when it's a consumer product made by the same Chinese sweatshop workers that make your other e-waste. Obligatory Futurama \"there might be one left\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":30676863,"author":"sandworm101","month":"2022-03","story":"Medical student surgically implants Bluetooth into own ear to cheat in final","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Futurama did it.","text":"Futurama did it. Implanted vision devices have been a few years away, for decades. The nerve-wire interface isn't a simple problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29354961,"author":"wodenokoto","month":"2021-11","story":"Apple leaker Kuo: Apple plans to replace the iPhone with AR in 10 years","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think the eyePhone from Futurama clearly beats a handheld phone","text":"I think the eyePhone from Futurama clearly beats a handheld phone, but it might be a bit more than 10 years away. [VIDEO] Despite being a more than 10 year old iPhone parody it is pretty spot on even today. Very little has happened in smartphone land. (this is also the origin of the \"shut up and take my money\"-meme)"}]},"wPrUmViN_5c":{"wy":"Used as a running joke whenever RISC, RISC-V or processor architecture comes up.","m":[{"id":19120028,"author":"Zenst","month":"2019-02","story":"Building a RISC-V PC","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it stems from this scene","text":"Generally true, though that quote since then has been used so many times, I suspect that even google has lost count. For those in the dark still, it stems from this scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16079901,"author":"busterarm","month":"2018-01","story":"“Intel Core 2 bugs will assuredly be exploitable from userland code” (2007)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was more trying to point to RISC in general (\"RISC is good\" [VIDEO] ). What I really wanted to say is that we've tried but RISC hasn't really been successful in the (mass consumer) market, which because of ARM is only partially true. In truth you're correct, and ARM is sort of an unruly mess at this point, but that's a much larger discussion."},{"id":13333734,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2017-01","story":"Hands on with the First Open Source Microcontroller","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here's what you're missing: [VIDEO] Followed up with the best attempt at a pickup line by a hacker in the movies. Back before the movies had them making out with random women in bathrooms over job offers involving sex, alcohol, bombs, and Houdini tributes. Hackers got really cool really fast. ;)"}]},"w_rKA6ROAVk":{"wy":"Cited critically in policing threads as evidence of what kind of recruits and culture police departments seek.","m":[{"id":29400115,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2021-12","story":"Texas woman wins round in lawsuit challenging swat teams destruction of her home","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They were actively recruiting for the type of person who would enjoy being on a SWAT team like this.","text":"Here's a Newport Beach (California) police recruiting video: [VIDEO] They were actively recruiting for the type of person who would enjoy being on a SWAT team like this. [LINK]"},{"id":26085269,"author":"Stratoscope","month":"2021-02","story":"Police playing music while being filmed, seemingly to trigger copyright filters","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They illustrate beautifully the two mindsets you describe.","text":"> The warrior mindset is at the root of so many of these problems. We need to disband all of the police forces and start again with a new culture based around a guardian mindset and community service. Nearly ten years ago, Reason posted a comparison of police recruitment videos from Decatur, Georgia, and Newport Beach, California. They illustrate beautifully the two mindsets you describe. [LINK] The videos don't play on that page any more, so here are direct links. Decatur: [LINK] Newport Beach: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23431642,"author":"thaumasiotes","month":"2020-06","story":"Slack Removed a Blog Post Showing How Police Use Its Tech","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"What the police are supposed to be is determined by local culture.","text":"What the police are supposed to be is determined by local culture. Here's a police recruitment ad from Georgia: [LINK] Here's one from California: [VIDEO] (The original blog post that highlighted the difference between these is down, but here's an archive link: [LINK] . I pulled the youtube URLs because the archived page doesn't appear to work.)"},{"id":23373814,"author":"PaulDavisThe1st","month":"2020-05","story":"Police act like laws don't apply to them because of Qualified Immunity","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here's a police recruitment ad (admittedly from more than a decade ago) [VIDEO] (Newport Beach Police Department recruiting video, from 2008) And the problem is just the salary and QI? I don't think so."}]},"xnhJWusyj4I":{"wy":"Cited critically as an example of post-truth politics and appeals to feeling over data.","m":[{"id":40403771,"author":"zer00eyz","month":"2024-05","story":"Sam and Greg's response to OpenAI Safety researcher claims","stance":"reference","why":"Other side speaking overtly","quote":"here is the other side talking rather overtly","text":"I didn't say DEI for a reason, here is the other side talking rather overtly: [VIDEO] ... Pick a topic and someone is gonna have a really bent take on how things would be... and keen on jamming that take down someone else's throat so they can feel better. Everyone has shitty takes that they want others to buy into... everyone. Is Taiwan a country? I would say yes, most Americans would (I hope). Ask ChatGPT... 4chan is still a thing, the library is still a thing. The nuclear boy scouts made a giant mess before there was an internet. As for safety, well, it's gonna be a long time before shutting down the world isnt a weekend project for a hand full of people if they are allowed."},{"id":19801818,"author":"ranie93","month":"2019-05","story":"Amazon Ring is hiring editors to push local crime news to its users","stance":"reference","why":"Gingrich on perception of violence versus trends","quote":"","text":"Here's a relevant opinion from Newt Gingrich on the matter of perception of violence vs. actual trends [VIDEO]"},{"id":48301215,"author":"peteforde","month":"2026-05","story":"I think Anthropic and OpenAI have found product-market fit","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"terrifying in hind-sight","text":"I'm reminded of that [terrifying in hind-sight] Newt Gingrich interview in which he was more concerned about his constituents feelings about things getting worse than any silly statistics provided by government agencies. [VIDEO]"},{"id":44540715,"author":"zer00eyz","month":"2025-07","story":"Bad Actors Are Grooming LLMs to Produce Falsehoods","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is my favorite example of it, where one literally tells you what the play is while it's getting made","text":"> Now, why are you spending misinformation? Are you going to claim that US politicians don't do the exact same thing? This is my favorite example of it, where one literally tells you what the play is while it's getting made: [VIDEO] Feelings not facts."},{"id":44068271,"author":"chneu","month":"2025-05","story":"Trump administration halts Harvard's ability to enroll international students","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he openly admitted to manipulating people to get them to feel certain ways.","text":"Newt Gingrich dropped the famous \"feels over reals\" line on CNN. \"You have your facts on the left and we have our facts on the right\" is basically what he said. Along with \"If people feel one way then that's real.\" He isn't wrong, but he openly admitted to manipulating people to get them to feel certain ways. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19978477,"author":"satellitec4t","month":"2019-05","story":"A Solution for Loneliness: Get out and volunteer, research suggests","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The post-truth era in the West is clearly a product of the religious right","text":"> Another is a worldview that values selflessness, altruism, and honesty. I don't think those things can exist apart from religion Religion doesn't have a monopoly on those ideals. They are not even solely human ideals. Many mammals display them in certain scenarios. > (at a cultural level, anyway). Many of the least religious countries are the best places to live, and the most religious, the worst. I went to Norway (the least religious country in western Europe), and they often don't check tickets on the train or at the entrances of places. When you ask someone why not, because people could lie, you get an answer like.. \"because one wouldn't do that\" (ie cheat the system). I got so used to it that I would lose my train tickets in my bag because I didn't need them. The only place I needed them was getting off the train at the airport because there was a machine that checked it. > because as soon as you decide morality is relative then everyone is free to choose the morality that is most convenient for them/their tribe. Religions do this. There are many sects with different rules. People pick and choose what rules to obey all the time. Not being religious doesn't make one a moral relativist. Ethics and morality can be reasoned about. > I think this explains much of our political divide and the \"post-Truth\" era generally The post-truth era in the West is clearly a product of the religious right: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17709459,"author":"glitcher","month":"2018-08","story":"Study Finds Increased Incarceration Has Marginal-To-Zero Impact on Crime","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here's Newt Gingrich's classic \"debate\" between FBI crime statistics and his own \"feelings\".","text":"Too many politicians and voters are in the habit of ignoring facts and instead going with their gut feelings, even when the facts directly contradict their \"feelings\". And unfortunately fear mongering for votes can be very effective. Here's Newt Gingrich's classic \"debate\" between FBI crime statistics and his own \"feelings\". Notice particularly the language he uses to frame actual facts that don't agree with his ideology: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12218740,"author":"cmurf","month":"2016-08","story":"Can mythbusters like Snopes.com keep up in a post-truth era?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Voters want politicians to side with them on the basis of their feelings.","text":"According to Newt, voters don't want the truth in politics, especially because it's awkward. Voters want politicians to side with them on the basis of their feelings. [VIDEO]"}]},"yDp3cB5fHXQ":{"wy":"Used as a reference example of blatant plagiarism, including in debates about AI training and academic plagiarism.","m":[{"id":47249776,"author":"amiga386","month":"2026-03","story":"\"It Turns Out\"","stance":"reference","why":"Long video source of a quote about writing reviews being valuable.","quote":"For those that don't have 4 hours to spare","text":"For those that don't have 4 hours to spare: [VIDEO] > But it turns out writing a good review is really difficult. For example, I use the phrase \"it turns out\" more than once every video by accident because I'm bad at it. I'm not even joking. I've written \"it turns out\" in the next section without realizing it. That's how fuckin' bad I am. > Being able to write a good review is a unique and difficult skill. Creative people often have trouble recognizing their skills as skills because eventually they feel like second nature, and they don't feel real and practical like building a house or domming. But it turns ...in... that this stuff actually is valuable. If it wasn't, people wouldn't be stealing it."},{"id":38829219,"author":"gruez","month":"2024-01","story":"I Vote on Plagiarism Cases at Harvard College. Gay's Getting Off Easy","stance":"reference","why":"Example of blatant plagiarism going unaddressed for years.","quote":"","text":">Presuming the allegations are true, I find it interesting that it went unaddressed for so long. The matter was seemingly systematically ignored for almost 30 years until [...] If the recent youtube plagiarism drama[1] is anything to go by, you can do blatant plagiarism for years, get called out by randoms, and still get away with it. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":38814798,"author":"gchamonlive","month":"2023-12","story":"Things are about to get worse for generative AI","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example of plagiarism, contrasted with AI training.","quote":"","text":"It's plagiarism ( [VIDEO] ). It's not the same situation. You can't possibly expect someone to be exposed to the entirety of the internet like ChatGPT is. It is a matter of scale. If you still think they are the same thing, the industrial revolution was about scale and had transformative impacts in the society."}]},"yS7Jb58hcsc":{"wy":"HN cites it in tax and housing threads as a libertarian economist endorsing property or land taxes.","q":{"x":"Milton Friedman once commented that property tax is particularly unpopular because it's the only tax for which people still have to write a check","i":31074338,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31074338,"author":"lkbm","month":"2022-04","story":"TurboTax’s fight against free tax filing","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on why property tax is unpopular.","quote":"Milton Friedman once commented that property tax is particularly unpopular because it's the only tax for which people still have to write a check","text":"Milton Friedman once commented that property tax is particularly unpopular because it's the only tax for which people still have to write a check[0]. That said, we could make income tax more visible without requiring tax professionals. (e.g., end withholding and send me a monthly tax bill; show the money go into my bank account and then back out, whether automatically or forcing me to write a check.) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28172148,"author":"lukifer","month":"2021-08","story":"US real estate a safe haven for billions in dirty money, report says","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman's view on property taxes.","quote":"","text":"Many of the OG \"classical liberals\" had a much more nuanced take on privatizing land, supporting something more like usage rights, rather than fee-simple or alloidal land titles (private enclosure of land beyond subsistence was associated with aristocracy): \"All the property that is necessary to a man for the conservation of the individual and his propagation of the species is his natural right, which none can justly deprive him of; but all property superfluous to such purposes is the property of the public, who by their laws have created it, and who may therefore by other laws dispose of it, whenever the welfare of the public shall demand such disposition.\" - Benjamin Franklin \"Whenever there is in any country, uncultivated lands and unemployed poor, it is clear that the laws of property have been so far extended as to violate natural right. The earth is given as a common stock for man to labour and live on. If, for the encouragement of industry we allow it to be appropriated, we must take care that other employment be furnished to those excluded from the appropriation. If we do not the fundamental right to labour the earth returns to the unemployed.\" - Thomas Jefferson There is a wide range of middle-ground between the extremes of carving up the earth into micro-fiefdoms of absolute ownership, vs. all land being controlled by apparatchiks in the State Politburo. One of the most moderate is a tax on \"ground rents\", as proposed by Ricardo, Paine, and many others [0], to compe"},{"id":23796308,"author":"snidane","month":"2020-07","story":"Pandemic speeds largest test yet of universal basic income","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman on land value tax and negative income tax.","quote":"Both mentioned in this short clip [3:33 mins]","text":"Milton Friedman also proposed Land Value Tax as the least worst tax. Only the second least worst was this negative income tax. Both mentioned in this short clip [3:33 mins] \"Milton Friedman talks about property taxes\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":22199881,"author":"neilparikh","month":"2020-01","story":"California's housing bill SB 50 has died in the state Senate","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman considers property tax the least bad tax.","quote":"Milton Friedman, one of the most libertarian modern economists thinks property tax is the best tax","text":"> When you want to take what’s someone’s property and make sure you want to ‘allocate’ it to someone else who wants it..it’s called redistribution. No one's property is taken, they'll just sell it on the market to whoever can pay the most. That's not redistribution, it's called markets (what America is build on). I think we fundamentally disagree on economics, so I doubt we'll come any useful agreement here unfortunately. However, one thing I wanted to note is that Milton Friedman, one of the most libertarian modern economists thinks property tax is the best tax [1][2]. If a libertarian economist thinks a policy is good, yet you're comparing the policy to authoritarian socialist countries and re-education camps, you may want to consider that your mental model of economics/definitions of common words is extremely off base from how everyone else uses it. The Conservative Party in UK did the same thing, where they called land value tax (essentially what I'm arguing for here) a \"Marxist tax grab\", even when Marx opposed it! In general, using words to mean the exact opposite of what everyone else considers them is not a great strategy. Even ignoring that, I think claiming that property taxes are the \"same thing\" as re-education camps is a fairly tone-deaf statement to make (and one most readers would consider wrong), and it only serves to weaken the rest of your argument. [1]: in his words, the \"least bad tax\", but that's equivalent, since he considers taxes to be required [2]: se"}]}});
